export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"takeoffcraft","name":"TakeoffCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"What's new in 0.7","summary":"DWG and DXF drawings open as sheets with their own scale, exact counts and lengths from the drawing itself, the chosen subcontractor bid as a package's price, bid invitations with sealed prices, assistant tools for drawings, and USD from the 3D check view.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["whats new","what's new","release notes","changelog","changes","0.7","version","new features","dwg","dxf","drawing","layouts","model space","drawing scale","count from drawing","cad count","blocks","dynamic blocks","attributes","layers","xref","linked drawing","measure drawing","polyline","hatch","marked pdf","chosen bid","use a bid","send for bids","bid invitation","sealed bids","bid opening","late bid","subcontractor records","csv","assistant","mcp","usd","usdz","units","unit lists","compact","panels","dialogs","portuguese"],"headings":[{"id":"new","text":"New","level":2},{"id":"dwg-and-dxf-drawings","text":"DWG and DXF drawings","level":3},{"id":"exact-counts-and-lengths-from-a-drawing","text":"Exact counts and lengths from a drawing","level":3},{"id":"bids","text":"Bids","level":3},{"id":"ai-assistants","text":"AI assistants","level":3},{"id":"usd","text":"USD","level":3},{"id":"in-every-app","text":"In every app","level":2},{"id":"not-in-this-version-yet","text":"Not in this version yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThis is what changed in TakeoffCraft 0.7, compared with 0.6. Drawings now open as they are, with no plotting to PDF first, and a bid package can go out to subcontractors and come back priced.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new\"\u003ENew\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dwg-and-dxf-drawings\"\u003EDWG and DXF drawings\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpen a drawing as sheets.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or drop the files) takes DWG and DXF drawings beside PDFs. Model space and every layout become sheets in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E list, and a takeoff can cover drawings and PDFs together. Nothing is uploaded. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\"\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe scale comes from the drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E A layout starts at the scale of its biggest viewport, named as a preset when it is one, such as 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;. Model starts in the drawing&#039;s units. A drawing that has no units says &quot;No scale&quot; until you calibrate it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E still win. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing#the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-hash=\"the-scale\"\u003EThe scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayers and linked drawings.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar shows or hides the drawing&#039;s layers. Layers the file turned off or froze start hidden and are not counted unless you choose to. A linked drawing (xref) opened beside its host is drawn and counted where it is inserted; until it is open, the status bar says what is missing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing#layers\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-hash=\"layers\"\u003ELayers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe marked PDF works from a drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E draws a drawing&#039;s marked sheets as a PDF first, so the copy shows your marks and legend with no plotting step. It is drawn in black or the drawing&#039;s colours, without plot style tables. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-estimate#a-marked-pdf\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-estimate\" data-hash=\"a-marked-pdf\"\u003EA marked PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"exact-counts-and-lengths-from-a-drawing\"\u003EExact counts and lengths from a drawing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECount what the drawing says is there.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECAD count\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar counts a block by name (nested blocks, dynamic block states and block arrays included), everything of one type on a layer, or a text or attribute value. It counts what the sheet shows or the whole drawing once. Adding the marks is one undo step, and counting again adds only what is new. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E stays for PDFs and for symbols that are not blocks. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\"\u003ECount from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeasure from the drawing&#039;s own lines.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure drawing\u003C/span\u003E takes the lines, arcs, polylines, circles, hatches and solids you click or window as marks of a linear or area item. Arcs, bulges and a hatch&#039;s holes come out exact; ellipses and splines are measured in fine chords, and the bar says so. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-from-a-drawing\"\u003EMeasure from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"bids\"\u003EBids\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUse a subcontractor&#039;s bid as the package&#039;s price.\u003C/strong\u003E In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid packages and leveling…\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse\u003C/span\u003E on a bid. The package&#039;s items stay as quantities, one \u003Cstrong\u003EChosen bid\u003C/strong\u003E line carries the price, items the bid leaves out stay at your own cost, and overhead, profit and bond apply to it. Switching bids is one undo step; the workbook and the bid summary carry it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-leveling#use-a-bid-as-the-packages-price\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-leveling\" data-hash=\"use-a-bid-as-the-packages-price\"\u003EUse a bid as the package&#039;s price\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESend a package for bids.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend for bids…\u003C/span\u003E under a package emails each subcontractor a link to a page that needs no account: they see the scope and the sheets as a PDF, type a price on each line or mark it not included, add notes and up to three files, or decline. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGet prices\u003C/span\u003E pulls what came back into the leveling sheet, flagged \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb\u003C/strong\u003E. Sending is open only to accounts the CadCraft team has enabled while it is tested (see below). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\"\u003ESend a package for bids\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESealed until the opening.\u003C/strong\u003E Prices can stay sealed until an opening time you set: until then nobody sees a price, you included. At the opening every bid shows at once, and a bid that came after the due time is marked late. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-invitations#sealed-bids-and-the-opening\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\" data-hash=\"sealed-bids-and-the-opening\"\u003ESealed bids and the opening\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESubcontractor records.\u003C/strong\u003E Your account on getcadcraft.com keeps each company&#039;s contacts, trades, qualification answers, and insurance and license dates (flagged once past or within 30 days), with their past bids. Import and export them as CSV, and pick them when you send a package. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-invitations#on-the-website\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\" data-hash=\"on-the-website\"\u003EOn the website\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ai-assistants\"\u003EAI assistants\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAssistants can add to the takeoff.\u003C/strong\u003E With \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E ticked, your own assistant can add items (\u003Ccode\u003Eitems_add\u003C/code\u003E) and measure runs, areas and counts on a sheet (\u003Ccode\u003Emarks_add\u003C/code\u003E; lengths and areas only on a scaled sheet), each call one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAssistant tools for drawings.\u003C/strong\u003E On an open DWG or DXF an assistant can list its sheets and what can be counted, count exactly, add the count marks, and measure from the drawing&#039;s geometry. Without TakeoffCraft open, the CadCraft MCP server opens a drawing as a new takeoff and saves it, and \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft count\u003C/code\u003E counts from the command line. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/use-with-ai-assistants#what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\" data-hash=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EWhat an assistant can ask for\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"usd\"\u003EUSD\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe 3D check view as USD.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD package (.usdz)\u003C/span\u003E beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGLB\u003C/span\u003E in the 3D check view writes each wall, slab, plate, count post and run as its own object, named after its item and carrying its quantity, in metres. An assistant can export it too (\u003Ccode\u003Eusd_export\u003C/code\u003E). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/usd\"\u003EExport the 3D check view as USD\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-every-app\"\u003EIn every app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E has one list each for length, area, volume, angle and slope, with the exact sizes the units&#039; definitions give (the US survey foot is its own unit). Switch off the units you never use and they leave the unit lists; star one and new work starts in it. A file always keeps its own unit. In TakeoffCraft an item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E lists follow the switches, a new item starts in the starred length or area unit, roofing squares (SQ), acres and hectares are new area units, and a length box takes any unit&#039;s mark, such as \u003Ccode\u003E3 yd\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/unit-lists\" data-topic=\"basics/unit-lists\"\u003EUnits, typed values and the unit lists\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA denser layout and panels that tidy away.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout density\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompact\u003C/span\u003E fits more on the screen (touch screens and phones keep their large buttons). Each side panel has a slim title bar: drag it to dock or float the panel, drop it on another panel&#039;s bar to share one frame as tabs, pin it to slide away to the edge, or hide it and bring it back from the edge. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#make-the-layout-denser\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"make-the-layout-denser\"\u003EArrange the panels\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDialogs move.\u003C/strong\u003E Drag a dialog by its title bar out of the way of the drawing; it comes back where you left it. Double-click the title bar to centre it again. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#move-a-dialog\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"move-a-dialog\"\u003EMove a dialog\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERecipes for AI assistants.\u003C/strong\u003E Ten worked requests for your own assistant (tidy a drawing, plot a set, check drawings, ask a model questions, total a takeoff, try a part variant…), each with the tools it calls and the permissions it needs, and each replayed by our tests. The apps contain no AI. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003ERecipes for AI assistants\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELanguages.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Portugal)\u003C/strong\u003E is new in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanguage\u003C/span\u003E. It, \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Brasil)\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEspañol (México)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EBahasa Indonesia\u003C/strong\u003E now have every word of the apps, as drafts not yet reviewed by a native speaker. Help topics are in English.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E says why an update check failed (no file for your system yet, no network, a bad signature) instead of just &quot;Done&quot;, and stops bringing back a What&#039;s new card you closed until there is a newer release.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-in-this-version-yet\"\u003ENot in this version yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E0.7 is not code-signed.\u003C/strong\u003E Signing, and the Microsoft Store package, moved to 0.8 because the certificate validation has not come through yet. Check the SHA-256 beside each file on getcadcraft.com/download. On Windows, if SmartScreen stops the installer, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore info\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun anyway\u003C/span\u003E. On a Mac, the first time, right-click CadCraft Studio and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E. CadCraft Studio is not in an app store yet, and there is no iPad or iPhone build yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe angle and slope lists in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E are saved, but no box reads them yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBid invitations are switched off for most accounts.\u003C/strong\u003E While the feature is tested, mail to outside bidders goes out only from accounts the CadCraft team has enabled. Bidders&#039; pages work for every invitation already sent. Everyone else: download the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScope CSV\u003C/span\u003E, email it yourself and type the bids in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA CadCraft Studio window can&#039;t send bid invitations yet: it says to use TakeoffCraft in the browser. Opening a takeoff with sent requests, leveling and choosing a bid work in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAddenda, award letters, a scope sheet each bidder signs off and automatic reminders before an insurance date runs out are not built. A chosen bid does not reprice an alternate&#039;s bid area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrawings: plot style tables and CTB files are not applied, a binary DXF is refused (save it as ASCII DXF or DWG), linked drawings are found only among the open files by file name, and PDF underlays, images and OLE objects in a drawing are not drawn or counted. The counts and scales have been checked on sample drawings, not yet on drawings from a real job opened in the program that made them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn assistant can add items and marks but cannot move, change or delete marks that are already there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything else is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"This is what changed in TakeoffCraft 0.7, compared with 0.6. Drawings now open as they are, with no plotting to PDF first, and a bid package can go out to subcontractors and come back priced. New DWG and DXF drawings Open a drawing as sheets. Open plans in the File group (Ctrl+O, or drop the files) takes DWG and DXF drawings beside PDFs. Model space and every layout become sheets in the Sheets list, and a takeoff can cover drawings and PDFs together. Nothing is uploaded. See Open a DWG or DXF drawing. The scale comes from the drawing. A layout starts at the scale of its biggest viewport, named as a preset when it is one, such as 1/2\" = 1'-0\". Model starts in the drawing's units. A drawing that has no units says \"No scale\" until you calibrate it. Calibrate, Check and Preset still win. See The scale. Layers and linked drawings. Layers on the sheet bar shows or hides the drawing's layers. Layers the file turned off or froze start hidden and are not counted unless you choose to. A linked drawing (xref) opened beside its host is drawn and counted where it is inserted; until it is open, the status bar says what is missing. See Layers. The marked PDF works from a drawing. Export › Marked PDF draws a drawing's marked sheets as a PDF first, so the copy shows your marks and legend with no plotting step. It is drawn in black or the drawing's colours, without plot style tables. See A marked PDF. Exact counts and lengths from a drawing Count what the drawing says is there. CAD count on the sheet bar counts a block by name (nested blocks, dynamic block states and block arrays included), everything of one type on a layer, or a text or attribute value. It counts what the sheet shows or the whole drawing once. Adding the marks is one undo step, and counting again adds only what is new. Auto count stays for PDFs and for symbols that are not blocks. See Count from a drawing. Measure from the drawing's own lines. Measure drawing takes the lines, arcs, polylines, circles, hatches and solids you click or window as marks of a linear or area item. Arcs, bulges and a hatch's holes come out exact; ellipses and splines are measured in fine chords, and the bar says so. See Measure from a drawing. Bids Use a subcontractor's bid as the package's price. In Export › Bid packages and leveling…, choose Use on a bid. The package's items stay as quantities, one Chosen bid line carries the price, items the bid leaves out stay at your own cost, and overhead, profit and bond apply to it. Switching bids is one undo step; the workbook and the bid summary carry it. See Use a bid as the package's price. Send a package for bids. Send for bids… under a package emails each subcontractor a link to a page that needs no account: they see the scope and the sheets as a PDF, type a price on each line or mark it not included, add notes and up to three files, or decline. Get prices pulls what came back into the leveling sheet, flagged web. Sending is open only to accounts the CadCraft team has enabled while it is tested (see below). See Send a package for bids. Sealed until the opening. Prices can stay sealed until an opening time you set: until then nobody sees a price, you included. At the opening every bid shows at once, and a bid that came after the due time is marked late. See Sealed bids and the opening. Subcontractor records. Your account on getcadcraft.com keeps each company's contacts, trades, qualification answers, and insurance and license dates (flagged once past or within 30 days), with their past bids. Import and export them as CSV, and pick them when you send a package. See On the website. AI assistants Assistants can add to the takeoff. With Edit ticked, your own assistant can add items (items_add) and measure runs, areas and counts on a sheet (marks_add; lengths and areas only on a scaled sheet), each call one undo step. Assistant tools for drawings. On an open DWG or DXF an assistant can list its sheets and what can be counted, count exactly, add the count marks, and measure from the drawing's geometry. Without TakeoffCraft open, the CadCraft MCP server opens a drawing as a new takeoff and saves it, and cadcraft count counts from the command line. See What an assistant can ask for. USD The 3D check view as USD. USD package (.usdz) beside GLB in the 3D check view writes each wall, slab, plate, count post and run as its own object, named after its item and carrying its quantity, in metres. An assistant can export it too (usd_export). See Export the 3D check view as USD. In every app Units. Settings › General › Units has one list each for length, area, volume, angle and slope, with the exact sizes the units' definitions give (the US survey foot is its own unit). Switch off the units you never use and they leave the unit lists; star one and new work starts in it. A file always keeps its own unit. In TakeoffCraft an item's Unit lists follow the switches, a new item starts in the starred length or area unit, roofing squares (SQ), acres and hectares are new area units, and a length box takes any unit's mark, such as 3 yd. See Units, typed values and the unit lists. A denser layout and panels that tidy away. Settings › General › Layout density › Compact fits more on the screen (touch screens and phones keep their large buttons). Each side panel has a slim title bar: drag it to dock or float the panel, drop it on another panel's bar to share one frame as tabs, pin it to slide away to the edge, or hide it and bring it back from the edge. See Arrange the panels. Dialogs move. Drag a dialog by its title bar out of the way of the drawing; it comes back where you left it. Double-click the title bar to centre it again. See Move a dialog. Recipes for AI assistants. Ten worked requests for your own assistant (tidy a drawing, plot a set, check drawings, ask a model questions, total a takeoff, try a part variant…), each with the tools it calls and the permissions it needs, and each replayed by our tests. The apps contain no AI. See Recipes for AI assistants. Languages. Português (Portugal) is new in Settings › General › Language. It, Português (Brasil), Español (México) and Bahasa Indonesia now have every word of the apps, as drafts not yet reviewed by a native speaker. Help topics are in English. CadCraft Studio says why an update check failed (no file for your system yet, no network, a bad signature) instead of just \"Done\", and stops bringing back a What's new card you closed until there is a newer release. Not in this version yet 0.7 is not code-signed. Signing, and the Microsoft Store package, moved to 0.8 because the certificate validation has not come through yet. Check the SHA-256 beside each file on getcadcraft.com/download. On Windows, if SmartScreen stops the installer, choose More info, then Run anyway. On a Mac, the first time, right-click CadCraft Studio and choose Open. CadCraft Studio is not in an app store yet, and there is no iPad or iPhone build yet. The angle and slope lists in Units are saved, but no box reads them yet. Bid invitations are switched off for most accounts. While the feature is tested, mail to outside bidders goes out only from accounts the CadCraft team has enabled. Bidders' pages work for every invitation already sent. Everyone else: download the Scope CSV, email it yourself and type the bids in. A CadCraft Studio window can't send bid invitations yet: it says to use TakeoffCraft in the browser. Opening a takeoff with sent requests, leveling and choosing a bid work in Studio. Addenda, award letters, a scope sheet each bidder signs off and automatic reminders before an insurance date runs out are not built. A chosen bid does not reprice an alternate's bid area. Drawings: plot style tables and CTB files are not applied, a binary DXF is refused (save it as ASCII DXF or DWG), linked drawings are found only among the open files by file name, and PDF underlays, images and OLE objects in a drawing are not drawn or counted. The counts and scales have been checked on sample drawings, not yet on drawings from a real job opened in the program that made them. An assistant can add items and marks but cannot move, change or delete marks that are already there. Everything else is in Not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Getting started with TakeoffCraft","summary":"What TakeoffCraft does, where it runs, and your first measured and priced item from a PDF plan set.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["start","first takeoff","quick start","introduction","estimate","quantity takeoff","plan set","pdf"],"headings":[{"id":"where-it-runs","text":"Where it runs","level":2},{"id":"your-first-takeoff-in-ten-steps","text":"Your first takeoff in ten steps","level":2},{"id":"what-to-read-next","text":"What to read next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft measures plan sets, PDFs and DWG or DXF drawings, and turns the measurements into a priced estimate. You open the files (a drawing counts straight from its blocks: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\"\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E), give each sheet a scale (a drawing brings its own), make \u003Cstrong\u003Eitems\u003C/strong\u003E (a wall type, a floor finish, a fixture), and click on the sheets to take them off. Quantities, costs and the job total update as you go. You can export the estimate as a spreadsheet and a copy of the PDFs with your takeoff drawn in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you have done takeoff in other programs, read \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other takeoff software\u003C/a\u003E next. Some things work differently here.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-runs\"\u003EWhere it runs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft runs in your web browser. Open TakeoffCraft and the screen is ready at once; there is nothing to install.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYour PDFs stay on your computer.\u003C/strong\u003E They are opened by the browser and never uploaded. Saving is a download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt works in current Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt works with no internet connection once you choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar and it says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving is not available yet. For a desktop app, TakeoffCraft comes in CadCraft Studio, with the other seven apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-takeoff-in-ten-steps\"\u003EYour first takeoff in ten steps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis walk-through measures one wall type and prices it. It takes a few minutes on any plan you have.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group of the toolbar (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), pick one or more PDFs, and choose Open. You can also drop the PDFs anywhere on the page. The sheets appear on the left and the first sheet opens in the middle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the sheet&#039;s printed scale in its title block or under a drawing title.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group and pick that scale, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. The bar at the bottom of the sheet now says &quot;Scale 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click the two ends of a dimension printed on the drawing. The readout in the status bar at the bottom left should match the printed dimension. If it does not, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E, type a name like \u003Ccode\u003EInterior wall type A\u003C/code\u003E, leave the type on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear (length)\u003C/span\u003E, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the start of a wall, then each corner along it. Double-click, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click to finish the run. The run is drawn in the item&#039;s color and its length appears next to the item.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the item editor under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E9&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0.85\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial $/LF\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E1.20\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabor $/LF\u003C/span\u003E. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table at the bottom shows the length, the extended material and labor, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob total\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Your browser downloads a \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file. It holds your items, scales and marks, and reopens on top of the same PDFs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu in the toolbar and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E to download the estimate as a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop whatever you are doing and go back to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes any change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-to-read-next\"\u003EWhat to read next\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe TakeoffCraft screen\u003C/a\u003E names every part of the window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-plan-set\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-plan-set\"\u003EStart a takeoff and open a plan set\u003C/a\u003E covers several PDFs and the sheets list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E explains types, units, waste and rates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E turns one measurement into studs, sheets of board and more.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E lists every key.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft measures plan sets, PDFs and DWG or DXF drawings, and turns the measurements into a priced estimate. You open the files (a drawing counts straight from its blocks: see Open a DWG or DXF drawing), give each sheet a scale (a drawing brings its own), make items (a wall type, a floor finish, a fixture), and click on the sheets to take them off. Quantities, costs and the job total update as you go. You can export the estimate as a spreadsheet and a copy of the PDFs with your takeoff drawn in. If you have done takeoff in other programs, read Coming from other takeoff software next. Some things work differently here. Where it runs TakeoffCraft runs in your web browser. Open TakeoffCraft and the screen is ready at once; there is nothing to install. Your PDFs stay on your computer. They are opened by the browser and never uploaded. Saving is a download. It works in current Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari. See Browsers and computers. It works with no internet connection once you choose Save for offline in the app bar and it says Offline ready. See Working offline. Cloud saving is not available yet. For a desktop app, TakeoffCraft comes in CadCraft Studio, with the other seven apps. Your first takeoff in ten steps This walk-through measures one wall type and prices it. It takes a few minutes on any plan you have. Choose Open plans in the File group of the toolbar (or press Ctrl+O), pick one or more PDFs, and choose Open. You can also drop the PDFs anywhere on the page. The sheets appear on the left and the first sheet opens in the middle. Find the sheet's printed scale in its title block or under a drawing title. Open the Preset list in the Scale group and pick that scale, for example 1/4\" = 1'-0\". The bar at the bottom of the sheet now says \"Scale 1/4\" = 1'-0\"\". Choose Check (or press D) and click the two ends of a dimension printed on the drawing. The readout in the status bar at the bottom left should match the printed dimension. If it does not, see Set the scale. In the Items panel on the right, choose New item, type a name like Interior wall type A, leave the type on Linear (length), and choose Add. Choose Linear in the toolbar (or press L). Click the start of a wall, then each corner along it. Double-click, press Enter or right-click to finish the run. The run is drawn in the item's color and its length appears next to the item. In the item editor under Items, type 9'-0\" in Height, 0.85 in Material $/LF and 1.20 in Labor $/LF. The Quantities table at the bottom shows the length, the extended material and labor, and the Job total. Choose Save takeoff (or press Ctrl+S). Your browser downloads a .takeoff.json file. It holds your items, scales and marks, and reopens on top of the same PDFs. Open the Export menu in the toolbar and choose XLSX to download the estimate as a spreadsheet. Press Esc to stop whatever you are doing and go back to the Select tool. Ctrl+Z undoes any change. What to read next The TakeoffCraft screen names every part of the window. Start a takeoff and open a plan set covers several PDFs and the sheets list. Create takeoff items explains types, units, waste and rates. Assemblies and formulas turns one measurement into studs, sheets of board and more. Keyboard and mouse lists every key.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"The TakeoffCraft screen","summary":"A tour of the window by on-screen name - the toolbar, the sheets list, the sheet, the Items panel, the Quantities table and the status bar.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["screen","layout","layouts","ribbon","project browser","toolbox","info box","toolbar","panels","sheets list","items panel","quantities","status bar","tour","interface"],"headings":[{"id":"the-toolbar","text":"The toolbar","level":2},{"id":"sheets-left","text":"Sheets (left)","level":2},{"id":"the-sheet-middle","text":"The sheet (middle)","level":2},{"id":"items-right","text":"Items (right)","level":2},{"id":"quantities-bottom","text":"Quantities (bottom)","level":2},{"id":"status-bar-bottom","text":"Status bar (bottom)","level":2},{"id":"other-layouts","text":"Other layouts","level":2},{"id":"full-screen-and-themes","text":"Full screen and themes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft fills the browser window under the CadCraft app bar. From top to bottom and left to right:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPart\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it is for\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApp bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVery top\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe app name, the version, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbout TakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull screen\u003C/span\u003E button and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E theme switch.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EToolbar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnder the app bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery command, in groups: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery page of every open PDF, with a thumbnail, its scale and its mark count.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe open page. You measure here.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItems\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour takeoff items, the selected item&#039;s settings, and the selected mark.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantities\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBottom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantities and costs by item or by sheet, and the job total.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStatus bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVery bottom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe live measurement, messages, the 45° hint and the zoom.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar\"\u003EThe toolbar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHover over any button to see what it does and its key.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButtons\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E (empty takeoff), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen PDFs\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen takeoff\u003C/span\u003E (a \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E project), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, an on/off switch), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, an on/off switch), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tool you are using is shown pressed. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E buttons stay pressed while they are on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-left\"\u003ESheets (left)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESearch sheets\u003C/strong\u003E filters the list by sheet name, file name or page label.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach sheet shows a thumbnail (drawn as you scroll), its name, and either its scale or \u003Cstrong\u003ENo scale\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe right side of each row shows how many marks the sheet has. While an item is highlighted, it shows how many of that item&#039;s marks are on the sheet instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a sheet to open it. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E move to the next and previous sheet in the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen a takeoff covers PDFs that are not open, a note under the list names them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheet names come from the PDF: a page label such as \u003Ccode\u003EA-101\u003C/code\u003E becomes the sheet name. A page with no label is named after its file and page number, like &quot;Plans, page 3&quot;. With several PDFs open, labeled sheets also show the file name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-sheet-middle\"\u003EThe sheet (middle)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe bar under the sheet shows the sheet name and its scale, or &quot;No scale yet: pick a preset or calibrate&quot;. Its buttons: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E (when that app can take the PDF; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-plan-set\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-plan-set\"\u003EStart a takeoff and open a plan set\u003C/a\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E (an older version of the sheet over this one: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/compare-revisions\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/compare-revisions\"\u003ECompare a revised sheet\u003C/a\u003E), &quot;(n) to check&quot; (flagged marks, when there are any) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E (the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\"\u003E3D check view\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBefore any PDF is open, the middle shows a short five-step guide.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe wheel zooms at the pointer. Drag to pan. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E, a bar with its controls appears at the top of the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"items-right\"\u003EItems (right)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the top, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid area\u003C/span\u003E list: the building, floor or phase new marks go in (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-areas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-areas\"\u003EBid areas\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E opens a small form: a name, a type (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear (length)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe list shows each item&#039;s color, type icon, name and quantity. Click an item to draw with it and highlight its marks on every sheet. Click it again to stop highlighting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder the list, \u003Cstrong\u003EItem: (name)\u003C/strong\u003E is the item editor: name, type, color, unit, height or depth, waste, material and labor rates, the measured values, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E appears when you select a mark: which item it belongs to, its sheet, its measurements, and buttons such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a cut-out\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete mark\u003C/span\u003E. With several marks selected it becomes \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected marks\u003C/strong\u003E: how many of each item, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete marks\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe item editor ends with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy item\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate item\u003C/span\u003E: an item with its assembly and costs, without marks. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate marks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"quantities-bottom\"\u003EQuantities (bottom)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy item\u003C/span\u003E lists every item and its assembly components with quantity, waste, rates and extended costs. You can type waste and rates straight into the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy sheet\u003C/span\u003E lists each item&#039;s quantity on each sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA warning starting with \u003Cstrong\u003EIncomplete:\u003C/strong\u003E appears when something is left out of the totals.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob total\u003C/span\u003E is at the right of the header.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe arrow button at the far right hides or shows the table to give the sheet more room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/read-quantities\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/read-quantities\"\u003ERead quantities and the estimate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"status-bar-bottom\"\u003EStatus bar (bottom)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom left to right: the live measurement while you draw (length, area, radius or a check result), the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E box while you type a length, messages from TakeoffCraft, the reminder &quot;Shift snaps to 45°&quot; (or &quot;Snaps to 45°; Shift frees the angle&quot; when that is on in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/a\u003E), the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E button (snapping to the drawing&#039;s lines, on when highlighted; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), and the zoom percentage.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERead the status bar when something does not happen. TakeoffCraft explains there why it refused a click, for example &quot;This sheet has no scale yet&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"other-layouts\"\u003EOther layouts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything above is the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft classic\u003C/span\u003E layout, the start. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E offers two more; the same tools and keys work in all three, and picking \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft classic\u003C/span\u003E again puts every control back where it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELayout\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn TakeoffCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon and project browser\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff ribbon\u003C/span\u003E across the top (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E), a bar for the running tool, a tab for each sheet you open (a drawing&#039;s Model and layouts are sheets too) with the scale presets and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E under the sheet. The left panel holds \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E (the selected item, note or mark) over the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff browser\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E (click one to open it in a tab), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConditions\u003C/span\u003E (click one to draw with it) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEstimate\u003C/span\u003E (each item&#039;s total and the job total); the thumbnails are its second tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbox and info box\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe toolbar over the sheet, an info box with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid area\u003C/span\u003E, the running tool or the selection and the sheet bar, a column of the takeoff tools on the left, a tab for every sheet, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E and the zoom under the sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\"\u003E3D check view\u003C/a\u003E is open, the sheet tabs and the bar under the sheet step aside; close the 3D view to measure again. On a phone the phone layout always runs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"full-screen-and-themes\"\u003EFull screen and themes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull screen\u003C/span\u003E button in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (Control on every platform, also on a Mac), cycles through normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. With the bars hidden, move the pointer to the top edge to show them again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E is the light theme and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E the dark one. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E the drawing is shown inverted (light lines on dark) on screen only; exports are never changed.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft fills the browser window under the CadCraft app bar. From top to bottom and left to right: Part Where What it is for App bar Very top The app name, the version, About TakeoffCraft, Help (F1), Save for offline, Settings (Ctrl+,), the Full screen button and the Paper / Model theme switch. Toolbar Under the app bar Every command, in groups: File, Export, Scale, Takeoff, Edit, View. Sheets Left Every page of every open PDF, with a thumbnail, its scale and its mark count. The sheet Middle The open page. You measure here. Items Right Your takeoff items, the selected item's settings, and the selected mark. Quantities Bottom Quantities and costs by item or by sheet, and the job total. Status bar Very bottom The live measurement, messages, the 45° hint and the zoom. The toolbar Hover over any button to see what it does and its key. Group Buttons File New (empty takeoff), Open PDFs (Ctrl+O), Open takeoff (a .takeoff.json project), Save takeoff (Ctrl+S) Export One menu: XLSX, CSV, Marked PDF Scale Calibrate (K), Check (D), and the Preset list Takeoff Select (V), Linear (L), Area (A), Circle (O), Arc (R, an on/off switch), Deduct (E, an on/off switch), Cut-out (X), Count (C), Auto count (S) Edit Undo (Ctrl+Z), Redo (Ctrl+Y), Copy (Ctrl+C), Paste (Ctrl+V), and More: Cut, Copy, Paste, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all, Select none View Fit page (F), Fit width (W) The tool you are using is shown pressed. The Arc and Deduct buttons stay pressed while they are on. Sheets (left) Search sheets filters the list by sheet name, file name or page label. Each sheet shows a thumbnail (drawn as you scroll), its name, and either its scale or No scale. The right side of each row shows how many marks the sheet has. While an item is highlighted, it shows how many of that item's marks are on the sheet instead. Click a sheet to open it. Page Down and Page Up move to the next and previous sheet in the list. When a takeoff covers PDFs that are not open, a note under the list names them. See Save and reopen a takeoff. Sheet names come from the PDF: a page label such as A-101 becomes the sheet name. A page with no label is named after its file and page number, like \"Plans, page 3\". With several PDFs open, labeled sheets also show the file name. The sheet (middle) The bar under the sheet shows the sheet name and its scale, or \"No scale yet: pick a preset or calibrate\". Its buttons: MarkupCraft (when that app can take the PDF; see Start a takeoff and open a plan set), Compare (an older version of the sheet over this one: Compare a revised sheet), \"(n) to check\" (flagged marks, when there are any) and 3D (the 3D check view). Before any PDF is open, the middle shows a short five-step guide. The wheel zooms at the pointer. Drag to pan. See Keyboard and mouse. While you use Auto count, a bar with its controls appears at the top of the sheet. Items (right) At the top, the Bid area list: the building, floor or phase new marks go in (Bid areas). New item opens a small form: a name, a type (Linear (length), Area or Count) and Add. The list shows each item's color, type icon, name and quantity. Click an item to draw with it and highlight its marks on every sheet. Click it again to stop highlighting. Under the list, Item: (name) is the item editor: name, type, color, unit, height or depth, waste, material and labor rates, the measured values, and the Assembly. See Create takeoff items. Selected mark appears when you select a mark: which item it belongs to, its sheet, its measurements, and buttons such as Add a cut-out and Delete mark. With several marks selected it becomes Selected marks: how many of each item, and Copy, Duplicate, Cut and Delete marks. The item editor ends with Copy item and Duplicate item: an item with its assembly and costs, without marks. See Copy, paste and duplicate marks. Quantities (bottom) By item lists every item and its assembly components with quantity, waste, rates and extended costs. You can type waste and rates straight into the table. By sheet lists each item's quantity on each sheet. A warning starting with Incomplete: appears when something is left out of the totals. Job total is at the right of the header. The arrow button at the far right hides or shows the table to give the sheet more room. See Read quantities and the estimate. Status bar (bottom) From left to right: the live measurement while you draw (length, area, radius or a check result), the Type box while you type a length, messages from TakeoffCraft, the reminder \"Shift snaps to 45°\" (or \"Snaps to 45°; Shift frees the angle\" when that is on in Settings), the Snap button (snapping to the drawing's lines, on when highlighted; F3), and the zoom percentage. Read the status bar when something does not happen. TakeoffCraft explains there why it refused a click, for example \"This sheet has no scale yet\". Other layouts Everything above is the CadCraft classic layout, the start. Settings (Ctrl+,) › Global › Layout offers two more; the same tools and keys work in all three, and picking CadCraft classic again puts every control back where it was. Layout In TakeoffCraft Ribbon and project browser The Takeoff ribbon across the top (Menus, keys and mouse), a bar for the running tool, a tab for each sheet you open (a drawing's Model and layouts are sheets too) with the scale presets and Fit page under the sheet. The left panel holds Properties (the selected item, note or mark) over the Takeoff browser: Sheets (click one to open it in a tab), Conditions (click one to draw with it) and Estimate (each item's total and the job total); the thumbnails are its second tab. Toolbox and info box The toolbar over the sheet, an info box with the Bid area, the running tool or the selection and the sheet bar, a column of the takeoff tools on the left, a tab for every sheet, and Snap and the zoom under the sheet. While the 3D check view is open, the sheet tabs and the bar under the sheet step aside; close the 3D view to measure again. On a phone the phone layout always runs. Full screen and themes The Full screen button in the app bar, or Ctrl+Shift+F (Control on every platform, also on a Mac), cycles through normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. With the bars hidden, move the pointer to the top edge to show them again. Paper is the light theme and Model the dark one. In Model the drawing is shown inverted (light lines on dark) on screen only; exports are never changed.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"files","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Files TakeoffCraft opens and saves","summary":"PDFs in, a takeoff project file, spreadsheets and marked PDFs out - what each holds, where it goes, and what changes online, offline and between browsers.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["files","formats","pdf","takeoff.json","project file","xlsx","csv","marked pdf","download","save","open","offline"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-opens","text":"What it opens","level":2},{"id":"what-it-saves","text":"What it saves","level":2},{"id":"where-downloads-go","text":"Where downloads go","level":2},{"id":"why-the-project-file-and-the-pdfs-are-separate","text":"Why the project file and the PDFs are separate","level":2},{"id":"online-and-offline","text":"Online and offline","level":2},{"id":"spreadsheets","text":"Spreadsheets","level":2},{"id":"desktop-and-cloud","text":"Desktop and cloud","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-opens\"\u003EWhat it opens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPDF (\u003Ccode\u003E.pdf\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or drop on the page\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeveral at once. Each page becomes a sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoff project (\u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen takeoff\u003C/span\u003E, or drop on the page\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour items, scales and marks. You can drop it together with its PDFs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOther files dropped on the page are skipped, and the status bar names them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPassword-protected PDFs\u003C/strong\u003E do not open: &quot;(name) is password-protected. Open an unprotected copy.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe same PDF twice\u003C/strong\u003E is opened once: &quot;(name) is already open.&quot; TakeoffCraft knows files by their contents, not their names.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScanned PDFs\u003C/strong\u003E open and measure like any other; TakeoffCraft does not need the text in the PDF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELarge plan sets\u003C/strong\u003E: every PDF is read in full when it opens (to fingerprint it), so a very large file takes a moment. Thumbnails are drawn one at a time as you scroll.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-saves\"\u003EWhat it saves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery save is a download. Nothing is written back into your PDFs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou choose\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou get\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat is in it\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(name).takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItems, assemblies, rates, sheet scales and every mark, keyed to each PDF by its contents. No PDF pages.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(name).xlsx\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFour worksheets: Estimate (with live formulas), By sheet, Marks, Scales.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(name).csv\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe estimate as plain values, one row per item and component.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(PDF name) (takeoff).pdf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA copy of each open PDF that has marks, with the takeoff drawn on its pages and a legend. One file per PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe name is taken from the first PDF you opened in this takeoff. There is no rename box yet: rename the downloaded file if you want another name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-estimate\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-estimate\"\u003EExport the estimate and a marked PDF\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-columns\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-columns\"\u003EExport columns\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-downloads-go\"\u003EWhere downloads go\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour browser saves them where it saves every download, usually the Downloads folder. Find it in \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E. If your browser is set to ask where to save each file, it asks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E with several marked PDFs downloads several files at once. Chrome and Edge may ask once whether this site may download multiple files. Choose Allow.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari saves to Downloads unless you changed the download location in Safari Settings, under General.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"why-the-project-file-and-the-pdfs-are-separate\"\u003EWhy the project file and the PDFs are separate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file holds only your takeoff. It finds its PDFs again by their contents (a fingerprint of each file), so:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERenaming or moving a PDF does not matter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA PDF that was changed or saved again by another program counts as a different file, even with the same name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMarks on PDFs that are not open still count in the totals.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-and-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour PDFs and takeoffs never leave your computer, online or offline. The connection matters only for loading TakeoffCraft itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo work with no connection, save the app first, while you are online:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar at the top. The button counts up, &quot;Saving 12 of 40&quot;, while it saves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving TakeoffCraft keeps the app, this help, the PDF reader, the PDF writer used by \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E and the auto count worker in your browser, about 6 MB. Once it says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, everything works offline: opening PDFs, measuring, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe button says\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot saved in this browser yet. Choose it to save.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving (n) of (m)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving now.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaved. Choose it for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the offline copy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome files did not download. Check your connection and choose it again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis browser window cannot keep an offline copy (private windows and some managed computers block it).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMore, including how long a saved copy lasts in each browser: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline. If the app bar shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, everything works. If it does not, the parts TakeoffCraft has not loaded yet in this tab (the PDF writer for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E, the auto count worker) cannot load now, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E stops with &quot;The marked PDF could not be written&quot;. Your takeoff is safe either way: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E need no connection. Do not reload the tab until you are online again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:online\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOnline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are online. Before a site visit or a flight, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E and wait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"spreadsheets\"\u003ESpreadsheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe XLSX keeps live formulas, so a spreadsheet program recalculates the estimate when it opens the file and keeps it right when you change a rate or waste there. The workbook has been checked in LibreOffice Calc, which recalculated every total to the same cent. It has not been opened in Excel yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe CSV is plain values with a byte order mark, so spreadsheet programs read characters such as m² correctly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"desktop-and-cloud\"\u003EDesktop and cloud\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"shell:desktop\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EDesktop app\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom 0.5 the desktop app is CadCraft Studio: TakeoffCraft and the other seven apps in one install. There is no separate TakeoffCraft installer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving is not available yet. Keep your \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E files with the job&#039;s PDFs, and back them up like any other file. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it opens File How Notes PDF (.pdf) Open plans, Ctrl+O, or drop on the page Several at once. Each page becomes a sheet. Takeoff project (.takeoff.json) Open takeoff, or drop on the page Your items, scales and marks. You can drop it together with its PDFs. Other files dropped on the page are skipped, and the status bar names them. Password-protected PDFs do not open: \"(name) is password-protected. Open an unprotected copy.\" The same PDF twice is opened once: \"(name) is already open.\" TakeoffCraft knows files by their contents, not their names. Scanned PDFs open and measure like any other; TakeoffCraft does not need the text in the PDF. Large plan sets: every PDF is read in full when it opens (to fingerprint it), so a very large file takes a moment. Thumbnails are drawn one at a time as you scroll. What it saves Every save is a download. Nothing is written back into your PDFs. You choose You get What is in it Save takeoff (name).takeoff.json Items, assemblies, rates, sheet scales and every mark, keyed to each PDF by its contents. No PDF pages. XLSX (name).xlsx Four worksheets: Estimate (with live formulas), By sheet, Marks, Scales. CSV (name).csv The estimate as plain values, one row per item and component. Marked PDF (PDF name) (takeoff).pdf A copy of each open PDF that has marks, with the takeoff drawn on its pages and a legend. One file per PDF. The name is taken from the first PDF you opened in this takeoff. There is no rename box yet: rename the downloaded file if you want another name. See Export the estimate and a marked PDF and Export columns. Where downloads go Your browser saves them where it saves every download, usually the Downloads folder. Find it in Finder / File Explorer / your file manager. If your browser is set to ask where to save each file, it asks. Marked PDF with several marked PDFs downloads several files at once. Chrome and Edge may ask once whether this site may download multiple files. Choose Allow. Safari saves to Downloads unless you changed the download location in Safari Settings, under General. Why the project file and the PDFs are separate The .takeoff.json file holds only your takeoff. It finds its PDFs again by their contents (a fingerprint of each file), so: Renaming or moving a PDF does not matter. A PDF that was changed or saved again by another program counts as a different file, even with the same name. Marks on PDFs that are not open still count in the totals. See Save and reopen a takeoff. Online and offline Your PDFs and takeoffs never leave your computer, online or offline. The connection matters only for loading TakeoffCraft itself. To work with no connection, save the app first, while you are online: Choose Save for offline in the app bar at the top. The button counts up, \"Saving 12 of 40\", while it saves. Wait for Offline ready. Saving TakeoffCraft keeps the app, this help, the PDF reader, the PDF writer used by Marked PDF and the auto count worker in your browser, about 6 MB. Once it says Offline ready, everything works offline: opening PDFs, measuring, Auto count, Save takeoff, XLSX, CSV and Marked PDF. The button says Meaning Save for offline Not saved in this browser yet. Choose it to save. Saving (n) of (m) Saving now. Offline ready Saved. Choose it for Save again (get the newest version) or Remove the offline copy. Could not save Some files did not download. Check your connection and choose it again. Offline not available This browser window cannot keep an offline copy (private windows and some managed computers block it). More, including how long a saved copy lasts in each browser: Working offline. You are offline. If the app bar shows Offline ready, everything works. If it does not, the parts TakeoffCraft has not loaded yet in this tab (the PDF writer for Marked PDF, the auto count worker) cannot load now, and Marked PDF stops with \"The marked PDF could not be written\". Your takeoff is safe either way: Save takeoff, XLSX and CSV need no connection. Do not reload the tab until you are online again. You are online. Before a site visit or a flight, choose Save for offline and wait for Offline ready. Spreadsheets The XLSX keeps live formulas, so a spreadsheet program recalculates the estimate when it opens the file and keeps it right when you change a rate or waste there. The workbook has been checked in LibreOffice Calc, which recalculated every total to the same cent. It has not been opened in Excel yet. The CSV is plain values with a byte order mark, so spreadsheet programs read characters such as m² correctly. Desktop and cloud From 0.5 the desktop app is CadCraft Studio: TakeoffCraft and the other seven apps in one install. There is no separate TakeoffCraft installer. Cloud saving is not available yet. Keep your .takeoff.json files with the job's PDFs, and back them up like any other file. See Where your files go.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"open-plan-set","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Start a takeoff and open a plan set","summary":"Open one or several PDFs as a plan set, find your way round the sheets, and move between them.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["open","plan set","several pdfs","multiple files","sheets","drawings","page labels","sheet list","navigate","drop","handoff","open in another app","markup"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-pdfs","text":"Open the PDFs","level":2},{"id":"drawings-beside-pdfs","text":"Drawings beside PDFs","level":2},{"id":"a-pdf-handed-over-from-another-app","text":"A PDF handed over from another app","level":2},{"id":"find-a-sheet","text":"Find a sheet","level":2},{"id":"how-sheets-are-named","text":"How sheets are named","level":2},{"id":"look-around-a-sheet","text":"Look around a sheet","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA plan set is often several PDFs: architectural, structural, mechanical. TakeoffCraft opens them side by side as one list of sheets, and one takeoff can cover all of them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-pdfs\"\u003EOpen the PDFs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. (The other menu styles call it \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen PDFs…\u003C/span\u003E.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the file dialog, select every PDF of the set. Hold \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003ECommand\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E to pick several, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to pick a range.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose Open. A &quot;Opening (name)…&quot; message shows while each file is read. Then every page appears under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E and the first page of the first new PDF opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also drag the PDFs from \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E and drop them anywhere on TakeoffCraft. A &quot;Drop PDFs, DWG or DXF drawings, or a .takeoff.json&quot; frame appears while you drag.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo add more PDFs later, do the same again. PDFs that are already open are skipped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe start screen also lists the PDFs and takeoffs you opened recently, in browsers that let a page open a file again (Chrome and Edge). Click one to open it; the browser may ask for permission first. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EYour files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing is uploaded. The browser reads the PDFs on your computer, and they are gone from TakeoffCraft when you close the tab or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings-beside-pdfs\"\u003EDrawings beside PDFs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E also takes DWG and DXF drawings, alone or together with PDFs: each drawing&#039;s model space and layouts become sheets in the same list. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\"\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E, then \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\"\u003ECount from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pdf-handed-over-from-another-app\"\u003EA PDF handed over from another app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnother app of the lineup can hand you the PDF it has open: it opens TakeoffCraft in a new tab and the PDF opens there as if you had dropped it. The status bar says &quot;Opened (name), handed over from (app). The original file is unchanged.&quot; Nothing is uploaded; the file passes through this browser&#039;s own storage and is gone from it once it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the status bar says the PDF &quot;did not arrive&quot;, the tab waited too long or the PDF was already opened in another tab: open it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe other way round, when MarkupCraft runs where you are and can take a file, the sheet bar under the drawing has a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E button: it opens the PDF of the sheet on screen in MarkupCraft, in a new tab, to review and mark it up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-a-sheet\"\u003EFind a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E list on the left and click a sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in \u003Cstrong\u003ESearch sheets\u003C/strong\u003E at the top of the list to filter by sheet number, file name or page label. The count beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E shows &quot;3 of 40&quot; while a filter is on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the next sheet and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the previous one. They follow the list as filtered.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac keyboard without Page Up and Page Down keys, press fn with the up or down arrow.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-sheets-are-named\"\u003EHow sheets are named\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA page whose PDF gives it a label, such as \u003Ccode\u003EA-101\u003C/code\u003E, is named by that label.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA page with no label (or a label that is just its page number) is named &quot;(file name), page (n)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith several PDFs open, labeled sheets also carry the file name: &quot;A-101 — Architectural&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheet names cannot be typed in by hand yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-around-a-sheet\"\u003ELook around a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn the mouse wheel to zoom at the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with the left or middle button to pan. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E, or when you start on a mark&#039;s corner, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag, or drag with the middle button.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E to see the whole sheet; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E fits its width.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"=\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to zoom in and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to zoom out, around the middle of the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach sheet remembers its own zoom and position while it stays open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore you can measure a sheet, it needs a scale: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A plan set is often several PDFs: architectural, structural, mechanical. TakeoffCraft opens them side by side as one list of sheets, and one takeoff can cover all of them. Open the PDFs Choose Open plans in the File group, or press Ctrl+O. (The other menu styles call it Open PDFs….) In the file dialog, select every PDF of the set. Hold Command / Ctrl / Ctrl to pick several, or Shift to pick a range. Choose Open. A \"Opening (name)…\" message shows while each file is read. Then every page appears under Sheets and the first page of the first new PDF opens. You can also drag the PDFs from Finder / File Explorer / your file manager and drop them anywhere on TakeoffCraft. A \"Drop PDFs, DWG or DXF drawings, or a .takeoff.json\" frame appears while you drag. To add more PDFs later, do the same again. PDFs that are already open are skipped. The start screen also lists the PDFs and takeoffs you opened recently, in browsers that let a page open a file again (Chrome and Edge). Click one to open it; the browser may ask for permission first. See Your files. Nothing is uploaded. The browser reads the PDFs on your computer, and they are gone from TakeoffCraft when you close the tab or choose New. Drawings beside PDFs Open plans also takes DWG and DXF drawings, alone or together with PDFs: each drawing's model space and layouts become sheets in the same list. See Open a DWG or DXF drawing, then Count from a drawing. A PDF handed over from another app Another app of the lineup can hand you the PDF it has open: it opens TakeoffCraft in a new tab and the PDF opens there as if you had dropped it. The status bar says \"Opened (name), handed over from (app). The original file is unchanged.\" Nothing is uploaded; the file passes through this browser's own storage and is gone from it once it opens. If the status bar says the PDF \"did not arrive\", the tab waited too long or the PDF was already opened in another tab: open it with Open plans. The other way round, when MarkupCraft runs where you are and can take a file, the sheet bar under the drawing has a MarkupCraft button: it opens the PDF of the sheet on screen in MarkupCraft, in a new tab, to review and mark it up. Find a sheet Scroll the Sheets list on the left and click a sheet. Type in Search sheets at the top of the list to filter by sheet number, file name or page label. The count beside Sheets shows \"3 of 40\" while a filter is on. Press Page Down for the next sheet and Page Up for the previous one. They follow the list as filtered. On a Mac keyboard without Page Up and Page Down keys, press fn with the up or down arrow. How sheets are named A page whose PDF gives it a label, such as A-101, is named by that label. A page with no label (or a label that is just its page number) is named \"(file name), page (n)\". With several PDFs open, labeled sheets also carry the file name: \"A-101 — Architectural\". Sheet names cannot be typed in by hand yet. Look around a sheet Turn the mouse wheel to zoom at the pointer. Drag with the left or middle button to pan. With Auto count, or when you start on a mark's corner, hold Space while you drag, or drag with the middle button. Press F or choose Fit page to see the whole sheet; W or Fit width fits its width. Press + or = to zoom in and − to zoom out, around the middle of the view. Each sheet remembers its own zoom and position while it stays open. Next Before you can measure a sheet, it needs a scale: Set the scale.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"open-a-drawing","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Open a DWG or DXF drawing","summary":"Open a drawing file as sheets (model space and every layout), start with the scale the drawing gives, show or hide its layers, and bring in linked drawings.","section":"tasks","order":12,"keywords":["dwg","dxf","cad","drawing","open a drawing","layouts","model space","paper space","sheets","units","insunits","viewport scale","xref","linked drawing","layers","frozen","no pdf","plot","without plotting"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-drawing","text":"Open the drawing","level":2},{"id":"sheets","text":"Sheets","level":2},{"id":"the-scale","text":"The scale","level":2},{"id":"layers","text":"Layers","level":2},{"id":"linked-drawings-xrefs","text":"Linked drawings (xrefs)","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft opens a DWG or DXF drawing as it opens a PDF: you do not have to plot it to PDF first. Counting from the drawing itself is exact, because the drawing knows what each object is. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\"\u003ECount from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-drawing\"\u003EOpen the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the DWG or DXF file. You can select several, and PDFs with them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose Open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also drop drawing files on TakeoffCraft, like PDFs. Nothing is uploaded: the browser reads the file on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft reads DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 and ASCII DXF from R12 to 2018. It refuses a binary DXF (save it as ASCII, or as a DWG) and a file that is neither, and says why. A damaged file opens with what could be read, and a message says so; its counts may be incomplete.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets\"\u003ESheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E is the drawing&#039;s model space.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEach layout\u003C/strong\u003E (paper space tab) is a sheet, named by its tab, in tab order. A layout shows the model through its viewports, as it plots.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EZoom, pan, snapping to the drawing&#039;s own lines, marks, notes, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E and the rest work as on a PDF. A drawing&#039;s sheets are saved in the takeoff by the file&#039;s name and a fingerprint of its contents, like a PDF: open the same file again and the marks are on its sheets.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-scale\"\u003EThe scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sheet starts with the scale the drawing gives:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA layout\u003C/strong\u003E takes the scale of its biggest viewport. A scale such as 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; is named as the preset it is; any other is shown as &quot;From the drawing&#039;s viewport: 1&quot; = 2.5&#039;&quot;. If a layout has viewports at other scales, the biggest sets the sheet&#039;s scale: check the others with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E takes the drawing&#039;s units (inches, millimeters, feet…) as its scale, &quot;Drawing units (inches)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA drawing with no units\u003C/strong\u003E (the drawing never said whether a unit is an inch or a millimeter) has no scale: the sheet says &quot;No scale&quot;, and measuring on it is refused, as on a PDF with no scale. Counting needs no scale. Give it one with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list change a scale at any time and win over the drawing&#039;s. The preset list is off on Model, which has no paper size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"layers\"\u003ELayers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar lists the drawing&#039;s layers with how many objects each has on this sheet. Layers the file turned off or froze start hidden. Untick a layer to hide it, or tick a hidden one to show it: the sheet draws again, and what is hidden is left out of counts and of the objects offered to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure drawing\u003C/span\u003E, unless you ask to count hidden layers too. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E shows every layer; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the file&#039;s layers\u003C/span\u003E undoes your changes. Layer choices belong to this session, not to the takeoff file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"linked-drawings-xrefs\"\u003ELinked drawings (xrefs)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing that links other drawings shows them where they are inserted. TakeoffCraft finds a linked drawing among the \u003Cstrong\u003Eopen files\u003C/strong\u003E, by its file name: open the host drawing and its linked drawing together (or one after the other). Until then the status bar says how many linked drawings were not found, what they draw is missing, and counts say so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObjects TakeoffCraft cannot read or place (proxy objects, unknown classes) are listed in the count window by type; they are never dropped without a word.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVery large drawings are drawn in part, and counts stop at a cap, saying so. Open a PDF plot of such a drawing instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA drawing is drawn in black and in its own colors, darkened for white paper. Plot style tables and pen assignments are not applied.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft opens a DWG or DXF drawing as it opens a PDF: you do not have to plot it to PDF first. Counting from the drawing itself is exact, because the drawing knows what each object is. See Count from a drawing. Open the drawing Choose Open plans in the File group, or press Ctrl+O. Select the DWG or DXF file. You can select several, and PDFs with them. Choose Open. You can also drop drawing files on TakeoffCraft, like PDFs. Nothing is uploaded: the browser reads the file on your computer. TakeoffCraft reads DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 and ASCII DXF from R12 to 2018. It refuses a binary DXF (save it as ASCII, or as a DWG) and a file that is neither, and says why. A damaged file opens with what could be read, and a message says so; its counts may be incomplete. Sheets Model is the drawing's model space. Each layout (paper space tab) is a sheet, named by its tab, in tab order. A layout shows the model through its viewports, as it plots. Zoom, pan, snapping to the drawing's own lines, marks, notes, Calibrate and the rest work as on a PDF. A drawing's sheets are saved in the takeoff by the file's name and a fingerprint of its contents, like a PDF: open the same file again and the marks are on its sheets. The scale A sheet starts with the scale the drawing gives: A layout takes the scale of its biggest viewport. A scale such as 1/2\" = 1'-0\" is named as the preset it is; any other is shown as \"From the drawing's viewport: 1\" = 2.5'\". If a layout has viewports at other scales, the biggest sets the sheet's scale: check the others with Check. Model takes the drawing's units (inches, millimeters, feet…) as its scale, \"Drawing units (inches)\". A drawing with no units (the drawing never said whether a unit is an inch or a millimeter) has no scale: the sheet says \"No scale\", and measuring on it is refused, as on a PDF with no scale. Counting needs no scale. Give it one with Calibrate. Calibrate, Check and the Preset list change a scale at any time and win over the drawing's. The preset list is off on Model, which has no paper size. Layers Layers on the sheet bar lists the drawing's layers with how many objects each has on this sheet. Layers the file turned off or froze start hidden. Untick a layer to hide it, or tick a hidden one to show it: the sheet draws again, and what is hidden is left out of counts and of the objects offered to Measure drawing, unless you ask to count hidden layers too. Show all shows every layer; Back to the file's layers undoes your changes. Layer choices belong to this session, not to the takeoff file. Linked drawings (xrefs) A drawing that links other drawings shows them where they are inserted. TakeoffCraft finds a linked drawing among the open files, by its file name: open the host drawing and its linked drawing together (or one after the other). Until then the status bar says how many linked drawings were not found, what they draw is missing, and counts say so. Limits Objects TakeoffCraft cannot read or place (proxy objects, unknown classes) are listed in the count window by type; they are never dropped without a word. Very large drawings are drawn in part, and counts stop at a cap, saying so. Open a PDF plot of such a drawing instead. A drawing is drawn in black and in its own colors, darkened for white paper. Plot style tables and pen assignments are not applied.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"set-the-scale","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Set the scale","summary":"Give each sheet a scale with a preset or by calibrating on a known dimension, check it, and understand why unscaled sheets are left out of the totals.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["detect scale","read scale","scale from text","all sheets","scale","calibrate","preset","check scale","verify","known dimension","unscaled","no scale","ratio","set scale"],"headings":[{"id":"use-a-preset-when-the-pdf-is-printed-at-full-size","text":"Use a preset (when the PDF is printed at full size)","level":2},{"id":"read-the-scales-from-the-sheets-text","text":"Read the scales from the sheets' text","level":2},{"id":"calibrate-on-a-known-dimension","text":"Calibrate on a known dimension","level":2},{"id":"check-the-scale","text":"Check the scale","level":2},{"id":"change-a-scale","text":"Change a scale","level":2},{"id":"why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero","text":"Why unscaled sheets are not counted as zero","level":2},{"id":"tips","text":"Tips","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery sheet has its own scale. You set it, or let TakeoffCraft read it from the sheets&#039; text (below), and check it. Until a sheet has one, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E refuse to measure on it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E work without a scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheets list shows each sheet&#039;s scale, or \u003Cstrong\u003ENo scale\u003C/strong\u003E. The bar under the open sheet says the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-a-preset-when-the-pdf-is-printed-at-full-size\"\u003EUse a preset (when the PDF is printed at full size)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the scale printed on the drawing, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E under Architectural. The status bar says &quot;Scale for this sheet: 1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck it (below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA preset assumes the PDF is the real paper size the drawing was made for. If someone made the PDF with &quot;fit to page&quot;, or it is a half-size set, the preset is wrong. Calibrate instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe full list is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/scale-presets\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/scale-presets\"\u003EScale presets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-scales-from-the-sheets-text\"\u003ERead the scales from the sheets&#039; text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost drawings say their scale: \u003Ccode\u003ESCALE: 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E in the title block, \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E under a view. TakeoffCraft can read it and set every sheet at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom the sheets&#039; text…\u003C/span\u003E (at the top).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe list shows every sheet: its scale now, and what its text says — \u003Cstrong\u003ESays 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ENo scale in its text\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003ESays not to scale\u003C/strong\u003E (NTS).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESheets without a scale that say one are ticked. Tick or untick any, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet the ticked scales\u003C/span\u003E. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes them all back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck one dimension on each sheet with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E (below): a preset is only right when the PDF is printed at full size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt reads architectural (\u003Ccode\u003E3/16&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), engineering (\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 20&#039;\u003C/code\u003E) and metric (\u003Ccode\u003E1:50\u003C/code\u003E) notations that match a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/scale-presets\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/scale-presets\"\u003Epreset\u003C/a\u003E. When a sheet says more than one scale (a plan with enlarged details), it takes the one it says most often, and one written after the word SCALE counts double; the others are shown in red so you can check. A scanned sheet has no text: set its scale by hand.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"calibrate-on-a-known-dimension\"\u003ECalibrate on a known dimension\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse this when there is no printed scale you trust, the set was resized, or the sheet says &quot;not to scale&quot; but has dimensions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the sheet and zoom in on a long dimension you know, such as a grid line spacing or an overall building dimension. The longer, the more accurate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one end of the dimension. Zoom in on each end for precision; you can zoom and pan between the clicks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the other end. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the line at 0°, 45° or 90°. The \u003Cstrong\u003ECalibrate this sheet\u003C/strong\u003E dialog opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the real distance in \u003Cstrong\u003EReal distance\u003C/strong\u003E, for example \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E24.5\u003C/code\u003E (decimal feet), \u003Ccode\u003E30&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E7.5 m\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/entry-formats\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/entry-formats\"\u003ELength, rate and percent entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/span\u003E. The status bar shows the scale as &quot;1&quot; = (n)&#039;&quot;, and when it is within 0.5% of a preset it says which one, for example &quot;which matches 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; (pick that preset for an exact scale)&quot;. TakeoffCraft goes back to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the calibration matches a preset, picking that preset gives an exact scale instead of one that depends on where you clicked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-the-scale\"\u003ECheck the scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlways check a new scale on a second dimension.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick both ends of a dimension printed on the drawing. The status bar shows &quot;Check: 12&#039;-6&quot;&quot; with the same length in decimal feet and meters, and the check line stays on the sheet with its length.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECompare it with the printed dimension. If it is off, calibrate on a longer dimension.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to clear the check line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-scale\"\u003EChange a scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick another preset or calibrate again. If the sheet already has measurements, TakeoffCraft asks \u003Cstrong\u003EChange this sheet&#039;s scale?\u003C/strong\u003E and says how many measurements will be measured again. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChange the scale\u003C/span\u003E. Every run and area on the sheet is re-measured at the new scale. You can undo it with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero\"\u003EWhy unscaled sheets are not counted as zero\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA mark on a sheet with no scale has no real length or area. TakeoffCraft never counts it as zero, which would quietly make your estimate too low. Instead it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eleaves those marks out of the totals,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eshows &quot;+(n) unscaled&quot; next to the item in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eshows &quot;Incomplete: (n) marks on sheets with no scale (not in the totals)&quot; in the table header,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ewrites the count into the notes column of the spreadsheet export.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis can happen when you open a project whose scales were never set. Give the sheet a scale and the marks are counted at once. Count marks are always counted; they need no scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tips\"\u003ETips\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the scale on every sheet you will measure before you start. Scan the sheets list for \u003Cstrong\u003ENo scale\u003C/strong\u003E to find the ones left (the search box looks at sheet names, not scales).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDetails and enlarged plans on the same sheet often use another scale. TakeoffCraft has one scale per sheet; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMetric drawings: pick a metric preset such as \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E, or calibrate with a metric distance like \u003Ccode\u003E6000 mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Every sheet has its own scale. You set it, or let TakeoffCraft read it from the sheets' text (below), and check it. Until a sheet has one, Linear, Area, Circle and Cut-out refuse to measure on it. Count and Auto count work without a scale. The sheets list shows each sheet's scale, or No scale. The bar under the open sheet says the same. Use a preset (when the PDF is printed at full size) Open the sheet. Open the Preset list in the Scale group. Pick the scale printed on the drawing, for example 1/8\" = 1'-0\" under Architectural. The status bar says \"Scale for this sheet: 1/8\" = 1'-0\".\" Check it (below). A preset assumes the PDF is the real paper size the drawing was made for. If someone made the PDF with \"fit to page\", or it is a half-size set, the preset is wrong. Calibrate instead. The full list is in Scale presets. Read the scales from the sheets' text Most drawings say their scale: SCALE: 1/4\" = 1'-0\" in the title block, 1:100 under a view. TakeoffCraft can read it and set every sheet at once. Open the Preset list and choose From the sheets' text… (at the top). The list shows every sheet: its scale now, and what its text says — Says 1/4\" = 1'-0\", No scale in its text, or Says not to scale (NTS). Sheets without a scale that say one are ticked. Tick or untick any, then choose Set the ticked scales. One Ctrl+Z takes them all back. Check one dimension on each sheet with Check (below): a preset is only right when the PDF is printed at full size. It reads architectural (3/16\" = 1'-0\", 1 1/2\" = 1'-0\"), engineering (1\" = 20') and metric (1:50) notations that match a preset. When a sheet says more than one scale (a plan with enlarged details), it takes the one it says most often, and one written after the word SCALE counts double; the others are shown in red so you can check. A scanned sheet has no text: set its scale by hand. Calibrate on a known dimension Use this when there is no printed scale you trust, the set was resized, or the sheet says \"not to scale\" but has dimensions. Open the sheet and zoom in on a long dimension you know, such as a grid line spacing or an overall building dimension. The longer, the more accurate. Choose Calibrate in the Scale group, or press K. Click one end of the dimension. Zoom in on each end for precision; you can zoom and pan between the clicks. Click the other end. Hold Shift to keep the line at 0°, 45° or 90°. The Calibrate this sheet dialog opens. Type the real distance in Real distance, for example 24'-6 1/2\", 24.5 (decimal feet), 30\" or 7.5 m. See Length, rate and percent entry. Choose Set the scale. The status bar shows the scale as \"1\" = (n)'\", and when it is within 0.5% of a preset it says which one, for example \"which matches 1/4\" = 1'-0\" (pick that preset for an exact scale)\". TakeoffCraft goes back to the Select tool. When the calibration matches a preset, picking that preset gives an exact scale instead of one that depends on where you clicked. Check the scale Always check a new scale on a second dimension. Choose Check in the Scale group, or press D. Click both ends of a dimension printed on the drawing. The status bar shows \"Check: 12'-6\"\" with the same length in decimal feet and meters, and the check line stays on the sheet with its length. Compare it with the printed dimension. If it is off, calibrate on a longer dimension. Press Esc to clear the check line. Change a scale Pick another preset or calibrate again. If the sheet already has measurements, TakeoffCraft asks Change this sheet's scale? and says how many measurements will be measured again. Choose Change the scale. Every run and area on the sheet is re-measured at the new scale. You can undo it with Ctrl+Z. Why unscaled sheets are not counted as zero A mark on a sheet with no scale has no real length or area. TakeoffCraft never counts it as zero, which would quietly make your estimate too low. Instead it: leaves those marks out of the totals, shows \"+(n) unscaled\" next to the item in Quantities, shows \"Incomplete: (n) marks on sheets with no scale (not in the totals)\" in the table header, writes the count into the notes column of the spreadsheet export. This can happen when you open a project whose scales were never set. Give the sheet a scale and the marks are counted at once. Count marks are always counted; they need no scale. Tips Set the scale on every sheet you will measure before you start. Scan the sheets list for No scale to find the ones left (the search box looks at sheet names, not scales). Details and enlarged plans on the same sheet often use another scale. TakeoffCraft has one scale per sheet; see What is not built yet. Metric drawings: pick a metric preset such as 1:100, or calibrate with a metric distance like 6000 mm.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"create-items","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Create takeoff items","summary":"Make linear, area and count items and set their unit, height or depth, waste and material and labor rates.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["crew","production rate","crew size","wage","hours","man-hours","worker-hours","crew-days","labor hours","item","new item","condition","multi-condition","also measure","several types","room","linear","area","count","unit","height","depth","waste","material","labor","rate","color","cost"],"headings":[{"id":"typed-quantities-no-drawing","text":"Typed quantities (no drawing)","level":2},{"id":"make-an-item","text":"Make an item","level":2},{"id":"pick-the-item-you-draw-with","text":"Pick the item you draw with","level":2},{"id":"set-up-the-item","text":"Set up the item","level":2},{"id":"one-item-several-kinds-of-marks","text":"One item, several kinds of marks","level":2},{"id":"rates-in-the-table","text":"Rates in the table","level":2},{"id":"how-pricing-works","text":"How pricing works","level":2},{"id":"labor-by-crew","text":"Labor by crew","level":2},{"id":"metric-items","text":"Metric items","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003Eitem\u003C/strong\u003E is one thing you measure and price: a wall type, a floor finish, a ceiling, a door, a light fixture. Every mark you draw belongs to an item. Other programs call these conditions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach item has a type (and can take other kinds of marks too, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items#one-item-several-kinds-of-marks\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-hash=\"one-item-several-kinds-of-marks\"\u003EOne item, several kinds of marks\u003C/a\u003E):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou draw\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt reports\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUnits\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERuns of connected points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength, number of runs, and wall area when it has a height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF or m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClosed outlines, with cut-outs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea, perimeter, number of areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESF, SY or m²\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne click per thing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEA\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyped quantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing: you type the quantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe number you typed (or one brought in from CivilCraft)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELS, EA, CY, m³, LF, m, SF, SY or m²\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typed-quantities-no-drawing\"\u003ETyped quantities (no drawing)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome things in an estimate are never drawn: mobilization, permits, a contingency, a lump-sum subcontract, the cut and fill CivilCraft worked out. Make them a \u003Cstrong\u003ETyped quantity\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E, type a name, pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETyped quantity (no drawing)\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the item editor pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E (LS for a lump sum) and type the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2.5\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1,250\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet waste and rates as for any other item. An assembly&#039;s formulas use \u003Ccode\u003EQ\u003C/code\u003E, the quantity, for example \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(Q / 12)\u003C/code\u003E truck loads.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA typed quantity takes no marks and never makes the estimate incomplete. When the takeoff has bid areas, the editor also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid area\u003C/span\u003E: the quantity then counts in that area, its alternates and its totals, just as a mark there would. Quantities brought in from another app say where they came from, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/earthwork#cut-and-fill-from-civilcraft\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/earthwork\" data-hash=\"cut-and-fill-from-civilcraft\"\u003EEarthwork and spot elevations\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-an-item\"\u003EMake an item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a name, like \u003Ccode\u003EInterior wall type A\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ECarpet tile\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the type: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear (length)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). The item appears in the list with its own color, becomes the item you draw with, and its editor opens below the list. The status bar tells you which tool to pick next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in the form to close it without adding an item.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you pick a measuring tool first, TakeoffCraft uses the last item of the right type, or opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E form with that type already chosen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-the-item-you-draw-with\"\u003EPick the item you draw with\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an item in the list. It becomes the item new marks go to, and its marks are highlighted on every sheet while the others fade. If the open sheet has none of its marks, TakeoffCraft jumps to a sheet that has. Click the item again to stop highlighting; it stays the item you draw with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicking a mark with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool also makes its item the current one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-up-the-item\"\u003ESet up the item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe item editor (\u003Cstrong\u003EItem: (name)\u003C/strong\u003E) has these fields. Each one is saved when you leave the field or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and each change can be undone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShown in the list, the tables and the exports.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear, Area or Count. It is locked once the item has marks (&quot;An item with marks keeps its type&quot;).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe color of its marks on screen and in the marked PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat the quantity is reported in: LF or m for linear; SF, SY or m² for area; EA for count.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlso measure\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe other kinds of marks the item takes, each with its own unit. See below.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E (linear)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA wall height. The item then also reports wall area (length × height).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E (area)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA depth or thickness, used in assembly formulas (for example concrete volume).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWaste %\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdded to the quantity before pricing: 10 means quantity × 1.10.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial $/LF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial cost per unit. The label follows the unit: $/SF, $/EA and so on.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabor $/LF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabor cost per unit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSI number\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts CSI MasterFormat number, like \u003Ccode\u003E09 21 16\u003C/code\u003E (or just the division, \u003Ccode\u003E09\u003C/code\u003E). The workbook&#039;s By division worksheet groups costs by it. Items from \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/trade-packs\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/trade-packs\"\u003Etrade packs\u003C/a\u003E have one already.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHeight and depth take feet and inches (\u003Ccode\u003E9&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E8&#039; 6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E) or decimal feet (\u003Ccode\u003E9\u003C/code\u003E) for imperial units, and meters (\u003Ccode\u003E2.7\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2700 mm\u003C/code\u003E) for metric units. Rates take amounts like \u003Ccode\u003E12.50\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E$1,250\u003C/code\u003E, up to four decimals. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/entry-formats\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/entry-formats\"\u003ELength, rate and percent entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelow the fields, \u003Cstrong\u003EMeasured\u003C/strong\u003E shows the totals the item&#039;s formulas can use, with their letters: Length (L), Runs (N), Wall area (A) for linear; Area (A), Perimeter (P), Areas (N) for area; Count (N) for count.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"one-item-several-kinds-of-marks\"\u003EOne item, several kinds of marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome things are measured more than one way: a room has a floor (an area), a base along its walls (a length) and doors (a count). Instead of three items, make one:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake the item with its main type, such as an area item \u003Ccode\u003ERoom 101\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlso measure\u003C/span\u003E in its editor, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E or both. Each one gets a unit next to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw with any tool whose kind the item takes: with \u003Ccode\u003ERoom 101\u003C/code\u003E current, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E draws its floor, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E its base and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E its doors. TakeoffCraft does not ask for another item.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe items list shows every quantity (\u003Ccode\u003E50.00 SF + 2 EA\u003C/code\u003E). In the quantities table the item&#039;s row has its main quantity, and each other kind gets its own row below it, such as \u003Cstrong\u003ERoom 101 (count)\u003C/strong\u003E, with its own waste and material and labor rates. The exports, the per-sheet table and the marked PDF&#039;s legend list them the same way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe main type&#039;s fields stay the main type&#039;s own: the height or depth, the pitch, back-outs and the 3D shape apply to its marks only. A base run drawn for an area item is measured plainly along the plan, and shows flat in 3D. Assembly formulas use the main type&#039;s totals.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA kind can be unticked only while the item has no marks of it; delete them, or move them to another item under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E, first. Marks can move to any item that takes their kind.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFurther down is the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E: components such as studs or board, each with its own formula and rates. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the bottom: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove up\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove down\u003C/span\u003E change the item&#039;s place in the list and in the exports, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete item\u003C/span\u003E removes it with all its marks (after asking; you can undo it).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EArea items also have \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E (roof planes measured on the slope: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/roofs-and-slopes\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/roofs-and-slopes\"\u003ERoofs and slopes\u003C/a\u003E) and \u003Cstrong\u003EBack-out\u003C/strong\u003E (their marks come off other items&#039; areas: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-areas#back-outs-columns-shafts-and-openings-taken-out-of-several-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-areas\" data-hash=\"back-outs-columns-shafts-and-openings-taken-out-of-several-items\"\u003EMeasure areas\u003C/a\u003E). Linear items have \u003Cstrong\u003ESlope\u003C/strong\u003E (Level, Rake, Hip or valley) and, for rakes and hips, \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rates-in-the-table\"\u003ERates in the table\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also type \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWaste %\u003C/span\u003E, material and labor rates straight into the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table at the bottom (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy item\u003C/span\u003E). It is the same setting as in the editor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-pricing-works\"\u003EHow pricing works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor each item and each assembly component:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWith waste\u003C/strong\u003E = quantity × (1 + waste %).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial\u003C/strong\u003E = with waste × material per unit, rounded to the cent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELabor\u003C/strong\u003E = with waste × labor per unit, rounded to the cent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETotal\u003C/strong\u003E = material + labor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob total\u003C/span\u003E adds every item and every component. An item can carry its own rates and an assembly at the same time; both are counted, so leave the item&#039;s rates at 0 when its components carry the cost.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"labor-by-crew\"\u003ELabor by crew\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInstead of a labor rate per unit, an item&#039;s labor can come from a crew: how much it puts in per hour, how many people, and what each earns. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabor by crew\u003C/span\u003E in the item editor, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrice labor from a crew\u003C/span\u003E and fill in:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExample\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProduction\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E20\u003C/code\u003E LF an hour (in the item&#039;s unit) for the whole crew\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECrew size\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E people\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWage $/hour each\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E45\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft works out, from the quantity with waste:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Ecrew-hours\u003C/strong\u003E = quantity ÷ production (110 LF ÷ 20 = 5.5),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eworker-hours\u003C/strong\u003E = crew-hours × crew size (11),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Ecrew-days\u003C/strong\u003E = crew-hours ÷ 8 (0.69),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Elabor\u003C/strong\u003E = worker-hours × wage ($495.00).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe editor shows the result and the rate it comes to ($4.50/LF). The item&#039;s own labor rate is then read-only, in the editor and in the table. The header of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table adds the job&#039;s worker-hours and crew-days. The exports note each crew&#039;s hours, and in the workbook the labor cell is a formula on the crew (\u003Ccode\u003EROUND(F2/20*2*45,2)\u003C/code\u003E). Untick the box to go back to a rate per unit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"metric-items\"\u003EMetric items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick m (linear) or m² (area) as the unit. Quantities, heights and formula values are then in meters. The sheet scale can be any preset or calibration; units and scales are independent.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\"\u003EMeasure linear runs\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-areas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-areas\"\u003EMeasure areas with cut-outs\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-by-hand\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-by-hand\"\u003ECount by hand\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"An item is one thing you measure and price: a wall type, a floor finish, a ceiling, a door, a light fixture. Every mark you draw belongs to an item. Other programs call these conditions. Each item has a type (and can take other kinds of marks too, see One item, several kinds of marks): Type You draw It reports Units Linear Runs of connected points Length, number of runs, and wall area when it has a height LF or m Area Closed outlines, with cut-outs Area, perimeter, number of areas SF, SY or m² Count One click per thing How many EA Typed quantity Nothing: you type the quantity The number you typed (or one brought in from CivilCraft) LS, EA, CY, m³, LF, m, SF, SY or m² Typed quantities (no drawing) Some things in an estimate are never drawn: mobilization, permits, a contingency, a lump-sum subcontract, the cut and fill CivilCraft worked out. Make them a Typed quantity: Choose New item, type a name, pick Typed quantity (no drawing) and choose Add. In the item editor pick the Unit (LS for a lump sum) and type the Quantity: 1, 2.5 or 1,250. Set waste and rates as for any other item. An assembly's formulas use Q, the quantity, for example ceil(Q / 12) truck loads. A typed quantity takes no marks and never makes the estimate incomplete. When the takeoff has bid areas, the editor also has Bid area: the quantity then counts in that area, its alternates and its totals, just as a mark there would. Quantities brought in from another app say where they came from, see Earthwork and spot elevations. Make an item In the Items panel on the right, choose New item. Type a name, like Interior wall type A or Carpet tile. Pick the type: Linear (length), Area or Count. Choose Add (or press Enter). The item appears in the list with its own color, becomes the item you draw with, and its editor opens below the list. The status bar tells you which tool to pick next. Press Esc in the form to close it without adding an item. If you pick a measuring tool first, TakeoffCraft uses the last item of the right type, or opens the New item form with that type already chosen. Pick the item you draw with Click an item in the list. It becomes the item new marks go to, and its marks are highlighted on every sheet while the others fade. If the open sheet has none of its marks, TakeoffCraft jumps to a sheet that has. Click the item again to stop highlighting; it stays the item you draw with. Clicking a mark with the Select tool also makes its item the current one. Set up the item The item editor (Item: (name)) has these fields. Each one is saved when you leave the field or press Enter, and each change can be undone. Field What it does Name Shown in the list, the tables and the exports. Type Linear, Area or Count. It is locked once the item has marks (\"An item with marks keeps its type\"). Color The color of its marks on screen and in the marked PDF. Unit What the quantity is reported in: LF or m for linear; SF, SY or m² for area; EA for count. Also measure The other kinds of marks the item takes, each with its own unit. See below. Height (linear) A wall height. The item then also reports wall area (length × height). Depth (area) A depth or thickness, used in assembly formulas (for example concrete volume). Waste % Added to the quantity before pricing: 10 means quantity × 1.10. Material $/LF Material cost per unit. The label follows the unit: $/SF, $/EA and so on. Labor $/LF Labor cost per unit. CSI number Its CSI MasterFormat number, like 09 21 16 (or just the division, 09). The workbook's By division worksheet groups costs by it. Items from trade packs have one already. Height and depth take feet and inches (9'-0\", 8' 6\") or decimal feet (9) for imperial units, and meters (2.7, 2700 mm) for metric units. Rates take amounts like 12.50 or $1,250, up to four decimals. See Length, rate and percent entry. Below the fields, Measured shows the totals the item's formulas can use, with their letters: Length (L), Runs (N), Wall area (A) for linear; Area (A), Perimeter (P), Areas (N) for area; Count (N) for count. One item, several kinds of marks Some things are measured more than one way: a room has a floor (an area), a base along its walls (a length) and doors (a count). Instead of three items, make one: Make the item with its main type, such as an area item Room 101. Under Also measure in its editor, tick Linear, Count or both. Each one gets a unit next to it. Draw with any tool whose kind the item takes: with Room 101 current, Area draws its floor, Linear its base and Count its doors. TakeoffCraft does not ask for another item. The items list shows every quantity (50.00 SF + 2 EA). In the quantities table the item's row has its main quantity, and each other kind gets its own row below it, such as Room 101 (count), with its own waste and material and labor rates. The exports, the per-sheet table and the marked PDF's legend list them the same way. The main type's fields stay the main type's own: the height or depth, the pitch, back-outs and the 3D shape apply to its marks only. A base run drawn for an area item is measured plainly along the plan, and shows flat in 3D. Assembly formulas use the main type's totals. A kind can be unticked only while the item has no marks of it; delete them, or move them to another item under Selected mark, first. Marks can move to any item that takes their kind. Further down is the Assembly: components such as studs or board, each with its own formula and rates. See Assemblies and formulas. At the bottom: Move up and Move down change the item's place in the list and in the exports, and Delete item removes it with all its marks (after asking; you can undo it). Area items also have Pitch (roof planes measured on the slope: Roofs and slopes) and Back-out (their marks come off other items' areas: Measure areas). Linear items have Slope (Level, Rake, Hip or valley) and, for rakes and hips, Pitch. Rates in the table You can also type Waste %, material and labor rates straight into the Quantities table at the bottom (By item). It is the same setting as in the editor. How pricing works For each item and each assembly component: With waste = quantity × (1 + waste %). Material = with waste × material per unit, rounded to the cent. Labor = with waste × labor per unit, rounded to the cent. Total = material + labor. The Job total adds every item and every component. An item can carry its own rates and an assembly at the same time; both are counted, so leave the item's rates at 0 when its components carry the cost. Labor by crew Instead of a labor rate per unit, an item's labor can come from a crew: how much it puts in per hour, how many people, and what each earns. Under Labor by crew in the item editor, tick Price labor from a crew and fill in: Field Example Production 20 LF an hour (in the item's unit) for the whole crew Crew size 2 people Wage $/hour each 45 TakeoffCraft works out, from the quantity with waste: crew-hours = quantity ÷ production (110 LF ÷ 20 = 5.5), worker-hours = crew-hours × crew size (11), crew-days = crew-hours ÷ 8 (0.69), labor = worker-hours × wage ($495.00). The editor shows the result and the rate it comes to ($4.50/LF). The item's own labor rate is then read-only, in the editor and in the table. The header of the Quantities table adds the job's worker-hours and crew-days. The exports note each crew's hours, and in the workbook the labor cell is a formula on the crew (ROUND(F2/20*2*45,2)). Untick the box to go back to a rate per unit. Metric items Pick m (linear) or m² (area) as the unit. Quantities, heights and formula values are then in meters. The sheet scale can be any preset or calibration; units and scales are independent. Next Measure linear runs Measure areas with cut-outs Count by hand","lang":"en"},{"slug":"measure-from-a-drawing","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Measure from a drawing","summary":"Take lengths and areas from the drawing's own lines, arcs, polylines and hatches by picking them, with exact arcs and holes, instead of tracing by clicks.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["measure drawing","geometry","polyline","arc","circle","hatch","bulge","exact length","exact area","pick","window","trace","from cad","linear","area"],"headings":[{"id":"take-objects-as-a-linear-or-area-item","text":"Take objects as a linear or area item","level":2},{"id":"what-each-kind-of-item-takes","text":"What each kind of item takes","level":2},{"id":"exact-and-nearly-exact","text":"Exact, and nearly exact","level":2},{"id":"what-it-takes-from-where","text":"What it takes from where","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure drawing\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar of a DWG or DXF sheet takes the drawing&#039;s own objects as marks, so you do not trace them by clicks. A polyline&#039;s length, an arc&#039;s length, a circle&#039;s area and a hatch&#039;s area with its holes come out exact, from the arithmetic the takeoff uses for every mark.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-objects-as-a-linear-or-area-item\"\u003ETake objects as a linear or area item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake or click a \u003Cstrong\u003Elinear\u003C/strong\u003E or an \u003Cstrong\u003Earea\u003C/strong\u003E item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E. The sheet needs a scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure drawing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the objects on the sheet to pick them, or drag a window round them: everything entirely inside it is picked. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E adds a window to what is picked; click a picked object again to drop it. The bar shows the total: &quot;6 runs: 108&#039;-9 1/8&quot; in all.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the add button, which names the number and the item, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd 6 to Edge\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Each object becomes one mark of the item, in one undo step. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears what you picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-each-kind-of-item-takes\"\u003EWhat each kind of item takes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Elinear\u003C/strong\u003E item takes lines, arcs, polylines, circles and splines by length. A closed shape counts by its perimeter. A hatch is left out: it has an area, not a length, and the bar says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003Earea\u003C/strong\u003E item takes closed shapes: closed polylines, circles, 2D solids, and hatches with their holes. Open runs are left out, and the bar says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"exact-and-nearly-exact\"\u003EExact, and nearly exact\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines, arcs (any bulge), polylines with bulges and circles are exact: lengths and areas use the arc&#039;s radius and sweep, not a picture of it. Ellipses, splines and hatches whose boundary is made of edges are measured in chords of one degree or finer, which is within a hundredth of a percent for ellipses and a tenth for the rest; the bar says how many of what you picked are curves of this kind.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-takes-from-where\"\u003EWhat it takes from where\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E: objects in model space. \u003Cstrong\u003EA layout\u003C/strong\u003E: its own lines, and the model objects inside a viewport, scaled through it. An object that crosses a viewport&#039;s edge is not offered (it plots clipped); an object two viewports both show is taken once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly layers the sheet shows. Blocks are counted, not measured: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\"\u003ECount from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E. 3D polylines, meshes, solids and regions are not offered.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Measure drawing on the sheet bar of a DWG or DXF sheet takes the drawing's own objects as marks, so you do not trace them by clicks. A polyline's length, an arc's length, a circle's area and a hatch's area with its holes come out exact, from the arithmetic the takeoff uses for every mark. Take objects as a linear or area item Make or click a linear or an area item under Items. The sheet needs a scale. Choose Measure drawing. Click the objects on the sheet to pick them, or drag a window round them: everything entirely inside it is picked. Shift adds a window to what is picked; click a picked object again to drop it. The bar shows the total: \"6 runs: 108'-9 1/8\" in all.\" Choose the add button, which names the number and the item, such as Add 6 to Edge (or press Enter). Each object becomes one mark of the item, in one undo step. Esc clears what you picked. What each kind of item takes A linear item takes lines, arcs, polylines, circles and splines by length. A closed shape counts by its perimeter. A hatch is left out: it has an area, not a length, and the bar says so. An area item takes closed shapes: closed polylines, circles, 2D solids, and hatches with their holes. Open runs are left out, and the bar says so. Exact, and nearly exact Lines, arcs (any bulge), polylines with bulges and circles are exact: lengths and areas use the arc's radius and sweep, not a picture of it. Ellipses, splines and hatches whose boundary is made of edges are measured in chords of one degree or finer, which is within a hundredth of a percent for ellipses and a tenth for the rest; the bar says how many of what you picked are curves of this kind. What it takes from where Model: objects in model space. A layout: its own lines, and the model objects inside a viewport, scaled through it. An object that crosses a viewport's edge is not offered (it plots clipped); an object two viewports both show is taken once. Only layers the sheet shows. Blocks are counted, not measured: see Count from a drawing. 3D polylines, meshes, solids and regions are not offered.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"snap-to-the-drawing","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Snap to the drawing's lines","summary":"Points jump onto the PDF's own linework while you measure or calibrate: ends, midpoints, crossings or anywhere on a line, with a marker that says which.","section":"tasks","order":35,"keywords":["snap","osnap","object snap","endpoint","midpoint","intersection","nearest","vector","linework","lines","precise","exact","f3","magnet","corner"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-snaps-to","text":"What it snaps to","level":2},{"id":"where-it-works","text":"Where it works","level":2},{"id":"sheets-it-cannot-snap-on","text":"Sheets it cannot snap on","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMost plan sets are vector PDFs: their walls and edges are real lines, not pixels. While you measure, TakeoffCraft snaps your clicks onto those lines, so a run starts exactly at a wall&#039;s corner and a calibration reads the dimension the drafter drew, not the pixel you hit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESnap is on when you start. Turn it off and on with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; the setting is kept in this browser (it is also under \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-snaps-to\"\u003EWhat it snaps to\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the pointer comes within about 10 screen pixels of a line, a marker shows where the click will land, with its name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMarker\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EName\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquare\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnd\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe end of a line, or a corner where lines meet. A curve&#039;s two ends count; the points along it do not.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMidpoint\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of a straight line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECross\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIntersection\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere two lines cross, or where one ends on another.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHourglass\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn the line\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe nearest point on a line, when no end, midpoint or crossing is close.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe nearest end, midpoint or crossing wins. Only when none is close does the point go onto the line itself. Zoom in to choose between points that sit close together: the 10 pixels are screen pixels, so at a higher zoom they cover less of the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-works\"\u003EWhere it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESnap works in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E, and when you drag a selected mark&#039;s corner. It also places arc points. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E and pasting are not snapped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA snapped point wins over the 45° snap of \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: to draw at 45° away from the lines, turn Snap off with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECalibrate with Snap on. Click near the two ends of a dimension line or a wall you know: both points land exactly on the drawing, and the scale comes out exact (the status bar then names the preset it matches).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-it-cannot-snap-on\"\u003ESheets it cannot snap on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScanned sheets\u003C/strong\u003E are pictures with no lines to snap to. The status bar says &quot;This sheet has no lines to snap to (a scanned page is a picture).&quot; and points go where you click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVery large drawings\u003C/strong\u003E: snapping reads up to 400,000 line segments per sheet. On a sheet with more, it says so and snaps to the first ones only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText\u003C/strong\u003E is not snapped to, unless the PDF draws its letters as lines (some plotters do).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheet&#039;s lines are read the first time you open it, in the background; a big sheet can take a second before the markers appear.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Most plan sets are vector PDFs: their walls and edges are real lines, not pixels. While you measure, TakeoffCraft snaps your clicks onto those lines, so a run starts exactly at a wall's corner and a calibration reads the dimension the drafter drew, not the pixel you hit. Snap is on when you start. Turn it off and on with Snap in the status bar or F3; the setting is kept in this browser (it is also under Settings). What it snaps to When the pointer comes within about 10 screen pixels of a line, a marker shows where the click will land, with its name: Marker Name Where Square End The end of a line, or a corner where lines meet. A curve's two ends count; the points along it do not. Triangle Midpoint The middle of a straight line. Cross Intersection Where two lines cross, or where one ends on another. Hourglass On the line The nearest point on a line, when no end, midpoint or crossing is close. The nearest end, midpoint or crossing wins. Only when none is close does the point go onto the line itself. Zoom in to choose between points that sit close together: the 10 pixels are screen pixels, so at a higher zoom they cover less of the sheet. Where it works Snap works in Linear, Area, Circle, Cut-out, Calibrate and Check, and when you drag a selected mark's corner. It also places arc points. Count, Auto count and pasting are not snapped. A snapped point wins over the 45° snap of Shift: to draw at 45° away from the lines, turn Snap off with F3 first. Calibrate with Snap on. Click near the two ends of a dimension line or a wall you know: both points land exactly on the drawing, and the scale comes out exact (the status bar then names the preset it matches). Sheets it cannot snap on Scanned sheets are pictures with no lines to snap to. The status bar says \"This sheet has no lines to snap to (a scanned page is a picture).\" and points go where you click. Very large drawings: snapping reads up to 400,000 line segments per sheet. On a sheet with more, it says so and snaps to the first ones only. Text is not snapped to, unless the PDF draws its letters as lines (some plotters do). The sheet's lines are read the first time you open it, in the background; a big sheet can take a second before the markers appear.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"measure-linear","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Measure linear runs","summary":"Take off walls, piping, trim and other lengths as runs, with straight segments, arcs and the Shift 45-degree snap.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["linear","length","run","polyline","walls","lf","linear feet","arc","curve","45 degrees","ortho","snap","segments","polylength","deduct","opening","door","gap","type","typed","coordinates","distance","direct distance","polar"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-a-run","text":"Draw a run","level":2},{"id":"straight-lines-at-45-steps","text":"Straight lines at 45° steps","level":2},{"id":"arcs-and-curved-walls","text":"Arcs and curved walls","level":2},{"id":"type-lengths-and-coordinates","text":"Type lengths and coordinates","level":2},{"id":"deduct-a-part-of-a-run","text":"Deduct a part of a run","level":2},{"id":"look-at-a-run-s-details","text":"Look at a run's details","level":2},{"id":"fix-a-run","text":"Fix a run","level":2},{"id":"wall-area","text":"Wall area","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA linear item measures length. Each run is a chain of points: straight segments, and arcs where you turn arc mode on. An item can have any number of runs on any number of sheets.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore you start: the sheet needs a scale (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E) and you need a linear item (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a vector PDF, your clicks snap onto the drawing&#039;s own lines: ends, midpoints and crossings (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\"\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s lines\u003C/a\u003E; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E turns it off).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-run\"\u003EDraw a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the linear item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the start of the run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each corner along it. The status bar shows &quot;Length&quot; (the run so far, to the pointer) and &quot;segment&quot; (the segment you are drawing), in feet and inches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFinish the run: double-click the last point, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or right-click. The run is drawn in the item&#039;s color and becomes the selected mark. The item&#039;s length in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E list and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table goes up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tool stays on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, so you can start the next run straight away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile drawing:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) removes the last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E throws the unfinished run away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDragging pans the sheet and the wheel zooms, without adding points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac trackpad, right-click is a two-finger click (turn on secondary click in System Settings, under Trackpad). TakeoffCraft reads only the secondary button, so a Control-click may be taken as a plain click and add a point. Double-click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always finish.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen, tap to place points, drag one finger to pan and pinch with two to zoom. Finish with a long press, a double tap, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E in the bar under the sheet (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E takes the last point back). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/troubleshooting#touch-screens\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"touch-screens\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"straight-lines-at-45-steps\"\u003EStraight lines at 45° steps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you place a point. The segment from the last point snaps to the nearest 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° direction, keeping the length you dragged along it. The status bar reminds you: &quot;Shift snaps to 45°&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn arc mode it applies to where an arc ends: the arc&#039;s chord goes at 45° steps from where it starts. The point you click on the curve is never snapped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arcs-and-curved-walls\"\u003EArcs and curved walls\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile drawing a run, place the point where the curve starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on arc mode: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The button stays pressed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick any point on the curve, somewhere along the middle of it. The status bar adds &quot;(arc: click where it ends)&quot; and the preview bends through your point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the curve ends. The arc is added. Its length is the exact arc length, not a string of short chords.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick another point on the next curve and its end to keep drawing arcs, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to go back to straight segments.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first drop the pending point on the curve; press again to remove points as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-lengths-and-coordinates\"\u003EType lengths and coordinates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter the first point, you can type where the next one goes instead of clicking. Type a digit, \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E: a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E box opens in the status bar with what you typed. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to place the point; the box stays open for the next one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe next point goes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12&#039;-6&quot; from the last point, toward the pointer (point the way, then type the length)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;&lt;45\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12&#039;-6&quot; at 45°, counted counter-clockwise from the screen&#039;s right (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;90\u003C/code\u003E is up, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;-90\u003C/code\u003E down)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@6&#039;,-4&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6&#039; right and 4&#039; down from the last point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6&#039;,4&#039;\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E#6&#039;,4&#039;\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6&#039; right and 4&#039; up from the first point of this run\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths are typed as everywhere in TakeoffCraft (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/entry-formats\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/entry-formats\"\u003EEntry formats\u003C/a\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12.5\u003C/code\u003E (feet), \u003Ccode\u003E30&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3.2 m\u003C/code\u003E. &quot;Right&quot; and &quot;up&quot; are as you see the sheet, also when the PDF page is turned.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on an empty box finishes the run, like \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the sheet; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box and keeps the run going. Typed points go through the same rules as clicks: deducts, arcs and closing an area all work. The same box works in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E; after a circle&#039;s center it takes the radius.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"deduct-a-part-of-a-run\"\u003EDeduct a part of a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wall run that passes a door or a storefront can be drawn straight through and still count only the wall: the segments across the opening are \u003Cstrong\u003Ededucts\u003C/strong\u003E. They are drawn dashed and thin, and not counted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile drawing:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlace the point where the opening starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The button stays pressed and the status bar says the next segments are deducts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the opening ends. The segment is drawn dashed; &quot;Length&quot; in the status bar leaves it out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again and carry on along the wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter drawing: select the run. Under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E, each segment has a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E box: tick it to deduct that segment, untick it to count it again. Each change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA run always keeps at least one segment that counts. The panel then shows \u003Cstrong\u003EDeducts\u003C/strong\u003E (the deducted length) and \u003Cstrong\u003EDrawn\u003C/strong\u003E (the whole run); \u003Cstrong\u003ELength\u003C/strong\u003E, the item&#039;s quantity, the wall area and the costs all use the length without the deducts. The 3D check view leaves a gap in the wall, the marked PDF draws the deducts dashed, and the exported \u003Cstrong\u003EMarks\u003C/strong\u003E sheet lists them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMoving a corner keeps the deducts. Adding or removing points (by drawing the run again) starts without them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-at-a-run-s-details\"\u003ELook at a run&#039;s details\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click the run. \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E on the right shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe item it belongs to (pick another linear item in the list to move the run there),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe sheet,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELength\u003C/strong\u003E in feet and inches and in LF,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe number of \u003Cstrong\u003ESegments\u003C/strong\u003E, and the \u003Cstrong\u003EWall area\u003C/strong\u003E when the item has a height,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eeach segment&#039;s length, with &quot;(arc)&quot; after arcs, and its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the sheet, each segment of the selected run is labeled with its length.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fix-a-run\"\u003EFix a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove a point\u003C/strong\u003E: select the run, then drag one of its square corner handles. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to snap the moved segment to 45° steps. An arc keeps its bulge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBend an arc\u003C/strong\u003E: a selected run shows a round handle in the middle of each arc. Drag it: the arc passes through where you let go, so it bows more, less, or the other way. Dragged onto the straight line between its ends, it becomes a straight segment. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it (&quot;Bend arc&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete a run\u003C/strong\u003E: select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete mark\u003C/span\u003E in the panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; redo with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou cannot insert a point in the middle of a run yet. Delete the run and draw it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"wall-area\"\u003EWall area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive the linear item a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E (for example \u003Ccode\u003E9&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E). It then reports \u003Cstrong\u003EWall area (A)\u003C/strong\u003E = length × height, in SF (or m² for a metric item), and the wall area goes into the notes column of the spreadsheet export. Use it in assemblies for board, paint or insulation: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A linear item measures length. Each run is a chain of points: straight segments, and arcs where you turn arc mode on. An item can have any number of runs on any number of sheets. Before you start: the sheet needs a scale (Set the scale) and you need a linear item (Create takeoff items). On a vector PDF, your clicks snap onto the drawing's own lines: ends, midpoints and crossings (Snap to the drawing's lines; F3 turns it off). Draw a run Click the linear item under Items. Choose Linear in the toolbar, or press L. Click the start of the run. Click each corner along it. The status bar shows \"Length\" (the run so far, to the pointer) and \"segment\" (the segment you are drawing), in feet and inches. Finish the run: double-click the last point, press Enter, or right-click. The run is drawn in the item's color and becomes the selected mark. The item's length in the Items list and the Quantities table goes up. The tool stays on Linear, so you can start the next run straight away. Press Esc to go back to Select. While drawing: Backspace (or Delete) removes the last point. Esc throws the unfinished run away. Dragging pans the sheet and the wheel zooms, without adding points. On a Mac trackpad, right-click is a two-finger click (turn on secondary click in System Settings, under Trackpad). TakeoffCraft reads only the secondary button, so a Control-click may be taken as a plain click and add a point. Double-click or Enter always finish. On a touch screen, tap to place points, drag one finger to pan and pinch with two to zoom. Finish with a long press, a double tap, or Finish in the bar under the sheet (Back takes the last point back). See Troubleshooting. Straight lines at 45° steps Hold Shift while you place a point. The segment from the last point snaps to the nearest 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° direction, keeping the length you dragged along it. The status bar reminds you: \"Shift snaps to 45°\". In arc mode it applies to where an arc ends: the arc's chord goes at 45° steps from where it starts. The point you click on the curve is never snapped. Arcs and curved walls While drawing a run, place the point where the curve starts. Turn on arc mode: choose Arc in the toolbar or press R. The button stays pressed. Click any point on the curve, somewhere along the middle of it. The status bar adds \"(arc: click where it ends)\" and the preview bends through your point. Click where the curve ends. The arc is added. Its length is the exact arc length, not a string of short chords. Click another point on the next curve and its end to keep drawing arcs, or press R again to go back to straight segments. Backspace or Esc first drop the pending point on the curve; press again to remove points as usual. Type lengths and coordinates After the first point, you can type where the next one goes instead of clicking. Type a digit, @ or #: a Type box opens in the status bar with what you typed. Press Enter to place the point; the box stays open for the next one. Type The next point goes 12'6\" 12'-6\" from the last point, toward the pointer (point the way, then type the length) 12'6\"\u003C45 12'-6\" at 45°, counted counter-clockwise from the screen's right (\u003C90 is up, \u003C-90 down) @6',-4' 6' right and 4' down from the last point 6',4' (or #6',4') 6' right and 4' up from the first point of this run Lengths are typed as everywhere in TakeoffCraft (Entry formats): 12'-6 1/2\", 12.5 (feet), 30\", 3.2 m. \"Right\" and \"up\" are as you see the sheet, also when the PDF page is turned. Enter on an empty box finishes the run, like Enter on the sheet; Esc closes the box and keeps the run going. Typed points go through the same rules as clicks: deducts, arcs and closing an area all work. The same box works in Area and Cut-out; after a circle's center it takes the radius. Deduct a part of a run A wall run that passes a door or a storefront can be drawn straight through and still count only the wall: the segments across the opening are deducts. They are drawn dashed and thin, and not counted. While drawing: Place the point where the opening starts. Choose Deduct in the toolbar or press E. The button stays pressed and the status bar says the next segments are deducts. Click where the opening ends. The segment is drawn dashed; \"Length\" in the status bar leaves it out. Press E again and carry on along the wall. After drawing: select the run. Under Selected mark, each segment has a Deduct box: tick it to deduct that segment, untick it to count it again. Each change is one undo step. A run always keeps at least one segment that counts. The panel then shows Deducts (the deducted length) and Drawn (the whole run); Length, the item's quantity, the wall area and the costs all use the length without the deducts. The 3D check view leaves a gap in the wall, the marked PDF draws the deducts dashed, and the exported Marks sheet lists them. Moving a corner keeps the deducts. Adding or removing points (by drawing the run again) starts without them. Look at a run's details Choose Select (V) and click the run. Selected mark on the right shows: the item it belongs to (pick another linear item in the list to move the run there), the sheet, Length in feet and inches and in LF, the number of Segments, and the Wall area when the item has a height, each segment's length, with \"(arc)\" after arcs, and its Deduct box. On the sheet, each segment of the selected run is labeled with its length. Fix a run Move a point: select the run, then drag one of its square corner handles. Hold Shift to snap the moved segment to 45° steps. An arc keeps its bulge. Bend an arc: a selected run shows a round handle in the middle of each arc. Drag it: the arc passes through where you let go, so it bows more, less, or the other way. Dragged onto the straight line between its ends, it becomes a straight segment. Ctrl+Z undoes it (\"Bend arc\"). Delete a run: select it and press Delete, choose Delete in the toolbar's More menu, or Delete mark in the panel. Undo: Ctrl+Z; redo with Ctrl+Y. You cannot insert a point in the middle of a run yet. Delete the run and draw it again. Wall area Give the linear item a Height (for example 9'-0\"). It then reports Wall area (A) = length × height, in SF (or m² for a metric item), and the wall area goes into the notes column of the spreadsheet export. Use it in assemblies for board, paint or insulation: see Assemblies and formulas.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"measure-areas","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Measure areas with cut-outs","summary":"Outline floors, ceilings and slabs, cut holes out of them, add curved edges, circles and round cut-outs, and read area and perimeter.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["room fill","flood fill","paint bucket","click inside a room","auto area","back-out","backout","column","shaft","deduct from several items","area","polygon","outline","square feet","sf","cut-out","hole","deduct","circle","round","arc","perimeter","floor","ceiling","slab"],"headings":[{"id":"outline-an-area","text":"Outline an area","level":2},{"id":"curved-edges","text":"Curved edges","level":2},{"id":"a-circle","text":"A circle","level":2},{"id":"fill-a-room-with-one-click","text":"Fill a room with one click","level":2},{"id":"cut-a-hole-out-of-an-area","text":"Cut a hole out of an area","level":2},{"id":"back-outs-columns-shafts-and-openings-taken-out-of-several-items","text":"Back-outs: columns, shafts and openings taken out of several items","level":2},{"id":"read-the-area","text":"Read the area","level":2},{"id":"fix-an-area","text":"Fix an area","level":2},{"id":"depth-for-volumes","text":"Depth for volumes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn area item measures enclosed area. Each outline can have holes cut out of it (a shaft, a column, a planter), and its edges can be straight or curved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore you start: the sheet needs a scale (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E) and you need an area item (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"outline-an-area\"\u003EOutline an area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the area item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each corner of the room or slab in turn. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for 0°, 45° and 90° edges. From the third point on, the outline fills in and the status bar shows &quot;Area&quot; and &quot;perimeter&quot;. A small ring appears round the first point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose the outline: click the first point (inside its ring), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, double-click, or right-click. The area is filled lightly in the item&#039;s color, labeled with its square feet, and becomes the selected mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes the last corner; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E throws the outline away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn outline that crosses itself is refused: &quot;The outline crosses itself, so its area would be wrong. Draw it again without crossing.&quot; Draw a figure-eight shape as two areas instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"curved-edges\"\u003ECurved edges\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile outlining, turn on arc mode (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). After a corner, click a point along the curve, then where the curve ends. To close the outline with a curve, make that last click on the first point. The area and perimeter use the exact curve. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again for straight edges. To change a curve afterwards, select the area and drag the round handle in the middle of the arc. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear#arcs-and-curved-walls\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-hash=\"arcs-and-curved-walls\"\u003EMeasure linear runs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-circle\"\u003EA circle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith an area item, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the center.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick any point on the edge. While you move, the status bar shows the radius and area. The circle is added as an area mark with an exact area and perimeter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-a-room-with-one-click\"\u003EFill a room with one click\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the rooms are drawn as closed walls, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) takes a floor off in one click:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick an area item (a floor finish, say) and give the sheet its scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick inside a room, in the open floor (not on a line or a label).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft reads the sheet&#039;s lines, finds the space around the click and outlines it along the walls&#039; inner faces, as an area of the current item. Door openings up to \u003Cstrong\u003E4&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E are treated as closed, so the outline runs straight across the doorway; \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings › TakeoffCraft › Room fill closes openings up to\u003C/strong\u003E changes that width (3&#039;-0&quot; to 8&#039;-0&quot;). Room names, furniture and fixtures inside the room do not make holes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt refuses, saying why, when:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe click is on a line (&quot;That is on a line&quot;);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe space is not closed: an opening wider than the setting lets it run out to the sheet&#039;s edge. Raise the setting, or outline that room by hand with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe space is tiny (a symbol, not a room).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a door is wider than the setting, the fill runs into the next room and outlines both together: undo (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), raise the setting, and click again. Check the outline like any area: drag its corners to fix it. A scanned sheet works the same, but its lines must be dark enough; light grey linework may not stop the fill.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-a-hole-out-of-an-area\"\u003ECut a hole out of an area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click the area. It shows square handles at its corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a cut-out\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners of the hole, inside the area. Arc mode works here too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose it like an area: click its first point, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, double-click or right-click. The hole is left unfilled, and the status bar says &quot;Cut-out added. Draw another, or press Esc.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw more holes in the same area, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a round hole, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a round cut-out\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E, click its center, then a point on its edge.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA cut-out must sit fully inside the area and must not overlap another cut-out. Otherwise TakeoffCraft refuses it: &quot;A cut-out has to sit fully inside the area.&quot; or &quot;Cut-outs cannot overlap each other.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo take the last hole out again, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove last cut-out\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"back-outs-columns-shafts-and-openings-taken-out-of-several-items\"\u003EBack-outs: columns, shafts and openings taken out of several items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA cut-out belongs to one area mark. A \u003Cstrong\u003Eback-out\u003C/strong\u003E is drawn once and comes out of every area it overlaps, for the items you choose: draw the columns once, and they come off the floor finish, the ceiling and the slab together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake an area item for them, such as &quot;Columns&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn its item settings, tick \u003Cstrong\u003EBack-out: its marks take their area out of other items\u003C/strong\u003E. A list of your other area items appears, all ticked: untick the ones the back-outs should not touch (paint on the walls, say).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETake off the columns with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, as usual. Each one is drawn dashed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery area mark of a ticked item on the same sheet loses the part a back-out covers: the whole back-out when it sits inside, only the overlapping part when it crosses the edge, and nothing inside a cut-out. The selected area&#039;s panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003EBack-outs\u003C/strong\u003E and the area left. The item&#039;s quantity, its assembly formulas, the costs, the 3D check view and the export all use the area left; the estimate&#039;s notes say how much back-outs took out. The back-out item itself counts its own area (leave its costs at zero if you do not price it).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA back-out has to be \u003Cstrong\u003Econvex\u003C/strong\u003E: a rectangle, a circle or any outline without an inward corner. TakeoffCraft refuses to draw one that is not; draw an L-shaped shaft as two back-outs. Back-outs cannot have cut-outs, and two back-outs that overlap each other both count.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe perimeter (\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E) does not change: back-outs take out area only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-area\"\u003ERead the area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the area. \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe net area: outline minus cut-outs. This is what the item counts.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOutline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe gross area inside the outline (shown when there are cut-outs).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut-outs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many holes, and their total area.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPerimeter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe outline&#039;s edge length, \u003Cstrong\u003Eplus\u003C/strong\u003E the edges of every cut-out (&quot;with cut-outs&quot;).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe perimeter includes cut-out edges on purpose, for things like edge forms or base that run round a hole as well. If you need only the outer edge, keep holes out of that item or measure the outer edge as a linear run.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E decides how the total is reported: SF, SY or m². The \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E panel and the status bar always show square feet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fix-an-area\"\u003EFix an area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove a corner\u003C/strong\u003E: select the area and drag a corner handle (holes have handles too). If the new shape would cross itself or push a hole outside, the corner is put back: &quot;The point was put back.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove to another item\u003C/strong\u003E: pick another area item in the \u003Cstrong\u003EItem\u003C/strong\u003E list under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete mark\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"depth-for-volumes\"\u003EDepth for volumes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive the area item a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E (for example \u003Ccode\u003E0&#039;-4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E for a slab) and use \u003Ccode\u003EA * H / 27\u003C/code\u003E in an assembly component to get cubic yards. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"An area item measures enclosed area. Each outline can have holes cut out of it (a shaft, a column, a planter), and its edges can be straight or curved. Before you start: the sheet needs a scale (Set the scale) and you need an area item (Create takeoff items). Outline an area Click the area item under Items. Choose Area in the toolbar, or press A. Click each corner of the room or slab in turn. Hold Shift for 0°, 45° and 90° edges. From the third point on, the outline fills in and the status bar shows \"Area\" and \"perimeter\". A small ring appears round the first point. Close the outline: click the first point (inside its ring), press Enter, double-click, or right-click. The area is filled lightly in the item's color, labeled with its square feet, and becomes the selected mark. Backspace removes the last corner; Esc throws the outline away. An outline that crosses itself is refused: \"The outline crosses itself, so its area would be wrong. Draw it again without crossing.\" Draw a figure-eight shape as two areas instead. Curved edges While outlining, turn on arc mode (Arc or R). After a corner, click a point along the curve, then where the curve ends. To close the outline with a curve, make that last click on the first point. The area and perimeter use the exact curve. Press R again for straight edges. To change a curve afterwards, select the area and drag the round handle in the middle of the arc. See Measure linear runs. A circle With an area item, choose Circle or press O. Click the center. Click any point on the edge. While you move, the status bar shows the radius and area. The circle is added as an area mark with an exact area and perimeter. Fill a room with one click When the rooms are drawn as closed walls, Room (B) takes a floor off in one click: Pick an area item (a floor finish, say) and give the sheet its scale. Choose Room in the Takeoff group, or press B. Click inside a room, in the open floor (not on a line or a label). TakeoffCraft reads the sheet's lines, finds the space around the click and outlines it along the walls' inner faces, as an area of the current item. Door openings up to 4'-0\" are treated as closed, so the outline runs straight across the doorway; Settings › TakeoffCraft › Room fill closes openings up to changes that width (3'-0\" to 8'-0\"). Room names, furniture and fixtures inside the room do not make holes. It refuses, saying why, when: the click is on a line (\"That is on a line\"); the space is not closed: an opening wider than the setting lets it run out to the sheet's edge. Raise the setting, or outline that room by hand with Area; the space is tiny (a symbol, not a room). When a door is wider than the setting, the fill runs into the next room and outlines both together: undo (Ctrl+Z), raise the setting, and click again. Check the outline like any area: drag its corners to fix it. A scanned sheet works the same, but its lines must be dark enough; light grey linework may not stop the fill. Cut a hole out of an area Choose Select (V) and click the area. It shows square handles at its corners. Choose Cut-out in the toolbar (or press X), or Add a cut-out under Selected mark. Click the corners of the hole, inside the area. Arc mode works here too. Close it like an area: click its first point, press Enter, double-click or right-click. The hole is left unfilled, and the status bar says \"Cut-out added. Draw another, or press Esc.\" Draw more holes in the same area, or press Esc. For a round hole, choose Add a round cut-out under Selected mark, click its center, then a point on its edge. A cut-out must sit fully inside the area and must not overlap another cut-out. Otherwise TakeoffCraft refuses it: \"A cut-out has to sit fully inside the area.\" or \"Cut-outs cannot overlap each other.\" To take the last hole out again, choose Remove last cut-out. Back-outs: columns, shafts and openings taken out of several items A cut-out belongs to one area mark. A back-out is drawn once and comes out of every area it overlaps, for the items you choose: draw the columns once, and they come off the floor finish, the ceiling and the slab together. Make an area item for them, such as \"Columns\". In its item settings, tick Back-out: its marks take their area out of other items. A list of your other area items appears, all ticked: untick the ones the back-outs should not touch (paint on the walls, say). Take off the columns with Area or Circle, as usual. Each one is drawn dashed. Every area mark of a ticked item on the same sheet loses the part a back-out covers: the whole back-out when it sits inside, only the overlapping part when it crosses the edge, and nothing inside a cut-out. The selected area's panel shows Back-outs and the area left. The item's quantity, its assembly formulas, the costs, the 3D check view and the export all use the area left; the estimate's notes say how much back-outs took out. The back-out item itself counts its own area (leave its costs at zero if you do not price it). A back-out has to be convex: a rectangle, a circle or any outline without an inward corner. TakeoffCraft refuses to draw one that is not; draw an L-shaped shaft as two back-outs. Back-outs cannot have cut-outs, and two back-outs that overlap each other both count. The perimeter (P) does not change: back-outs take out area only. Read the area Select the area. Selected mark shows: Line Meaning Area The net area: outline minus cut-outs. This is what the item counts. Outline The gross area inside the outline (shown when there are cut-outs). Cut-outs How many holes, and their total area. Perimeter The outline's edge length, plus the edges of every cut-out (\"with cut-outs\"). The perimeter includes cut-out edges on purpose, for things like edge forms or base that run round a hole as well. If you need only the outer edge, keep holes out of that item or measure the outer edge as a linear run. The item's Unit decides how the total is reported: SF, SY or m². The Selected mark panel and the status bar always show square feet. Fix an area Move a corner: select the area and drag a corner handle (holes have handles too). If the new shape would cross itself or push a hole outside, the corner is put back: \"The point was put back.\" Move to another item: pick another area item in the Item list under Selected mark. Delete: Delete, Delete in the toolbar's More menu, or Delete mark. Depth for volumes Give the area item a Depth (for example 0'-4\" for a slab) and use A * H / 27 in an assembly component to get cubic yards. See Assemblies and formulas.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"roofs-and-slopes","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Roofs and slopes","summary":"Give an area its pitch to measure the roof surface from the plan, and measure rakes, hips and valleys along their slope.","section":"tasks","order":55,"keywords":["roof","pitch","slope","slope factor","rise","run","6:12","rake","hip","valley","gable","roofing","squares","sloped area","true length","plan area"],"headings":[{"id":"typing-a-pitch","text":"Typing a pitch","level":2},{"id":"roof-areas","text":"Roof areas","level":2},{"id":"rakes-hips-and-valleys","text":"Rakes, hips and valleys","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA roof plan shows each roof plane flat, as seen from above. The roof itself is bigger: a plane at 6:12 has about 12% more surface than its plan, and its sloped edges are longer than they look. Give the item its pitch and TakeoffCraft measures along the slope.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-a-pitch\"\u003ETyping a pitch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pitch is the rise per 12 of run, as on the roof plan&#039;s pitch symbols. Type it as \u003Ccode\u003E6:12\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6/12\u003C/code\u003E or just \u003Ccode\u003E6\u003C/code\u003E; another run works too (\u003Ccode\u003E4:10\u003C/code\u003E is 4.8:12). An angle works with a degree sign: \u003Ccode\u003E26.57°\u003C/code\u003E. Blank or \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E is flat.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"roof-areas\"\u003ERoof areas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake an area item for the roofing, or open the one you have.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn its settings, type the \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E, such as \u003Ccode\u003E6:12\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOutline each roof plane on the plan with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach plane counts its plan area times the slope factor: √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)²), 1.118 at 6:12 and 1.414 at 12:12. Select a plane to see its \u003Cstrong\u003EArea\u003C/strong\u003E (on the slope), its \u003Cstrong\u003EPlan area\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003ESlope factor\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlanes with another pitch: select the plane and type its own \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E in its panel. Leave it blank to use the item&#039;s. The 3D check view, the assemblies (\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E is the sloped area), the costs and the export all use the sloped area. Back-outs on a pitched plane, such as skylights, come off at the same slope.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe perimeter (\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E) stays the plan perimeter. Measure eaves, rakes, hips and valleys as runs (below).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rakes-hips-and-valleys\"\u003ERakes, hips and valleys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake a linear item, such as &quot;Rake edge&quot; or &quot;Hips&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet its \u003Cstrong\u003ESlope\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003ERake\u003C/strong\u003E (a sloped edge, like a gable&#039;s) or \u003Cstrong\u003EHip or valley\u003C/strong\u003E (the line where two planes meet), and type the \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E of the roof planes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the runs on the plan with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA rake counts its plan length times the slope factor. A hip or valley runs at 45° across the plan between two planes of the same pitch, so its true length is its plan length times √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)² ÷ 2): 1.061 at 6:12, 1.225 at 12:12.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne run can mix them. Select it: each segment in its panel has a slope list — \u003Cstrong\u003EItem&#039;s\u003C/strong\u003E (the item&#039;s setting), \u003Cstrong\u003ELevel\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ERake\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EHip or valley\u003C/strong\u003E — and its length along the slope. A run&#039;s own \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E in the panel replaces the item&#039;s for all its segments. The panel also shows the \u003Cstrong\u003EPlan length\u003C/strong\u003E, and the labels on the sheet show the lengths along the slope.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDeducts still work: a deducted segment counts nothing, sloped or not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHips and valleys assume both planes have the same pitch. For planes of different pitches, measure the hip by its true length elsewhere, or draw it as a rake with an equivalent pitch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"A roof plan shows each roof plane flat, as seen from above. The roof itself is bigger: a plane at 6:12 has about 12% more surface than its plan, and its sloped edges are longer than they look. Give the item its pitch and TakeoffCraft measures along the slope. Typing a pitch A pitch is the rise per 12 of run, as on the roof plan's pitch symbols. Type it as 6:12, 6/12 or just 6; another run works too (4:10 is 4.8:12). An angle works with a degree sign: 26.57°. Blank or 0 is flat. Roof areas Make an area item for the roofing, or open the one you have. In its settings, type the Pitch, such as 6:12. Outline each roof plane on the plan with Area, as usual. Each plane counts its plan area times the slope factor: √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)²), 1.118 at 6:12 and 1.414 at 12:12. Select a plane to see its Area (on the slope), its Plan area and the Slope factor. Planes with another pitch: select the plane and type its own Pitch in its panel. Leave it blank to use the item's. The 3D check view, the assemblies (A is the sloped area), the costs and the export all use the sloped area. Back-outs on a pitched plane, such as skylights, come off at the same slope. The perimeter (P) stays the plan perimeter. Measure eaves, rakes, hips and valleys as runs (below). Rakes, hips and valleys Make a linear item, such as \"Rake edge\" or \"Hips\". Set its Slope to Rake (a sloped edge, like a gable's) or Hip or valley (the line where two planes meet), and type the Pitch of the roof planes. Draw the runs on the plan with Linear. A rake counts its plan length times the slope factor. A hip or valley runs at 45° across the plan between two planes of the same pitch, so its true length is its plan length times √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)² ÷ 2): 1.061 at 6:12, 1.225 at 12:12. One run can mix them. Select it: each segment in its panel has a slope list — Item's (the item's setting), Level, Rake or Hip or valley — and its length along the slope. A run's own Pitch in the panel replaces the item's for all its segments. The panel also shows the Plan length, and the labels on the sheet show the lengths along the slope. Deducts still work: a deducted segment counts nothing, sloped or not. Hips and valleys assume both planes have the same pitch. For planes of different pitches, measure the hip by its true length elsewhere, or draw it as a rake with an equivalent pitch.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"count-by-hand","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Count by hand","summary":"Click each door, fixture or device to count it, check your counts, and fix mistakes.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["count","each","ea","fixtures","doors","devices","symbols","tally","click count","manual count"],"headings":[{"id":"count","text":"Count","level":2},{"id":"fix-a-count","text":"Fix a count","level":2},{"id":"many-identical-symbols","text":"Many identical symbols","level":2},{"id":"using-counts-in-formulas","text":"Using counts in formulas","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA count item counts things: doors, light fixtures, outlets, plumbing fixtures. Each click on the sheet adds one count mark. Counting does not need a scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"count\"\u003ECount\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake or click a count item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E type).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each one on the sheet. Each click puts a dot in the item&#039;s color, and the item&#039;s count goes up by one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the next sheet (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and keep counting. The tool stays on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when you are done. TakeoffCraft goes back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDragging still pans and the wheel still zooms while you count, so you can move round the sheet without adding dots.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the count item to highlight its dots on every sheet. The sheets list then shows how many are on each sheet, which helps you find the sheets you have not counted yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fix-a-count\"\u003EFix a count\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove a dot\u003C/strong\u003E: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), click the dot, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete mark\u003C/span\u003E. Or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E straight after a wrong click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove a dot\u003C/strong\u003E: select it, then drag it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECounted under the wrong item\u003C/strong\u003E: select the dot and pick the right count item in the \u003Cstrong\u003EItem\u003C/strong\u003E list under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"many-identical-symbols\"\u003EMany identical symbols\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a symbol that repeats many times on a sheet, try \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/auto-count\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/a\u003E: box one, review what it finds, and add them all in one step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"using-counts-in-formulas\"\u003EUsing counts in formulas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA count item&#039;s quantity is \u003Cstrong\u003EN\u003C/strong\u003E. Assembly components can use it, for example hinges \u003Ccode\u003EN * 3\u003C/code\u003E for doors. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A count item counts things: doors, light fixtures, outlets, plumbing fixtures. Each click on the sheet adds one count mark. Counting does not need a scale. Count Make or click a count item under Items (Count type). Choose Count in the toolbar, or press C. Click each one on the sheet. Each click puts a dot in the item's color, and the item's count goes up by one. Move to the next sheet (Page Down) and keep counting. The tool stays on Count. Press Esc when you are done. TakeoffCraft goes back to Select. Dragging still pans and the wheel still zooms while you count, so you can move round the sheet without adding dots. Click the count item to highlight its dots on every sheet. The sheets list then shows how many are on each sheet, which helps you find the sheets you have not counted yet. Fix a count Remove a dot: choose Select (V), click the dot, and press Delete or choose Delete mark. Or press Ctrl+Z straight after a wrong click. Move a dot: select it, then drag it. Counted under the wrong item: select the dot and pick the right count item in the Item list under Selected mark. Many identical symbols For a symbol that repeats many times on a sheet, try Auto count: box one, review what it finds, and add them all in one step. Using counts in formulas A count item's quantity is N. Assembly components can use it, for example hinges N * 3 for doors. See Assemblies and formulas.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"auto-count","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Auto count a symbol","summary":"Box one symbol, let TakeoffCraft find the others on this sheet or every open sheet, review the matches with the slider, and add them in one step.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["any angle","rotated","turned","other scale","different scale","auto count","automatic count","symbol search","find symbols","pattern","template","match","detect","count tool"],"headings":[{"id":"find-the-symbols","text":"Find the symbols","level":2},{"id":"review-the-matches","text":"Review the matches","level":2},{"id":"add-them","text":"Add them","level":2},{"id":"what-it-finds-and-what-it-misses","text":"What it finds, and what it misses","level":2},{"id":"speed-and-browsers","text":"Speed and browsers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E finds every copy of a symbol after you box one. You then review the matches on the sheet and add the ones you accept as count marks. It is a helper, not a guarantee: always review before you add.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-the-symbols\"\u003EFind the symbols\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake or click a count item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a sheet where the symbol appears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A bar appears at the top of the sheet: &quot;Drag a box around one symbol on this sheet.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo search every open sheet, not just this one, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESearch all open sheets\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZoom in so the symbol is easy to see, then drag a box snugly round one copy of it. Include the whole symbol; blank paper round it is trimmed off. The bar shows &quot;Searching…&quot; with a progress bar and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E button. The page stays responsive: you can zoom, pan and move between sheets. Choosing another tool, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, cancels the search.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen it finishes, the matches appear on the sheet as boxes in the item&#039;s color, and the bar shows a summary such as &quot;22 matches, 19 to add (3 already counted). Click a box to reject or restore it.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E, dragging on the sheet draws a box instead of panning. To pan, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag, or drag with the middle button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"review-the-matches\"\u003EReview the matches\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClick a box\u003C/strong\u003E to reject it. A rejected box is dashed and crossed out. Click it again to restore it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch\u003C/span\u003E slider\u003C/strong\u003E (50% to 95%, starting at 75%). Higher shows only close matches; lower shows looser ones. The slider only filters what was found; it does not search again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlready counted\u003C/strong\u003E: matches on a spot that already has a count mark of this item start rejected, so you do not count anything twice.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther sheets\u003C/strong\u003E: when you searched all open sheets, move between sheets with the sheets list or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and review each one. The summary says how many sheets have matches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-them\"\u003EAdd them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E. The button says how many, for example &quot;Add 19 marks&quot;. The count marks are added at the center of each accepted box: &quot;Added 19 count marks to &quot;Duplex receptacle&quot;. Ctrl+Z removes them all.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole batch is one undo step, so one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo drop the review without adding anything, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Picking another tool also discards it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-finds-and-what-it-misses\"\u003EWhat it finds, and what it misses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft compares the drawing, as pixels, with the symbol you boxed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt finds the symbol turned 0°, 90°, 180° and 270°. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003EAny angle (slower)\u003C/strong\u003E on the auto count bar before you draw the box to find it at any angle as well: it tries every 15°, then fine-tunes each find to the degree and a half. That takes about six times as long. It does not find a mirrored symbol (unless the symbol looks the same mirrored).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cstrong\u003ESearch all open sheets\u003C/strong\u003E, a sheet at another scale is searched at the size the symbol has there: a fixture drawn to scale on a 1/8&quot; plan is half the size it is on a 1/4&quot; plan, and is still found. Such a sheet is also searched at the box&#039;s own size, for tags and symbols printed at one size on every sheet. This needs a scale on both sheets; a sheet with no scale is searched at the box&#039;s size only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVery similar symbols can match each other: a duplex and a quad receptacle, or two fixture tags that differ by one letter. Raise the slider until only the right ones remain, and reject the rest by hand.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA symbol crossed by other lines, hatching or text scores lower and may need a lower slider setting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVery small boxes are refused: &quot;That box is too small. Draw it around the whole symbol.&quot; An empty box is refused too: &quot;That box is almost empty. Draw it around a symbol.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA sheet that would be very large at the size needed for this symbol is skipped, and the summary says &quot;1 sheet was too large to search at this symbol size.&quot; Box a larger symbol, or count that sheet by hand.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf nothing is found, the status bar says &quot;No matches. Lower the match slider, or box the symbol more tightly.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"speed-and-browsers\"\u003ESpeed and browsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe search runs in a background worker, so the page stays responsive and you can cancel at any time. Each sheet is first drawn at the working size on the page itself, which can make a very large sheet pause for a moment. It runs in current Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari. Searching many sheets takes longer; the progress bar moves sheet by sheet. Offline, auto count works when the app bar shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E keeps the worker too. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Auto count finds every copy of a symbol after you box one. You then review the matches on the sheet and add the ones you accept as count marks. It is a helper, not a guarantee: always review before you add. Find the symbols Make or click a count item under Items. Open a sheet where the symbol appears. Choose Auto count in the toolbar, or press S. A bar appears at the top of the sheet: \"Drag a box around one symbol on this sheet.\" To search every open sheet, not just this one, tick Search all open sheets. Zoom in so the symbol is easy to see, then drag a box snugly round one copy of it. Include the whole symbol; blank paper round it is trimmed off. The bar shows \"Searching…\" with a progress bar and a Cancel button. The page stays responsive: you can zoom, pan and move between sheets. Choosing another tool, or Esc, cancels the search. When it finishes, the matches appear on the sheet as boxes in the item's color, and the bar shows a summary such as \"22 matches, 19 to add (3 already counted). Click a box to reject or restore it.\" In Auto count, dragging on the sheet draws a box instead of panning. To pan, hold Space and drag, or drag with the middle button. Review the matches Click a box to reject it. A rejected box is dashed and crossed out. Click it again to restore it. Move the Match slider (50% to 95%, starting at 75%). Higher shows only close matches; lower shows looser ones. The slider only filters what was found; it does not search again. Already counted: matches on a spot that already has a count mark of this item start rejected, so you do not count anything twice. Other sheets: when you searched all open sheets, move between sheets with the sheets list or Page Down and review each one. The summary says how many sheets have matches. Add them Choose Add. The button says how many, for example \"Add 19 marks\". The count marks are added at the center of each accepted box: \"Added 19 count marks to \"Duplex receptacle\". Ctrl+Z removes them all.\" The whole batch is one undo step, so one Ctrl+Z removes it. To drop the review without adding anything, choose Discard or press Esc. Picking another tool also discards it. What it finds, and what it misses TakeoffCraft compares the drawing, as pixels, with the symbol you boxed. It finds the symbol turned 0°, 90°, 180° and 270°. Tick Any angle (slower) on the auto count bar before you draw the box to find it at any angle as well: it tries every 15°, then fine-tunes each find to the degree and a half. That takes about six times as long. It does not find a mirrored symbol (unless the symbol looks the same mirrored). With Search all open sheets, a sheet at another scale is searched at the size the symbol has there: a fixture drawn to scale on a 1/8\" plan is half the size it is on a 1/4\" plan, and is still found. Such a sheet is also searched at the box's own size, for tags and symbols printed at one size on every sheet. This needs a scale on both sheets; a sheet with no scale is searched at the box's size only. Very similar symbols can match each other: a duplex and a quad receptacle, or two fixture tags that differ by one letter. Raise the slider until only the right ones remain, and reject the rest by hand. A symbol crossed by other lines, hatching or text scores lower and may need a lower slider setting. Very small boxes are refused: \"That box is too small. Draw it around the whole symbol.\" An empty box is refused too: \"That box is almost empty. Draw it around a symbol.\" A sheet that would be very large at the size needed for this symbol is skipped, and the summary says \"1 sheet was too large to search at this symbol size.\" Box a larger symbol, or count that sheet by hand. If nothing is found, the status bar says \"No matches. Lower the match slider, or box the symbol more tightly.\" Speed and browsers The search runs in a background worker, so the page stays responsive and you can cancel at any time. Each sheet is first drawn at the working size on the page itself, which can make a very large sheet pause for a moment. It runs in current Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari. Searching many sheets takes longer; the progress bar moves sheet by sheet. Offline, auto count works when the app bar shows Offline ready: Save for offline keeps the worker too. See Working offline.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"count-from-a-drawing","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Count from a drawing","summary":"Count blocks, objects on a layer, or text straight from a DWG or DXF: exact, with nested blocks, dynamic block variants and block arrays handled, in one undo step.","section":"tasks","order":71,"keywords":["count from drawing","cad count","block count","count blocks","insert","block name","nested blocks","dynamic block","variant","minsert","array","layer","entity type","circles","text","attribute","tag","hidden layers","exact count","how to","doors","fixtures"],"headings":[{"id":"count-a-block","text":"Count a block","level":2},{"id":"what-counts","text":"What counts","level":3},{"id":"what-does-not","text":"What does not","level":3},{"id":"what-this-sheet-shows-or-the-whole-drawing","text":"What this sheet shows, or the whole drawing","level":2},{"id":"count-counts-again","text":"Count counts again","level":2},{"id":"by-layer-and-type","text":"By layer and type","level":2},{"id":"by-text","text":"By text","level":2},{"id":"when-to-use-auto-count-instead","text":"When to use Auto count instead","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECAD count\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar (the command \u003Cstrong\u003ECount from the drawing\u003C/strong\u003E in the other menu styles) counts what a DWG or DXF says is there. Where \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E looks for a picture of a symbol and can miss one or find a false one, this counts the objects: every insert of a block named \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-36\u003C/code\u003E is one door. Open the drawing first: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/open-a-drawing\"\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"count-a-block\"\u003ECount a block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a sheet of the drawing. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECAD count\u003C/span\u003E is on the sheet bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose it. The window lists the blocks with how many of each there are. Search by name, then pick one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the line under the list: &quot;22 found, 22 to add.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to\u003C/span\u003E, pick a count item, or leave \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA new count item\u003C/span\u003E and give it a name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the add button, which names the number: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd 22 marks\u003C/span\u003E. The marks appear at the block&#039;s insertion points, and the item&#039;s quantity is the count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is one undo step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the marks (and a new item) away together. The estimate, the spreadsheet and the marked PDF treat the item like any other count item.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-counts\"\u003EWhat counts\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery insert of the block name\u003C/strong\u003E, whatever its scale and angle, in model space or paper space. Block names do not care about case.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBlock arrays\u003C/strong\u003E (MINSERT): each copy in the array is one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENested blocks.\u003C/strong\u003E A block placed inside another block counts at every depth. The list says &quot;6 inside other blocks&quot;; untick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount blocks inside other blocks too\u003C/span\u003E to count only inserts placed directly in the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDynamic blocks.\u003C/strong\u003E A dynamic block counts under its own name, not under the anonymous copies (\u003Ccode\u003E*U12\u003C/code\u003E) the file keeps. Its states are listed apart (&quot;Single 12, Double 4&quot;): pick a state to count only those, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery variant\u003C/span\u003E. When a file does not say which state a copy shows, those are listed as &quot;(state not known)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayer filter.\u003C/strong\u003E A block on several layers: pick one under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOn layer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-does-not\"\u003EWhat does not\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHidden layers.\u003C/strong\u003E An object on a layer that is off or frozen in the file (or that you hid in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E) is left out, and the line says how many: &quot;3 on hidden layers left out&quot;. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount them too\u003C/span\u003E to include them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings that are not open\u003C/strong\u003E are not counted; the window says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EObjects stacked exactly on another of the same kind\u003C/strong\u003E are counted as two (they are two objects) and the window says so: it is usually a drafting slip worth a look.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-this-sheet-shows-or-the-whole-drawing\"\u003EWhat this sheet shows, or the whole drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhat this sheet shows\u003C/strong\u003E counts the objects the sheet shows: on a layout, the model objects inside its viewports (a layer frozen in a viewport is not counted there) plus the layout&#039;s own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe whole drawing, every object once\u003C/strong\u003E counts model space once, with the marks on the Model sheet, and does not count what several layouts show again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECounting what a layout shows and counting what Model shows give the same number for the same objects; do not add both to one item.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"count-counts-again\"\u003ECount counts again\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECounting the same block again with the same item adds only what is new: &quot;22 already counted.&quot; Each mark stands for one object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-layer-and-type\"\u003EBy layer and type\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy layer and type\u003C/span\u003E pick a layer and an object type (circles, polylines, hatches…): &quot;all circles on E-LITE&quot;. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlso count objects inside blocks\u003C/span\u003E to include those inside block definitions, at each place the block is inserted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-text\"\u003EBy text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy text\u003C/span\u003E type the text, such as \u003Ccode\u003EA3\u003C/code\u003E, and say whether it is exactly that, contains it, or has it as a whole word. Text objects, multileader text and block \u003Cstrong\u003Eattribute values\u003C/strong\u003E all count (a tag attribute \u003Ccode\u003EITEM = A3\u003C/code\u003E is found); pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn attribute\u003C/span\u003E to count one attribute only. The most common texts are listed to click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-to-use-auto-count-instead\"\u003EWhen to use Auto count instead\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E for PDFs and for symbols in a drawing that are not blocks (drawn loose, or exploded). Drawn as blocks, count them here.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"CAD count on the sheet bar (the command Count from the drawing in the other menu styles) counts what a DWG or DXF says is there. Where Auto count looks for a picture of a symbol and can miss one or find a false one, this counts the objects: every insert of a block named DOOR-36 is one door. Open the drawing first: Open a DWG or DXF drawing. Count a block Open a sheet of the drawing. CAD count is on the sheet bar. Choose it. The window lists the blocks with how many of each there are. Search by name, then pick one. Check the line under the list: \"22 found, 22 to add.\" Under Add to, pick a count item, or leave A new count item and give it a name. Choose the add button, which names the number: Add 22 marks. The marks appear at the block's insertion points, and the item's quantity is the count. It is one undo step: Ctrl+Z takes the marks (and a new item) away together. The estimate, the spreadsheet and the marked PDF treat the item like any other count item. What counts Every insert of the block name, whatever its scale and angle, in model space or paper space. Block names do not care about case. Block arrays (MINSERT): each copy in the array is one. Nested blocks. A block placed inside another block counts at every depth. The list says \"6 inside other blocks\"; untick Count blocks inside other blocks too to count only inserts placed directly in the drawing. Dynamic blocks. A dynamic block counts under its own name, not under the anonymous copies (*U12) the file keeps. Its states are listed apart (\"Single 12, Double 4\"): pick a state to count only those, or Every variant. When a file does not say which state a copy shows, those are listed as \"(state not known)\". Layer filter. A block on several layers: pick one under On layer. What does not Hidden layers. An object on a layer that is off or frozen in the file (or that you hid in Layers) is left out, and the line says how many: \"3 on hidden layers left out\". Tick Count them too to include them. Linked drawings that are not open are not counted; the window says so. Objects stacked exactly on another of the same kind are counted as two (they are two objects) and the window says so: it is usually a drafting slip worth a look. What this sheet shows, or the whole drawing What this sheet shows counts the objects the sheet shows: on a layout, the model objects inside its viewports (a layer frozen in a viewport is not counted there) plus the layout's own. The whole drawing, every object once counts model space once, with the marks on the Model sheet, and does not count what several layouts show again. Counting what a layout shows and counting what Model shows give the same number for the same objects; do not add both to one item. Count counts again Counting the same block again with the same item adds only what is new: \"22 already counted.\" Each mark stands for one object. By layer and type On By layer and type pick a layer and an object type (circles, polylines, hatches…): \"all circles on E-LITE\". Tick Also count objects inside blocks to include those inside block definitions, at each place the block is inserted. By text On By text type the text, such as A3, and say whether it is exactly that, contains it, or has it as a whole word. Text objects, multileader text and block attribute values all count (a tag attribute ITEM = A3 is found); pick In attribute to count one attribute only. The most common texts are listed to click. When to use Auto count instead Use Auto count for PDFs and for symbols in a drawing that are not blocks (drawn loose, or exploded). Drawn as blocks, count them here.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"copy-paste","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Copy, paste and duplicate marks","summary":"Copy marks with their items, paste them where you click or in the same place on another sheet, duplicate them, cut, delete and select all, each one step to undo.","section":"tasks","order":75,"keywords":["copy","paste","cut","duplicate","paste in place","clipboard","select all","select none","multiple marks","typical floor","repeat","same plan","another sheet","copy item","duplicate item"],"headings":[{"id":"select-the-marks","text":"Select the marks","level":2},{"id":"copy-and-paste-where-you-click","text":"Copy and paste where you click","level":2},{"id":"paste-in-place","text":"Paste in place","level":2},{"id":"the-scale-must-match","text":"The scale must match","level":2},{"id":"duplicate","text":"Duplicate","level":2},{"id":"cut-and-delete","text":"Cut and delete","level":2},{"id":"copy-an-item","text":"Copy an item","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group of the toolbar holds \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E. Its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu has the rest, each with its key: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E. Every one of them is one step to undo with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-the-marks\"\u003ESelect the marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click a mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click more marks to add them. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click on a selected mark takes it out again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E) to take every mark on the open sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith several marks selected, the right panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected marks\u003C/strong\u003E: how many of each item, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete marks\u003C/span\u003E. Corner handles show only when one mark is selected. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E) clears the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-and-paste-where-you-click\"\u003ECopy and paste where you click\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the marks and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E). The marks are copied with their items, including each item&#039;s assembly, waste and costs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGo to the sheet you want them on (it can be the same sheet).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E). A dashed copy follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it goes. The middle of the copied marks lands at the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels and adds nothing. The pasted marks are selected afterwards, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes them away again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the same takeoff, pasted marks go on the \u003Cstrong\u003Esame items\u003C/strong\u003E, so their quantities add to those items. In another takeoff (another tab, or after \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E), an item with the same name and type is used; otherwise the item is added with its assembly. If the name is taken by an item of another type, the added item is called &quot;(name) (copy)&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPasted marks go in the bid area chosen at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel (or in none). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-areas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-areas\"\u003EBid areas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-in-place\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E) puts the copy at exactly the same spot on the open sheet, by its position on the page. Use it for a typical floor: take off level 2, copy, open level 3, paste in place.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPaste in place needs a sheet the \u003Cstrong\u003Esame size\u003C/strong\u003E as the one you copied from. On a sheet of another size TakeoffCraft says so and pastes nothing; use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and click instead. On the sheet you copied from, the copy lands on top of the originals.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-scale-must-match\"\u003EThe scale must match\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths and areas depend on the sheet&#039;s scale. A run that measures 10&#039; at 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; would measure 20&#039; on a sheet at 1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;. So TakeoffCraft pastes runs and areas only onto a sheet with the \u003Cstrong\u003Esame scale\u003C/strong\u003E as the sheet they came from, and says plainly when it does not:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe open sheet has no scale yet: give it one first (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E), then paste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe open sheet has another scale: the message names both scales. Paste on a sheet at the same scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECounts need no scale: copied counts paste anywhere.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"duplicate\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E) copies the selected marks and puts the copy on the pointer straight away; click where it goes. Nothing is added until you click, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leaves nothing. Duplicate does not change what \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E pastes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-and-delete\"\u003ECut and delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E) copies the selected marks and deletes them. Paste them where they belong, or undo the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E) deletes the selected marks without copying them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-an-item\"\u003ECopy an item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe item editor (under \u003Cstrong\u003EItems\u003C/strong\u003E) ends with:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy item\u003C/span\u003E: copies the item with its assembly, waste and costs, without marks. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E then adds a copy of it to this takeoff, or to another one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate item\u003C/span\u003E: adds a copy at once, named &quot;(name) (copy)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays on this computer: it is kept in this tab and in the browser&#039;s storage, so another TakeoffCraft tab can paste it. Nothing is uploaded, and the system clipboard is not used, so you cannot paste marks into other programs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA copy comes from one sheet at a time. Marks are pasted as they are: there is no rotating or mirroring a copy yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The Edit group of the toolbar holds Undo, Redo, Copy and Paste. Its More menu has the rest, each with its key: Cut, Copy, Paste, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all and Select none. Every one of them is one step to undo with Ctrl+Z. Select the marks Pick Select (V) and click a mark. Hold Shift and click more marks to add them. Shift + click on a selected mark takes it out again. Or press Ctrl+A (Select all) to take every mark on the open sheet. With several marks selected, the right panel shows Selected marks: how many of each item, with Copy, Duplicate, Cut and Delete marks. Corner handles show only when one mark is selected. Esc (Select none) clears the selection. Copy and paste where you click Select the marks and press Ctrl+C (Copy). The marks are copied with their items, including each item's assembly, waste and costs. Go to the sheet you want them on (it can be the same sheet). Press Ctrl+V (Paste). A dashed copy follows the pointer. Click where it goes. The middle of the copied marks lands at the pointer. Esc cancels and adds nothing. The pasted marks are selected afterwards, so Delete or Ctrl+Z takes them away again. In the same takeoff, pasted marks go on the same items, so their quantities add to those items. In another takeoff (another tab, or after New), an item with the same name and type is used; otherwise the item is added with its assembly. If the name is taken by an item of another type, the added item is called \"(name) (copy)\". Pasted marks go in the bid area chosen at the top of the Items panel (or in none). See Bid areas. Paste in place Ctrl+Shift+V (Paste in place) puts the copy at exactly the same spot on the open sheet, by its position on the page. Use it for a typical floor: take off level 2, copy, open level 3, paste in place. Paste in place needs a sheet the same size as the one you copied from. On a sheet of another size TakeoffCraft says so and pastes nothing; use Paste and click instead. On the sheet you copied from, the copy lands on top of the originals. The scale must match Lengths and areas depend on the sheet's scale. A run that measures 10' at 1/4\" = 1'-0\" would measure 20' on a sheet at 1/8\" = 1'-0\". So TakeoffCraft pastes runs and areas only onto a sheet with the same scale as the sheet they came from, and says plainly when it does not: The open sheet has no scale yet: give it one first (Set the scale), then paste. The open sheet has another scale: the message names both scales. Paste on a sheet at the same scale. Counts need no scale: copied counts paste anywhere. Duplicate Ctrl+D (Duplicate) copies the selected marks and puts the copy on the pointer straight away; click where it goes. Nothing is added until you click, and Esc leaves nothing. Duplicate does not change what Paste pastes. Cut and delete Ctrl+X (Cut) copies the selected marks and deletes them. Paste them where they belong, or undo the cut. Delete or Backspace (Delete) deletes the selected marks without copying them. Copy an item The item editor (under Items) ends with: Copy item: copies the item with its assembly, waste and costs, without marks. Paste then adds a copy of it to this takeoff, or to another one. Duplicate item: adds a copy at once, named \"(name) (copy)\". The copy stays on this computer: it is kept in this tab and in the browser's storage, so another TakeoffCraft tab can paste it. Nothing is uploaded, and the system clipboard is not used, so you cannot paste marks into other programs. A copy comes from one sheet at a time. Marks are pasted as they are: there is no rotating or mirroring a copy yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"assemblies","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Assemblies and formulas","summary":"Add components to an item and let formulas turn its length, area or count into studs, plates, sheets of board, concrete and hardware, with a worked wall example.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["assembly","components","formula","studs","drywall","sheets","plates","concrete","cubic yards","hardware","recipe","typical","item parts"],"headings":[{"id":"variables-you-can-use","text":"Variables you can use","level":2},{"id":"worked-example-an-interior-wall","text":"Worked example: an interior wall","level":2},{"id":"more-formulas","text":"More formulas","level":2},{"id":"when-a-formula-is-wrong","text":"When a formula is wrong","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2},{"id":"remove-a-component","text":"Remove a component","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn item&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Eassembly\u003C/strong\u003E is a list of components, each with a quantity formula and its own waste and rates. The formula uses the item&#039;s measured totals, so one takeoff of a wall gives you studs, plates and board at once, and they update as you measure.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"variables-you-can-use\"\u003EVariables you can use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVariables\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E length, \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E height, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E wall area (L × H), \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E number of runs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E area, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E perimeter (outline + cut-outs), \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E depth or thickness, \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E number of areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E count\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EValues are in the item&#039;s own units: feet and square feet for LF and SF items, meters and square meters for m and m² items. For an SY item, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E is in square yards while \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E stay in feet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFormulas use \u003Ccode\u003E+ - * /\u003C/code\u003E (or × ÷), parentheses, numbers, and the functions \u003Ccode\u003Eceil\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Efloor\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eround\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emin\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emax\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eabs\u003C/code\u003E. The full rules are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/formulas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/formulas\"\u003EFormula language\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"worked-example-an-interior-wall\"\u003EWorked example: an interior wall\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESay you have taken off \u003Cstrong\u003EInterior wall type A\u003C/strong\u003E as a linear item: 4 runs totaling 120 LF, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E set to \u003Ccode\u003E9&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. Its \u003Cstrong\u003EMeasured\u003C/strong\u003E values read L = 120, N = 4, A = 1,080 (SF of wall, one face).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd component\u003C/span\u003E. A component called &quot;Component&quot; appears, with the formula \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and its result below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EStuds 16 in o.c.\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EEA\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity formula\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(L * 12 / 16) + N\u003C/code\u003E. The result updates as you type: &quot;= 94 EA&quot;. That is one stud every 16 inches along 120 feet (90), plus one extra to close each of the 4 runs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd component\u003C/span\u003E again for the plates: name \u003Ccode\u003EPlates, top and bottom\u003C/code\u003E, unit \u003Ccode\u003ELF\u003C/code\u003E, formula \u003Ccode\u003EL * 2\u003C/code\u003E. Result: 240 LF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the board: name \u003Ccode\u003EGypsum board 4x8, both sides\u003C/code\u003E, unit \u003Ccode\u003Esheets\u003C/code\u003E, formula \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(A * 2 / 32)\u003C/code\u003E. Result: 68 sheets (2,160 SF of board ÷ 32 SF per sheet = 67.5, rounded up).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in each component&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWaste %\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial $\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabor $\u003C/span\u003E. Each component gets its own row under the item in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E, priced like an item, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob total\u003C/span\u003E includes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the wall cost is all in the components, leave the item&#039;s own material and labor rates at 0, or it is counted twice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWaste is applied after the formula and is not rounded: 68 sheets with 10% waste is 74.8 sheets. To order whole sheets, put the waste inside the formula, like \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(A * 2 / 32 * 1.1)\u003C/code\u003E, and leave the component&#039;s waste at 0.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-formulas\"\u003EMore formulas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormula\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStuds at 24 in on center\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(L / 2) + N\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInsulation batts, SF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear with height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaint, both sides, gallons at 350 SF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear with height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(A * 2 / 350)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETile cartons at 15 SF, 10% extra\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(A * 1.1 / 15)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBase or edge trim, LF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConcrete, cubic yards\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea with depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA * H / 27\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConcrete rounded up to a quarter yard\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea with depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(A * H / 27 * 4) / 4\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHinges, three per door\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN * 3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAt least one box, one per 12\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emax(1, ceil(N / 12))\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-formula-is-wrong\"\u003EWhen a formula is wrong\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line under the formula turns into an error message, for example \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;X&quot; is not a measured value here. Use L, H, A, N.\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EA &quot;)&quot; is missing.\u003C/code\u003E The component then has no amounts, its row in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E shows &quot;Formula error: …&quot;, and the header says &quot;Incomplete: 1 formula error (not in the totals)&quot;. Fix the formula and the totals update.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA formula that divides by zero is an error too (&quot;The formula divides by zero.&quot;), for example \u003Ccode\u003EA / H\u003C/code\u003E on an item with no depth.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFormulas run on the item&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Etotals\u003C/strong\u003E, not run by run. \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(L * 12 / 16) + N\u003C/code\u003E adds one stud per run; there is no per-run rounding yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA formula can only use its own item&#039;s variables, not another item&#039;s.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUp to 50 components per item, 500 characters per formula.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"remove-a-component\"\u003ERemove a component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove component\u003C/span\u003E under it. You can undo it with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"An item's assembly is a list of components, each with a quantity formula and its own waste and rates. The formula uses the item's measured totals, so one takeoff of a wall gives you studs, plates and board at once, and they update as you measure. Variables you can use Item type Variables Linear L length, H height, A wall area (L × H), N number of runs Area A area, P perimeter (outline + cut-outs), H depth or thickness, N number of areas Count N count Values are in the item's own units: feet and square feet for LF and SF items, meters and square meters for m and m² items. For an SY item, A is in square yards while P and H stay in feet. Formulas use + - * / (or × ÷), parentheses, numbers, and the functions ceil, floor, round, min, max and abs. The full rules are in Formula language. Worked example: an interior wall Say you have taken off Interior wall type A as a linear item: 4 runs totaling 120 LF, with Height set to 9'-0\". Its Measured values read L = 120, N = 4, A = 1,080 (SF of wall, one face). Select the item under Items. In Assembly, choose Add component. A component called \"Component\" appears, with the formula L and its result below. Type Studs 16 in o.c. in Name and EA in Unit. In Quantity formula, type ceil(L * 12 / 16) + N. The result updates as you type: \"= 94 EA\". That is one stud every 16 inches along 120 feet (90), plus one extra to close each of the 4 runs. Choose Add component again for the plates: name Plates, top and bottom, unit LF, formula L * 2. Result: 240 LF. Add the board: name Gypsum board 4x8, both sides, unit sheets, formula ceil(A * 2 / 32). Result: 68 sheets (2,160 SF of board ÷ 32 SF per sheet = 67.5, rounded up). Fill in each component's Waste %, Material $ and Labor $. Each component gets its own row under the item in Quantities, priced like an item, and the Job total includes it. If the wall cost is all in the components, leave the item's own material and labor rates at 0, or it is counted twice. Waste is applied after the formula and is not rounded: 68 sheets with 10% waste is 74.8 sheets. To order whole sheets, put the waste inside the formula, like ceil(A * 2 / 32 * 1.1), and leave the component's waste at 0. More formulas What Item Formula Studs at 24 in on center Linear ceil(L / 2) + N Insulation batts, SF Linear with height A Paint, both sides, gallons at 350 SF Linear with height ceil(A * 2 / 350) Tile cartons at 15 SF, 10% extra Area ceil(A * 1.1 / 15) Base or edge trim, LF Area P Concrete, cubic yards Area with depth A * H / 27 Concrete rounded up to a quarter yard Area with depth ceil(A * H / 27 * 4) / 4 Hinges, three per door Count N * 3 At least one box, one per 12 Count max(1, ceil(N / 12)) When a formula is wrong The line under the formula turns into an error message, for example \"X\" is not a measured value here. Use L, H, A, N. or A \")\" is missing. The component then has no amounts, its row in Quantities shows \"Formula error: …\", and the header says \"Incomplete: 1 formula error (not in the totals)\". Fix the formula and the totals update. A formula that divides by zero is an error too (\"The formula divides by zero.\"), for example A / H on an item with no depth. Limits Formulas run on the item's totals, not run by run. ceil(L * 12 / 16) + N adds one stud per run; there is no per-run rounding yet. A formula can only use its own item's variables, not another item's. Up to 50 components per item, 500 characters per formula. Remove a component Choose Remove component under it. You can undo it with Ctrl+Z.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"price-library","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Price library","summary":"Keep your items, rates and assemblies in a library, import it from a CSV or a past estimate, add items from it to any takeoff, and update a takeoff's prices from it.","section":"tasks","order":82,"keywords":["library","trade packs","price list","price book","cost database","import csv","rates","assemblies","reuse","template items","update prices","reprice"],"headings":[{"id":"fill-the-library","text":"Fill the library","level":2},{"id":"use-it-in-a-takeoff","text":"Use it in a takeoff","level":2},{"id":"the-csv-columns","text":"The CSV columns","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003Eprice library\u003C/strong\u003E holds items you use on job after job: their type, unit, height, waste, material and labor rates, and their assembly. It is kept in this browser, apart from any takeoff, so every takeoff you open here can use it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-the-library\"\u003EFill the library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport CSV…\u003C/span\u003E reads a spreadsheet saved as CSV (see the columns below). Items whose name and type are already in the library are replaced; the rest are added. Rows it cannot read are skipped, and it says which and why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd this takeoff&#039;s items\u003C/span\u003E copies every item of the open takeoff into the library, with its rates and assembly (not its marks).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe estimate CSV TakeoffCraft exports (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-estimate\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-estimate\"\u003EExport the estimate\u003C/a\u003E) imports as it is: last job&#039;s prices seed the next one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrade packs…\u003C/span\u003E installs free assemblies for a trade, such as drywall, electrical or concrete, without prices. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/trade-packs\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/trade-packs\"\u003ETrade packs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport CSV\u003C/span\u003E saves the library as a CSV, to keep a copy, to edit it in a spreadsheet or to give it to a colleague. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E empties it (click twice).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe library lives in this browser&#039;s storage. Another browser, another computer or a private window has its own (or none). Export it to move it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-it-in-a-takeoff\"\u003EUse it in a takeoff\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick items (type in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind in the library\u003C/span\u003E to narrow the list) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to takeoff\u003C/span\u003E. They are added with their assemblies in one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back. A name already in the takeoff gets a number, like \u003Ccode\u003ECarpet (2)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate prices\u003C/span\u003E gives the takeoff&#039;s items the library&#039;s waste and rates, for every item whose name and type match a library item, and the same for assembly components of the same name. Heights, units and formulas stay as they are. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts all the old prices back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-csv-columns\"\u003EThe CSV columns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first row names the columns, in any order; other columns are ignored.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat goes in it\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem (or Name)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s name. Required.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear, Area or Count (LF, SF and EA work too). Left empty, the unit decides.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF or m; SF, SY or m²; EA. Empty: the type&#039;s usual unit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWaste %\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E10%\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial per unit, Labor per unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERates like \u003Ccode\u003E2.10\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E$1,250\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHeight (or Depth)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E9&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E9\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003E2.7\u003C/code\u003E for metric units.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EComponent, Formula\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA row with a Component name (or the Type \u003Ccode\u003EComponent\u003C/code\u003E) is part of the assembly of the item above it. Its Unit, Waste and rates are its own; its Formula uses the item&#039;s letters, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EItem,Type,Unit,Waste %,Material per unit,Labor per unit,Height,Component,Formula\nPartition type A,Linear,LF,10,2.10,3.33,9&#039;-0&quot;,,\n,Component,EA,,4.25,,,Studs 16 in. o.c.,ceil(L / 1.333) + N\nVCT flooring,Area,SF,5,1.85,1.10,,,\nDuplex outlets,Count,EA,,3.50,45,,,\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECommas, semicolons or tabs between columns all work.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The price library holds items you use on job after job: their type, unit, height, waste, material and labor rates, and their assembly. It is kept in this browser, apart from any takeoff, so every takeoff you open here can use it. Open it with Library at the top of the Items panel. Fill the library Import CSV… reads a spreadsheet saved as CSV (see the columns below). Items whose name and type are already in the library are replaced; the rest are added. Rows it cannot read are skipped, and it says which and why. Add this takeoff's items copies every item of the open takeoff into the library, with its rates and assembly (not its marks). The estimate CSV TakeoffCraft exports (Export the estimate) imports as it is: last job's prices seed the next one. Trade packs… installs free assemblies for a trade, such as drywall, electrical or concrete, without prices. See Trade packs. Export CSV saves the library as a CSV, to keep a copy, to edit it in a spreadsheet or to give it to a colleague. Clear empties it (click twice). The library lives in this browser's storage. Another browser, another computer or a private window has its own (or none). Export it to move it. Use it in a takeoff Tick items (type in Find in the library to narrow the list) and choose Add to takeoff. They are added with their assemblies in one step that Ctrl+Z takes back. A name already in the takeoff gets a number, like Carpet (2). Update prices gives the takeoff's items the library's waste and rates, for every item whose name and type match a library item, and the same for assembly components of the same name. Heights, units and formulas stay as they are. One Ctrl+Z puts all the old prices back. The CSV columns The first row names the columns, in any order; other columns are ignored. Column What goes in it Item (or Name) The item's name. Required. Type Linear, Area or Count (LF, SF and EA work too). Left empty, the unit decides. Unit LF or m; SF, SY or m²; EA. Empty: the type's usual unit. Waste % 10 or 10%. Material per unit, Labor per unit Rates like 2.10 or $1,250. Height (or Depth) 9'-0\", 9, or 2.7 for metric units. Component, Formula A row with a Component name (or the Type Component) is part of the assembly of the item above it. Its Unit, Waste and rates are its own; its Formula uses the item's letters, see Assemblies and formulas. Item,Type,Unit,Waste %,Material per unit,Labor per unit,Height,Component,Formula Partition type A,Linear,LF,10,2.10,3.33,9'-0\",, ,Component,EA,,4.25,,,Studs 16 in. o.c.,ceil(L / 1.333) + N VCT flooring,Area,SF,5,1.85,1.10,,, Duplex outlets,Count,EA,,3.50,45,,, Commas, semicolons or tabs between columns all work.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"trade-packs","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Trade packs","summary":"Install free assemblies for a trade into your price library, remove them in one step, and share your own library as a pack.","section":"tasks","order":83,"keywords":["trade pack","pack","assemblies","templates","starter items","electrical","drywall","framing","painting","concrete","flooring","masonry","roofing","siding","carpentry","plumbing","hvac","insulation","landscape","decking","general contractor","csi","masterformat","cc0","install","remove","share"],"headings":[{"id":"install-a-pack","text":"Install a pack","level":2},{"id":"remove-a-pack","text":"Remove a pack","level":2},{"id":"what-is-in-a-pack","text":"What is in a pack","level":2},{"id":"share-your-library-as-a-pack","text":"Share your library as a pack","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Etrade pack\u003C/strong\u003E is a set of ready-made items for one trade: each with its unit, its height or depth, its waste and its \u003Cstrong\u003Eassembly\u003C/strong\u003E, the parts and formulas that turn a measured run, area or count into materials. A drywall partition, for example, brings its track, studs, sheets, screws, tape and compound.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPacks install into your \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/price-library\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/price-library\"\u003Eprice library\u003C/a\u003E, and from there you add their items to any takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"install-a-pack\"\u003EInstall a pack\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrade packs…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E: imperial (LF, SF), metric (m, m²) or both. Every assembly comes in both systems.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInstall\u003C/span\u003E beside a pack.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pack&#039;s items appear in the library, tagged with the pack&#039;s name. To use them, tick them in the library and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to takeoff\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA pack brings no prices.\u003C/strong\u003E Its items show &quot;set your rate&quot; in the library. Rates are yours: type them in the library or the item editor, or import your price list as a CSV and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate prices\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you already have an item of the same name and type in your library, the pack leaves yours as it is. Installing a pack again replaces its own items with the new version.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"remove-a-pack\"\u003ERemove a pack\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrade packs…\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E beside an installed pack, then once more to confirm. Every item that came from it leaves the library in one step. Items you already added to a takeoff stay in that takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-a-pack\"\u003EWhat is in a pack\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESources and details\u003C/span\u003E under a pack to see what each assembly was written from: building codes, standards and trade associations&#039; installation guides. In the library, hold the pointer over an item to read its sources and its assumptions, such as stud spacing or a paint&#039;s spread rate. Check them against your job and your products&#039; data sheets, and change the formulas where they differ (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery pack is marked \u003Cstrong\u003EStarter: not reviewed by an estimator yet\u003C/strong\u003E until working estimators have reviewed it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach item also carries its CSI MasterFormat number, such as \u003Ccode\u003E09 21 16\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-your-library-as-a-pack\"\u003EShare your library as a pack\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport as pack…\u003C/span\u003E in the library saves it as a pack file (\u003Ccode\u003E.ccpack.json\u003C/code\u003E) that anyone can install with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a pack file…\u003C/span\u003E. By default it leaves out the items that came from installed packs, and it carries no rates. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInclude my rates as sample rates\u003C/span\u003E to send your rates too: they go out labelled with the region and the date you give, and whoever installs the pack chooses whether to use them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPack files are plain data, never programs. TakeoffCraft checks every part of one before it uses it and leaves out anything it cannot read.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A trade pack is a set of ready-made items for one trade: each with its unit, its height or depth, its waste and its assembly, the parts and formulas that turn a measured run, area or count into materials. A drywall partition, for example, brings its track, studs, sheets, screws, tape and compound. Packs install into your price library, and from there you add their items to any takeoff. Install a pack Open Library at the top of the Items panel, then Trade packs…. Choose the Units: imperial (LF, SF), metric (m, m²) or both. Every assembly comes in both systems. Choose Install beside a pack. The pack's items appear in the library, tagged with the pack's name. To use them, tick them in the library and choose Add to takeoff. A pack brings no prices. Its items show \"set your rate\" in the library. Rates are yours: type them in the library or the item editor, or import your price list as a CSV and use Update prices. If you already have an item of the same name and type in your library, the pack leaves yours as it is. Installing a pack again replaces its own items with the new version. Remove a pack In Trade packs…, choose Remove beside an installed pack, then once more to confirm. Every item that came from it leaves the library in one step. Items you already added to a takeoff stay in that takeoff. What is in a pack Choose Sources and details under a pack to see what each assembly was written from: building codes, standards and trade associations' installation guides. In the library, hold the pointer over an item to read its sources and its assumptions, such as stud spacing or a paint's spread rate. Check them against your job and your products' data sheets, and change the formulas where they differ (see Assemblies and formulas). Every pack is marked Starter: not reviewed by an estimator yet until working estimators have reviewed it. Each item also carries its CSI MasterFormat number, such as 09 21 16. Share your library as a pack Export as pack… in the library saves it as a pack file (.ccpack.json) that anyone can install with Open a pack file…. By default it leaves out the items that came from installed packs, and it carries no rates. Tick Include my rates as sample rates to send your rates too: they go out labelled with the region and the date you give, and whoever installs the pack chooses whether to use them. Pack files are plain data, never programs. TakeoffCraft checks every part of one before it uses it and leaves out anything it cannot read.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"earthwork","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Earthwork and spot elevations","summary":"Mark spot elevations on a site plan, hand them to CivilCraft for the surface and cut and fill, and take simple excavation quantities here.","section":"tasks","order":84,"keywords":["earthwork","excavation","cut","fill","cut and fill","grading","spot elevation","elevations","topo","surface","triangulation","civilcraft","points","pnezd","trench"],"headings":[{"id":"simple-excavation-here","text":"Simple excavation here","level":2},{"id":"spot-elevations-for-civilcraft","text":"Spot elevations for CivilCraft","level":2},{"id":"cut-and-fill-from-civilcraft","text":"Cut and fill from CivilCraft","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft measures what is drawn on a sheet. Earthwork needs a ground surface, so it is shared with CivilCraft, which builds surfaces from points, grades them and works out cut and fill and haul.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"simple-excavation-here\"\u003ESimple excavation here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a footprint dug to one depth, an area item with a \u003Cstrong\u003Edepth\u003C/strong\u003E is enough: the volume is area × depth. The general contractor \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/trade-packs\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/trade-packs\"\u003Etrade pack\u003C/a\u003E has a bulk excavation item (bank volume, loose volume and truck loads) and a utility trench item (excavation, bedding and backfill by length and depth).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"spot-elevations-for-civilcraft\"\u003ESpot elevations for CivilCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the site sheet&#039;s scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake a \u003Cstrong\u003ECount\u003C/strong\u003E item, for example &quot;Existing grade&quot;, and click each spot elevation on the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a mark and type its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E in the Selected mark panel: \u003Ccode\u003E102.5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E102&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E31.24 m\u003C/code\u003E. Negative and zero elevations are fine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpot elevations\u003C/span\u003E. You get a points file (\u003Ccode\u003E.csv\u003C/code\u003E) with Point, Northing, Easting, Elevation and Description, one row per marked point of the open sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen that file in CivilCraft (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/import-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/import-points\"\u003EImport points\u003C/a\u003E). It builds the surface from the points (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/build-surface\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/build-surface\"\u003EBuild a surface\u003C/a\u003E); from there it compares surfaces and gives cut and fill (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/volumes\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/volumes\"\u003EVolumes\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENorthing and easting are the sheet&#039;s own coordinates in feet, measured from the page&#039;s corner at its scale: distances and volumes between the points are true, but they are not your site&#039;s survey coordinates. Move them onto the site&#039;s coordinates in CivilCraft when you need to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints from different sheets do not share coordinates, so the export takes the open sheet only. Elevations are saved with the takeoff and carried by copy and paste.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-and-fill-from-civilcraft\"\u003ECut and fill from CivilCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft&#039;s earthwork comes back as two items you can price:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CivilCraft, grade your pads and corridors, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend to TakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/volumes\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/volumes\"\u003EEarthwork volumes\u003C/a\u003E). It saves a quantities file (\u003Ccode\u003E.quantities.json\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn TakeoffCraft, open it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen takeoff\u003C/span\u003E, or drop it on the app. Your takeoff stays open: the file adds \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork: cut\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork: fill\u003C/strong\u003E as \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items#typed-quantities-no-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-hash=\"typed-quantities-no-drawing\"\u003Etyped quantities\u003C/a\u003E in cubic yards (CY) for a feet project, or cubic metres (m³) for a metre project. It is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERename them, set waste, rates and assemblies as you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the design changes, send again from CivilCraft and open the new file: the same two items get the new quantities. Their names, rates, waste and assemblies stay as you set them. If the project&#039;s unit changed, the status bar says so: check the rates. A file from another CivilCraft project adds its own two items.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe quantities are bank volumes, as CivilCraft measured them (grid method): no shrink or swell. Add those as waste, or with a formula such as \u003Ccode\u003EQ * 1.25\u003C/code\u003E for loose volume. The item editor shows where each one came from and when; the estimate&#039;s notes column says it too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe quantities file is plain JSON (\u003Ccode\u003E&quot;format&quot;: &quot;cadcraft-quantities&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), so a script or another program can write one too: an app name, a project, and items with a key, a name, a unit and a quantity.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft measures what is drawn on a sheet. Earthwork needs a ground surface, so it is shared with CivilCraft, which builds surfaces from points, grades them and works out cut and fill and haul. Simple excavation here For a footprint dug to one depth, an area item with a depth is enough: the volume is area × depth. The general contractor trade pack has a bulk excavation item (bank volume, loose volume and truck loads) and a utility trench item (excavation, bedding and backfill by length and depth). Spot elevations for CivilCraft Set the site sheet's scale. Make a Count item, for example \"Existing grade\", and click each spot elevation on the sheet. Select a mark and type its Elevation in the Selected mark panel: 102.5, 102'-6\" or 31.24 m. Negative and zero elevations are fine. Open the Export menu and choose Spot elevations. You get a points file (.csv) with Point, Northing, Easting, Elevation and Description, one row per marked point of the open sheet. Open that file in CivilCraft (see Import points). It builds the surface from the points (Build a surface); from there it compares surfaces and gives cut and fill (Volumes). Northing and easting are the sheet's own coordinates in feet, measured from the page's corner at its scale: distances and volumes between the points are true, but they are not your site's survey coordinates. Move them onto the site's coordinates in CivilCraft when you need to. Points from different sheets do not share coordinates, so the export takes the open sheet only. Elevations are saved with the takeoff and carried by copy and paste. Cut and fill from CivilCraft CivilCraft's earthwork comes back as two items you can price: In CivilCraft, grade your pads and corridors, then choose Send to TakeoffCraft under Earthwork (Earthwork volumes). It saves a quantities file (.quantities.json). In TakeoffCraft, open it with Open takeoff, or drop it on the app. Your takeoff stays open: the file adds Earthwork: cut and Earthwork: fill as typed quantities in cubic yards (CY) for a feet project, or cubic metres (m³) for a metre project. It is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. Rename them, set waste, rates and assemblies as you like. When the design changes, send again from CivilCraft and open the new file: the same two items get the new quantities. Their names, rates, waste and assemblies stay as you set them. If the project's unit changed, the status bar says so: check the rates. A file from another CivilCraft project adds its own two items. The quantities are bank volumes, as CivilCraft measured them (grid method): no shrink or swell. Add those as waste, or with a formula such as Q * 1.25 for loose volume. The item editor shows where each one came from and when; the estimate's notes column says it too. The quantities file is plain JSON (\"format\": \"cadcraft-quantities\"), so a script or another program can write one too: an app name, a project, and items with a key, a name, a unit and a quantity.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"bid-areas","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Bid areas","summary":"Tag marks by building, floor or phase, take off into one area at a time, and read quantities and costs for each area.","section":"tasks","order":85,"keywords":["add alternate","deduct alternate","base bid","alternates","bid area","area","building","floor","level","phase","zone","alternate","breakdown","split","typical floor","subtotal","by area"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-bid-area","text":"Make a bid area","level":2},{"id":"take-off-into-an-area","text":"Take off into an area","level":2},{"id":"move-marks-to-another-area","text":"Move marks to another area","level":2},{"id":"rename-reorder-or-delete","text":"Rename, reorder or delete","level":2},{"id":"read-the-quantities-by-area","text":"Read the quantities by area","level":2},{"id":"alternates-base-bid-plus-adds-and-deducts","text":"Alternates: base bid plus adds and deducts","level":2},{"id":"in-the-export","text":"In the export","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA bid area is a part of the job you price on its own: a building, a floor, a phase, a wing. Each mark can be in one bid area. The quantities and the estimate can then be read for each area, with a subtotal for each, while the job total stays the sum of everything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-bid-area\"\u003EMake a bid area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panel, open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid area\u003C/span\u003E list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003ENew bid area…\u003C/strong\u003E, type a name such as &quot;Building A&quot; or &quot;Level 2&quot;, and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EMake it\u003C/strong\u003E. The new area becomes the current one. The status bar says &quot;New marks go in (name). Marks in other bid areas are dimmed.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-off-into-an-area\"\u003ETake off into an area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a bid area is chosen in the list, every new mark goes in it: runs, areas, circles, counts, the marks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E adds, and what you paste. Marks in other bid areas, and marks in none, are dimmed on the sheet so you can see what belongs where.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003ENo bid area\u003C/strong\u003E in the list to take off without one; nothing is dimmed then.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETypical floors: take off Level 2, select its marks (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the sheet), copy them, choose Level 3 in the list and paste. The copies go in Level 3. When the floors are the same and you do not need them apart, a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/typical-groups\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/typical-groups\"\u003Etypical group\u003C/a\u003E counts one floor several times instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-marks-to-another-area\"\u003EMove marks to another area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect one mark or several (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected marks\u003C/strong\u003E), pick the area in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid area\u003C/span\u003E field. The status bar says &quot;3 marks moved to Level 2.&quot; It is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rename-reorder-or-delete\"\u003ERename, reorder or delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EManage bid areas…\u003C/strong\u003E in the list. Type over a name to rename it, use the arrows to change the order (the tables and the workbook follow it), or delete one with the bin. Deleting a bid area keeps its marks: they are then in no bid area. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes each change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-quantities-by-area\"\u003ERead the quantities by area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy area\u003C/span\u003E (it shows once the takeoff has a bid area). Each area lists the items it holds, with their quantity, unit and total, then a subtotal row. Marks in no bid area come last, as \u003Cstrong\u003ENo bid area\u003C/strong\u003E. Click an item row to highlight the item and make that area the current one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssemblies are worked out for each area from that area&#039;s own totals. A formula that rounds up, such as \u003Ccode\u003Eceil(L / 4)\u003C/code\u003E, can then give a little more in the areas together than for the whole job, as it would on a bid priced area by area.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"alternates-base-bid-plus-adds-and-deducts\"\u003EAlternates: base bid plus adds and deducts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA bid area can be an \u003Cstrong\u003Ealternate\u003C/strong\u003E: work the owner may add to the bid, or take out of it. In \u003Cstrong\u003EManage bid areas…\u003C/strong\u003E, each area has a list next to its name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoice\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase bid\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the bid, as usual.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd alternate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPriced on its own and \u003Cstrong\u003Eleft out of the base bid\u003C/strong\u003E: a carpet upgrade, an extra canopy.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct alternate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the base bid, and offered as a deduction: &quot;delete the canopy&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETake off an alternate&#039;s work into its bid area like any other area. Once the takeoff has an alternate:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe header of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table says \u003Cstrong\u003EBase bid\u003C/strong\u003E instead of Job total: the whole takeoff without the add alternates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy area\u003C/span\u003E ends with the base bid and one row per alternate: \u003Cstrong\u003E+$300.00\u003C/strong\u003E for an add, \u003Cstrong\u003E−$1,050.00\u003C/strong\u003E for a deduct.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn alternate prices only the items that have marks in it, with their assemblies, so an assembly part of another item does not creep in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the exports, the CSV ends with the base bid and the alternates, and the workbook gets an \u003Cstrong\u003EAlternates\u003C/strong\u003E worksheet: the base bid, each alternate&#039;s items and its change, and the base bid with every add alternate taken.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-export\"\u003EIn the export\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E adds a \u003Cstrong\u003EBy bid area\u003C/strong\u003E worksheet: each area&#039;s items and components with quantity, quantity with waste, material, labor and total, a subtotal per area (a \u003Ccode\u003ESUM\u003C/code\u003E formula) and \u003Cstrong\u003EAll bid areas\u003C/strong\u003E at the end. The \u003Cstrong\u003EMarks\u003C/strong\u003E worksheet gets a \u003Cstrong\u003EBid area\u003C/strong\u003E column. A takeoff without bid areas exports as before. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-columns\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-columns\"\u003EExport columns\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe bid areas are saved in the project file with the marks.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A bid area is a part of the job you price on its own: a building, a floor, a phase, a wing. Each mark can be in one bid area. The quantities and the estimate can then be read for each area, with a subtotal for each, while the job total stays the sum of everything. Make a bid area At the top of the Items panel, open the Bid area list. Choose New bid area…, type a name such as \"Building A\" or \"Level 2\", and choose Make it. The new area becomes the current one. The status bar says \"New marks go in (name). Marks in other bid areas are dimmed.\" Take off into an area While a bid area is chosen in the list, every new mark goes in it: runs, areas, circles, counts, the marks Auto count adds, and what you paste. Marks in other bid areas, and marks in none, are dimmed on the sheet so you can see what belongs where. Choose No bid area in the list to take off without one; nothing is dimmed then. Typical floors: take off Level 2, select its marks (Ctrl+A on the sheet), copy them, choose Level 3 in the list and paste. The copies go in Level 3. When the floors are the same and you do not need them apart, a typical group counts one floor several times instead. Move marks to another area Select one mark or several (Shift + click, or Ctrl+A). Under Selected mark (or Selected marks), pick the area in the Bid area field. The status bar says \"3 marks moved to Level 2.\" It is one undo step. Rename, reorder or delete Choose Manage bid areas… in the list. Type over a name to rename it, use the arrows to change the order (the tables and the workbook follow it), or delete one with the bin. Deleting a bid area keeps its marks: they are then in no bid area. Ctrl+Z undoes each change. Read the quantities by area In the Quantities table, choose By area (it shows once the takeoff has a bid area). Each area lists the items it holds, with their quantity, unit and total, then a subtotal row. Marks in no bid area come last, as No bid area. Click an item row to highlight the item and make that area the current one. Assemblies are worked out for each area from that area's own totals. A formula that rounds up, such as ceil(L / 4), can then give a little more in the areas together than for the whole job, as it would on a bid priced area by area. Alternates: base bid plus adds and deducts A bid area can be an alternate: work the owner may add to the bid, or take out of it. In Manage bid areas…, each area has a list next to its name: Choice What it does Base bid In the bid, as usual. Add alternate Priced on its own and left out of the base bid: a carpet upgrade, an extra canopy. Deduct alternate In the base bid, and offered as a deduction: \"delete the canopy\". Take off an alternate's work into its bid area like any other area. Once the takeoff has an alternate: The header of the Quantities table says Base bid instead of Job total: the whole takeoff without the add alternates. By area ends with the base bid and one row per alternate: +$300.00 for an add, −$1,050.00 for a deduct. An alternate prices only the items that have marks in it, with their assemblies, so an assembly part of another item does not creep in. In the exports, the CSV ends with the base bid and the alternates, and the workbook gets an Alternates worksheet: the base bid, each alternate's items and its change, and the base bid with every add alternate taken. In the export XLSX adds a By bid area worksheet: each area's items and components with quantity, quantity with waste, material, labor and total, a subtotal per area (a SUM formula) and All bid areas at the end. The Marks worksheet gets a Bid area column. A takeoff without bid areas exports as before. See Export columns. The bid areas are saved in the project file with the marks.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"typical-groups","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Typical groups","summary":"Take off a typical unit, room or floor once and count it as many times as it repeats, change the count later, and see which marks are typical.","section":"tasks","order":86,"keywords":["typical","typical group","repeat","repeat count","multiply","times","unit","hotel room","apartment","typical floor","x24","copies"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-typical-group","text":"Make a typical group","level":2},{"id":"change-it-later","text":"Change it later","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA building often repeats: 24 identical hotel rooms, 6 typical floors, 40 parking bays with the same striping. A \u003Cstrong\u003Etypical group\u003C/strong\u003E lets you take off one of them and count it as many times as it repeats, without drawing the copies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-typical-group\"\u003EMake a typical group\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETake off one unit: its walls, its floor, its outlets — any items.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect its marks with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool: click one, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click the others (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste#select-the-marks\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-hash=\"select-the-marks\"\u003ESelect the marks\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETypical\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew typical group…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name (\u003Ccode\u003EUnit A\u003C/code\u003E) and how many times it repeats (\u003Ccode\u003E24\u003C/code\u003E), then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake group\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery quantity of those marks now counts 24 times: a 10 ft wall run gives 240 ft, an 80 SF floor 1,920 SF, two outlets 48. Costs follow, and assembly formulas see the repeated totals (\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E counts each run 24 times too).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the sheet, a dashed box goes round the group&#039;s marks with its name and count, like \u003Cstrong\u003EUnit A ×24\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-it-later\"\u003EChange it later\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe groups are listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ETypical groups\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange the number after \u003Cstrong\u003E×\u003C/strong\u003E to change how many times it counts (1 to 10,000). Every quantity updates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERename it in its name box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe box icon selects its marks (and shows their sheet).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe bin deletes the group. Its marks stay, counted once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo add marks to a group or take them out, select them and pick the group (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot typical (×1)\u003C/span\u003E) under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETypical\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA group&#039;s marks can be on several sheets, and of any items. Each mark is in one group at most.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopies you paste are not in the group: a pasted copy of a typical unit would otherwise count 24 times again. Use a group for &quot;the same thing N times&quot;, and copy and paste for the copies you draw.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe marks are drawn once, so the 3D view and the marked PDF show one unit; the quantities, the exports and the per-sheet table count it ×N. The \u003Cstrong\u003EMarks\u003C/strong\u003E sheet of the Excel export says which marks are typical and how many times they count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith bid areas, a group&#039;s marks count ×N in their own bid area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A building often repeats: 24 identical hotel rooms, 6 typical floors, 40 parking bays with the same striping. A typical group lets you take off one of them and count it as many times as it repeats, without drawing the copies. Make a typical group Take off one unit: its walls, its floor, its outlets — any items. Select its marks with the Select tool: click one, then Shift + click the others (Select the marks). Under Selected mark, open Typical and choose New typical group…. Give it a name (Unit A) and how many times it repeats (24), then choose Make group. Every quantity of those marks now counts 24 times: a 10 ft wall run gives 240 ft, an 80 SF floor 1,920 SF, two outlets 48. Costs follow, and assembly formulas see the repeated totals (N counts each run 24 times too). On the sheet, a dashed box goes round the group's marks with its name and count, like Unit A ×24. Change it later The groups are listed under Typical groups in the right panel: Change the number after × to change how many times it counts (1 to 10,000). Every quantity updates. Rename it in its name box. The box icon selects its marks (and shows their sheet). The bin deletes the group. Its marks stay, counted once. To add marks to a group or take them out, select them and pick the group (or Not typical (×1)) under Typical. Good to know A group's marks can be on several sheets, and of any items. Each mark is in one group at most. Copies you paste are not in the group: a pasted copy of a typical unit would otherwise count 24 times again. Use a group for \"the same thing N times\", and copy and paste for the copies you draw. The marks are drawn once, so the 3D view and the marked PDF show one unit; the quantities, the exports and the per-sheet table count it ×N. The Marks sheet of the Excel export says which marks are typical and how many times they count. With bid areas, a group's marks count ×N in their own bid area.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"read-quantities","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Read quantities and the estimate","summary":"Use the Quantities table by item and by sheet, the job total, highlighting, and the Incomplete warning.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["quantities","totals","by item","by sheet","job total","estimate","summary","report","highlight","incomplete","costs"],"headings":[{"id":"by-item","text":"By item","level":2},{"id":"by-sheet","text":"By sheet","level":2},{"id":"by-area","text":"By area","level":2},{"id":"the-incomplete-warning","text":"The Incomplete warning","level":2},{"id":"highlight-an-item","text":"Highlight an item","level":2},{"id":"item-totals-in-the-side-panel","text":"Item totals in the side panel","level":2},{"id":"marks-on-pdfs-that-are-not-open","text":"Marks on PDFs that are not open","level":2},{"id":"more-room","text":"More room","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table at the bottom of the window is your live estimate. It changes the moment you draw, delete, rescale or edit a rate. Quantities are never stored; they are measured again from the marks every time, so they cannot drift out of date.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-item\"\u003EBy item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy item\u003C/span\u003E (the default). Each item has a row, and each of its assembly components a row under it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName, with the item&#039;s color. &quot;+(n) unscaled&quot; means some marks are on sheets with no scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measured total in the item&#039;s unit (components: the formula result).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF, m, SF, SY, m², EA, or the component&#039;s unit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWaste %\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyped here or in the item editor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith waste\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity × (1 + waste).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial/unit, Labor/unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERates; you can type them here.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial, Labor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith waste × rate, rounded to the cent.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial + labor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe last row, \u003Cstrong\u003EJob total\u003C/strong\u003E, adds the material, labor and total columns. The same total shows at the right of the table&#039;s header.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType into a waste or rate cell and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click away to apply it. A value TakeoffCraft cannot read is outlined in red and the status bar says why, for example &quot;Type an amount like 12.50.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an item&#039;s row to highlight its marks on every sheet and jump to a sheet that has them (the same as clicking it under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-sheet\"\u003EBy sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy sheet\u003C/span\u003E to see each item&#039;s quantity on each sheet, with the number of marks. Click a row to open that sheet with the item highlighted. Use it to check that every sheet was taken off, or to split quantities by floor or area of work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-area\"\u003EBy area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the takeoff has bid areas, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy area\u003C/span\u003E lists each area&#039;s items and a subtotal. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-areas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-areas\"\u003EBid areas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-incomplete-warning\"\u003EThe Incomplete warning\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen something is left out of the totals, the header shows a warning, for example:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;Incomplete: 1 formula error, 6 marks on sheets with no scale (not in the totals)&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFormula errors\u003C/strong\u003E: an assembly component&#039;s formula cannot be worked out. Its row says &quot;Formula error: …&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies#when-a-formula-is-wrong\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-hash=\"when-a-formula-is-wrong\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMarks on sheets with no scale\u003C/strong\u003E: give those sheets a scale. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale#why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-hash=\"why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same notes go into the exported spreadsheet, so an incomplete estimate never looks complete.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"highlight-an-item\"\u003EHighlight an item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E or in the table. Its marks stay at full strength on every sheet and the rest fade. The sheets list shows &quot;(n) highlighted&quot; on each sheet with its marks. Click the item again to stop highlighting.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"item-totals-in-the-side-panel\"\u003EItem totals in the side panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach item in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E list shows its quantity too. The item editor&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EMeasured\u003C/strong\u003E block shows the values its formulas use: length, runs and wall area; area, perimeter and number of areas; or count.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"marks-on-pdfs-that-are-not-open\"\u003EMarks on PDFs that are not open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA takeoff can include marks on PDFs you have not opened this time. They still count in every total; the sheets list names those PDFs. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-room\"\u003EMore room\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe arrow button at the right of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E header hides the table so the sheet gets more room. Choose it again to bring the table back.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Quantities table at the bottom of the window is your live estimate. It changes the moment you draw, delete, rescale or edit a rate. Quantities are never stored; they are measured again from the marks every time, so they cannot drift out of date. By item Choose By item (the default). Each item has a row, and each of its assembly components a row under it. Column Meaning Item Name, with the item's color. \"+(n) unscaled\" means some marks are on sheets with no scale. Quantity The measured total in the item's unit (components: the formula result). Unit LF, m, SF, SY, m², EA, or the component's unit. Waste % Typed here or in the item editor. With waste Quantity × (1 + waste). Material/unit, Labor/unit Rates; you can type them here. Material, Labor With waste × rate, rounded to the cent. Total Material + labor. The last row, Job total, adds the material, labor and total columns. The same total shows at the right of the table's header. Type into a waste or rate cell and press Enter or click away to apply it. A value TakeoffCraft cannot read is outlined in red and the status bar says why, for example \"Type an amount like 12.50.\" Click an item's row to highlight its marks on every sheet and jump to a sheet that has them (the same as clicking it under Items). By sheet Choose By sheet to see each item's quantity on each sheet, with the number of marks. Click a row to open that sheet with the item highlighted. Use it to check that every sheet was taken off, or to split quantities by floor or area of work. By area When the takeoff has bid areas, By area lists each area's items and a subtotal. See Bid areas. The Incomplete warning When something is left out of the totals, the header shows a warning, for example: \"Incomplete: 1 formula error, 6 marks on sheets with no scale (not in the totals)\" Formula errors: an assembly component's formula cannot be worked out. Its row says \"Formula error: …\". See Assemblies and formulas. Marks on sheets with no scale: give those sheets a scale. See Set the scale. The same notes go into the exported spreadsheet, so an incomplete estimate never looks complete. Highlight an item Click an item under Items or in the table. Its marks stay at full strength on every sheet and the rest fade. The sheets list shows \"(n) highlighted\" on each sheet with its marks. Click the item again to stop highlighting. Item totals in the side panel Each item in the Items list shows its quantity too. The item editor's Measured block shows the values its formulas use: length, runs and wall area; area, perimeter and number of areas; or count. Marks on PDFs that are not open A takeoff can include marks on PDFs you have not opened this time. They still count in every total; the sheets list names those PDFs. See Save and reopen a takeoff. More room The arrow button at the right of the Quantities header hides the table so the sheet gets more room. Choose it again to bring the table back.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"check-in-3d","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Check the takeoff in 3D","summary":"See the open sheet's walls, slabs and counts standing on the drawing, orbit round them, and click one to select its mark.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["png","image","picture","screenshot","stack","floors","levels","elevation","glb","gltf","export 3d","wall thickness","base height","opening","door head","3d","three d","check","view","walls","wall height","slab","depth","volume","orbit","model","picking","isometric","perspective"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-3d-view","text":"Open the 3D view","level":2},{"id":"what-you-see","text":"What you see","level":2},{"id":"look-around","text":"Look around","level":2},{"id":"walls-with-a-thickness-a-base-and-openings","text":"Walls with a thickness, a base and openings","level":2},{"id":"stack-the-floors","text":"Stack the floors","level":2},{"id":"save-the-3d-view-as-an-image","text":"Save the 3D view as an image","level":2},{"id":"save-the-3d-view-as-a-model","text":"Save the 3D view as a model","level":2},{"id":"select-a-mark-from-3d","text":"Select a mark from 3D","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D view is a check, not a second drawing: it stands the takeoff of the sheet on screen up on the sheet itself, so a missing wall, a doubled slab or a stray count shows at a glance.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore you start: the sheet needs a scale (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E). Heights and depths need real feet, so on a sheet with no scale \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E says so and stays on the flat sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-3d-view\"\u003EOpen the 3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShow the sheet you want to check.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E on the bar that shows the sheet&#039;s name and scale, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first time, the 3D view takes a moment to load. The sheet is drawn on the ground, and the takeoff stands on it in each item&#039;s color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo go back, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E again, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the sheet\u003C/span\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Choosing a drawing tool (Linear, Area, Count and so on) also goes back, since marks are drawn on the flat sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt shows the sheet on screen; change sheets in the sheets list, or with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and the 3D view follows. To see several floors at once, stack them (below).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-see\"\u003EWhat you see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMark\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn 3D\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear run, item with a height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA wall of that height along the run. Curved runs follow the curve.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear run, item with no height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line on the sheet. Give the item a height under \u003Cstrong\u003EItem\u003C/strong\u003E to stand it up.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea, item with a depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA slab of that depth, with its cut-outs open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea, item with no depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA thin 2&quot; plate, so it still shows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA small post on each counted point.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe height is a linear item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E field and the depth an area item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E field, under \u003Cstrong\u003EItem\u003C/strong\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E). Change it, and the 3D view updates at once. The note under the sheet name says what the sheet holds, for example &quot;This sheet in 3D: 2 walls, 1 slab, 3 counts&quot;, and how many runs or areas have no height or depth. Clicks pass through it to the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe numbers do not change in 3D: a wall&#039;s area is its exact length times its height, and a slab&#039;s volume is its net area (outline minus cut-outs, curves exact) times its depth. Curves are drawn as short straight pieces only on screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-around\"\u003ELook around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag, or drag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit round the takeoff.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + drag, or drag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in and out at the pointer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger swipe on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pans). A pinch zooms.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStandard views, framed on the takeoff. The 3D view opens in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerspective\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPerspective on or off (parallel when off).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom extents\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow or hide the drawing on the ground.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe view cube, top right\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a face, edge or corner to look from there; drag it to orbit; drag its compass ring to turn; the house button goes back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe corner shows a view cube, a small X, Y, Z axis icon, or nothing: choose in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, on the TakeoffCraft tab, under \u003Cstrong\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/settings\"\u003ETakeoffCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E). The flat sheet has no corner indicator.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne finger orbits; two fingers pinch to zoom and move to pan. A tap selects.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"walls-with-a-thickness-a-base-and-openings\"\u003EWalls with a thickness, a base and openings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA linear item with a height stands as a thin strip. To see it as a real wall, give the item its shape under \u003Cstrong\u003EIn 3D\u003C/strong\u003E in its settings:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWall thickness\u003C/strong\u003E (8&quot;, 0.2 m): the wall becomes a solid, centred on the line you drew, with mitred corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBase height\u003C/strong\u003E: the wall starts that far above the floor (a soffit, an upper cabinet run). Area items have it too: a ceiling at 9&#039;-0&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening head\u003C/strong\u003E: a deducted segment (a door, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear#deduct-a-part-of-a-run\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-hash=\"deduct-a-part-of-a-run\"\u003EDeduct a part of a run\u003C/a\u003E) keeps the wall above that height, so a door shows as a door. Without it, a deducted segment leaves a gap to the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese only shape the 3D view: the quantities (length, wall area, costs) stay as they were.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stack-the-floors\"\u003EStack the floors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the PDFs of every floor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the 3D view, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevels…\u003C/span\u003E and type each floor&#039;s elevation, such as \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E for the ground floor and \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;\u003C/code\u003E for the next. Blank leaves a sheet out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStack\u003C/span\u003E. Every sheet with a level shows its takeoff at its height; the sheet on screen lies under its own floor, the others are outlined. The note says how many floors are stacked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheets line up by their place on the page, so this works for floor plans plotted alike (the same sheet size and position). A sheet with a level but no scale is left out, and the note says so. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStack\u003C/span\u003E again for one sheet at a time. The levels are saved with the takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-3d-view-as-an-image\"\u003ESave the 3D view as an image\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPNG\u003C/span\u003E on the 3D bar for a picture of the view as it is on screen (\u003Ccode\u003E… 3D.png\u003C/code\u003E); \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPNG\u003C/span\u003E makes it twice the size (\u003Ccode\u003E… 3D@2x.png\u003C/code\u003E), for print.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-3d-view-as-a-model\"\u003ESave the 3D view as a model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGLB\u003C/span\u003E on the 3D bar. You get a \u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E file (glTF, binary) of what the view shows — the floor on screen, or every stacked floor — that other 3D programs open: one object per mark, named after its item, in the item&#039;s colour, in metres with Y up. The sheet itself and the outlines are not in it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-a-mark-from-3d\"\u003ESelect a mark from 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a wall, slab, plate or post. Its mark becomes the selected mark, exactly as if you had clicked it on the sheet: it shows under \u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E, its item becomes current, and the status bar gives its length and height, or its area, depth and cubic yards. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click adds marks to the selection or takes them out. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes the selected marks and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings them back; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E selects none. Back on the sheet, the same marks stay selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing follows the theme: in Model, the sheet on the ground goes dark like the flat view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The 3D view is a check, not a second drawing: it stands the takeoff of the sheet on screen up on the sheet itself, so a missing wall, a doubled slab or a stray count shows at a glance. Before you start: the sheet needs a scale (Set the scale). Heights and depths need real feet, so on a sheet with no scale 3D says so and stays on the flat sheet. Open the 3D view Show the sheet you want to check. Choose 3D on the bar that shows the sheet's name and scale, or press 3. The first time, the 3D view takes a moment to load. The sheet is drawn on the ground, and the takeoff stands on it in each item's color. To go back, choose 3D again, Back to the sheet, or press 3. Choosing a drawing tool (Linear, Area, Count and so on) also goes back, since marks are drawn on the flat sheet. It shows the sheet on screen; change sheets in the sheets list, or with Page Down and Page Up, and the 3D view follows. To see several floors at once, stack them (below). What you see Mark In 3D Linear run, item with a height A wall of that height along the run. Curved runs follow the curve. Linear run, item with no height A line on the sheet. Give the item a height under Item to stand it up. Area, item with a depth A slab of that depth, with its cut-outs open. Area, item with no depth A thin 2\" plate, so it still shows. Count A small post on each counted point. The height is a linear item's Height field and the depth an area item's Depth field, under Item (see Create takeoff items). Change it, and the 3D view updates at once. The note under the sheet name says what the sheet holds, for example \"This sheet in 3D: 2 walls, 1 slab, 3 counts\", and how many runs or areas have no height or depth. Clicks pass through it to the view. The numbers do not change in 3D: a wall's area is its exact length times its height, and a slab's volume is its net area (outline minus cut-outs, curves exact) times its depth. Curves are drawn as short straight pieces only on screen. Look around Action What it does Drag, or drag with the middle button Orbit round the takeoff. Shift + drag, or drag with the right button Pan. Mouse wheel Zoom in and out at the pointer. Two-finger swipe on a trackpad Orbit (Shift pans). A pinch zooms. Iso, Top, Front, Back, Left, Right, Bottom Standard views, framed on the takeoff. The 3D view opens in Iso. Perspective Perspective on or off (parallel when off). Zoom extents or F The whole sheet. Sheet Show or hide the drawing on the ground. The view cube, top right Click a face, edge or corner to look from there; drag it to orbit; drag its compass ring to turn; the house button goes back to Iso. The corner shows a view cube, a small X, Y, Z axis icon, or nothing: choose in Settings, on the TakeoffCraft tab, under Corner indicator (TakeoffCraft settings). The flat sheet has no corner indicator. One finger orbits; two fingers pinch to zoom and move to pan. A tap selects. Walls with a thickness, a base and openings A linear item with a height stands as a thin strip. To see it as a real wall, give the item its shape under In 3D in its settings: Wall thickness (8\", 0.2 m): the wall becomes a solid, centred on the line you drew, with mitred corners. Base height: the wall starts that far above the floor (a soffit, an upper cabinet run). Area items have it too: a ceiling at 9'-0\". Opening head: a deducted segment (a door, Deduct a part of a run) keeps the wall above that height, so a door shows as a door. Without it, a deducted segment leaves a gap to the top. These only shape the 3D view: the quantities (length, wall area, costs) stay as they were. Stack the floors Open the PDFs of every floor. In the 3D view, choose Levels… and type each floor's elevation, such as 0 for the ground floor and 12' for the next. Blank leaves a sheet out. Choose Stack. Every sheet with a level shows its takeoff at its height; the sheet on screen lies under its own floor, the others are outlined. The note says how many floors are stacked. Sheets line up by their place on the page, so this works for floor plans plotted alike (the same sheet size and position). A sheet with a level but no scale is left out, and the note says so. Choose Stack again for one sheet at a time. The levels are saved with the takeoff. Save the 3D view as an image Choose PNG on the 3D bar for a picture of the view as it is on screen (… 3D.png); Shift + click PNG makes it twice the size (… 3D@2x.png), for print. Save the 3D view as a model Choose GLB on the 3D bar. You get a .glb file (glTF, binary) of what the view shows — the floor on screen, or every stacked floor — that other 3D programs open: one object per mark, named after its item, in the item's colour, in metres with Y up. The sheet itself and the outlines are not in it. Select a mark from 3D Click a wall, slab, plate or post. Its mark becomes the selected mark, exactly as if you had clicked it on the sheet: it shows under Selected mark, its item becomes current, and the status bar gives its length and height, or its area, depth and cubic yards. Shift + click adds marks to the selection or takes them out. Delete deletes the selected marks and Ctrl+Z brings them back; Esc selects none. Back on the sheet, the same marks stay selected. The drawing follows the theme: in Model, the sheet on the ground goes dark like the flat view.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"markups","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Notes, clouds and highlights","summary":"Mark questions and changes on the sheets with notes, revision clouds and highlights, track each as open, answered or closed, and send them in the marked PDF.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["markup","markups","note","comment","question","rfi","cloud","revision cloud","highlight","status","open","answered","closed","review","markups list"],"headings":[{"id":"add-one","text":"Add one","level":2},{"id":"status-and-editing","text":"Status and editing","level":2},{"id":"the-list","text":"The list","level":2},{"id":"share-them","text":"Share them","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkups are for what you need to remember or ask while taking off: &quot;confirm the base type&quot;, a revision cloud round a changed wall, a highlighted note on the plan. They sit on the sheet with a status, are listed in one place, and print in the marked PDF. They are \u003Cstrong\u003Enever counted\u003C/strong\u003E: no quantity, no cost.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-one\"\u003EAdd one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/span\u003E on the bar above the sheet and pick:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENote\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick where it goes, type the note, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E. It shows as a round pin with its first line beside it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick one corner, then the opposite one, and type a note (or leave it empty). A scalloped revision cloud goes round the box.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHighlight\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick two corners: a see-through yellow box, with a note if you like.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after the first corner cancels. The tool stays on for the next one; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E goes back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"status-and-editing\"\u003EStatus and editing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a markup with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E to select it. The right panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003EMarkup\u003C/strong\u003E: its kind and sheet, its \u003Cstrong\u003EStatus\u003C/strong\u003E — Open, Answered or Closed — and its text, which you can change. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E) removes it; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA note&#039;s pin is orange while open, blue once answered and grey when closed; a closed cloud turns grey.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-list\"\u003EThe list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMarkups\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel lists every markup of the takeoff with its status, and says how many are still open. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen only\u003C/strong\u003E to see what is left to settle. Click a row to go to its sheet and select it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-them\"\u003EShare them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E draws them on the pages: highlights, clouds and pins, each with a box giving its status in capitals (OPEN, ANSWERED, CLOSED) and its text. A page with markups but no takeoff marks is included too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E adds a \u003Cstrong\u003EMarkups\u003C/strong\u003E worksheet: sheet, kind, status and text, one row each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThey are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E and in a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen#keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-hash=\"keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Markups are for what you need to remember or ask while taking off: \"confirm the base type\", a revision cloud round a changed wall, a highlighted note on the plan. They sit on the sheet with a status, are listed in one place, and print in the marked PDF. They are never counted: no quantity, no cost. Add one Open Markup on the bar above the sheet and pick: Markup How Note Click where it goes, type the note, choose Add. It shows as a round pin with its first line beside it. Cloud Click one corner, then the opposite one, and type a note (or leave it empty). A scalloped revision cloud goes round the box. Highlight Click two corners: a see-through yellow box, with a note if you like. Esc after the first corner cancels. The tool stays on for the next one; V goes back to Select. Status and editing Click a markup with Select to select it. The right panel shows Markup: its kind and sheet, its Status — Open, Answered or Closed — and its text, which you can change. Delete (or Delete) removes it; Ctrl+Z brings it back. A note's pin is orange while open, blue once answered and grey when closed; a closed cloud turns grey. The list Markups in the right panel lists every markup of the takeoff with its status, and says how many are still open. Tick Open only to see what is left to settle. Click a row to go to its sheet and select it. Share them Marked PDF draws them on the pages: highlights, clouds and pins, each with a box giving its status in capitals (OPEN, ANSWERED, CLOSED) and its text. A page with markups but no takeoff marks is included too. XLSX adds a Markups worksheet: sheet, kind, status and text, one row each. They are saved in the .takeoff.json and in a PDF with the takeoff inside.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"export-estimate","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Export the estimate and a marked PDF","summary":"Download the estimate as an XLSX workbook with live formulas or as CSV, and copies of your PDFs with the takeoff drawn in.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["proposal","bid summary","export","xlsx","spreadsheet","workbook","csv","marked pdf","print takeoff","report","share","send","legend"],"headings":[{"id":"the-estimate-as-a-spreadsheet-xlsx","text":"The estimate as a spreadsheet (XLSX)","level":2},{"id":"the-estimate-as-csv","text":"The estimate as CSV","level":2},{"id":"a-marked-pdf","text":"A marked PDF","level":2},{"id":"nothing-to-export","text":"Nothing to export?","level":2},{"id":"save-the-takeoff-too","text":"Save the takeoff too","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EExports are downloads. They are made on your computer from the takeoff and the open PDFs; nothing is sent anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-estimate-as-a-spreadsheet-xlsx\"\u003EThe estimate as a spreadsheet (XLSX)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu in the toolbar and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E. Your browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E(name).xlsx\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says &quot;Exported (name).xlsx (estimate with live formulas, by sheet, marks, scales).&quot; and adds a warning when the estimate is incomplete.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen it in your spreadsheet program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe workbook has four worksheets, and the reports below when they apply:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWorksheet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it holds\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEstimate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA row per item and per component: quantity, waste, quantity with waste, rates, material, labor, total, notes. The job total at the bottom.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBy sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach item&#039;s quantity on each sheet, with the number of marks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery mark: sheet, item, type, quantity, and detail (segment lengths, gross and cut-out areas, perimeter).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScales\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach sheet that has a scale or marks: its scale, how it was set (preset, or calibrated on which distance), and feet per PDF unit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoff summary\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach item&#039;s quantity, CSI number, marks and sheets, without prices.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery assembly part added up across the takeoff (the same part used by several items is one line), with waste and material cost, and the items that use it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuote request\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe material list to send a supplier: quantities with waste, an empty unit-price column, and extensions and a total that fill in as prices are typed.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBy division\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial, labor and total by CSI MasterFormat division, when items have a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSI number\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-summary\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-summary\"\u003Ebid summary\u003C/a\u003E, the Estimate worksheet also ends with its markups (labor burden, sales tax, overhead, profit, bond and the bid), as live formulas.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Estimate worksheet keeps \u003Cstrong\u003Elive formulas\u003C/strong\u003E: quantity with waste, the rounded material and labor amounts, each row&#039;s total and the job total. Spreadsheet programs recalculate them, so you can change a rate or waste in the spreadsheet and the totals follow. The numbers TakeoffCraft computed are stored in the cells as well, and match to the cent.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe notes column lists each component&#039;s formula, formula errors, marks on sheets with no scale, wall area and perimeter. When anything is left out, the job total row says &quot;Incomplete: see the notes above&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe workbook has been checked in LibreOffice Calc, which recalculated it to the same totals. It has not been opened in Excel yet. Column by column details are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-columns\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-columns\"\u003EExport columns\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-estimate-as-csv\"\u003EThe estimate as CSV\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E. You get \u003Ccode\u003E(name).csv\u003C/code\u003E: the same rows and columns as the Estimate worksheet, as plain values (no formulas), and the job total. Use it to paste into another estimating or accounting system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe CSV has only the estimate; for quantities by sheet and the mark list, use XLSX. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial list\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu saves the material list alone as CSV, for ordering.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-marked-pdf\"\u003EA marked PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the PDFs whose marks you want drawn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E. &quot;Writing the marked PDF…&quot; shows, then your browser downloads one file per marked PDF, named \u003Ccode\u003E(PDF name) (takeoff).pdf\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the copy:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eruns are drawn as lines in their item&#039;s color,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eareas are filled lightly with a solid outline, and cut-outs left open,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ecounts are dots,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eeach page with marks gets a small legend in its bottom-left corner: each item&#039;s color, name and quantity on that page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour original PDFs are not changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENotes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly PDFs that are open are written. Marks on PDFs that are not open stay in the totals but are not drawn; open those PDFs first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENames in the legend use a standard PDF font. Letters it cannot show print as &quot;?&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn encrypted PDF cannot be copied with marks: &quot;This PDF is password-protected or encrypted, so a marked copy cannot be written. The takeoff itself is fine; export the spreadsheet instead.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E in a tab loads the PDF writer. Offline, it is there only if the app bar shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith several marked PDFs, Chrome and Edge may ask once whether this site may download multiple files. Choose Allow, or you get only the first file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nothing-to-export\"\u003ENothing to export?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E need at least one item: &quot;Nothing to export yet: make an item and take it off.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E needs marks on an open PDF: &quot;No marks on the open PDFs yet.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-takeoff-too\"\u003ESave the takeoff too\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn export is a report. To keep working on the takeoff later, also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo add overhead, profit, tax and bond and send a proposal, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-summary\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-summary\"\u003EBid summary and proposal\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Exports are downloads. They are made on your computer from the takeoff and the open PDFs; nothing is sent anywhere. The estimate as a spreadsheet (XLSX) Open the Export menu in the toolbar and choose XLSX. Your browser downloads (name).xlsx. The status bar says \"Exported (name).xlsx (estimate with live formulas, by sheet, marks, scales).\" and adds a warning when the estimate is incomplete. Open it in your spreadsheet program. The workbook has four worksheets, and the reports below when they apply: Worksheet What it holds Estimate A row per item and per component: quantity, waste, quantity with waste, rates, material, labor, total, notes. The job total at the bottom. By sheet Each item's quantity on each sheet, with the number of marks. Marks Every mark: sheet, item, type, quantity, and detail (segment lengths, gross and cut-out areas, perimeter). Scales Each sheet that has a scale or marks: its scale, how it was set (preset, or calibrated on which distance), and feet per PDF unit. Takeoff summary Each item's quantity, CSI number, marks and sheets, without prices. Material list Every assembly part added up across the takeoff (the same part used by several items is one line), with waste and material cost, and the items that use it. Quote request The material list to send a supplier: quantities with waste, an empty unit-price column, and extensions and a total that fill in as prices are typed. By division Material, labor and total by CSI MasterFormat division, when items have a CSI number. With a bid summary, the Estimate worksheet also ends with its markups (labor burden, sales tax, overhead, profit, bond and the bid), as live formulas. The Estimate worksheet keeps live formulas: quantity with waste, the rounded material and labor amounts, each row's total and the job total. Spreadsheet programs recalculate them, so you can change a rate or waste in the spreadsheet and the totals follow. The numbers TakeoffCraft computed are stored in the cells as well, and match to the cent. The notes column lists each component's formula, formula errors, marks on sheets with no scale, wall area and perimeter. When anything is left out, the job total row says \"Incomplete: see the notes above\". The workbook has been checked in LibreOffice Calc, which recalculated it to the same totals. It has not been opened in Excel yet. Column by column details are in Export columns. The estimate as CSV Open the Export menu and choose CSV. You get (name).csv: the same rows and columns as the Estimate worksheet, as plain values (no formulas), and the job total. Use it to paste into another estimating or accounting system. The CSV has only the estimate; for quantities by sheet and the mark list, use XLSX. Material list in the same menu saves the material list alone as CSV, for ordering. A marked PDF Open the PDFs whose marks you want drawn. Open the Export menu and choose Marked PDF. \"Writing the marked PDF…\" shows, then your browser downloads one file per marked PDF, named (PDF name) (takeoff).pdf. In the copy: runs are drawn as lines in their item's color, areas are filled lightly with a solid outline, and cut-outs left open, counts are dots, each page with marks gets a small legend in its bottom-left corner: each item's color, name and quantity on that page. Your original PDFs are not changed. Notes: Only PDFs that are open are written. Marks on PDFs that are not open stay in the totals but are not drawn; open those PDFs first. Names in the legend use a standard PDF font. Letters it cannot show print as \"?\". An encrypted PDF cannot be copied with marks: \"This PDF is password-protected or encrypted, so a marked copy cannot be written. The takeoff itself is fine; export the spreadsheet instead.\" The first Marked PDF in a tab loads the PDF writer. Offline, it is there only if the app bar shows Offline ready; see Working offline. With several marked PDFs, Chrome and Edge may ask once whether this site may download multiple files. Choose Allow, or you get only the first file. Nothing to export? XLSX and CSV need at least one item: \"Nothing to export yet: make an item and take it off.\" Marked PDF needs marks on an open PDF: \"No marks on the open PDFs yet.\" Save the takeoff too An export is a report. To keep working on the takeoff later, also choose Save takeoff: see Save and reopen a takeoff. To add overhead, profit, tax and bond and send a proposal, see Bid summary and proposal.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"bid-summary","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Bid summary and proposal","summary":"Add labor burden, sales tax, overhead, profit and bond to the estimate, write inclusions and exclusions, and download a proposal PDF to send.","section":"tasks","order":101,"keywords":["bid","labor burden","burden","payroll","proposal","quote","overhead","profit","markup","margin","sales tax","bond","inclusions","exclusions","terms","signature","client","price"],"headings":[{"id":"the-markups","text":"The markups","level":2},{"id":"the-proposal","text":"The proposal","level":2},{"id":"in-the-exports","text":"In the exports","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe estimate prices what you took off. The \u003Cstrong\u003Ebid summary\u003C/strong\u003E turns it into a price: it adds sales tax, overhead, profit and bond, and makes a \u003Cstrong\u003Eproposal PDF\u003C/strong\u003E with your inclusions and exclusions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen it from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid summary &amp; proposal…\u003C/span\u003E. Everything in it is saved with the takeoff, and each change can be undone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-markups\"\u003EThe markups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType a percent in any of the five (leave the others at 0). They are worked out in this order, each on what is above it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWorked out as\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial, Labor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe estimate&#039;s totals (the base bid when the takeoff has \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-areas#alternates-base-bid-plus-adds-and-deducts\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-areas\" data-hash=\"alternates-base-bid-plus-adds-and-deducts\"\u003Ealternates\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabor burden\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBurden % × labor: payroll taxes, insurance and benefits on your crews&#039; wages.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECost\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial + labor + labor burden.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESales tax on material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETax % × material.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOverhead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOverhead % × (cost + tax).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfit % × (cost + tax + overhead).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBond\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBond % × everything above.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBid\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sum of the lines.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach line is rounded to the cent. The workbook shows the same lines under the Estimate worksheet&#039;s total, with live formulas. The summary updates as you type: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes a percent. Each alternate is shown with the same markups on its own cost, so the owner sees what taking it really changes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-proposal\"\u003EThe proposal\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFill in what the proposal should say:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject\u003C/strong\u003E (the takeoff&#039;s name when empty), \u003Cstrong\u003ETo\u003C/strong\u003E (the client) and \u003Cstrong\u003EFrom\u003C/strong\u003E (your company).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInclusions\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EExclusions\u003C/strong\u003E: one per line; a leading dash or bullet is fine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETerms\u003C/strong\u003E: free text, such as &quot;Valid for 30 days.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EList the items and quantities\u003C/strong\u003E: the scope, item by item, with quantities and no prices. Untick it for a lump-sum proposal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProposal PDF\u003C/span\u003E. The PDF (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;takeoff&gt; - proposal.pdf\u003C/code\u003E) has the date, the price block with each markup and the bid, the alternates, the scope, the inclusions and exclusions, the terms, and lines to sign and date. If some quantities could not be measured (a sheet without a scale), it says so under the price.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-exports\"\u003EIn the exports\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnce the takeoff has a bid summary, the CSV ends with it and the workbook gets a \u003Cstrong\u003EBid summary\u003C/strong\u003E sheet, with each line, its percent and its amount. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-estimate\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-estimate\"\u003EExport the estimate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The estimate prices what you took off. The bid summary turns it into a price: it adds sales tax, overhead, profit and bond, and makes a proposal PDF with your inclusions and exclusions. Open it from Export › Bid summary & proposal…. Everything in it is saved with the takeoff, and each change can be undone. The markups Type a percent in any of the five (leave the others at 0). They are worked out in this order, each on what is above it: Line Worked out as Material, Labor The estimate's totals (the base bid when the takeoff has alternates). Labor burden Burden % × labor: payroll taxes, insurance and benefits on your crews' wages. Cost Material + labor + labor burden. Sales tax on material Tax % × material. Overhead Overhead % × (cost + tax). Profit Profit % × (cost + tax + overhead). Bond Bond % × everything above. Bid The sum of the lines. Each line is rounded to the cent. The workbook shows the same lines under the Estimate worksheet's total, with live formulas. The summary updates as you type: Enter takes a percent. Each alternate is shown with the same markups on its own cost, so the owner sees what taking it really changes. The proposal Fill in what the proposal should say: Project (the takeoff's name when empty), To (the client) and From (your company). Inclusions and Exclusions: one per line; a leading dash or bullet is fine. Terms: free text, such as \"Valid for 30 days.\" List the items and quantities: the scope, item by item, with quantities and no prices. Untick it for a lump-sum proposal. Choose Proposal PDF. The PDF (\u003Ctakeoff\u003E - proposal.pdf) has the date, the price block with each markup and the bid, the alternates, the scope, the inclusions and exclusions, the terms, and lines to sign and date. If some quantities could not be measured (a sheet without a scale), it says so under the price. In the exports Once the takeoff has a bid summary, the CSV ends with it and the workbook gets a Bid summary sheet, with each line, its percent and its amount. See Export the estimate.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"bid-leveling","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Bid packages and bid leveling","summary":"Split the estimate into packages by trade, send each package's scope to subcontractors, compare the bids that come back on the same scope, and use the chosen bid as the package's price.","section":"tasks","order":102,"keywords":["bid leveling","leveling","bid package","subcontractor","sub bid","scope","plug","trade","csi division","compare bids","low bid","bid tab","bidder","award","chosen bid","use a bid","subcontract price"],"headings":[{"id":"packages","text":"Packages","level":2},{"id":"enter-the-bids","text":"Enter the bids","level":2},{"id":"how-the-bids-are-leveled","text":"How the bids are leveled","level":2},{"id":"use-a-bid-as-the-package-s-price","text":"Use a bid as the package's price","level":2},{"id":"bids-that-come-back-by-email","text":"Bids that come back by email","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA general contractor sends each trade&#039;s scope to subcontractors and compares what comes back. TakeoffCraft does both from the takeoff: open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid packages and leveling…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"packages\"\u003EPackages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is one package for each CSI division your items use (the first two digits of an item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSI number\u003C/span\u003E, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E). Items without a number make a package of their own. Each package shows its items, how many sheets their marks are on, and our own cost of it (material and labor before markups, from the base bid).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScope CSV\u003C/span\u003E to download what to send a subcontractor: each item with its CSI number, quantity, unit and the sheets it is on. It has no prices. For material suppliers, the workbook&#039;s quote request has the parts list (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/export-estimate\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/export-estimate\"\u003EExport the estimate\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"enter-the-bids\"\u003EEnter the bids\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the empty row under the package, type the bidder&#039;s name and the amount of the bid, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd the bid\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a bid leaves part of the scope out, open its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft out\u003C/span\u003E list and tick those items.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo correct a name or an amount, type over it in the table; the × button removes a bid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change is one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back, and the bids are saved with the takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-bids-are-leveled\"\u003EHow the bids are leveled\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBids rarely cover the same scope. Leveling fills each left-out item with a \u003Cstrong\u003Eplug\u003C/strong\u003E at our own cost of it, so every bid is compared on the whole package:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeveled = the bid + the plugs\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe lowest leveled bid is marked \u003Cstrong\u003ELow\u003C/strong\u003E; on a tie, the one entered first. \u003Cstrong\u003EAgainst our cost\u003C/strong\u003E is the leveled bid minus our own cost of the package: negative means under it. A bid that looks lowest can lose once its missing scope is counted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe workbook gets a \u003Cstrong\u003EBid leveling\u003C/strong\u003E sheet with every package that has bids: our estimate, each bid, its plugs, the leveled total and the difference, as live formulas, and what each bid left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-a-bid-as-the-package-s-price\"\u003EUse a bid as the package&#039;s price\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse\u003C/span\u003E on a bid and it becomes the price of the whole package in your estimate: the package&#039;s own pricing (the items, their second conditions and assembly parts) drops out of the job total, the items stay as quantities with your own rates for reference, and one line, \u003Cstrong\u003EChosen bid\u003C/strong\u003E, carries the bid&#039;s amount. Items the bid leaves out stay at your own cost, which is what leveling already counted as the plug. Choose another bid, or choose the same one again to take it back; each is one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the bid summary the sub bids are one line inside the cost: overhead, profit and bond are added on top of them like any other cost; sales tax (on material only) and labor burden (on labor) are not. The workbook&#039;s Estimate sheet carries the chosen bid as its own row and the markups block adds it, with live formulas. The material list leaves out an awarded package&#039;s material, because the subcontractor supplies it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA package inside an alternate&#039;s bid area is not repriced: alternates and bid-area subtotals still use your own prices.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bids-that-come-back-by-email\"\u003EBids that come back by email\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not have to type the bids in. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend for bids…\u003C/span\u003E under a package emails each subcontractor a link to a page where they type a price for every line; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGet prices\u003C/span\u003E pulls what they sent into this table. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\"\u003ESend a package for bids\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETyping a bid in by hand still works, and a hand-typed bid and one from the site sit side by side. Bids that came back from the site are flagged \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb, late\u003C/strong\u003E when they arrived after the due time); their price changes only when the bidder sends a new version, so your own edits to it stay until then.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"A general contractor sends each trade's scope to subcontractors and compares what comes back. TakeoffCraft does both from the takeoff: open Export › Bid packages and leveling…. Packages There is one package for each CSI division your items use (the first two digits of an item's CSI number, see Create takeoff items). Items without a number make a package of their own. Each package shows its items, how many sheets their marks are on, and our own cost of it (material and labor before markups, from the base bid). Choose Scope CSV to download what to send a subcontractor: each item with its CSI number, quantity, unit and the sheets it is on. It has no prices. For material suppliers, the workbook's quote request has the parts list (see Export the estimate). Enter the bids In the empty row under the package, type the bidder's name and the amount of the bid, then choose Add the bid. If a bid leaves part of the scope out, open its Left out list and tick those items. To correct a name or an amount, type over it in the table; the × button removes a bid. Each change is one step that Ctrl+Z takes back, and the bids are saved with the takeoff. How the bids are leveled Bids rarely cover the same scope. Leveling fills each left-out item with a plug at our own cost of it, so every bid is compared on the whole package: Leveled = the bid + the plugs The lowest leveled bid is marked Low; on a tie, the one entered first. Against our cost is the leveled bid minus our own cost of the package: negative means under it. A bid that looks lowest can lose once its missing scope is counted. The workbook gets a Bid leveling sheet with every package that has bids: our estimate, each bid, its plugs, the leveled total and the difference, as live formulas, and what each bid left out. Use a bid as the package's price Choose Use on a bid and it becomes the price of the whole package in your estimate: the package's own pricing (the items, their second conditions and assembly parts) drops out of the job total, the items stay as quantities with your own rates for reference, and one line, Chosen bid, carries the bid's amount. Items the bid leaves out stay at your own cost, which is what leveling already counted as the plug. Choose another bid, or choose the same one again to take it back; each is one step that Ctrl+Z undoes. In the bid summary the sub bids are one line inside the cost: overhead, profit and bond are added on top of them like any other cost; sales tax (on material only) and labor burden (on labor) are not. The workbook's Estimate sheet carries the chosen bid as its own row and the markups block adds it, with live formulas. The material list leaves out an awarded package's material, because the subcontractor supplies it. A package inside an alternate's bid area is not repriced: alternates and bid-area subtotals still use your own prices. Bids that come back by email You do not have to type the bids in. Send for bids… under a package emails each subcontractor a link to a page where they type a price for every line; Get prices pulls what they sent into this table. See Send a package for bids. Typing a bid in by hand still works, and a hand-typed bid and one from the site sit side by side. Bids that came back from the site are flagged web (or web, late when they arrived after the due time); their price changes only when the bidder sends a new version, so your own edits to it stay until then.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"bid-invitations","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Send a package for bids","summary":"Email a bid package to subcontractors, let them type prices on a page that needs no account, keep the prices sealed until the opening time, and pull them into the leveling sheet.","section":"tasks","order":103,"keywords":["send for bids","invitation","bid invitation","bidder","subcontractor","invite","email","price request","sealed bids","bid opening","bid day","late bid","due date","reminder","subcontractor records","insurance","qualification","csv","connect","sheets pdf"],"headings":[{"id":"before-you-send","text":"Before you send","level":2},{"id":"send-a-package","text":"Send a package","level":2},{"id":"what-the-bidder-sees","text":"What the bidder sees","level":2},{"id":"sealed-bids-and-the-opening","text":"Sealed bids and the opening","level":2},{"id":"get-the-prices","text":"Get the prices","level":2},{"id":"on-the-website","text":"On the website","level":2},{"id":"who-sees-what-and-how-long","text":"Who sees what, and how long","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBid packages and leveling…\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend for bids…\u003C/span\u003E sends one package to the people you choose. The website emails each of them a link; they open a page, see the scope and type a price for every line. You see the prices in the leveling sheet, ready to compare (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-leveling\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-leveling\"\u003EBid packages and bid leveling\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESending is \u003Cstrong\u003Enot open to everyone yet.\u003C/strong\u003E While the feature is being tested it works for accounts the CadCraft team has enabled; if yours is not, the dialog says so. The bidder&#039;s page works for every invitation that was sent.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"before-you-send\"\u003EBefore you send\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYou need a CadCraft account with a verified email address. TakeoffCraft in the browser asks you to \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect\u003C/strong\u003E: a tab opens on getcadcraft.com, you press Connect, and that tab gets a sign-in that works for 30 days, for bid packages only, until you close the TakeoffCraft tab. You can remove it any time on your security page. A Studio window cannot send yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive each item a CSI number: a package is the items of one CSI division (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\"\u003ECreate takeoff items\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScale the sheets the package&#039;s marks are on, so the quantities the bidders see are measured.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-package\"\u003ESend a package\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend for bids…\u003C/span\u003E under the package.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in the title, project and an optional message, and the \u003Cstrong\u003Edue time\u003C/strong\u003E in your own time zone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave \u003Cstrong\u003EKeep prices sealed\u003C/strong\u003E ticked to hide every price until the \u003Cstrong\u003Eopening time\u003C/strong\u003E (not before the due time), or untick it to see prices as they arrive.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave the sheets box ticked to send a PDF of the sheets that hold the package&#039;s marks, with the marks drawn in. Prices of yours are never in what is sent: only item names, CSI numbers, quantities, units and sheet names.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the people to invite, one per line, as \u003Ccode\u003EName &lt;name@company.com&gt;\u003C/code\u003E or just the address, or tick people from your subcontractor records. Up to 60 at a time, 100 per package.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend invitations\u003C/span\u003E. Each person gets one plain email with your name and email address, the due time and the link; replies go to you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog tells you how many were sent and names anyone skipped: already invited to this package, asked not to get bid invitations, not a valid address, or over a sending limit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-bidder-sees\"\u003EWhat the bidder sees\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA page at a long private link, with no sign-in. It shows who invited them, the due time, whether prices are sealed, your message and the sheets PDF, then every line with its quantity. For each line they type a price or tick \u003Cstrong\u003ENot included\u003C/strong\u003E, and may add a note; they add notes for the whole package and up to 3 files (a quote, a scope letter, a certificate). They can save again and change their prices until the opening, or \u003Cstrong\u003Edecline\u003C/strong\u003E, with a box to ask that CadCraft not send them bid invitations again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sealed-bids-and-the-opening\"\u003ESealed bids and the opening\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore the opening time \u003Cstrong\u003Enobody\u003C/strong\u003E sees a price, a note or a file: not you, not in TakeoffCraft, not on your Bid packages page. You see who was invited and whether they answered. At the opening time every bid shows at once. A bid saved after the due time is accepted and marked \u003Cstrong\u003Elate\u003C/strong\u003E; the leveling still lists it, and lateness is a mark, not a ruling. After the opening a bidder cannot change a price. With no sealing, a bid is final once it is due.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-the-prices\"\u003EGet the prices\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGet prices\u003C/span\u003E asks the website and adds each answer to the package as a bid, flagged \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb\u003C/strong\u003E. TakeoffCraft also asks quietly each time you open the dialog while it is connected. A bid whose bidder saved a new version is updated in place; a bid you edited stays as you left it until they send a new version. Each pull is one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes. Left-out lines come across as the scope the bid leaves out, so leveling fills them at your own cost.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-the-website\"\u003EOn the website\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour account has two more pages once you can send: \u003Cstrong\u003EBid packages\u003C/strong\u003E lists what you sent, who answered, the late bids and the files, lets you send a reminder (a fresh link; the old one stops working), close, reopen or cancel a package; \u003Cstrong\u003ESubcontractors\u003C/strong\u003E keeps a record for each company: contacts, trades, qualification answers (years in business, bonding capacity, EMR, safety record, union or open shop, references), license and insurance expiry dates, flagged once past or within 30 days, and their past bids. Import and export the records as CSV: one row per contact, with the columns the export shows; a company you already have is updated, never repeated.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-sees-what-and-how-long\"\u003EWho sees what, and how long\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA bidder&#039;s prices, notes and files go to you and to the CadCraft team, who run the page. They are kept for 24 months after the due date, the files for 180 days; then they are deleted. The bidder&#039;s email address is the one you entered; it is used only to send the invitation and any reminder for it. One address gets at most 3 invitations a day from everyone together, and you can send up to 200 a day.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAddenda, award letters, a signed-off scope sheet per bidder and automatic reminders before an insurance date runs out are not built. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Export › Bid packages and leveling… › Send for bids… sends one package to the people you choose. The website emails each of them a link; they open a page, see the scope and type a price for every line. You see the prices in the leveling sheet, ready to compare (Bid packages and bid leveling). Sending is not open to everyone yet. While the feature is being tested it works for accounts the CadCraft team has enabled; if yours is not, the dialog says so. The bidder's page works for every invitation that was sent. Before you send You need a CadCraft account with a verified email address. TakeoffCraft in the browser asks you to Connect: a tab opens on getcadcraft.com, you press Connect, and that tab gets a sign-in that works for 30 days, for bid packages only, until you close the TakeoffCraft tab. You can remove it any time on your security page. A Studio window cannot send yet. Give each item a CSI number: a package is the items of one CSI division (Create takeoff items). Scale the sheets the package's marks are on, so the quantities the bidders see are measured. Send a package Choose Send for bids… under the package. Fill in the title, project and an optional message, and the due time in your own time zone. Leave Keep prices sealed ticked to hide every price until the opening time (not before the due time), or untick it to see prices as they arrive. Leave the sheets box ticked to send a PDF of the sheets that hold the package's marks, with the marks drawn in. Prices of yours are never in what is sent: only item names, CSI numbers, quantities, units and sheet names. Type the people to invite, one per line, as Name \u003Cname@company.com\u003E or just the address, or tick people from your subcontractor records. Up to 60 at a time, 100 per package. Choose Send invitations. Each person gets one plain email with your name and email address, the due time and the link; replies go to you. The dialog tells you how many were sent and names anyone skipped: already invited to this package, asked not to get bid invitations, not a valid address, or over a sending limit. What the bidder sees A page at a long private link, with no sign-in. It shows who invited them, the due time, whether prices are sealed, your message and the sheets PDF, then every line with its quantity. For each line they type a price or tick Not included, and may add a note; they add notes for the whole package and up to 3 files (a quote, a scope letter, a certificate). They can save again and change their prices until the opening, or decline, with a box to ask that CadCraft not send them bid invitations again. Sealed bids and the opening Before the opening time nobody sees a price, a note or a file: not you, not in TakeoffCraft, not on your Bid packages page. You see who was invited and whether they answered. At the opening time every bid shows at once. A bid saved after the due time is accepted and marked late; the leveling still lists it, and lateness is a mark, not a ruling. After the opening a bidder cannot change a price. With no sealing, a bid is final once it is due. Get the prices Get prices asks the website and adds each answer to the package as a bid, flagged web. TakeoffCraft also asks quietly each time you open the dialog while it is connected. A bid whose bidder saved a new version is updated in place; a bid you edited stays as you left it until they send a new version. Each pull is one step that Ctrl+Z undoes. Left-out lines come across as the scope the bid leaves out, so leveling fills them at your own cost. On the website Your account has two more pages once you can send: Bid packages lists what you sent, who answered, the late bids and the files, lets you send a reminder (a fresh link; the old one stops working), close, reopen or cancel a package; Subcontractors keeps a record for each company: contacts, trades, qualification answers (years in business, bonding capacity, EMR, safety record, union or open shop, references), license and insurance expiry dates, flagged once past or within 30 days, and their past bids. Import and export the records as CSV: one row per contact, with the columns the export shows; a company you already have is updated, never repeated. Who sees what, and how long A bidder's prices, notes and files go to you and to the CadCraft team, who run the page. They are kept for 24 months after the due date, the files for 180 days; then they are deleted. The bidder's email address is the one you entered; it is used only to send the invitation and any reminder for it. One address gets at most 3 invitations a day from everyone together, and you can send up to 200 a day. Not built yet Addenda, award letters, a signed-off scope sheet per bidder and automatic reminders before an insurance date runs out are not built. See Not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"save-and-reopen","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Save and reopen a takeoff","summary":"Save the takeoff as a .takeoff.json project, reopen it with the same PDFs, and what happens when a PDF is missing or has changed.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["takeoff inside pdf","embed","attachment","save","open","reopen","project","takeoff.json","resume","continue","revised pdf","changed pdf","missing pdf","backup","autosave","recover","crash"],"headings":[{"id":"save","text":"Save","level":2},{"id":"keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf","text":"Keep the takeoff inside the PDF","level":2},{"id":"if-the-tab-or-the-browser-stops","text":"If the tab or the browser stops","level":2},{"id":"reopen","text":"Reopen","level":2},{"id":"when-a-pdf-is-missing","text":"When a PDF is missing","level":2},{"id":"when-a-pdf-has-changed","text":"When a PDF has changed","level":2},{"id":"a-damaged-or-foreign-file","text":"A damaged or foreign file","level":2},{"id":"undo-history","text":"Undo history","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA takeoff is saved as a small project file, \u003Ccode\u003E(name).takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E. It holds your items, assemblies, rates, every sheet&#039;s scale and every mark. It does \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E hold the PDFs; it finds them again by their contents.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save\"\u003ESave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Your browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E(name).takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says &quot;Saved (name).takeoff.json. It reopens on top of the same PDFs (they are matched by their contents).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the file next to the job&#039;s PDFs, so the two stay together.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach save downloads a new file. Your browser may name the second one \u003Ccode\u003E(name) (1).takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E; keep the newest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe name comes from the first PDF you opened in this takeoff.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESave takeoff is still the only way to keep a takeoff as a file. While you work, TakeoffCraft also keeps a recovery copy in this browser (see below), and cloud saving is not available yet. If you try to close the tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks whether to leave.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf\"\u003EKeep the takeoff inside the PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/span\u003E downloads a copy of each open PDF, named like \u003Ccode\u003EPlans (with takeoff).pdf\u003C/code\u003E, that carries the whole takeoff as an attachment: items, marks, scales, bid areas, everything the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E holds. Its pages are untouched, so it looks and prints like the original, and other PDF readers list the takeoff under their attachments.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen that PDF in TakeoffCraft (any way you open PDFs) and it asks \u003Cstrong\u003EThis PDF has a takeoff inside\u003C/strong\u003E: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the takeoff\u003C/span\u003E to get it back, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJust the PDF\u003C/span\u003E to only open the drawing. The copy stands for the PDF it was made from, so a \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E saved from either opens on both.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith several PDFs, each copy carries the whole takeoff; open them together. Saving again from a copy replaces the takeoff inside it, it does not add a second one. The \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E is still the file to keep: it is small, and it is what autosave and the desktop app write.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-the-tab-or-the-browser-stops\"\u003EIf the tab or the browser stops\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA couple of seconds after each change, TakeoffCraft keeps a copy of the takeoff in this browser&#039;s own storage, together with its PDFs when they add up to 64 MB or less. Nothing is uploaded. When you save the takeoff, start a new one or open another project, the copy goes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the tab crashes, the browser or computer stops, or you leave with unsaved changes, the next time you open TakeoffCraft it asks \u003Cstrong\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/strong\u003E and lists the takeoff by name. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003ERecover\u003C/strong\u003E to get it back: the items, marks and scales return, and its PDFs open again if they were kept. The status bar says &quot;Recovered (name). It is not saved to a file yet: Save takeoff keeps it.&quot; When the PDFs were too large to keep, open them again; the marks appear on their sheets. More: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover your work\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reopen\"\u003EReopen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen TakeoffCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file and its PDFs on the page together. Or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E for the PDFs and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen takeoff\u003C/span\u003E for the project, in either order. The status bar says &quot;Opened (name): 12 items, 340 marks.&quot; Every sheet gets its scale back and the marks appear on their sheets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe order of the PDFs does not matter, and neither do their file names or folders. TakeoffCraft recognizes each PDF by a fingerprint of its contents.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you open a project while the current takeoff has unsaved changes, TakeoffCraft asks first: \u003Cstrong\u003ECancel\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003ESave takeoff first\u003C/strong\u003E. Opening a project replaces the current items and marks; PDFs that are already open stay open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-pdf-is-missing\"\u003EWhen a PDF is missing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you open the project without all its PDFs, the status bar adds &quot;Open its PDFs to see the marks (listed under Sheets).&quot; Under the sheets list, a note says:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;This takeoff also covers PDFs that are not open. Their quantities are counted; open them to see and edit their marks:&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003Efollowed by the file names and sizes. Their marks still count in every total and export, with the scales they were measured at. Open those PDFs to see and edit the marks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-pdf-has-changed\"\u003EWhen a PDF has changed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA PDF that was revised, or just saved again by another program, has different contents, so it is a \u003Cstrong\u003Edifferent file\u003C/strong\u003E to TakeoffCraft, even with the same name. In the note under the sheets list, such a file says &quot;a file with this name is open, but its contents differ&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe marks stay attached to the old version. They keep counting, at their old positions and scales.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe revised PDF opens as new sheets with no scale and no marks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo move the takeoff onto the revision, open both PDFs and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E on the new sheet: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/compare-revisions\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/compare-revisions\"\u003ECompare a revised sheet and move the takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-damaged-or-foreign-file\"\u003EA damaged or foreign file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft checks every project file before using it. Messages you may see:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;(file) is not a TakeoffCraft project (it is not valid JSON).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This is not a TakeoffCraft project file.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This project was saved by a newer TakeoffCraft. Open it there.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;3 marks were damaged and left out.&quot; (the rest opens)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-history\"\u003EUndo history\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUndo history starts fresh when you open a project. Saving does not clear it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A takeoff is saved as a small project file, (name).takeoff.json. It holds your items, assemblies, rates, every sheet's scale and every mark. It does not hold the PDFs; it finds them again by their contents. Save Choose Save takeoff in the File group, or press Ctrl+S. Your browser downloads (name).takeoff.json. The status bar says \"Saved (name).takeoff.json. It reopens on top of the same PDFs (they are matched by their contents).\" Move the file next to the job's PDFs, so the two stay together. Each save downloads a new file. Your browser may name the second one (name) (1).takeoff.json; keep the newest. The name comes from the first PDF you opened in this takeoff. Save takeoff is still the only way to keep a takeoff as a file. While you work, TakeoffCraft also keeps a recovery copy in this browser (see below), and cloud saving is not available yet. If you try to close the tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks whether to leave. Keep the takeoff inside the PDF Export › PDF with the takeoff inside downloads a copy of each open PDF, named like Plans (with takeoff).pdf, that carries the whole takeoff as an attachment: items, marks, scales, bid areas, everything the .takeoff.json holds. Its pages are untouched, so it looks and prints like the original, and other PDF readers list the takeoff under their attachments. Open that PDF in TakeoffCraft (any way you open PDFs) and it asks This PDF has a takeoff inside: choose Open the takeoff to get it back, or Just the PDF to only open the drawing. The copy stands for the PDF it was made from, so a .takeoff.json saved from either opens on both. With several PDFs, each copy carries the whole takeoff; open them together. Saving again from a copy replaces the takeoff inside it, it does not add a second one. The .takeoff.json is still the file to keep: it is small, and it is what autosave and the desktop app write. If the tab or the browser stops A couple of seconds after each change, TakeoffCraft keeps a copy of the takeoff in this browser's own storage, together with its PDFs when they add up to 64 MB or less. Nothing is uploaded. When you save the takeoff, start a new one or open another project, the copy goes. If the tab crashes, the browser or computer stops, or you leave with unsaved changes, the next time you open TakeoffCraft it asks Recover your work? and lists the takeoff by name. Choose Recover to get it back: the items, marks and scales return, and its PDFs open again if they were kept. The status bar says \"Recovered (name). It is not saved to a file yet: Save takeoff keeps it.\" When the PDFs were too large to keep, open them again; the marks appear on their sheets. More: Recover your work. Reopen Open TakeoffCraft. Drop the .takeoff.json file and its PDFs on the page together. Or choose Open plans for the PDFs and Open takeoff for the project, in either order. The status bar says \"Opened (name): 12 items, 340 marks.\" Every sheet gets its scale back and the marks appear on their sheets. The order of the PDFs does not matter, and neither do their file names or folders. TakeoffCraft recognizes each PDF by a fingerprint of its contents. If you open a project while the current takeoff has unsaved changes, TakeoffCraft asks first: Cancel, Discard changes, or Save takeoff first. Opening a project replaces the current items and marks; PDFs that are already open stay open. When a PDF is missing If you open the project without all its PDFs, the status bar adds \"Open its PDFs to see the marks (listed under Sheets).\" Under the sheets list, a note says: \"This takeoff also covers PDFs that are not open. Their quantities are counted; open them to see and edit their marks:\" followed by the file names and sizes. Their marks still count in every total and export, with the scales they were measured at. Open those PDFs to see and edit the marks. When a PDF has changed A PDF that was revised, or just saved again by another program, has different contents, so it is a different file to TakeoffCraft, even with the same name. In the note under the sheets list, such a file says \"a file with this name is open, but its contents differ\". The marks stay attached to the old version. They keep counting, at their old positions and scales. The revised PDF opens as new sheets with no scale and no marks. To move the takeoff onto the revision, open both PDFs and use Compare on the new sheet: Compare a revised sheet and move the takeoff. A damaged or foreign file TakeoffCraft checks every project file before using it. Messages you may see: \"(file) is not a TakeoffCraft project (it is not valid JSON).\" \"This is not a TakeoffCraft project file.\" \"This project was saved by a newer TakeoffCraft. Open it there.\" \"3 marks were damaged and left out.\" (the rest opens) Undo history Undo history starts fresh when you open a project. Saving does not clear it.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"compare-revisions","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Compare a revised sheet and move the takeoff","summary":"Lay the old sheet over its revision, line them up, see what changed, and move the takeoff onto the new sheet with the marks to check flagged.","section":"tasks","order":115,"keywords":["revision","revised","compare","overlay","old","new","changes","addendum","bulletin","reissue","move takeoff","transfer","carry over","align","flagged","check"],"headings":[{"id":"compare","text":"Compare","level":2},{"id":"line-them-up","text":"Line them up","level":2},{"id":"find-what-changed","text":"Find what changed","level":2},{"id":"move-the-takeoff","text":"Move the takeoff","level":2},{"id":"check-the-flagged-marks","text":"Check the flagged marks","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhen a sheet is reissued, you do not have to take it off again. Open the old PDF and the new one, compare the two sheets, and move the takeoff across. TakeoffCraft flags every mark where the drawing changed, so you check those and keep the rest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"compare\"\u003ECompare\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen both PDFs: the old set and the revised one (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E; they can be in one file or several).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShow the \u003Cstrong\u003Enew\u003C/strong\u003E sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar under the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the old sheet in the list. TakeoffCraft suggests the sheet with the same number in the other PDF, or the same page. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003ECompare\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe old drawing now lies over the new one in red. Where the two agree, the red lines sit on the black ones; where they differ, you see red lines alone (removed) and black lines alone (added). A bar at the bottom of the sheet holds the steps below; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E ends comparing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"line-them-up\"\u003ELine them up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReissued sheets are often plotted a little off: moved on the page, or at a slightly different size. If the red drawing does not sit on the black one, choose \u003Cstrong\u003EAlign by two points\u003C/strong\u003E and click four times:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea point on the old (red) drawing, such as a column center or a building corner,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe same point on the new (black) drawing,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea second point on the red drawing, far from the first,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe same point on the black drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe red drawing moves, turns and scales onto the black one. The status bar says how much bigger the new drawing is (&quot;size × 1.000&quot; when it is the same). Zoom in for the picks: the closer you click, the better the fit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-what-changed\"\u003EFind what changed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EFind changes\u003C/strong\u003E. TakeoffCraft compares the two drawings&#039; ink: ink only on the old sheet turns red, ink only on the new sheet turns blue, and the status bar counts them. Lines that moved by a hair (a re-plot) are not counted as changes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt also looks at every mark on the old sheet and counts the ones that sit where the drawing changed:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea run or an area when the drawing changed at one of its ends or corners, or along at least about a sixth of its length;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea count when anything changed right next to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-the-takeoff\"\u003EMove the takeoff\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EMove takeoff here\u003C/strong\u003E. Every mark on the old sheet moves to the new one, through the alignment, in one undo step. The ones next to a change are flagged: they are drawn with an orange halo, and the sheet bar shows &quot;(n) to check&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the new sheet had no scale, it takes the old sheet&#039;s scale (adjusted when the drawings had to be scaled to line up). If it already had a different scale, TakeoffCraft asks first: moved marks are measured at the new sheet&#039;s scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-the-flagged-marks\"\u003ECheck the flagged marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose &quot;(n) to check&quot; on the sheet bar. It zooms to the next flagged mark and selects it; its panel says why it is flagged.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFix the mark if the drawing changed under it (drag its corners, add or delete marks, or take it off again).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EChecked\u003C/strong\u003E in its panel. The flag goes, and the count on the sheet bar drops.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe flags are saved with the takeoff, so you can check them later.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EComparing works on vector PDFs and on scans alike, because it compares what is drawn. A scan that is skewed or stretched may line up only roughly with two points; check more marks by eye then.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"When a sheet is reissued, you do not have to take it off again. Open the old PDF and the new one, compare the two sheets, and move the takeoff across. TakeoffCraft flags every mark where the drawing changed, so you check those and keep the rest. Compare Open both PDFs: the old set and the revised one (Open plans; they can be in one file or several). Show the new sheet. Choose Compare on the sheet bar under the drawing. Pick the old sheet in the list. TakeoffCraft suggests the sheet with the same number in the other PDF, or the same page. Choose Compare. The old drawing now lies over the new one in red. Where the two agree, the red lines sit on the black ones; where they differ, you see red lines alone (removed) and black lines alone (added). A bar at the bottom of the sheet holds the steps below; Esc or Close ends comparing. Line them up Reissued sheets are often plotted a little off: moved on the page, or at a slightly different size. If the red drawing does not sit on the black one, choose Align by two points and click four times: a point on the old (red) drawing, such as a column center or a building corner, the same point on the new (black) drawing, a second point on the red drawing, far from the first, the same point on the black drawing. The red drawing moves, turns and scales onto the black one. The status bar says how much bigger the new drawing is (\"size × 1.000\" when it is the same). Zoom in for the picks: the closer you click, the better the fit. Find what changed Choose Find changes. TakeoffCraft compares the two drawings' ink: ink only on the old sheet turns red, ink only on the new sheet turns blue, and the status bar counts them. Lines that moved by a hair (a re-plot) are not counted as changes. It also looks at every mark on the old sheet and counts the ones that sit where the drawing changed: a run or an area when the drawing changed at one of its ends or corners, or along at least about a sixth of its length; a count when anything changed right next to it. Move the takeoff Choose Move takeoff here. Every mark on the old sheet moves to the new one, through the alignment, in one undo step. The ones next to a change are flagged: they are drawn with an orange halo, and the sheet bar shows \"(n) to check\". If the new sheet had no scale, it takes the old sheet's scale (adjusted when the drawings had to be scaled to line up). If it already had a different scale, TakeoffCraft asks first: moved marks are measured at the new sheet's scale. Check the flagged marks Choose \"(n) to check\" on the sheet bar. It zooms to the next flagged mark and selects it; its panel says why it is flagged. Fix the mark if the drawing changed under it (drag its corners, add or delete marks, or take it off again). Choose Checked in its panel. The flag goes, and the count on the sheet bar drops. The flags are saved with the takeoff, so you can check them later. Comparing works on vector PDFs and on scans alike, because it compares what is drawn. A scan that is skewed or stretched may line up only roughly with two points; check more marks by eye then.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"use-with-ai-assistants","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Use TakeoffCraft with an AI assistant","summary":"Let your own AI assistant or script read the takeoff's quantities and estimate, add items and marks, count and measure on a DWG or DXF, and export: permissions, connecting through CadCraft Studio, and the activity log.","section":"tasks","order":118,"keywords":["ai","assistant","agent","mcp","model context protocol","script","automation","quantities","estimate","permissions","disconnect","activity"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"connect-your-assistant","text":"Connect your assistant","level":2},{"id":"you-stay-in-charge","text":"You stay in charge","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for","text":"What an assistant can ask for","level":2},{"id":"if-something-doesn-t-work","text":"If something doesn't work","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with TakeoffCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft has \u003Cstrong\u003Eno AI of its own\u003C/strong\u003E and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). TakeoffCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use TakeoffCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. It is off until you do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what it may do: \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked to start with; tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E only when you need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, TakeoffCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset TakeoffCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E turns it off too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connect-your-assistant\"\u003EConnect your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants connect through \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect an assistant\u003C/span\u003E and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings to paste). Never share the token.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser tab can&#039;t open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"you-stay-in-charge\"\u003EYou stay in charge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery change is one undo step\u003C/strong\u003E, labelled with the assistant&#039;s name for it (for example &quot;Assistant: …&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the assistant&#039;s last change as a whole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou know when one is connected.\u003C/strong\u003E A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivity\u003C/span\u003E lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the log\u003C/span\u003E keeps a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EWhat an assistant can ask for\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPermission\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eapp_info\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esheets_list\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe takeoff open, its PDFs and sheets, each sheet&#039;s scale and marks\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eitems_list\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe items with their units, rates and assembly formulas\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_quantities\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantities per item or per sheet; marks on sheets with no scale are listed, never counted as zero\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_estimate\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe priced estimate as TakeoffCraft computes it, with its totals\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_export\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft&#039;s own CSV or XLSX export\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eitems_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdds items with their units, rates and waste\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emarks_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasures runs, areas and counts on a sheet. Lengths and areas only on a sheet with a scale, never on an unscaled one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecad_sheets\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecad_catalog\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecad_count\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn a DWG or DXF you opened: its sheets and their scales, what can be counted (blocks, layers and types, texts), and exact counts, as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECAD count\u003C/span\u003E makes them (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/count-from-a-drawing\"\u003ECount from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecad_count_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounts in the drawing and puts a count mark on each object found, in one step; counting again adds only what is new\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecad_measure\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasures a linear or an area item from the drawing&#039;s own lines, arcs, polylines and hatches, as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure drawing\u003C/span\u003E does (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-from-a-drawing\"\u003EMeasure from a drawing\u003C/a\u003E); only on a sheet with a scale\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eusd_export\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA scaled sheet&#039;s measured runs, areas and counts as a 3D check model in USD (\u003Ccode\u003E.usdz\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.usda\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.usdc\u003C/code\u003E), as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD package (.usdz)\u003C/span\u003E in the 3D check view writes it (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/usd\"\u003EExport the 3D check view as USD\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E ticked, an assistant adds items and marks (each call one undo step); it cannot move, change or delete marks that are already there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same quantities and estimate are available without opening TakeoffCraft: the CadCraft MCP server opens a \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file in a folder you name (\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_open\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_quantities\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_estimate\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_export\u003C/code\u003E), and \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft info Job.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E prints them on the command line. It also opens a DWG or DXF as a new takeoff on its sheets (scaled from the drawing as TakeoffCraft scales them), counts and measures on it with the same \u003Ccode\u003Ecad_*\u003C/code\u003E tools, adds items and saves the takeoff (\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_save\u003C/code\u003E); \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft count plan.dwg --block SHELF-18X48 --sheet K-101\u003C/code\u003E counts from the command line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-something-doesn-t-work\"\u003EIf something doesn&#039;t work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe assistant sees no TakeoffCraft tools:\u003C/strong\u003E check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;needs the export permission&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E tick \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, then ask again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;401&quot; or &quot;token&quot; errors:\u003C/strong\u003E the token changed (Studio restarted, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew token\u003C/span\u003E). Copy the new one into the assistant&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-also\"\u003ESee also\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003ERecipes for AI assistants\u003C/a\u003E: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with TakeoffCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch. TakeoffCraft has no AI of its own and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). TakeoffCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,) and go to the TakeoffCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use TakeoffCraft. It is off until you do. Choose what it may do: Read is ticked to start with; tick Edit, Save or Export only when you need them. Tools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, TakeoffCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once; Reset TakeoffCraft settings turns it off too. Connect your assistant Assistants connect through CadCraft Studio on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose Connect an assistant and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings to paste). Never share the token. A browser tab can't open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer. You stay in charge Every change is one undo step, labelled with the assistant's name for it (for example \"Assistant: …\"). Ctrl+Z takes back the assistant's last change as a whole. You know when one is connected. A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to Disconnect it. Activity. Settings › TakeoffCraft › Assistants › Activity lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. Save the log keeps a copy. What an assistant can ask for Tool Permission What it does app_info, sheets_list Read The takeoff open, its PDFs and sheets, each sheet's scale and marks items_list Read The items with their units, rates and assembly formulas takeoff_quantities Read Quantities per item or per sheet; marks on sheets with no scale are listed, never counted as zero takeoff_estimate Read The priced estimate as TakeoffCraft computes it, with its totals takeoff_export Export TakeoffCraft's own CSV or XLSX export items_add Edit Adds items with their units, rates and waste marks_add Edit Measures runs, areas and counts on a sheet. Lengths and areas only on a sheet with a scale, never on an unscaled one cad_sheets, cad_catalog, cad_count Read On a DWG or DXF you opened: its sheets and their scales, what can be counted (blocks, layers and types, texts), and exact counts, as CAD count makes them (Count from a drawing) cad_count_add Edit Counts in the drawing and puts a count mark on each object found, in one step; counting again adds only what is new cad_measure Edit Measures a linear or an area item from the drawing's own lines, arcs, polylines and hatches, as Measure drawing does (Measure from a drawing); only on a sheet with a scale usd_export Export A scaled sheet's measured runs, areas and counts as a 3D check model in USD (.usdz, .usda or .usdc), as USD package (.usdz) in the 3D check view writes it (Export the 3D check view as USD) With Edit ticked, an assistant adds items and marks (each call one undo step); it cannot move, change or delete marks that are already there. The same quantities and estimate are available without opening TakeoffCraft: the CadCraft MCP server opens a .takeoff.json file in a folder you name (takeoff_open, takeoff_quantities, takeoff_estimate, takeoff_export), and cadcraft info Job.takeoff.json prints them on the command line. It also opens a DWG or DXF as a new takeoff on its sheets (scaled from the drawing as TakeoffCraft scales them), counts and measures on it with the same cad_* tools, adds items and saves the takeoff (takeoff_save); cadcraft count plan.dwg --block SHELF-18X48 --sheet K-101 counts from the command line. If something doesn't work The assistant sees no TakeoffCraft tools: check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio. \"needs the export permission\": tick Export under Assistants, then ask again. \"401\" or \"token\" errors: the token changed (Studio restarted, or New token). Copy the new one into the assistant's settings. See also Recipes for AI assistants: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-live","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Take off one job together, live","summary":"What changes in TakeoffCraft while you are in a CadCraft Studio live session: everyone's items and marks in one takeoff, the same totals for all, your own undo, and joining from a browser on the same network.","section":"tasks","order":119,"keywords":["live","studio live","together","collaborate","session","share live","join","items","marks","totals","estimate","undo","host"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"everyone-measures-at-once","text":"Everyone measures at once","level":2},{"id":"undo-undoes-your-own-work","text":"Undo undoes your own work","level":2},{"id":"join-from-a-browser","text":"Join from a browser","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat happens to a takeoff while several people measure the same job at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the session (Share live, Join); this page is about the takeoff itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everyone-measures-at-once\"\u003EEveryone measures at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host&#039;s PDFs come to everyone who joins. Add items, measure runs, areas and counts, set scales and prices as usual. Your own marks show at once; other people&#039;s appear a moment after they make them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETotals are the same for everyone:\u003C/strong\u003E each window works the quantities and the estimate out from the marks, so nobody&#039;s totals can drift.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo people change one item or mark:\u003C/strong\u003E different things are both kept; the same thing goes to the later change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA mark on an item someone just deleted\u003C/strong\u003E is taken back, and the status bar says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach person&#039;s new items and marks get numbers of their own, so two people never make the same one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-undoes-your-own-work\"\u003EUndo undoes your own work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes your own last change and leaves everyone else&#039;s alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-from-a-browser\"\u003EJoin from a browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. TakeoffCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host&#039;s takeoff and its PDFs once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the host saves the takeoff. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn What happens to a takeoff while several people measure the same job at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the session (Share live, Join); this page is about the takeoff itself. Everyone measures at once The host's PDFs come to everyone who joins. Add items, measure runs, areas and counts, set scales and prices as usual. Your own marks show at once; other people's appear a moment after they make them. Totals are the same for everyone: each window works the quantities and the estimate out from the marks, so nobody's totals can drift. Two people change one item or mark: different things are both kept; the same thing goes to the later change. A mark on an item someone just deleted is taken back, and the status bar says why. Each person's new items and marks get numbers of their own, so two people never make the same one. Undo undoes your own work Ctrl+Z undoes your own last change and leaves everyone else's alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who. Join from a browser A teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host's code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. TakeoffCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host's takeoff and its PDFs once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet. When the session ends Only the host saves the takeoff. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"new-takeoff","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Start a new takeoff","summary":"Clear everything and start an empty takeoff without reloading the page, and keep your work safe first.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["new","new takeoff","clear","reset","start over","empty","close pdfs","next job"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E closes every PDF and starts an empty takeoff on the same page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave the current takeoff if you want to keep it: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf there are unsaved changes, TakeoffCraft asks \u003Cstrong\u003EUnsaved takeoff\u003C/strong\u003E: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff first\u003C/span\u003E (it downloads the project, then clears), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E. The window goes back to its empty state: no PDFs, no items, no marks, no scales. The status bar says &quot;New takeoff. Open PDFs to begin.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat is cleared:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eevery open PDF, its sheets and thumbnails,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eitems, assemblies, marks and scales,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eeach sheet&#039;s zoom and position,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe undo history (you cannot undo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Earc mode, and any auto count search or review.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour files on disk are not touched. To pick up the old takeoff again, open its \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E and PDFs: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach new takeoff starts with no items. To bring items over, copy them first: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy item\u003C/span\u003E under Items (or copy marks, which carry their items), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E in the new takeoff. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate marks\u003C/a\u003E. For items you use on every job, keep them in your \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/price-library\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/price-library\"\u003Eprice library\u003C/a\u003E and add them from there, or install a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/trade-packs\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/trade-packs\"\u003Etrade pack\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"New closes every PDF and starts an empty takeoff on the same page. Save the current takeoff if you want to keep it: Save takeoff or Ctrl+S. Choose New in the File group. If there are unsaved changes, TakeoffCraft asks Unsaved takeoff: choose Save takeoff first (it downloads the project, then clears), Discard changes, or Cancel. The window goes back to its empty state: no PDFs, no items, no marks, no scales. The status bar says \"New takeoff. Open PDFs to begin.\" What is cleared: every open PDF, its sheets and thumbnails, items, assemblies, marks and scales, each sheet's zoom and position, the undo history (you cannot undo New), arc mode, and any auto count search or review. Your files on disk are not touched. To pick up the old takeoff again, open its .takeoff.json and PDFs: see Save and reopen a takeoff. Each new takeoff starts with no items. To bring items over, copy them first: Copy item under Items (or copy marks, which carry their items), then Paste in the new takeoff. See Copy, paste and duplicate marks. For items you use on every job, keep them in your price library and add them from there, or install a trade pack.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"usd","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Export the 3D check view as USD","summary":"Write the walls, slabs, plates and runs of the 3D check view as a USD package, each with its item and quantity.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["usd","usdz","openusd","export","3d","check view","walls","slabs","rendering"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIn the 3D check view, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD package (.usdz)\u003C/span\u003E beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGLB\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach object (a wall, a slab, a plate, a count post or a run) is one object in the file, named after its item, carrying the item&#039;s name and the quantity the view measured. Slabs and plates are see-through as in the view. The file is in metres with Z up.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"In the 3D check view, choose USD package (.usdz) beside GLB. Each object (a wall, a slab, a plate, a count post or a run) is one object in the file, named after its item, carrying the item's name and the quantity the view measured. Slabs and plates are see-through as in the view. The file is in metres with Z up.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Coming from other takeoff software","summary":"How the habits of dedicated takeoff programs and PDF markup tools map to TakeoffCraft, and the differences that trip people up.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","migrate","conditions","parts","measurement tools","markups list","quantity link","takeoff habits","differences","compare"],"headings":[{"id":"habits-mapped","text":"Habits mapped","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-trip-people-up","text":"Differences that trip people up","level":2},{"id":"not-here-yet","text":"Not here yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you have done takeoff in PlanSwift or with the measurement tools in Bluebeam Revu, most of TakeoffCraft will feel familiar: a plan set, a scale per sheet, things you measure, and a priced total. The way the pieces fit together is different in places, and those places are below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you know PlanSwift or On-Screen Takeoff, pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff desk\u003C/span\u003E menu style in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E: a menu bar with File, Edit, View, Scale, Takeoff, Items, Markup and Export over a row of the takeoff tools, with the scale presets on the sheet bar. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"habits-mapped\"\u003EHabits mapped\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn TakeoffCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening a job or project that stores the plans (PlanSwift)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the PDFs and the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E project side by side. The project stores only the takeoff; the PDFs stay your own files.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA markups list with notes, clouds and highlights (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar: notes, clouds and highlights with a status (Open, Answered, Closed), listed under \u003Cstrong\u003EMarkups\u003C/strong\u003E, drawn in the marked PDF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/markups\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/markups\"\u003ENotes, clouds and highlights\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasurements saved as markups inside the PDF (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe takeoff lives in the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E writes a copy with the takeoff drawn in, for sharing; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/span\u003E writes a copy that carries the takeoff, to reopen in TakeoffCraft.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoff items (PlanSwift), custom measurement tools saved in the Tool Chest (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EItems\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E: name, type, color, unit, height or depth, waste, rates.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParts and assemblies with formulas (PlanSwift)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E: components with a formula using \u003Ccode\u003EL H A N P\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESetting the page scale, or calibrating (both)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group, per sheet. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E measures a known dimension.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApplying one scale to a page range\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot yet: set each sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EViewports with different scales on one sheet (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot yet: one scale per sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylength and area tools (Bluebeam), linear and area takeoff (PlanSwift)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHolding Shift for straight lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E snaps to 45° steps, the same idea.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnapping to the drawing&#039;s lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn by default: ends, midpoints, intersections and the nearest point on a line, with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it off, as in CAD programs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount tool (both)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVisual search for symbols (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): box one symbol, review, add.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut-outs or deductions in areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) on a selected area, and round cut-outs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDeductions on linear runs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the run straight through and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E) for the opening. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\"\u003EMeasure linear runs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArc segments in a polyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): click a point on the curve, then its end.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe Markups list with quantity columns (Bluebeam)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy item\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy sheet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEstimating grid with pricing (PlanSwift)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same table: waste, material and labor per unit, extended totals, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob total\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExporting to Excel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E with live formulas, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEscape to stop a tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels and returns to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click to finish\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click finishes a run or closes an area here too. There is no right-click menu.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-trip-people-up\"\u003EDifferences that trip people up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe takeoff is not inside your PDF.\u003C/strong\u003E Nothing you do changes your PDFs. Save the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file and keep it with the drawings. Sending someone the marked PDF does not send them an editable takeoff: send the project file and the original PDFs, or a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen#keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-hash=\"keep-the-takeoff-inside-the-pdf\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPDFs are matched by contents, not by name.\u003C/strong\u003E A revised sheet saved over the old file name is a new file to TakeoffCraft, with no scale and no marks. Your old marks stay on the old version and keep counting. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen#when-a-pdf-has-changed\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-hash=\"when-a-pdf-has-changed\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnscaled marks are left out, not counted as zero.\u003C/strong\u003E If a sheet has no scale, its runs and areas do not count, and the table says &quot;Incomplete&quot;. In many programs you would just see 0. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale#why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-hash=\"why-unscaled-sheets-are-not-counted-as-zero\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThere is no page-size guessing.\u003C/strong\u003E Presets assume the PDF is printed at full size. A half-size or &quot;fit to page&quot; PDF needs \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E. Always use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne item, one main type, more if you ask.\u003C/strong\u003E An item is linear, area or count, and its type locks once it has marks. A condition that gives you both an area and its perimeter is an area item: its perimeter is the \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E variable. A wall with both length and area is a linear item with a height. For a multi-condition item, such as a room with a floor, a base and doors, tick the other kinds under \u003Cstrong\u003EAlso measure\u003C/strong\u003E: each is priced on its own row. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/create-items#one-item-several-kinds-of-marks\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/create-items\" data-hash=\"one-item-several-kinds-of-marks\"\u003EOne item, several kinds of marks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFormulas run on totals.\u003C/strong\u003E Assembly formulas use the item&#039;s totals across every sheet, not each run separately. \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E (the number of runs or areas) is there for per-run extras, like one extra stud per wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPerimeter includes cut-outs.\u003C/strong\u003E An area&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E adds the edges of its holes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-areas#read-the-area\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-areas\" data-hash=\"read-the-area\"\u003EMeasure areas with cut-outs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDragging pans.\u003C/strong\u003E A left-button drag on the sheet pans, in every tool except \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E. You never need to switch to a pan tool. The wheel zooms; it does not scroll.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA recovery copy, not a cloud.\u003C/strong\u003E TakeoffCraft keeps a copy of your work in this browser and offers it back after a crash (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen#if-the-tab-or-the-browser-stops\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-hash=\"if-the-tab-or-the-browser-stops\"\u003EIf the tab or the browser stops\u003C/a\u003E), but the takeoff is yours to save: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Cloud saving is not available yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything is in the browser.\u003C/strong\u003E There is nothing to install, and the plans are never uploaded. For a desktop app, TakeoffCraft comes in CadCraft Studio, with the other seven apps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-here-yet\"\u003ENot here yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPer-sheet scale regions are not built yet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E for the full list and what to do instead.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you have done takeoff in PlanSwift or with the measurement tools in Bluebeam Revu, most of TakeoffCraft will feel familiar: a plan set, a scale per sheet, things you measure, and a priced total. The way the pieces fit together is different in places, and those places are below. If you know PlanSwift or On-Screen Takeoff, pick the Takeoff desk menu style in Settings › TakeoffCraft › Menus, keys and mouse: a menu bar with File, Edit, View, Scale, Takeoff, Items, Markup and Export over a row of the takeoff tools, with the scale presets on the sheet bar. See Menus, keys and mouse. Habits mapped You are used to In TakeoffCraft Opening a job or project that stores the plans (PlanSwift) Open the PDFs and the .takeoff.json project side by side. The project stores only the takeoff; the PDFs stay your own files. A markups list with notes, clouds and highlights (Bluebeam) Markup on the sheet bar: notes, clouds and highlights with a status (Open, Answered, Closed), listed under Markups, drawn in the marked PDF. See Notes, clouds and highlights. Measurements saved as markups inside the PDF (Bluebeam) The takeoff lives in the .takeoff.json file. Marked PDF writes a copy with the takeoff drawn in, for sharing; PDF with the takeoff inside writes a copy that carries the takeoff, to reopen in TakeoffCraft. Takeoff items (PlanSwift), custom measurement tools saved in the Tool Chest (Bluebeam) Items under Items: name, type, color, unit, height or depth, waste, rates. Parts and assemblies with formulas (PlanSwift) The item's Assembly: components with a formula using L H A N P. Setting the page scale, or calibrating (both) Preset list or Calibrate in the Scale group, per sheet. Check measures a known dimension. Applying one scale to a page range Not yet: set each sheet. Viewports with different scales on one sheet (Bluebeam) Not yet: one scale per sheet. Polylength and area tools (Bluebeam), linear and area takeoff (PlanSwift) Linear (L) and Area (A). Holding Shift for straight lines Shift snaps to 45° steps, the same idea. Snapping to the drawing's lines On by default: ends, midpoints, intersections and the nearest point on a line, with F3 to turn it off, as in CAD programs. Count tool (both) Count (C). Visual search for symbols (Bluebeam) Auto count (S): box one symbol, review, add. Cut-outs or deductions in areas Cut-out (X) on a selected area, and round cut-outs. Deductions on linear runs Draw the run straight through and press E (Deduct) for the opening. See Measure linear runs. Arc segments in a polyline Arc (R): click a point on the curve, then its end. The Markups list with quantity columns (Bluebeam) The Quantities table, By item or By sheet. Estimating grid with pricing (PlanSwift) The same table: waste, material and labor per unit, extended totals, Job total. Exporting to Excel XLSX with live formulas, or CSV. Escape to stop a tool Esc cancels and returns to Select. Right-click to finish Right-click finishes a run or closes an area here too. There is no right-click menu. Differences that trip people up The takeoff is not inside your PDF. Nothing you do changes your PDFs. Save the .takeoff.json file and keep it with the drawings. Sending someone the marked PDF does not send them an editable takeoff: send the project file and the original PDFs, or a PDF with the takeoff inside. PDFs are matched by contents, not by name. A revised sheet saved over the old file name is a new file to TakeoffCraft, with no scale and no marks. Your old marks stay on the old version and keep counting. See Save and reopen a takeoff. Unscaled marks are left out, not counted as zero. If a sheet has no scale, its runs and areas do not count, and the table says \"Incomplete\". In many programs you would just see 0. See Set the scale. There is no page-size guessing. Presets assume the PDF is printed at full size. A half-size or \"fit to page\" PDF needs Calibrate. Always use Check. One item, one main type, more if you ask. An item is linear, area or count, and its type locks once it has marks. A condition that gives you both an area and its perimeter is an area item: its perimeter is the P variable. A wall with both length and area is a linear item with a height. For a multi-condition item, such as a room with a floor, a base and doors, tick the other kinds under Also measure: each is priced on its own row. See One item, several kinds of marks. Formulas run on totals. Assembly formulas use the item's totals across every sheet, not each run separately. N (the number of runs or areas) is there for per-run extras, like one extra stud per wall. Perimeter includes cut-outs. An area's P adds the edges of its holes. See Measure areas with cut-outs. Dragging pans. A left-button drag on the sheet pans, in every tool except Auto count. You never need to switch to a pan tool. The wheel zooms; it does not scroll. A recovery copy, not a cloud. TakeoffCraft keeps a copy of your work in this browser and offers it back after a crash (If the tab or the browser stops), but the takeoff is yours to save: Ctrl+S. Cloud saving is not available yet. Everything is in the browser. There is nothing to install, and the plans are never uploaded. For a desktop app, TakeoffCraft comes in CadCraft Studio, with the other seven apps. Not here yet Per-sheet scale regions are not built yet. See What is not built yet for the full list and what to do instead.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"settings","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"TakeoffCraft settings","summary":"Show inches to 1/16, 1/8 or 1/4, start new items with a waste percentage, choose where the Auto count slider starts, snap to the drawing's lines or to 45° without holding Shift, set how wide a door Room fill closes, and pick the 3D view's corner indicator.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["settings","preferences","options","precision","fractions","inches","rounding","waste","default waste","auto count","threshold","match","slider","snap","45","angle","shift","view cube","axis icon","corner indicator","3d"],"headings":[{"id":"feet-and-inches","text":"Feet and inches","level":2},{"id":"waste-to-start-with","text":"Waste to start with","level":2},{"id":"auto-count-where-the-match-slider-starts","text":"Auto count: where the match slider starts","level":2},{"id":"snap-to-the-drawing-s-own-lines","text":"Snap to the drawing's own lines","level":2},{"id":"room-fill-how-wide-an-opening-it-closes","text":"Room fill: how wide an opening it closes","level":2},{"id":"snap-to-45-without-holding-shift","text":"Snap to 45° without holding Shift","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, panels, toolbar). TakeoffCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset TakeoffCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E puts them back to the start.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"feet-and-inches\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShow lengths to the nearest \u003Cstrong\u003E1/16&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E (the start), \u003Cstrong\u003E1/8&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003E1/4&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E. This changes how lengths read on the sheet, in the panels, in the quantities and in the status bar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the display is rounded. Quantities, costs and the exports are worked out from the exact lengths, and the exports always show 1/16&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"waste-to-start-with\"\u003EWaste to start with\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe waste percentage every new item starts with: 0% (the start), 2%, 3%, 5%, 7.5%, 10%, 15% or 20%. You can still type another waste for each item, in the item editor or straight in the quantities table. Items you already have keep their own waste.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"auto-count-where-the-match-slider-starts\"\u003EAuto count: where the match slider starts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch\u003C/span\u003E slider sits when an \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/auto-count\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/a\u003E search finishes, from 50% to 95%; 75% is the start. Lower offers more look-alikes for you to reject; higher offers fewer. You can still move the slider during each review.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"snap-to-the-drawing-s-own-lines\"\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s own lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn at the start. While you measure or calibrate, a point near a line of the PDF jumps to its end, its midpoint, a crossing or the nearest point on it. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar turn it on and off too. More: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\"\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s lines\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"room-fill-how-wide-an-opening-it-closes\"\u003ERoom fill: how wide an opening it closes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom fill closes openings up to\u003C/span\u003E sets how wide a door opening or gap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E tool treats as closed: 3&#039;-0&quot; to 8&#039;-0&quot;, 4&#039;-0&quot; at the start. Wider than your widest door lets a room run into the next one; narrower than a door leaves the room open. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-areas#fill-a-room-with-one-click\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-areas\" data-hash=\"fill-a-room-with-one-click\"\u003EFill a room with one click\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"snap-to-45-without-holding-shift\"\u003ESnap to 45° without holding Shift\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOff at the start. When it is on, runs, outlines, cut-outs and dragged corners go at 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° from the last point without holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to click at any angle. The status bar then reads &quot;Snaps to 45°; Shift frees the angle&quot;. The point you click on an arc&#039;s curve and circles are never snapped; an arc&#039;s end is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\"\u003E3D check view\u003C/a\u003E only: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView cube\u003C/span\u003E (the start: a cube at the top right to click, drag and turn), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis icon\u003C/span\u003E (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom right) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E. It changes at once and is kept in this browser. The flat sheet never shows one.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, TakeoffCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, panels, toolbar). TakeoffCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. Reset TakeoffCraft settings puts them back to the start. Feet and inches Show lengths to the nearest 1/16\" (the start), 1/8\" or 1/4\". This changes how lengths read on the sheet, in the panels, in the quantities and in the status bar. Only the display is rounded. Quantities, costs and the exports are worked out from the exact lengths, and the exports always show 1/16\". Waste to start with The waste percentage every new item starts with: 0% (the start), 2%, 3%, 5%, 7.5%, 10%, 15% or 20%. You can still type another waste for each item, in the item editor or straight in the quantities table. Items you already have keep their own waste. Auto count: where the match slider starts Where the Match slider sits when an Auto count search finishes, from 50% to 95%; 75% is the start. Lower offers more look-alikes for you to reject; higher offers fewer. You can still move the slider during each review. Snap to the drawing's own lines On at the start. While you measure or calibrate, a point near a line of the PDF jumps to its end, its midpoint, a crossing or the nearest point on it. F3 and Snap in the status bar turn it on and off too. More: Snap to the drawing's lines. Room fill: how wide an opening it closes Room fill closes openings up to sets how wide a door opening or gap the Room tool treats as closed: 3'-0\" to 8'-0\", 4'-0\" at the start. Wider than your widest door lets a room run into the next one; narrower than a door leaves the room open. See Fill a room with one click. Snap to 45° without holding Shift Off at the start. When it is on, runs, outlines, cut-outs and dragged corners go at 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° from the last point without holding Shift; hold Shift to click at any angle. The status bar then reads \"Snaps to 45°; Shift frees the angle\". The point you click on an arc's curve and circles are never snapped; an arc's end is. Corner indicator For the 3D check view only: View cube (the start: a cube at the top right to click, drag and turn), Axis icon (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom right) or None. It changes at once and is kept in this browser. The flat sheet never shows one.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"menus-keys-and-mouse","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Menus, keys and mouse","summary":"Pick a menu style that matches the way you already work, give any command your own key, move commands between menus, and set what the wheel and the mouse buttons do.","section":"reference","order":6,"keywords":["menu style","menus","menu bar","ribbon","ribbon tabs","toolbar","keyboard shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","customize","remap","rebind","mouse","wheel","invert","zoom direction","middle button","right button","pan","export","import","switching","familiar","takeoff desk","conditions"],"headings":[{"id":"the-three-styles","text":"The three styles","level":2},{"id":"make-it-your-own","text":"Make it your own","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"take-it-with-you","text":"Take it with you","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft&#039;s commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A \u003Cstrong\u003Emenu style\u003C/strong\u003E only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-three-styles\"\u003EThe three styles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft&#039;s own toolbar: File, Export, Scale, Takeoff, Edit and View, with the single-letter tool keys. The start.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff desk\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA menu bar (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E) over one row of the takeoff tools, the way on-screen takeoff programs lay theirs out. Items are called conditions on the row. The same keys, plus \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a new item. The scale presets move to the sheet bar, beside the sheet&#039;s scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff ribbon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERibbon tabs named for the work: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E (measure, shape, scale, from the drawing, conditions), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEstimate\u003C/span\u003E (the workbook, marked PDFs, the bid), with a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu and a quick row for open, save, undo and redo. The tools you use all day are large, their neighbours small and stacked. The same keys; the scale presets move to the sheet bar.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three styles keep TakeoffCraft&#039;s keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E select, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E linear, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E area, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E circle, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E room, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cut-out, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E count, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E auto count, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E calibrate, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E check, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E arc mode, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deduct mode, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to fit, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E the 3D check view, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E snap, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheet bar&#039;s tools (notes, clouds, highlights, Compare, 3D) wait until a PDF is open; picked before that, the status line says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA key that belongs to TakeoffCraft&#039;s own layout but that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (&quot;In My keys, Count is Ctrl+Alt+C&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the arrows always keep their meaning. While you draw a run or an outline, paste or review an auto count, the letters and the numbers of a typed length stay with the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-it-your-own\"\u003EMake it your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the style closest to what you want, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustomize…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys and menus\u003C/span\u003E, find a command (the search box helps). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a key\u003C/span\u003E and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith the menu bar, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere it sits\u003C/span\u003E moves the command to another menu, or to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys only\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMouse\u003C/span\u003E, set the wheel and the buttons that pan the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving a built-in style makes your own copy (&quot;My Takeoff desk&quot;); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms in\u003C/span\u003E (the start) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms out\u003C/span\u003E, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan the sheet with\u003C/strong\u003E: the middle button, the right button, or both. A left drag on empty paper and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with a drag always pan; a right click without dragging always finishes a run or an outline.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag in the 3D views\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEach view&#039;s own\u003C/span\u003E keeps the 3D check view&#039;s buttons (left and middle orbit, right pans). Or set the middle and right buttons to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the button swaps orbit and pan. The left button always orbits and picks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-it-with-you\"\u003ETake it with you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the style in use as a small \u003Ccode\u003E.ccinput.json\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E adds one, from another computer or from a colleague. An imported file can only point at TakeoffCraft&#039;s own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status line. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this style\u003C/span\u003E removes one of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese settings stay in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this style in every app\u003C/span\u003E under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a menu bar or a ribbon), with the changes you made in each app. TakeoffCraft has no style with a command line: when one is chosen for every app, TakeoffCraft keeps its own toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff ribbon\u003C/span\u003E is also the ribbon of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon and project browser\u003C/span\u003E layout (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E): see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe TakeoffCraft screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelated: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/settings\"\u003ETakeoffCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other takeoff software\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft's commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A menu style only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in Settings (Ctrl+,) › TakeoffCraft › Menus, keys and mouse; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it. The three styles Style What you get TakeoffCraft TakeoffCraft's own toolbar: File, Export, Scale, Takeoff, Edit and View, with the single-letter tool keys. The start. Takeoff desk A menu bar (File, Edit, View, Scale, Takeoff, Items, Markup, Export) over one row of the takeoff tools, the way on-screen takeoff programs lay theirs out. Items are called conditions on the row. The same keys, plus N for a new item. The scale presets move to the sheet bar, beside the sheet's scale. Takeoff ribbon Ribbon tabs named for the work: Takeoff (measure, shape, scale, from the drawing, conditions), Edit, View, Markup and Estimate (the workbook, marked PDFs, the bid), with a File menu and a quick row for open, save, undo and redo. The tools you use all day are large, their neighbours small and stacked. The same keys; the scale presets move to the sheet bar. All three styles keep TakeoffCraft's keys: V select, L linear, A area, O circle, B room, X cut-out, C count, S auto count, K calibrate, D check, R arc mode, E deduct mode, F and W to fit, 3 the 3D check view, F3 snap, and Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+A. The sheet bar's tools (notes, clouds, highlights, Compare, 3D) wait until a PDF is open; picked before that, the status line says so. A key that belongs to TakeoffCraft's own layout but that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (\"In My keys, Count is Ctrl+Alt+C\"). Delete, Esc, Enter, Tab, Space and the arrows always keep their meaning. While you draw a run or an outline, paste or review an auto count, the letters and the numbers of a typed length stay with the drawing. Make it your own Pick the style closest to what you want, then click Customize…. On Keys and menus, find a command (the search box helps). Add a key and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off. With the menu bar, Where it sits moves the command to another menu, or to Keys only. On Mouse, set the wheel and the buttons that pan the sheet. Give it a name and click Save. Saving a built-in style makes your own copy (\"My Takeoff desk\"); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save. Mouse Mouse wheel: Scroll up zooms in (the start) or Scroll up zooms out, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move. Pan the sheet with: the middle button, the right button, or both. A left drag on empty paper and Space with a drag always pan; a right click without dragging always finishes a run or an outline. Drag in the 3D views: Each view's own keeps the 3D check view's buttons (left and middle orbit, right pans). Or set the middle and right buttons to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; Shift with the button swaps orbit and pan. The left button always orbits and picks. Take it with you Export saves the style in use as a small .ccinput.json file; Import… adds one, from another computer or from a colleague. An imported file can only point at TakeoffCraft's own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status line. Delete this style removes one of your own. These settings stay in this browser. Use this style in every app under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a menu bar or a ribbon), with the changes you made in each app. TakeoffCraft has no style with a command line: when one is chosen for every app, TakeoffCraft keeps its own toolbar. The Takeoff ribbon is also the ribbon of the Ribbon and project browser layout (Settings › Global › Layout): see The TakeoffCraft screen. Related: Keys and mouse · TakeoffCraft settings · Coming from other takeoff software","lang":"en"},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Keyboard and mouse","summary":"Every key, mouse, trackpad and touch action in TakeoffCraft, grouped, with the platform differences.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keys","shortcuts","hotkeys","keyboard","mouse","trackpad","touch","pan","zoom","wheel","right-click","double-click","space","shift","copy","paste","cut","duplicate","select all"],"headings":[{"id":"tools","text":"Tools","level":2},{"id":"while-drawing","text":"While drawing","level":2},{"id":"selecting-and-editing","text":"Selecting and editing","level":2},{"id":"copy-and-paste","text":"Copy and paste","level":2},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":2},{"id":"view-and-sheets","text":"View and sheets","level":2},{"id":"3d-view","text":"3D view","level":2},{"id":"auto-count","text":"Auto count","level":2},{"id":"help","text":"Help","level":2},{"id":"dialogs-and-fields","text":"Dialogs and fields","level":2},{"id":"platform-notes","text":"Platform notes","level":2},{"id":"keys-the-browser-keeps","text":"Keys the browser keeps","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELetter keys work when the pointer is anywhere on the page and you are not typing in a field. While a dialog is open, its own keys apply. Letters work with or without \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tools\"\u003ETools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom\u003C/span\u003E (fill a room with one click)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E on or off (in Linear, Area and Cut-out)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeduct\u003C/span\u003E on or off: the next segments of a run are drawn but not counted\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA digit, \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E while drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E box: type a length or coordinates, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E places the point (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/measure-linear#type-lengths-and-coordinates\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/measure-linear\" data-hash=\"type-lengths-and-coordinates\"\u003EMeasure linear runs\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E (select an area first)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E the scale\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"while-drawing\"\u003EWhile drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace a point (a dot, with Count).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s lines on or off (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/snap-to-the-drawing\"\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s lines\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held while clicking\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnap the segment to 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° from the last point. Not for arc points or circles.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish a run, or close an area or cut-out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish a run, or close an area or cut-out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click (without moving)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish a run, or close an area or cut-out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the first point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose an area or cut-out (from the third point).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove the last point (first the pending arc point).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop the pending arc point; then cancel the unfinished mark; then go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"selecting-and-editing\"\u003ESelecting and editing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a mark (Select tool)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect it and make its item current.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click a mark\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd it to the selection, or take it out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect every mark on the open sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a corner handle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove that point (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E snaps to 45°). Works when one mark is selected.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selected marks (when you are not drawing).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect none: clear the selection and the check line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-and-paste\"\u003ECopy and paste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the selected marks, with their items.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut: copy the selected marks, then delete them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste: the copy follows the pointer; click where it goes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place: the same spot on the open sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate the selected marks: the copy follows the pointer; click where it goes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut down nothing: cancel a paste or duplicate that is on the pointer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe letters on their own still pick tools (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E); only \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with them copies and pastes. In a text field these keys copy and paste text as usual. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate marks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes the selected mark when you are not drawing. If that happens by accident, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen plans\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese two work even while you are typing in a field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"view-and-sheets\"\u003EView and sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in and out at the pointer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag (left, middle or right button)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E, a left drag draws the search box instead.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held + drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan, in any tool.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"=\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in at the middle of the view.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom out at the middle of the view.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENext or previous sheet in the sheets list.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECycle full screen modes (Control on every platform, also on a Mac).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen or close this help, a panel at the side.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe 3D check view of the sheet, on or off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-view\"\u003E3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag, or drag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + drag, or drag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom at the pointer. On a trackpad, a two-finger swipe orbits and a pinch zooms.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect the mark under the pointer (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click adds or takes out).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom extents.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBack to the flat sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/check-in-3d\"\u003ECheck the takeoff in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox the symbol to search for (starts the search).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a match box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReject it, or restore it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancel a running search, or discard the review.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"help\"\u003EHelp\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen or close the help panel (also the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button in the app bar). It works in every tool, even while typing in a field.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose the help panel. In its search box, the first Esc clears the search. While the panel is open, an Esc pressed after clicking the sheet closes the panel first; press it again to cancel in TakeoffCraft.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn many Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press fn with F1, or turn on &quot;Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys&quot; in System Settings, under Keyboard.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dialogs-and-fields\"\u003EDialogs and fields\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn a field: apply the value. In the calibrate dialog: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose a dialog (as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E); close the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E form.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove to the next field.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"platform-notes\"\u003EPlatform notes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is the Command key: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Full screen is the exception: Control+Shift+F.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERight-click is a two-finger click on a trackpad, once secondary click is on in System Settings, under Trackpad. TakeoffCraft reads only the secondary button; a Control-click may count as a plain click and add a point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo fingers scrolling on a trackpad zoom the sheet (they do not scroll it). To pan, press and drag, or hold Space and drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPage Up and Page Down are fn with the up and down arrows on keyboards without them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBackspace is the key labeled delete.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Ctrl: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a laptop touchpad, a two-finger scroll zooms the sheet. To pan, press and drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ELinux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Ctrl. Some desktops use Ctrl+Alt or Super shortcuts of their own; TakeoffCraft uses none of them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a touchpad, a two-finger scroll zooms the sheet. To pan, press and drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft reads touch as a single pointer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap places a point, selects a mark or adds a count dot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with one finger pans the sheet (in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E it draws the search box).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPinch-to-zoom is not supported yet. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a keyboard.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo close an area, tap its first point. To finish a run, double-tap where your browser turns a double tap into a double-click, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a keyboard.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThere is no live length readout until you tap, because there is no hover.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA keyboard and mouse or trackpad is the better way to do takeoff today.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EKeys the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBrowsers keep some shortcuts for themselves, and a page cannot use them: closing the tab (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), new tab or window (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), and browser zoom (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command +\"\u003E⌘+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl++\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command −\"\u003E⌘−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). That is why TakeoffCraft zooms the sheet with the wheel and the bare \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keys. If you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command +\"\u003E⌘+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl++\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E by mistake, the whole page grows; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 0\"\u003E⌘0\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+0\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Letter keys work when the pointer is anywhere on the page and you are not typing in a field. While a dialog is open, its own keys apply. Letters work with or without Shift. Tools Key Tool V Select L Linear A Area O Circle B Room (fill a room with one click) R Arc on or off (in Linear, Area and Cut-out) E Deduct on or off: the next segments of a run are drawn but not counted A digit, @ or # while drawing Open the Type box: type a length or coordinates, Enter places the point (Measure linear runs) X Cut-out (select an area first) C Count S Auto count K Calibrate D Check the scale While drawing Key or action What it does Click Place a point (a dot, with Count). F3 Snap to the drawing's lines on or off (Snap to the drawing's lines). Shift held while clicking Snap the segment to 0°, 45°, 90° or 135° from the last point. Not for arc points or circles. Double-click Finish a run, or close an area or cut-out. Enter Finish a run, or close an area or cut-out. Right-click (without moving) Finish a run, or close an area or cut-out. Click the first point Close an area or cut-out (from the third point). Backspace or Delete Remove the last point (first the pending arc point). Esc Drop the pending arc point; then cancel the unfinished mark; then go back to Select. Selecting and editing Key or action What it does Click a mark (Select tool) Select it and make its item current. Shift + click a mark Add it to the selection, or take it out. Ctrl+A Select every mark on the open sheet. Drag a corner handle Move that point (Shift snaps to 45°). Works when one mark is selected. Delete or Backspace Delete the selected marks (when you are not drawing). Esc Select none: clear the selection and the check line. Ctrl+Z Undo. Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo. Copy and paste Key What it does Ctrl+C Copy the selected marks, with their items. Ctrl+X Cut: copy the selected marks, then delete them. Ctrl+V Paste: the copy follows the pointer; click where it goes. Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place: the same spot on the open sheet. Ctrl+D Duplicate the selected marks: the copy follows the pointer; click where it goes. Esc Put down nothing: cancel a paste or duplicate that is on the pointer. The letters on their own still pick tools (C is Count, D is Check, X is Cut-out, V is Select, A is Area); only Ctrl with them copies and pastes. In a text field these keys copy and paste text as usual. See Copy, paste and duplicate marks. Backspace deletes the selected mark when you are not drawing. If that happens by accident, Ctrl+Z brings it back. Files Key What it does Ctrl+O Open plans Ctrl+S Save takeoff These two work even while you are typing in a field. View and sheets Key or action What it does Mouse wheel Zoom in and out at the pointer. Drag (left, middle or right button) Pan. In Auto count, a left drag draws the search box instead. Space held + drag Pan, in any tool. F Fit page W Fit width + or = Zoom in at the middle of the view. − Zoom out at the middle of the view. Page Down / Page Up Next or previous sheet in the sheets list. Ctrl+Shift+F Cycle full screen modes (Control on every platform, also on a Mac). F1 Open or close this help, a panel at the side. 3 The 3D check view of the sheet, on or off. 3D view Key or action What it does Drag, or drag with the middle button Orbit. Shift + drag, or drag with the right button Pan. Mouse wheel Zoom at the pointer. On a trackpad, a two-finger swipe orbits and a pinch zooms. Click Select the mark under the pointer (Shift + click adds or takes out). F or W Zoom extents. 3 Back to the flat sheet. See Check the takeoff in 3D. Auto count Key or action What it does Drag a box Box the symbol to search for (starts the search). Click a match box Reject it, or restore it. Esc Cancel a running search, or discard the review. Help Key What it does F1 Open or close the help panel (also the Help button in the app bar). It works in every tool, even while typing in a field. Esc Close the help panel. In its search box, the first Esc clears the search. While the panel is open, an Esc pressed after clicking the sheet closes the panel first; press it again to cancel in TakeoffCraft. On many Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press fn with F1, or turn on \"Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys\" in System Settings, under Keyboard. Dialogs and fields Key What it does Enter In a field: apply the value. In the calibrate dialog: Set the scale. Esc Close a dialog (as Cancel); close the New item form. Tab Move to the next field. Platform notes Ctrl is the Command key: Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O. Full screen is the exception: Control+Shift+F. Right-click is a two-finger click on a trackpad, once secondary click is on in System Settings, under Trackpad. TakeoffCraft reads only the secondary button; a Control-click may count as a plain click and add a point. Two fingers scrolling on a trackpad zoom the sheet (they do not scroll it). To pan, press and drag, or hold Space and drag. Page Up and Page Down are fn with the up and down arrows on keyboards without them. Backspace is the key labeled delete. Ctrl is Ctrl: Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O. On a laptop touchpad, a two-finger scroll zooms the sheet. To pan, press and drag. Ctrl is Ctrl. Some desktops use Ctrl+Alt or Super shortcuts of their own; TakeoffCraft uses none of them. On a touchpad, a two-finger scroll zooms the sheet. To pan, press and drag. TakeoffCraft reads touch as a single pointer: Tap places a point, selects a mark or adds a count dot. Drag with one finger pans the sheet (in Auto count it draws the search box). Pinch-to-zoom is not supported yet. Use Fit page and Fit width, or + and − on a keyboard. To close an area, tap its first point. To finish a run, double-tap where your browser turns a double tap into a double-click, or press Enter on a keyboard. There is no live length readout until you tap, because there is no hover. A keyboard and mouse or trackpad is the better way to do takeoff today. Keys the browser keeps Browsers keep some shortcuts for themselves, and a page cannot use them: closing the tab (Ctrl+W), new tab or window (Ctrl+T, Ctrl+N), and browser zoom (Ctrl++, Ctrl+−). That is why TakeoffCraft zooms the sheet with the wheel and the bare + and − keys. If you press Ctrl++ by mistake, the whole page grows; Ctrl+0 puts it back. See Keys, mice and trackpads.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"entry-formats","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Length, rate and percent entry","summary":"What you can type for distances, heights, money rates and waste percentages, with examples and the messages for input TakeoffCraft cannot read.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["input","format","feet inches","fractions","decimal feet","metric","meters","millimeters","rate","money","dollars","percent","waste","typing"],"headings":[{"id":"lengths","text":"Lengths","level":2},{"id":"arithmetic","text":"Arithmetic","level":3},{"id":"how-lengths-are-shown","text":"How lengths are shown","level":2},{"id":"rates-material-and-labor-per-unit","text":"Rates (material and labor per unit)","level":2},{"id":"waste-percent","text":"Waste percent","level":2},{"id":"names-and-units","text":"Names and units","level":2},{"id":"formulas","text":"Formulas","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"lengths\"\u003ELengths\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUsed by \u003Cstrong\u003EReal distance\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E, and by \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E in the item editor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 feet 6½ inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet 6½ inches (a space instead of the dash)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6-1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet 6½ inches (dashes between the parts)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E30&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E30 inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6½ inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E¾ inch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24 ft 6 in\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 feet 6 inches (also \u003Ccode\u003Efoot\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Efeet\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Einch\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Einches\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24.5\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24.5 in the default unit (see below)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24.5&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24.5 feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E7.5 m\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E7.5 meters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E750 cm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E750 centimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E7500 mm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E7,500 millimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA plain number is in \u003Cstrong\u003Efeet\u003C/strong\u003E for calibration and for items in LF, SF or SY, and in \u003Cstrong\u003Emeters\u003C/strong\u003E for items in m or m².\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECurly quotes and the prime marks from word processors (′ ″ ’ ”) work like \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. Two apostrophes \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;&#039;\u003C/code\u003E count as \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECommas are ignored: \u003Ccode\u003E1,200 mm\u003C/code\u003E is 1200 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFractions need whole numbers on both sides: \u003Ccode\u003E6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E works, \u003Ccode\u003E6.5 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E does not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZero and negative distances are refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMessages:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Type a distance, like 24&#039;-0&quot; or 7.5 m.&quot; (the field is empty)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Type a distance greater than zero.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Use feet and inches (24&#039;-6 1/2&quot;), decimal feet, m, cm or mm.&quot; (an unknown unit)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;That distance could not be read. Try 24&#039;-6 1/2&quot; or 24.5.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo clear an item&#039;s height or depth, empty the field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"arithmetic\"\u003EArithmetic\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery length field, and the type-in box while you draw, also takes a calculation with \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E and brackets: \u003Ccode\u003E4+7/16\u003C/code\u003E (4.4375 in the default unit), \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot; + 3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E(24&#039;-0&quot;)/3\u003C/code\u003E (8 feet), \u003Ccode\u003E2*450mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA dash written into a length is still part of it: \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E is 24 feet 6 inches and \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2\u003C/code\u003E is 6½. Put spaces round a dash to subtract: \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039; - 6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E is 23&#039;-6&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA plain number multiplies or divides a length as a number; added to a length it is in the default unit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eabs\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eround\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eceil\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Efloor\u003C/code\u003E work too. A calculation that cannot be read is refused with where it went wrong.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-lengths-are-shown\"\u003EHow lengths are shown\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths show as feet, inches and sixteenths: \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0&#039;-0 3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. Values round to the nearest 1/16 inch for display; the quantities themselves are not rounded. Decimal values show with two decimals and thousands separators, like \u003Ccode\u003E1,234.50 LF\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rates-material-and-labor-per-unit\"\u003ERates (material and labor per unit)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12.5\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E$12.50\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E$12.50\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E$12.50\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1,250\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E$1,250.00\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E0.385\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E$0.385 (up to four decimals; more are rounded)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(empty)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E$0\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMessage: &quot;Type an amount like 12.50.&quot; or &quot;That amount is too large.&quot; Negative rates are not accepted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExtended amounts (material, labor, totals) are worked out to the cent: quantity with waste × rate, rounded half away from zero, exactly like a spreadsheet&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003EROUND(x, 2)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"waste-percent\"\u003EWaste percent\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E7.5%\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E7.5%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(empty)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUp to two decimals, from 0% to 1000%. Messages: &quot;Type a percentage like 10 or 7.5.&quot; or &quot;Waste above 1000% is not allowed.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"names-and-units\"\u003ENames and units\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EItem and component names: up to 200 characters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA component&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E: any text up to 20 characters, like \u003Ccode\u003EEA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esheets\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECY\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egal\u003C/code\u003E. It is a label only; TakeoffCraft does not convert it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"formulas\"\u003EFormulas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssembly formulas have their own rules: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/formulas\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/formulas\"\u003EFormula language\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Lengths Used by Real distance in Calibrate, and by Height and Depth in the item editor. You type It means 24'-0\" 24 feet 24'-6 1/2\" 24 feet 6½ inches 12' 6 1/2\" 12 feet 6½ inches (a space instead of the dash) 12'-6-1/2\" 12 feet 6½ inches (dashes between the parts) 24' 24 feet 30\" 30 inches 6 1/2\" 6½ inches 3/4\" ¾ inch 24 ft 6 in 24 feet 6 inches (also foot, feet, inch, inches) 24.5 24.5 in the default unit (see below) 24.5' 24.5 feet 7.5 m 7.5 meters 750 cm 750 centimeters 7500 mm 7,500 millimeters A plain number is in feet for calibration and for items in LF, SF or SY, and in meters for items in m or m². Curly quotes and the prime marks from word processors (′ ″ ’ ”) work like ' and \". Two apostrophes '' count as \". Commas are ignored: 1,200 mm is 1200 mm. Fractions need whole numbers on both sides: 6 1/2\" works, 6.5 1/2\" does not. Zero and negative distances are refused. Messages: \"Type a distance, like 24'-0\" or 7.5 m.\" (the field is empty) \"Type a distance greater than zero.\" \"Use feet and inches (24'-6 1/2\"), decimal feet, m, cm or mm.\" (an unknown unit) \"That distance could not be read. Try 24'-6 1/2\" or 24.5.\" To clear an item's height or depth, empty the field. Arithmetic Every length field, and the type-in box while you draw, also takes a calculation with +, -, *, / and brackets: 4+7/16 (4.4375 in the default unit), 12'6\" + 3/4\", (24'-0\")/3 (8 feet), 2*450mm. A dash written into a length is still part of it: 24'-6\" is 24 feet 6 inches and 6-1/2 is 6½. Put spaces round a dash to subtract: 24' - 6\" is 23'-6\". A plain number multiplies or divides a length as a number; added to a length it is in the default unit. abs, round, ceil and floor work too. A calculation that cannot be read is refused with where it went wrong. How lengths are shown Lengths show as feet, inches and sixteenths: 12'-6 1/2\", 0'-0 3/4\". Values round to the nearest 1/16 inch for display; the quantities themselves are not rounded. Decimal values show with two decimals and thousands separators, like 1,234.50 LF. Rates (material and labor per unit) You type It means 12.5 $12.50 $12.50 $12.50 1,250 $1,250.00 0.385 $0.385 (up to four decimals; more are rounded) (empty) $0 Message: \"Type an amount like 12.50.\" or \"That amount is too large.\" Negative rates are not accepted. Extended amounts (material, labor, totals) are worked out to the cent: quantity with waste × rate, rounded half away from zero, exactly like a spreadsheet's ROUND(x, 2). Waste percent You type It means 10 10% 7.5% 7.5% (empty) 0% Up to two decimals, from 0% to 1000%. Messages: \"Type a percentage like 10 or 7.5.\" or \"Waste above 1000% is not allowed.\" Names and units Item and component names: up to 200 characters. A component's Unit: any text up to 20 characters, like EA, sheets, CY, gal. It is a label only; TakeoffCraft does not convert it. Formulas Assembly formulas have their own rules: see Formula language.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"scale-presets","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Scale presets","summary":"Every preset in the Preset list - architectural, engineering and metric - and what each assumes about the PDF.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["scale","presets","architectural scale","engineering scale","metric scale","ratio","1/4 inch","1/8 inch","1:100","full size"],"headings":[{"id":"architectural","text":"Architectural","level":2},{"id":"engineering","text":"Engineering","level":2},{"id":"metric","text":"Metric","level":2},{"id":"calibrated-scales","text":"Calibrated scales","level":2},{"id":"unusual-pdfs","text":"Unusual PDFs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E group has 30 scales in three groups. A preset sets the open sheet&#039;s scale exactly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery preset assumes the PDF page is the drawing&#039;s real paper size: one inch on the PDF page is one inch of paper (PDF pages measure 72 points per inch). If the PDF was resized, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E after setting any scale. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"architectural\"\u003EArchitectural\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPreset\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOne paper inch is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/16&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E16 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3/32&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10 ft 8 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3/16&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E5 ft 4 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 ft 8 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1 ft 4 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"engineering\"\u003EEngineering\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPreset\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOne paper inch is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 20&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 30&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E30 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 40&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E40 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 50&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E50 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 60&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E60 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1&quot; = 100&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E100 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"metric\"\u003EMetric\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1:1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:10\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:20\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:25\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:50\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:200\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:250\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:500\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1:1000\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA metric ratio means one unit on paper is that many units in reality: at \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E, 10 mm on the page is 1 m.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"calibrated-scales\"\u003ECalibrated scales\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA calibrated scale shows as &quot;Calibrated 1&quot; = 4.002&#039; on 24&#039;-6&quot;&quot; in the bar under the sheet: real feet per paper inch, and the distance you typed. When it is within 0.5% of a preset, the bar adds &quot;(matches 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;)&quot; and the sheets list shows the preset&#039;s name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"unusual-pdfs\"\u003EUnusual PDFs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome PDFs declare a larger page unit (a &quot;user unit&quot;); TakeoffCraft takes it into account for presets and for the scale shown. Pages rotated in the PDF are measured the same way as upright ones.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Preset list in the Scale group has 30 scales in three groups. A preset sets the open sheet's scale exactly. Every preset assumes the PDF page is the drawing's real paper size: one inch on the PDF page is one inch of paper (PDF pages measure 72 points per inch). If the PDF was resized, use Calibrate. Use Check after setting any scale. See Set the scale. Architectural Preset One paper inch is 1/16\" = 1'-0\" 16 ft 3/32\" = 1'-0\" 10 ft 8 in 1/8\" = 1'-0\" 8 ft 3/16\" = 1'-0\" 5 ft 4 in 1/4\" = 1'-0\" 4 ft 3/8\" = 1'-0\" 2 ft 8 in 1/2\" = 1'-0\" 2 ft 3/4\" = 1'-0\" 1 ft 4 in 1\" = 1'-0\" 1 ft 1 1/2\" = 1'-0\" 8 in 3\" = 1'-0\" 4 in Engineering Preset One paper inch is 1\" = 10' 10 ft 1\" = 20' 20 ft 1\" = 30' 30 ft 1\" = 40' 40 ft 1\" = 50' 50 ft 1\" = 60' 60 ft 1\" = 100' 100 ft Metric 1:1, 1:2, 1:5, 1:10, 1:20, 1:25, 1:50, 1:100, 1:200, 1:250, 1:500, 1:1000. A metric ratio means one unit on paper is that many units in reality: at 1:100, 10 mm on the page is 1 m. Calibrated scales A calibrated scale shows as \"Calibrated 1\" = 4.002' on 24'-6\"\" in the bar under the sheet: real feet per paper inch, and the distance you typed. When it is within 0.5% of a preset, the bar adds \"(matches 1/4\" = 1'-0\")\" and the sheets list shows the preset's name. Unusual PDFs Some PDFs declare a larger page unit (a \"user unit\"); TakeoffCraft takes it into account for presets and for the scale shown. Pages rotated in the PDF are measured the same way as upright ones.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"formulas","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Formula language","summary":"The rules for assembly quantity formulas - variables, operators, functions, rounding and every error message.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["formula","expression","syntax","functions","ceil","floor","round","min","max","abs","variables","operators","calculation"],"headings":[{"id":"variables","text":"Variables","level":2},{"id":"operators","text":"Operators","level":2},{"id":"functions","text":"Functions","level":2},{"id":"errors","text":"Errors","level":2},{"id":"safe-by-design","text":"Safe by design","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAssembly components get their quantity from a formula. A formula is a small arithmetic expression over the item&#039;s measured values. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/assemblies\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies and formulas\u003C/a\u003E for worked examples.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"variables\"\u003EVariables\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVariable\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s height\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall area, L × H\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber of runs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal net area (outlines minus cut-outs)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal perimeter, including cut-out edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s depth or thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber of areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe count\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVariables are in the item&#039;s units: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/units\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/units\"\u003EUnits\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUpper and lower case are the same: \u003Ccode\u003El\u003C/code\u003E is \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly the variables of the item&#039;s own type work. \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E on a linear item gives \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;P&quot; is not a measured value here. Use L, H, A, N.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn empty height or depth is 0.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"operators\"\u003EOperators\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWrite\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E−\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESubtract, or a negative value\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E×\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMultiply\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E÷\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDivide\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E( )\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGroup\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMultiply and divide go before add and subtract: \u003Ccode\u003EL * 2 + 4\u003C/code\u003E is (L × 2) + 4. Use parentheses when in doubt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENumbers can have decimals (\u003Ccode\u003E1.333\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.5\u003C/code\u003E) and exponents (\u003Ccode\u003E1e3\u003C/code\u003E = 1000). Spaces are ignored.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"functions\"\u003EFunctions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFunction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExample\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(x)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound up to a whole number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil(67.5)\u003C/code\u003E = 68\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efloor(x)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound down to a whole number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efloor(67.5)\u003C/code\u003E = 67\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eround(x)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound to the nearest whole number, halves away from zero\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eround(2.5)\u003C/code\u003E = 3\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eround(x, d)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound to d decimals (0 to 10)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eround(3.14159, 2)\u003C/code\u003E = 3.14\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emin(a, b, …)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe smallest value\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emin(N, 10)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emax(a, b, …)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe largest value\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emax(1, ceil(N / 12))\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eabs(x)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe value without its sign\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eabs(-3)\u003C/code\u003E = 3\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Efloor\u003C/code\u003E ignore tiny computer rounding noise, so a value that should be exactly 1 is never rounded up to 2.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"errors\"\u003EErrors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA formula that cannot be read or worked out shows its error under the field, and the component is left out of the totals (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E header says &quot;Incomplete&quot;). Messages:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a formula, like L or ceil(L / 1.333) + 1.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe formula is empty.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;X&quot; is not a measured value here. Use …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse one of the listed variables.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;X&quot; is not a function here. Use ceil, floor, round, min, max, abs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse one of those functions.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;#&quot; is not allowed in a formula.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove that character (units, \u003Ccode\u003E%\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E$\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E are not allowed).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA &quot;)&quot; is missing. / There is a &quot;)&quot; without its &quot;(&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBalance the parentheses.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA value is missing before &quot;)&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESomething like \u003Ccode\u003Emax(1, )\u003C/code\u003E: fill in the value.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe formula ends too soon.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESomething like \u003Ccode\u003EL *\u003C/code\u003E: finish it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnexpected &quot;…&quot;. Is an operator missing?\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo values side by side, like \u003Ccode\u003E2L\u003C/code\u003E: write \u003Ccode\u003E2 * L\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eceil() takes 1 value.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive the function the right number of values.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eround(x, digits) needs whole digits from 0 to 10.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a whole number of decimals.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe formula divides by zero.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUsually H is 0: set the item&#039;s height or depth.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe formula does not give a number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe result is too large to be a number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat formula is too long (500 characters at most).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShorten it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"safe-by-design\"\u003ESafe by design\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA formula can only do arithmetic with the variables and functions above. It cannot run code or read anything else, so a formula in a project file someone sends you is harmless.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Assembly components get their quantity from a formula. A formula is a small arithmetic expression over the item's measured values. See Assemblies and formulas for worked examples. Variables Item type Variable Meaning Linear L Total length Linear H The item's height Linear A Wall area, L × H Linear N Number of runs Area A Total net area (outlines minus cut-outs) Area P Total perimeter, including cut-out edges Area H The item's depth or thickness Area N Number of areas Count N The count Variables are in the item's units: see Units. Upper and lower case are the same: l is L. Only the variables of the item's own type work. P on a linear item gives \"P\" is not a measured value here. Use L, H, A, N. An empty height or depth is 0. Operators Write Meaning + Add - or − Subtract, or a negative value * or × Multiply / or ÷ Divide ( ) Group Multiply and divide go before add and subtract: L * 2 + 4 is (L × 2) + 4. Use parentheses when in doubt. Numbers can have decimals (1.333, .5) and exponents (1e3 = 1000). Spaces are ignored. Functions Function Result Example ceil(x) Round up to a whole number ceil(67.5) = 68 floor(x) Round down to a whole number floor(67.5) = 67 round(x) Round to the nearest whole number, halves away from zero round(2.5) = 3 round(x, d) Round to d decimals (0 to 10) round(3.14159, 2) = 3.14 min(a, b, …) The smallest value min(N, 10) max(a, b, …) The largest value max(1, ceil(N / 12)) abs(x) The value without its sign abs(-3) = 3 ceil and floor ignore tiny computer rounding noise, so a value that should be exactly 1 is never rounded up to 2. Errors A formula that cannot be read or worked out shows its error under the field, and the component is left out of the totals (the Quantities header says \"Incomplete\"). Messages: Message Fix Type a formula, like L or ceil(L / 1.333) + 1. The formula is empty. \"X\" is not a measured value here. Use … Use one of the listed variables. \"X\" is not a function here. Use ceil, floor, round, min, max, abs. Use one of those functions. \"#\" is not allowed in a formula. Remove that character (units, %, $ and = are not allowed). A \")\" is missing. / There is a \")\" without its \"(\". Balance the parentheses. A value is missing before \")\". Something like max(1, ): fill in the value. The formula ends too soon. Something like L *: finish it. Unexpected \"…\". Is an operator missing? Two values side by side, like 2L: write 2 * L. ceil() takes 1 value. Give the function the right number of values. round(x, digits) needs whole digits from 0 to 10. Use a whole number of decimals. The formula divides by zero. Usually H is 0: set the item's height or depth. The formula does not give a number. The result is too large to be a number. That formula is too long (500 characters at most). Shorten it. Safe by design A formula can only do arithmetic with the variables and functions above. It cannot run code or read anything else, so a formula in a project file someone sends you is harmless.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"units","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Units","summary":"The units an item can report in, the units its formula variables use, and how imperial and metric mix.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["units","lf","sf","sy","sq","square yards","roofing squares","acres","hectares","meters","square meters","ea","each","metric","imperial","conversion","default unit"],"headings":[{"id":"item-units","text":"Item units","level":2},{"id":"units-of-the-formula-variables","text":"Units of the formula variables","level":2},{"id":"where-units-are-fixed","text":"Where units are fixed","level":2},{"id":"scales-and-units-are-separate","text":"Scales and units are separate","level":2},{"id":"precision","text":"Precision","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"item-units\"\u003EItem units\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUnit\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EName\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Elinear feet (default)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare feet (default)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare yards\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESQ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eroofing squares (100 square feet)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eac\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eacres\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em²\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare meters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eha\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ehectares\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEA\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eeach\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E in the item editor at any time, even after measuring; the quantity is converted at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnit\u003C/span\u003E list shows the units turned on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E, and always the item&#039;s own unit. A new item starts in the length or area unit starred there, when it is one of the item&#039;s units; otherwise in LF or SF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/unit-lists\" data-topic=\"basics/unit-lists\"\u003EUnits, typed values and the unit lists\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"units-of-the-formula-variables\"\u003EUnits of the formula variables\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem unit\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EL, P, H\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EA\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare feet (wall area)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare meters (wall area)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare yards\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESQ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquares\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eac\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eacres\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em²\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare meters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eha\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ehectares\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor SY items, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E is in square yards while \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E stay in feet. A volume from an SY item is \u003Ccode\u003EA * 9 * H / 27\u003C/code\u003E cubic yards; using SF for slabs is simpler.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-units-are-fixed\"\u003EWhere units are fixed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome readouts always use feet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe live readout in the status bar while drawing (feet-inches, SF),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESelected mark\u003C/strong\u003E (feet-inches, LF and SF),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe check result (feet-inches, with decimal feet and meters),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Esegment lengths and perimeters in the spreadsheet&#039;s Marks worksheet (feet-inches).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EItem totals, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table, formulas and the Estimate worksheet use the item&#039;s own unit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scales-and-units-are-separate\"\u003EScales and units are separate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sheet&#039;s scale says how big the drawing is; an item&#039;s unit says how to report it. A metric drawing at \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E can be taken off in LF, and an imperial drawing in m². Calibrating accepts any unit: \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E7.5 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E7500 mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"precision\"\u003EPrecision\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft measures exactly from the points you click, including true arc lengths and areas, and does not round quantities. Display rounds lengths to 1/16 inch and decimals to two places. Money is rounded only at the extended amounts, to the cent.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Item units Item type Unit Name Linear LF linear feet (default) Linear m meters Area SF square feet (default) Area SY square yards Area SQ roofing squares (100 square feet) Area ac acres Area m² square meters Area ha hectares Count EA each Change it with Unit in the item editor at any time, even after measuring; the quantity is converted at once. The Unit list shows the units turned on in Settings › General › Units, and always the item's own unit. A new item starts in the length or area unit starred there, when it is one of the item's units; otherwise in LF or SF. See Units, typed values and the unit lists. Units of the formula variables Item unit L, P, H A LF feet square feet (wall area) m meters square meters (wall area) SF feet square feet SY feet square yards SQ feet squares ac feet acres m² meters square meters ha meters hectares For SY items, A is in square yards while P and H stay in feet. A volume from an SY item is A * 9 * H / 27 cubic yards; using SF for slabs is simpler. Where units are fixed Some readouts always use feet: the live readout in the status bar while drawing (feet-inches, SF), Selected mark (feet-inches, LF and SF), the check result (feet-inches, with decimal feet and meters), segment lengths and perimeters in the spreadsheet's Marks worksheet (feet-inches). Item totals, the Quantities table, formulas and the Estimate worksheet use the item's own unit. Scales and units are separate A sheet's scale says how big the drawing is; an item's unit says how to report it. A metric drawing at 1:100 can be taken off in LF, and an imperial drawing in m². Calibrating accepts any unit: 24'-6\", 7.5 m, 7500 mm. Precision TakeoffCraft measures exactly from the points you click, including true arc lengths and areas, and does not round quantities. Display rounds lengths to 1/16 inch and decimals to two places. Money is rounded only at the extended amounts, to the cent.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"export-columns","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Export columns","summary":"Every worksheet and column of the XLSX export and the CSV, with the formulas the Estimate worksheet carries.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["export","columns","xlsx","csv","worksheet","estimate sheet","by sheet","marks","scales","formulas","fields"],"headings":[{"id":"estimate-xlsx-worksheet-and-csv","text":"Estimate (XLSX worksheet and CSV)","level":2},{"id":"by-sheet-xlsx-only","text":"By sheet (XLSX only)","level":2},{"id":"marks-xlsx-only","text":"Marks (XLSX only)","level":2},{"id":"by-bid-area-xlsx-only-when-the-takeoff-has-bid-areas","text":"By bid area (XLSX only, when the takeoff has bid areas)","level":2},{"id":"scales-xlsx-only","text":"Scales (XLSX only)","level":2},{"id":"file-names","text":"File names","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"estimate-xlsx-worksheet-and-csv\"\u003EEstimate (XLSX worksheet and CSV)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne row per item, followed by a row per component of its assembly (indented). Then an empty row and the job total.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EContents\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the XLSX\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA Item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem name (bold) or component name (indented)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EB Type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear, Area, Count or Component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC Unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF, m, SF, SY, m², EA, or the component&#039;s unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ED Quantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasured total, or the formula result\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EE Waste %\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWaste\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPercent (0.10 = 10%)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF Qty with waste\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity × (1 + waste)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula \u003Ccode\u003ED*(1+E)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EG Material per unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial rate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH Labor per unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabor rate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EI Material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtended material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula \u003Ccode\u003EROUND(F*G,2)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJ Labor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtended labor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula \u003Ccode\u003EROUND(F*H,2)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EK Total\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial + labor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula \u003Ccode\u003EI+J\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EL Notes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula used, formula error, marks on sheets with no scale, wall area, perimeter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EJob total row: \u003Ccode\u003ESUM\u003C/code\u003E of columns I, J and K over every row above, and &quot;Incomplete: see the notes above&quot; in Notes when something was left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA row with a formula error has no quantity or amounts; its note gives the error. The header row is frozen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the CSV, the same columns hold plain values, Waste % is a number of percent (10 for 10%), and money is in dollars with two decimals. Text that a spreadsheet might take for a formula (starting with \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E) gets a leading \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E so it stays text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-sheet-xlsx-only\"\u003EBy sheet (XLSX only)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EContents\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet name (with the PDF name when it is not open)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat item&#039;s quantity on that sheet, in its unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber of marks\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"marks-xlsx-only\"\u003EMarks (XLSX only)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne row per mark.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EContents\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear, Area or Count\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength, net area, or 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELF or m; SF, SY or m²; EA\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDetail\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear: the segment lengths, deducted ones marked &quot;(deduct)&quot;, and &quot;deducts (length) of (length) drawn&quot;. Area: gross and cut-out areas when there are cut-outs, and the perimeter. &quot;Sheet has no scale&quot; for unscaled marks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBid area\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly when the takeoff has bid areas: the mark&#039;s area, or &quot;No bid area&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"by-bid-area-xlsx-only-when-the-takeoff-has-bid-areas\"\u003EBy bid area (XLSX only, when the takeoff has bid areas)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBid area\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe area&#039;s name, or &quot;No bid area&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EItem\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item, or an indented component under it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item&#039;s or component&#039;s unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasured in that area (components: the formula on the area&#039;s totals)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQty with waste\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity × (1 + waste)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial, Labor, Total\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtended to the cent, as on Estimate\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENotes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula, errors and unscaled marks, as on Estimate\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach area ends with a &quot;(name) subtotal&quot; row whose costs are \u003Ccode\u003ESUM\u003C/code\u003E formulas over its rows; the last row, \u003Cstrong\u003EAll bid areas\u003C/strong\u003E, adds the subtotals. The \u003Cstrong\u003EMarks\u003C/strong\u003E worksheet then has a seventh column, \u003Cstrong\u003EBid area\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scales-xlsx-only\"\u003EScales (XLSX only)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne row per sheet that has a scale or marks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EContents\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScale\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe preset&#039;s name, or the calibrated scale, or &quot;No scale&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Preset&quot;, or &quot;Calibrated on (the distance you typed)&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeet per PDF unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe exact scale factor TakeoffCraft uses\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"file-names\"\u003EFile names\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(name).xlsx\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E(name).csv\u003C/code\u003E, where the name comes from the first PDF opened in the takeoff.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe CSV is UTF-8 with a byte order mark and Windows line endings, which spreadsheet programs on every platform read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Estimate (XLSX worksheet and CSV) One row per item, followed by a row per component of its assembly (indented). Then an empty row and the job total. Column Contents In the XLSX A Item Item name (bold) or component name (indented) Text B Type Linear, Area, Count or Component Text C Unit LF, m, SF, SY, m², EA, or the component's unit Text D Quantity Measured total, or the formula result Number E Waste % Waste Percent (0.10 = 10%) F Qty with waste Quantity × (1 + waste) Formula D*(1+E) G Material per unit Material rate Number H Labor per unit Labor rate Number I Material Extended material Formula ROUND(F*G,2) J Labor Extended labor Formula ROUND(F*H,2) K Total Material + labor Formula I+J L Notes Formula used, formula error, marks on sheets with no scale, wall area, perimeter Text Job total row: SUM of columns I, J and K over every row above, and \"Incomplete: see the notes above\" in Notes when something was left out. A row with a formula error has no quantity or amounts; its note gives the error. The header row is frozen. In the CSV, the same columns hold plain values, Waste % is a number of percent (10 for 10%), and money is in dollars with two decimals. Text that a spreadsheet might take for a formula (starting with =, +, - or @) gets a leading ' so it stays text. By sheet (XLSX only) Column Contents Sheet Sheet name (with the PDF name when it is not open) Item Item name Quantity That item's quantity on that sheet, in its unit Unit The item's unit Marks Number of marks Marks (XLSX only) One row per mark. Column Contents Sheet Sheet name Item Item name Type Linear, Area or Count Quantity Length, net area, or 1 Unit LF or m; SF, SY or m²; EA Detail Linear: the segment lengths, deducted ones marked \"(deduct)\", and \"deducts (length) of (length) drawn\". Area: gross and cut-out areas when there are cut-outs, and the perimeter. \"Sheet has no scale\" for unscaled marks. Bid area Only when the takeoff has bid areas: the mark's area, or \"No bid area\" By bid area (XLSX only, when the takeoff has bid areas) Column What Bid area The area's name, or \"No bid area\" Item The item, or an indented component under it Unit The item's or component's unit Quantity Measured in that area (components: the formula on the area's totals) Qty with waste Quantity × (1 + waste) Material, Labor, Total Extended to the cent, as on Estimate Notes Formula, errors and unscaled marks, as on Estimate Each area ends with a \"(name) subtotal\" row whose costs are SUM formulas over its rows; the last row, All bid areas, adds the subtotals. The Marks worksheet then has a seventh column, Bid area. Scales (XLSX only) One row per sheet that has a scale or marks. Column Contents Sheet Sheet name Scale The preset's name, or the calibrated scale, or \"No scale\" How \"Preset\", or \"Calibrated on (the distance you typed)\" Feet per PDF unit The exact scale factor TakeoffCraft uses File names (name).xlsx and (name).csv, where the name comes from the first PDF opened in the takeoff. The CSV is UTF-8 with a byte order mark and Windows line endings, which spreadsheet programs on every platform read.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Symptoms, causes and fixes for the messages and surprises you may meet in TakeoffCraft, quoted as the app shows them.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problems","errors","not working","fix","help","refused","wont measure","no scale","missing marks","export failed","offline","touch"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-files","text":"Opening files","level":2},{"id":"measuring","text":"Measuring","level":2},{"id":"scale","text":"Scale","level":2},{"id":"quantities-and-formulas","text":"Quantities and formulas","level":2},{"id":"auto-count","text":"Auto count","level":2},{"id":"saving-and-exporting","text":"Saving and exporting","level":2},{"id":"keys-and-mouse","text":"Keys and mouse","level":2},{"id":"touch-screens","text":"Touch screens","level":2},{"id":"working-offline","text":"Working offline","level":2},{"id":"still-stuck","text":"Still stuck","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft explains most refusals in the status bar at the bottom of the window. Find the message below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-files\"\u003EOpening files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDF reader could not load. Reload the page to try again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe app&#039;s PDF reader did not load, usually because you are offline and the app was never saved for offline use.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReconnect and reload. Then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E and wait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(name) is password-protected. Open an unprotected copy.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDF needs a password to open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk for an unprotected copy, or remove the password in a PDF program you trust.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(name) could not be read as a PDF (…)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is damaged or not really a PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownload it again, or re-save it from the program that made it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(name) is already open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same PDF (same contents) is open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing to do.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft opens PDFs and .takeoff.json projects. Skipped: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOther file types were dropped.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConvert drawings to PDF first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing happens when I drop files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe files were dropped outside the browser window, or the browser opened the PDF in a new tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop onto the TakeoffCraft page itself; the &quot;Drop PDFs or a .takeoff.json&quot; frame shows when it will work.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening a big set takes a while\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery PDF is read in full to fingerprint it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWait for the &quot;Opening (name)…&quot; message to clear. Thumbnails fill in as you scroll.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measuring\"\u003EMeasuring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a PDF first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo sheet is open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis sheet has no scale yet, so it can&#039;t be measured. Pick a preset scale or calibrate first (counts work without one).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear, area, circle and cut-out tools need a scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/set-the-scale\"\u003ESet the scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake a linear item first (name it under Items), then click on the sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere is no item of that type.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFill in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew item\u003C/span\u003E form that opened, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick an item to take off first (Items panel).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe current item was deleted.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick an item under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA run needs at least two points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou finished after one click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick at least two points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn area needs at least three points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EToo few corners.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick at least three corners.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe outline crosses itself, so its area would be wrong. Draw it again without crossing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA figure-eight shape.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw it as two areas, or go round the edge in order.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThose points enclose no area.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAll the points are on one line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick corners that enclose a space.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect an area mark first (Select tool), then choose Cut-out and draw the hole inside it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut-out\u003C/span\u003E needs a selected area.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click the area, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA cut-out has to sit fully inside the area.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe hole crosses or leaves the outline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the hole inside the area.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut-outs cannot overlap each other.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo holes overlap.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw one hole round both, or keep them apart.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E… The point was put back.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA dragged corner would make the outline cross itself or push a hole outside.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag to a place that keeps the shape valid.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a point on the circle&#039;s edge, away from its center.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe second click was too close to the center.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick further out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClicks pan the sheet instead of adding points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe pointer moved more than a few pixels while the button was down; that counts as a drag.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick without moving, especially on a trackpad.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShift does not snap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou are placing a point on an arc, or drawing a circle.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe 45° snap applies to straight segments only.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints jump onto a nearby line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnap to the drawing&#039;s lines is on.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in to click between lines, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn Snap off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo snap marker appears\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnap is off, the sheet is a scan, or the tool does not snap (Count, Auto count, pasting).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; on a scan there are no lines to snap to.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe length is wrong\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe scale is wrong (often a resized PDF with a preset).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E on a known dimension, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scale\"\u003EScale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick two points further apart.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe calibration points are almost on top of each other.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECalibrate on a longer dimension.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA message in the calibrate dialog, like &quot;That distance could not be read&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe distance is in a form TakeoffCraft cannot read.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/entry-formats\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/entry-formats\"\u003ELength, rate and percent entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis sheet has no scale yet, so there is nothing to check. Calibrate it, or pick a preset.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E on an unscaled sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet a scale first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E list is grayed out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo sheet is open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"quantities-and-formulas\"\u003EQuantities and formulas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIncomplete: … (not in the totals)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA formula error, or marks on sheets with no scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/read-quantities#the-incomplete-warning\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/read-quantities\" data-hash=\"the-incomplete-warning\"\u003ERead quantities and the estimate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E+(n) unscaled next to an item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome of its marks are on sheets with no scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive those sheets a scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormula error: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe formula cannot be worked out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/formulas#errors\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/formulas\" data-hash=\"errors\"\u003EFormula language\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis item has marks, so its type cannot change.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe type is locked once there are marks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake a new item of the other type and redraw the marks with it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn item needs a name.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name field was emptied.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a name.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType an amount like 12.50. / Type a percentage like 10 or 7.5.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA rate or waste could not be read.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/entry-formats\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/entry-formats\"\u003ELength, rate and percent entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA total looks doubled\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe item has its own rates and components that carry the same cost.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet the item&#039;s own rates to 0 when its components carry the cost.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis item&#039;s marks are on PDFs that are not open.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou clicked an item whose marks are all on PDFs you have not opened.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen those PDFs; their names are listed under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat box is too small. Draw it around the whole symbol.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe box is tiny.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in and box the whole symbol.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat box is almost empty. Draw it around a symbol.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe box holds only paper.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox the symbol itself.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo matches. Lower the match slider, or box the symbol more tightly.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing scored high enough.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox the symbol tightly, or drag the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch\u003C/span\u003E slider lower.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EToo many matches, including wrong symbols\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimilar symbols score close to the real one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERaise the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch\u003C/span\u003E slider and click wrong boxes to reject them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMatches missing on other sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThose sheets draw the symbol at another size, or at an angle that is not a 90° step.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESearch those sheets with a box drawn there, or count by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(n) sheets were too large to search at this symbol size.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet would be too big at the working size.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox a larger symbol, or count that sheet by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe search failed: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe background search stopped.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETry again; reload the page if it repeats (save first). Offline, it works only once the app bar shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDragging draws a box instead of panning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat is how \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/span\u003E works.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag, or drag with the middle button.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving-and-exporting\"\u003ESaving and exporting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing to export yet: make an item and take it off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere are no items.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake an item first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo marks on the open PDFs yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E has nothing to draw on the open PDFs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the PDFs that have marks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis PDF is password-protected or encrypted, so a marked copy cannot be written. …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDF is encrypted.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E instead, or use an unencrypted copy of the PDF (it then needs its own takeoff, since it is a different file).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe marked PDF could not be written: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOften: offline, and the app was not saved for offline use, so the PDF writer is missing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGo online and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E again. To avoid it, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E and wait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly one marked PDF downloaded\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser blocked several downloads at once.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAllow multiple downloads for this site, and export again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(file) is not a TakeoffCraft project (it is not valid JSON). / This is not a TakeoffCraft project file.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrong file, or a damaged one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis project was saved by a newer TakeoffCraft. Open it there.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file comes from a newer build.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it in the newer build.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(n) marks were damaged and left out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPart of the file was damaged or edited by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe rest opened. Redraw the missing marks, or open an older copy.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarks missing after reopening\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDFs are not open, or one of them changed.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen#when-a-pdf-has-changed\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/save-and-reopen\" data-hash=\"when-a-pdf-has-changed\"\u003ESave and reopen a takeoff\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser asks &quot;Leave site?&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere are unsaved changes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStay, and save with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-and-mouse\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESymptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELetter keys do nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe cursor is in a field (search, item editor, table).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick on the sheet, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E out of the field.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe wheel zooms instead of scrolling\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe wheel always zooms the sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag to pan.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole page got bigger or smaller\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBrowser zoom (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command +\"\u003E⌘+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl++\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command −\"\u003E⌘−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 0\"\u003E⌘0\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+0\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Zoom the sheet with the wheel or the bare \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA mark disappeared when I pressed Backspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace deletes the selected mark when you are not drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click does not finish on a Mac\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl-click arrives as a normal click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a two-finger click with secondary click turned on, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"touch-screens\"\u003ETouch screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETap\u003C/strong\u003E places a point (or selects a mark with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E). One finger dragging pans.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo fingers\u003C/strong\u003E pinch to zoom about the point between them, and pan when they move together. Putting a second finger down cancels what the first one started, so a pinch never leaves a point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFinish\u003C/strong\u003E a run or an outline with a \u003Cstrong\u003Elong press\u003C/strong\u003E (the right click), a \u003Cstrong\u003Edouble tap\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the bottom of the sheet. The same bar has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E (take the last point back) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E. Areas also close by tapping the first point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFingers get bigger targets: points snap, close and pick from farther away, and corner handles are larger.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETyping lengths needs a keyboard; the on-screen keyboard works when the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E box is focused.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a tablet, a keyboard and a mouse or trackpad make takeoff much easier.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"working-offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome of the app&#039;s files did not download while saving for offline use.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck your connection and choose the button again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrivate windows and some managed computers block offline copies.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a normal window, or another browser.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Saving…&quot; never starts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou are offline; saving needs a connection.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave while online.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe app is old after an update\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offline copy is from an earlier day.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnline, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou want the space back\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offline copy is about 6 MB.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the offline copy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-stuck\"\u003EStill stuck\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReload the page if the app stops responding. Your takeoff is lost on reload unless you saved it, so save first with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E if you can. For browser questions see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft explains most refusals in the status bar at the bottom of the window. Find the message below. Opening files Message or symptom Cause Fix The PDF reader could not load. Reload the page to try again. The app's PDF reader did not load, usually because you are offline and the app was never saved for offline use. Reconnect and reload. Then choose Save for offline and wait for Offline ready; see Working offline. (name) is password-protected. Open an unprotected copy. The PDF needs a password to open. Ask for an unprotected copy, or remove the password in a PDF program you trust. (name) could not be read as a PDF (…) The file is damaged or not really a PDF. Download it again, or re-save it from the program that made it. (name) is already open. The same PDF (same contents) is open. Nothing to do. TakeoffCraft opens PDFs and .takeoff.json projects. Skipped: … Other file types were dropped. Convert drawings to PDF first. Nothing happens when I drop files The files were dropped outside the browser window, or the browser opened the PDF in a new tab. Drop onto the TakeoffCraft page itself; the \"Drop PDFs or a .takeoff.json\" frame shows when it will work. Opening a big set takes a while Every PDF is read in full to fingerprint it. Wait for the \"Opening (name)…\" message to clear. Thumbnails fill in as you scroll. Measuring Message or symptom Cause Fix Open a PDF first. No sheet is open. Open a PDF. This sheet has no scale yet, so it can't be measured. Pick a preset scale or calibrate first (counts work without one). Linear, area, circle and cut-out tools need a scale. Set the scale. Make a linear item first (name it under Items), then click on the sheet. There is no item of that type. Fill in the New item form that opened, and choose Add. Pick an item to take off first (Items panel). The current item was deleted. Click an item under Items. A run needs at least two points. You finished after one click. Click at least two points. An area needs at least three points. Too few corners. Click at least three corners. The outline crosses itself, so its area would be wrong. Draw it again without crossing. A figure-eight shape. Draw it as two areas, or go round the edge in order. Those points enclose no area. All the points are on one line. Click corners that enclose a space. Select an area mark first (Select tool), then choose Cut-out and draw the hole inside it. Cut-out needs a selected area. Press V, click the area, then X. A cut-out has to sit fully inside the area. The hole crosses or leaves the outline. Draw the hole inside the area. Cut-outs cannot overlap each other. Two holes overlap. Draw one hole round both, or keep them apart. … The point was put back. A dragged corner would make the outline cross itself or push a hole outside. Drag to a place that keeps the shape valid. Click a point on the circle's edge, away from its center. The second click was too close to the center. Click further out. Clicks pan the sheet instead of adding points The pointer moved more than a few pixels while the button was down; that counts as a drag. Click without moving, especially on a trackpad. Shift does not snap You are placing a point on an arc, or drawing a circle. The 45° snap applies to straight segments only. Points jump onto a nearby line Snap to the drawing's lines is on. Zoom in to click between lines, or press F3 to turn Snap off. No snap marker appears Snap is off, the sheet is a scan, or the tool does not snap (Count, Auto count, pasting). Press F3; on a scan there are no lines to snap to. The length is wrong The scale is wrong (often a resized PDF with a preset). Use Check on a known dimension, then Calibrate. Scale Message or symptom Cause Fix Pick two points further apart. The calibration points are almost on top of each other. Calibrate on a longer dimension. A message in the calibrate dialog, like \"That distance could not be read\" The distance is in a form TakeoffCraft cannot read. See Length, rate and percent entry. This sheet has no scale yet, so there is nothing to check. Calibrate it, or pick a preset. Check on an unscaled sheet. Set a scale first. The Preset list is grayed out No sheet is open. Open a PDF. Quantities and formulas Message or symptom Cause Fix Incomplete: … (not in the totals) A formula error, or marks on sheets with no scale. See Read quantities and the estimate. +(n) unscaled next to an item Some of its marks are on sheets with no scale. Give those sheets a scale. Formula error: … The formula cannot be worked out. See Formula language. This item has marks, so its type cannot change. The type is locked once there are marks. Make a new item of the other type and redraw the marks with it. An item needs a name. The name field was emptied. Type a name. Type an amount like 12.50. / Type a percentage like 10 or 7.5. A rate or waste could not be read. See Length, rate and percent entry. A total looks doubled The item has its own rates and components that carry the same cost. Set the item's own rates to 0 when its components carry the cost. This item's marks are on PDFs that are not open. You clicked an item whose marks are all on PDFs you have not opened. Open those PDFs; their names are listed under Sheets. Auto count Message or symptom Cause Fix That box is too small. Draw it around the whole symbol. The box is tiny. Zoom in and box the whole symbol. That box is almost empty. Draw it around a symbol. The box holds only paper. Box the symbol itself. No matches. Lower the match slider, or box the symbol more tightly. Nothing scored high enough. Box the symbol tightly, or drag the Match slider lower. Too many matches, including wrong symbols Similar symbols score close to the real one. Raise the Match slider and click wrong boxes to reject them. Matches missing on other sheets Those sheets draw the symbol at another size, or at an angle that is not a 90° step. Search those sheets with a box drawn there, or count by hand. (n) sheets were too large to search at this symbol size. The sheet would be too big at the working size. Box a larger symbol, or count that sheet by hand. The search failed: … The background search stopped. Try again; reload the page if it repeats (save first). Offline, it works only once the app bar shows Offline ready. Dragging draws a box instead of panning That is how Auto count works. Hold Space and drag, or drag with the middle button. Saving and exporting Message or symptom Cause Fix Nothing to export yet: make an item and take it off. There are no items. Make an item first. No marks on the open PDFs yet. Marked PDF has nothing to draw on the open PDFs. Open the PDFs that have marks. This PDF is password-protected or encrypted, so a marked copy cannot be written. … The PDF is encrypted. Export XLSX instead, or use an unencrypted copy of the PDF (it then needs its own takeoff, since it is a different file). The marked PDF could not be written: … Often: offline, and the app was not saved for offline use, so the PDF writer is missing. Go online and choose Marked PDF again. To avoid it, choose Save for offline and wait for Offline ready. Only one marked PDF downloaded The browser blocked several downloads at once. Allow multiple downloads for this site, and export again. (file) is not a TakeoffCraft project (it is not valid JSON). / This is not a TakeoffCraft project file. Wrong file, or a damaged one. Pick the .takeoff.json file. This project was saved by a newer TakeoffCraft. Open it there. The file comes from a newer build. Open it in the newer build. (n) marks were damaged and left out. Part of the file was damaged or edited by hand. The rest opened. Redraw the missing marks, or open an older copy. Marks missing after reopening The PDFs are not open, or one of them changed. See Save and reopen a takeoff. The browser asks \"Leave site?\" There are unsaved changes. Stay, and save with Ctrl+S. Keys and mouse Symptom Cause Fix Letter keys do nothing The cursor is in a field (search, item editor, table). Click on the sheet, or press Tab out of the field. The wheel zooms instead of scrolling The wheel always zooms the sheet. Drag to pan. The whole page got bigger or smaller Browser zoom (Ctrl++ or Ctrl+−). Press Ctrl+0. Zoom the sheet with the wheel or the bare + and −. A mark disappeared when I pressed Backspace Backspace deletes the selected mark when you are not drawing. Ctrl+Z brings it back. Right-click does not finish on a Mac Control-click arrives as a normal click. Use a two-finger click with secondary click turned on, or press Enter. Touch screens On a touch screen: Tap places a point (or selects a mark with Select). One finger dragging pans. Two fingers pinch to zoom about the point between them, and pan when they move together. Putting a second finger down cancels what the first one started, so a pinch never leaves a point. Finish a run or an outline with a long press (the right click), a double tap, or Finish in the bar at the bottom of the sheet. The same bar has Back (take the last point back) and Cancel. Areas also close by tapping the first point. Fingers get bigger targets: points snap, close and pick from farther away, and corner handles are larger. Typing lengths needs a keyboard; the on-screen keyboard works when the Type box is focused. On a tablet, a keyboard and a mouse or trackpad make takeoff much easier. Working offline Button or symptom Cause Fix Could not save Some of the app's files did not download while saving for offline use. Check your connection and choose the button again. Offline not available Private windows and some managed computers block offline copies. Use a normal window, or another browser. \"Saving…\" never starts You are offline; saving needs a connection. Save while online. The app is old after an update The offline copy is from an earlier day. Online, choose Offline ready, then Save again (get the newest version). You want the space back The offline copy is about 6 MB. Choose Offline ready, then Remove the offline copy. See Working offline. Still stuck Reload the page if the app stops responding. Your takeoff is lost on reload unless you saved it, so save first with Ctrl+S if you can. For browser questions see Browsers and computers.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"takeoffcraft","title":"What is not built yet","summary":"Takeoff features this build does not have, and what to do instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["limits","missing","not yet","roadmap","planned","workaround","per run","cloud","desktop"],"headings":[{"id":"measuring","text":"Measuring","level":2},{"id":"items-and-pricing","text":"Items and pricing","level":2},{"id":"drawings-dwg-and-dxf","text":"Drawings (DWG and DXF)","level":2},{"id":"auto-count","text":"Auto count","level":2},{"id":"files-and-sharing","text":"Files and sharing","level":2},{"id":"screen-and-input","text":"Screen and input","level":2},{"id":"3d-view","text":"3D view","level":2},{"id":"bidding","text":"Bidding","level":2},{"id":"panels","text":"Panels","level":2},{"id":"spreadsheets","text":"Spreadsheets","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft is in development. This list is what the current build does not do, and how to get the job done anyway.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measuring\"\u003EMeasuring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInserting a point in the middle of a run or outline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the mark and draw it again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox (window) selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click each mark, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for every mark on the sheet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate marks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERotating, mirroring or rescaling a pasted copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste on a sheet at the same scale and in the same orientation; draw the rest by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReading a scale from a scanned sheet, or from a graphic scale bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick the preset, or calibrate on the bar&#039;s length.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMore than one scale per sheet (detail areas, enlarged plans)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure the part at the sheet&#039;s scale, and take off the detail on its own enlarged sheet if the set has one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"items-and-pricing\"\u003EItems and pricing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly formulas that use an item&#039;s other kinds of marks (the doors of a room item in its floor&#039;s formulas)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormulas use the main type&#039;s totals; price the other kinds on their own rows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly formulas worked out run by run\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormulas use the item&#039;s totals; use \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E for per-run extras.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormulas that use another item&#039;s quantities\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERepeat the measurement in that item, or work it out in the spreadsheet export.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECrews on assembly components, or a working day other than 8 hours\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive the crew to the item; read crew-days for another day length as crew-hours ÷ your day.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkups other than notes, clouds and highlights (arrows, dimensions, stamps), or replies on a note\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrite it in the note&#039;s text; change its status as it is settled.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA price library shared by a team, or kept with your account\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe library is kept in this browser: export it as a CSV and import it on the other computer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrade packs reviewed by working estimators\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery pack is a starter: check its assumptions against your job and your products before you price with it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/trade-packs\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/trade-packs\"\u003ETrade packs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrices in trade packs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPacks bring assemblies only: set your own rates, or import your price list as a CSV.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERenaming the takeoff\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name comes from the first PDF; rename the downloaded files.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENaming sheets by hand\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheets are named from the PDF&#039;s page labels; search by file name and page.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings-dwg-and-dxf\"\u003EDrawings (DWG and DXF)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot style tables, pen assignments and CTB files on a drawing&#039;s sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA drawing is drawn black, or in its colors darkened for paper; line weights come from the drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening a binary DXF, or a DWG older than AutoCAD 2000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave it as an ASCII DXF or a newer DWG.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinding a linked drawing (xref) by the folder it sits in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the linked drawing&#039;s file too, with the host: it is found by file name among the open files.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasuring 3D polylines, meshes, solids and regions\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount them by layer and type, or measure the plan view with the Linear and Area tools.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText in a marked PDF from a drawing in scripts other than Latin\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECharacters Helvetica has no glyph for print as &quot;?&quot; in the marked PDF; the drawing on screen is unaffected.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA hand-over of a drawing to another app from the sheet bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the same file in that app.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA drawing that carries the takeoff inside it, as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/span\u003E does for PDFs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave takeoff\u003C/span\u003E: the \u003Ccode\u003E.takeoff.json\u003C/code\u003E reopens on the same drawing, found by its contents.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing or counting PDF underlays, images and OLE objects placed in a drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImages show as a frame only. Open the underlay PDF itself as a sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"auto-count\"\u003EAuto count\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinding symbols on a sheet with no scale drawn at another size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive the sheet its scale, or search it with a box drawn there.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinding mirrored symbols\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount those by hand, or search again with a box around a mirrored one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-and-sharing\"\u003EFiles and sharing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne PDF that both shows the marks and carries the takeoff\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarked PDF\u003C/span\u003E for people to see the marks; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF with the takeoff inside\u003C/span\u003E to reopen in TakeoffCraft.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA CSV of quantities by sheet or of the marks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E: its By sheet and Marks worksheets have them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available yet. Save project files to your computer and back them up.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA separate TakeoffCraft desktop app\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom 0.5 the desktop app is CadCraft Studio, with all eight apps in one install. TakeoffCraft also runs in the browser.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"screen-and-input\"\u003EScreen and input\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA right-click (or long-press) menu\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse the toolbar, the panels and the keys. A long press finishes a run or an outline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERotating the view\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPages rotated in the PDF already show upright.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-view\"\u003E3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing or editing marks in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGo back to the sheet (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) to draw; select and delete work in 3D.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELining up stacked floors plotted at different places on their pages\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStack floors plotted alike; check the others one sheet at a time.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount posts sized like the counted object\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery post on a sheet is the same size; it marks where a count is, not what it is.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bidding\"\u003EBidding\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAddenda to a package that was already sent, award letters, a scope sheet each bidder signs off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESend a new package, and write the award yourself. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/bid-invitations\"\u003ESend a package for bids\u003C/a\u003E covers what is built.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESending bid invitations from any account: while it is tested, mail to outside bidders is switched off except for accounts the CadCraft team has enabled\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownload the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScope CSV\u003C/span\u003E, email it yourself and type the bids in.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESending bid packages from a Studio window\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESend from TakeoffCraft in the browser.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERepricing an alternate&#039;s bid area with a chosen bid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlternates and bid-area subtotals use your own prices; adjust the alternate by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAutomatic reminders before a subcontractor&#039;s insurance or license date runs out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe date is flagged on the Subcontractors page once it is past or within 30 days; check it when you invite.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"panels\"\u003EPanels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoving the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantities\u003C/span\u003E table\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItems\u003C/span\u003E panels move. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EArrange the panels\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"spreadsheets\"\u003ESpreadsheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe XLSX workbook has been checked in LibreOffice Calc, which recalculated every total to the same cent as TakeoffCraft. It has not been opened in Excel yet. If a spreadsheet program shows something unexpected, the CSV export holds the same estimate as plain values.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"TakeoffCraft is in development. This list is what the current build does not do, and how to get the job done anyway. Measuring Not built yet Instead, today Inserting a point in the middle of a run or outline Delete the mark and draw it again. Box (window) selection Shift + click each mark, or Ctrl+A for every mark on the sheet. See Copy, paste and duplicate marks. Rotating, mirroring or rescaling a pasted copy Paste on a sheet at the same scale and in the same orientation; draw the rest by hand. Reading a scale from a scanned sheet, or from a graphic scale bar Pick the preset, or calibrate on the bar's length. More than one scale per sheet (detail areas, enlarged plans) Measure the part at the sheet's scale, and take off the detail on its own enlarged sheet if the set has one. Items and pricing Not built yet Instead, today Assembly formulas that use an item's other kinds of marks (the doors of a room item in its floor's formulas) Formulas use the main type's totals; price the other kinds on their own rows. Assembly formulas worked out run by run Formulas use the item's totals; use N for per-run extras. Formulas that use another item's quantities Repeat the measurement in that item, or work it out in the spreadsheet export. Crews on assembly components, or a working day other than 8 hours Give the crew to the item; read crew-days for another day length as crew-hours ÷ your day. Markups other than notes, clouds and highlights (arrows, dimensions, stamps), or replies on a note Write it in the note's text; change its status as it is settled. A price library shared by a team, or kept with your account The library is kept in this browser: export it as a CSV and import it on the other computer. Trade packs reviewed by working estimators Every pack is a starter: check its assumptions against your job and your products before you price with it. See Trade packs. Prices in trade packs Packs bring assemblies only: set your own rates, or import your price list as a CSV. Renaming the takeoff The name comes from the first PDF; rename the downloaded files. Naming sheets by hand Sheets are named from the PDF's page labels; search by file name and page. Drawings (DWG and DXF) Not built yet Instead, today Plot style tables, pen assignments and CTB files on a drawing's sheets A drawing is drawn black, or in its colors darkened for paper; line weights come from the drawing. Opening a binary DXF, or a DWG older than AutoCAD 2000 Save it as an ASCII DXF or a newer DWG. Finding a linked drawing (xref) by the folder it sits in Open the linked drawing's file too, with the host: it is found by file name among the open files. Measuring 3D polylines, meshes, solids and regions Count them by layer and type, or measure the plan view with the Linear and Area tools. Text in a marked PDF from a drawing in scripts other than Latin Characters Helvetica has no glyph for print as \"?\" in the marked PDF; the drawing on screen is unaffected. A hand-over of a drawing to another app from the sheet bar Open the same file in that app. A drawing that carries the takeoff inside it, as PDF with the takeoff inside does for PDFs Save takeoff: the .takeoff.json reopens on the same drawing, found by its contents. Drawing or counting PDF underlays, images and OLE objects placed in a drawing Images show as a frame only. Open the underlay PDF itself as a sheet. Auto count Not built yet Instead, today Finding symbols on a sheet with no scale drawn at another size Give the sheet its scale, or search it with a box drawn there. Finding mirrored symbols Count those by hand, or search again with a box around a mirrored one. Files and sharing Not built yet Instead, today One PDF that both shows the marks and carries the takeoff Marked PDF for people to see the marks; PDF with the takeoff inside to reopen in TakeoffCraft. A CSV of quantities by sheet or of the marks Use XLSX: its By sheet and Marks worksheets have them. Cloud saving Not available yet. Save project files to your computer and back them up. A separate TakeoffCraft desktop app From 0.5 the desktop app is CadCraft Studio, with all eight apps in one install. TakeoffCraft also runs in the browser. Screen and input Not built yet Instead, today A right-click (or long-press) menu Use the toolbar, the panels and the keys. A long press finishes a run or an outline. Rotating the view Pages rotated in the PDF already show upright. 3D view Not built yet Instead, today Drawing or editing marks in 3D Go back to the sheet (3) to draw; select and delete work in 3D. Lining up stacked floors plotted at different places on their pages Stack floors plotted alike; check the others one sheet at a time. Count posts sized like the counted object Every post on a sheet is the same size; it marks where a count is, not what it is. Bidding Not built yet Instead, today Addenda to a package that was already sent, award letters, a scope sheet each bidder signs off Send a new package, and write the award yourself. Send a package for bids covers what is built. Sending bid invitations from any account: while it is tested, mail to outside bidders is switched off except for accounts the CadCraft team has enabled Download the Scope CSV, email it yourself and type the bids in. Sending bid packages from a Studio window Send from TakeoffCraft in the browser. Repricing an alternate's bid area with a chosen bid Alternates and bid-area subtotals use your own prices; adjust the alternate by hand. Automatic reminders before a subcontractor's insurance or license date runs out The date is flagged on the Subcontractors page once it is past or within 30 days; check it when you invite. Panels Not built yet Instead, today Moving the Quantities table Only the Sheets and Items panels move. See Arrange the panels. Spreadsheets The XLSX workbook has been checked in LibreOffice Calc, which recalculated every total to the same cent as TakeoffCraft. It has not been opened in Excel yet. If a spreadsheet program shows something unexpected, the CSV export holds the same estimate as plain values.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Using this help","summary":"Open the help, search it, and see the steps for your computer.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["help","f1","search","tutorials","manual"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-help","text":"Open the help","level":2},{"id":"find-a-topic","text":"Find a topic","level":2},{"id":"steps-for-your-computer","text":"Steps for your computer","level":2},{"id":"the-same-help-on-the-website","text":"The same help on the website","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-help\"\u003EOpen the help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top of the app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E together with the \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E key (or the globe key), or use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EKeys on a Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-a-topic\"\u003EFind a topic\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs&#039; command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETopics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E returns to where you were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-for-your-computer\"\u003ESteps for your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example &quot;Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChange\u003C/span\u003E on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E to see every version of every step, each labeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-same-help-on-the-website\"\u003EThe same help on the website\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform&#039;s steps. In the app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running. Open the help Press F1, or choose Help in the bar at the top of the app. Press Esc or choose Close to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was. On most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press F1 together with the fn key (or the globe key), or use the Help button. See Keys on a Mac. Find a topic Type in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs' command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page. Topics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting. Links inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. Back returns to where you were. Steps for your computer Some steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example \"Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online\". Choose Change on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague. Choose Show all to see every version of every step, each labeled. Keyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: Ctrl+S reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux. The same help on the website The website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform's steps. In the app, Open on the website at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Browsers and computers","summary":"Which browsers the apps run in, what differs between them, and what your computer needs.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","browser","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","webgl","requirements","memory"],"headings":[{"id":"which-browser","text":"Which browser","level":2},{"id":"what-differs-between-browsers","text":"What differs between browsers","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-and-linux","text":"Mac, Windows and Linux","level":2},{"id":"tablets-and-touch-screens","text":"Tablets and touch screens","level":2},{"id":"what-your-computer-needs","text":"What your computer needs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-browser\"\u003EWhich browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an up-to-date version of one of these:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEngine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EWhat differs between browsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a folder.\u003C/strong\u003E Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving.\u003C/strong\u003E The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Chrome or Edge, turn on &quot;Ask where to save each file before downloading&quot; (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose &quot;Always ask you where to save files&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari, open Settings, then General, and set &quot;File download location&quot; to &quot;Ask for each download&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-and-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows and Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tablets-and-touch-screens\"\u003ETablets and touch screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app&#039;s help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-your-computer-needs\"\u003EWhat your computer needs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVery large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see Where your files go). Which browser Use an up-to-date version of one of these: Browser Engine Notes Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium The most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling. Firefox Gecko Works. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows. Safari WebKit Works on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks. All three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser. What differs between browsers Opening a folder. Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three. Saving. The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first. In Chrome or Edge, turn on \"Ask where to save each file before downloading\" (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time. In Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose \"Always ask you where to save files\" to pick a folder every time. In Safari, open Settings, then General, and set \"File download location\" to \"Ask for each download\" to pick a folder every time. Mac, Windows and Linux The apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Tablets and touch screens Some apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app's help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands. What your computer needs These are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements: 8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models). A graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser's settings. A screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them. Very large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Keys, mice and trackpads","summary":"How shortcuts are written, function keys on a Mac, right-clicking and panning without a mouse.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcut","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","function keys","trackpad","right click","middle button","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"how-shortcuts-are-written","text":"How shortcuts are written","level":2},{"id":"function-keys-on-a-mac","text":"Function keys on a Mac","level":2},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps","text":"Shortcuts the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad","text":"Mouse buttons on a trackpad","level":2},{"id":"typing-in-a-text-box","text":"Typing in a text box","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"how-shortcuts-are-written\"\u003EHow shortcuts are written\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWritten\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn a Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn Windows and Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe Delete key\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn and Delete (forward delete)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plus sign means hold the keys together. &quot;Then&quot; means press one after the other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works both ways. A few, such as full screen (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EFunction keys on a Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (or the globe key) while you press the key, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eopen \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard Shortcuts\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EFunction Keys\u003C/strong\u003E, and turn on &quot;Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EShortcuts the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a Mac and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;); choose to stay, then save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser&#039;s own full screen (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, or the green button on the window\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, which can hide the app&#039;s bars too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad\"\u003EMouse buttons on a trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click opens the browser&#039;s menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-in-a-text-box\"\u003ETyping in a text box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app&#039;s one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing &quot;L&quot; types an L. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"How shortcuts are written The help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it. Written On a Mac On Windows and Linux Ctrl+S Command (⌘) and S Ctrl and S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F Ctrl, Shift and F Alt+← Option (⌥) and ← Alt and ← Backspace the Delete key Backspace Delete fn and Delete (forward delete) Delete Enter Return Enter A plus sign means hold the keys together. \"Then\" means press one after the other. Most app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so Ctrl+Z works both ways. A few, such as full screen (Ctrl+Shift+F), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies. Function keys on a Mac On most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12: hold fn (or the globe key) while you press the key, or open System Settings, then Keyboard, then Keyboard Shortcuts, then Function Keys, and turn on \"Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys\". Some F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead. Shortcuts the browser keeps A web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T and Ctrl+W always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead. Ctrl+Q on a Mac and Alt+F4 on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"); choose to stay, then save. The browser's own full screen (Control-Command-F, or the green button on the window / F11) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on Ctrl+Shift+F, which can hide the app's bars too. Mouse buttons on a trackpad The apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse: Right-click: click with two fingers. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding Ctrl while you click opens the browser's menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it. Right-click: click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps. Typing in a text box While you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app's one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing \"L\" types an L. Press Esc or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Find a tool or setting","summary":"Press Ctrl+K and type a few letters to run any tool, change a setting or open a help topic.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["find","search","command palette","quick open","run command","ctrl k","cmd k","tool","setting","shortcut"],"headings":[{"id":"find-and-run","text":"Find and run","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don&#039;t need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-and-run\"\u003EFind and run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a few letters of what you want: &quot;fillet&quot;, &quot;panel text&quot;, &quot;units&quot;. Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETools that can&#039;t run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd-ons&#039; tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Every app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don't need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar. Find and run Press Ctrl+K, or choose Find in the bar at the top. Type a few letters of what you want: \"fillet\", \"panel text\", \"units\". Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list. Press Enter to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. Esc closes the box. A tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens Settings at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel. Things to know What you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away. Tools that can't run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out. Add-ons' tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Arrange the panels and use a second monitor","summary":"Dock the side panels left, right or at the bottom, stack them, group them as tabs, pin or hide them, float them over the drawing, make the layout denser, or move them to their own window on another screen.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["panels","dock","float","move panel","drag","layout","tabs","tab group","stack","pin","auto-hide","hide panel","close panel","compact","layout density","dense","second monitor","two screens","dual monitor","multiple monitors","window","pop-up","reset layout"],"headings":[{"id":"move-a-panel-in-the-window","text":"Move a panel in the window","level":2},{"id":"group-panels-as-tabs","text":"Group panels as tabs","level":2},{"id":"pin-hide-and-bring-back","text":"Pin, hide and bring back","level":2},{"id":"make-the-layout-denser","text":"Make the layout denser","level":2},{"id":"use-the-keyboard","text":"Use the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor","text":"Put the panels on a second monitor","level":2},{"id":"more-than-one-panels-window","text":"More than one panels window","level":2},{"id":"move-a-dialog","text":"Move a dialog","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-back","text":"Put the panels back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach panel has a title bar: a slim bar with the panel&#039;s name, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu, a pin and a close button at the top of the panel. Versions before 0.7 have the name and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-panel-in-the-window\"\u003EMove a panel in the window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the panel by its name at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanels can share one side: they stack, and each line between two of them drags to share the space (with a line focused, the arrow keys move it, and double-clicking it shares the space equally). Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger on the panel&#039;s name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"group-panels-as-tabs\"\u003EGroup panels as tabs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo panels can share one frame and show one at a time. Drag a panel by its name onto another panel&#039;s title bar (the bar is outlined while you are over it) and let go: the frame now has a tab for each panel, in its title bar. Click a tab to show that panel. With a tab focused, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E switch tabs, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E go to the first and last.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo take a panel out of the group, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove out of the tab group\u003C/span\u003E in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu, or drag it somewhere else. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETab with\u003C/span\u003E and a panel&#039;s name to group them without dragging.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pin-hide-and-bring-back\"\u003EPin, hide and bring back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe pin in a panel&#039;s title bar makes it \u003Cstrong\u003Eauto-hide\u003C/strong\u003E: the panel leaves its dock and becomes a small tab at that edge. Point at the tab, focus it or click it and the panel slides out over the drawing; move away (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and it slides back. Press the pin again, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnpin\u003C/span\u003E, to dock it for good.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe close button, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide this panel\u003C/span\u003E in the menu, hides the panel and closes up its dock. A dashed tab at the edge brings it back: click it, or focus it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-layout-denser\"\u003EMake the layout denser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout density\u003C/span\u003E has two choices. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComfortable\u003C/span\u003E is the default. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompact\u003C/span\u003E tightens the row heights, panel padding, toolbar buttons and panel text of that app so more fits on the screen. It is kept for each app. A touch screen keeps its large buttons in both, and a phone keeps its own layout. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E leaves the density as you set it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-the-keyboard\"\u003EUse the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button opens a menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the left\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock at the bottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloat over the drawing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin to the edge\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide this panel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETab with\u003C/span\u003E another panel, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it back\u003C/span\u003E. Tab to the button, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, move with the arrow keys and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor\"\u003EPut the panels on a second monitor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E on a panel, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove all panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E. You can also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-than-one-panels-window\"\u003EMore than one panels window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E opens another window for that panel, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to panels window 2\u003C/span\u003E (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called &quot;panels 2&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA panels window that has no panels left closes by itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the next one\u003C/span\u003E once for each window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-dialog\"\u003EMove a dialog\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery dialog can be moved out of the way of the drawing: press and drag its title bar. The dialog remembers where you left it, each dialog on its own, and comes back to that place the next time it opens. Double-click the title bar to centre it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom the keyboard, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+an arrow key with the focus inside the dialog to move it 16 pixels at a time; add \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for 64. A dialog that ends up off the screen (after a smaller window or screen) is brought back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone, dialogs stay full-width sheets and are not moved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, CadCraft&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog, which you may keep open while you work, has a button in its title bar, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen in its own window\u003C/span\u003E. The dialog moves into a window of its own that you can put on another monitor. Closing the dialog closes the window, and closing the window closes the dialog.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-back\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClosing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/span\u003E to bring the window back, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep them here\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E puts every panel back where it started.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one. Each panel has a title bar: a slim bar with the panel's name, a ⋯ menu, a pin and a close button at the top of the panel. Versions before 0.7 have the name and the ⋯ menu only. Move a panel in the window Drag the panel by its name at the top. While you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens. Drop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float. Panels can share one side: they stack, and each line between two of them drags to share the space (with a line focused, the arrow keys move it, and double-clicking it shares the space equally). Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back. A floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was. Drag with one finger on the panel's name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the ⋯ menu. Group panels as tabs Two panels can share one frame and show one at a time. Drag a panel by its name onto another panel's title bar (the bar is outlined while you are over it) and let go: the frame now has a tab for each panel, in its title bar. Click a tab to show that panel. With a tab focused, ← and → switch tabs, and Home and End go to the first and last. To take a panel out of the group, choose Move out of the tab group in its ⋯ menu, or drag it somewhere else. Choose Tab with and a panel's name to group them without dragging. Pin, hide and bring back The pin in a panel's title bar makes it auto-hide: the panel leaves its dock and becomes a small tab at that edge. Point at the tab, focus it or click it and the panel slides out over the drawing; move away (or press Esc) and it slides back. Press the pin again, or choose Unpin, to dock it for good. The close button, or Hide this panel in the menu, hides the panel and closes up its dock. A dashed tab at the edge brings it back: click it, or focus it and press Enter. Make the layout denser Settings › General › Layout density has two choices. Comfortable is the default. Compact tightens the row heights, panel padding, toolbar buttons and panel text of that app so more fits on the screen. It is kept for each app. A touch screen keeps its large buttons in both, and a phone keeps its own layout. Reset layout leaves the density as you set it. Use the keyboard Every panel's ⋯ button opens a menu: Dock on the left, Dock on the right, Dock at the bottom, Float over the drawing, Pin to the edge, Hide this panel, Tab with another panel, Move to a new window and Put it back. Tab to the button, press Enter, move with the arrow keys and press Enter again. Esc closes the menu. Put the panels on a second monitor Choose ⋯ on a panel, then Move all panels to a new window. You can also choose Move panels to a new window in Settings. A small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like. Keep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window. Shortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window. Chrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose Put it on my other screen in the panels window (or Put the panels on my other screen in a panel's ⋯ menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time. Drag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time. More than one panels window Each panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor. In a panel's ⋯ menu, Move to a new window opens another window for that panel, and Move to panels window 2 (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called \"panels 2\". Drag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was. A panels window that has no panels left closes by itself. After a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose Open the next one once for each window. Move a dialog Every dialog can be moved out of the way of the drawing: press and drag its title bar. The dialog remembers where you left it, each dialog on its own, and comes back to that place the next time it opens. Double-click the title bar to centre it again. From the keyboard, press Alt+Shift+an arrow key with the focus inside the dialog to move it 16 pixels at a time; add Ctrl for 64. A dialog that ends up off the screen (after a smaller window or screen) is brought back. On a phone, dialogs stay full-width sheets and are not moved. In CadCraft Studio, CadCraft's Plot to PDF dialog, which you may keep open while you work, has a button in its title bar, Open in its own window. The dialog moves into a window of its own that you can put on another monitor. Closing the dialog closes the window, and closing the window closes the dialog. Put the panels back Choose Put the panels back in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were. Closing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose Open it to bring the window back, or Keep them here. Settings › General › Reset layout puts every panel back where it started. If nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again. The layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"layout-ribbon","scope":"basics","title":"Trabajar con el diseño de cinta y navegador de proyecto","summary":"Cambie la aplicación a una cinta de pestañas sobre el dibujo, con Propiedades y un navegador de proyecto a la izquierda y una pestaña por cada vista abierta, y vuelva al diseño clásico cuando quiera.","section":"start","order":36,"keywords":["diseño","espacio de trabajo","cinta","pestañas de la cinta","navegador de proyecto","navegador","paletas","paleta de propiedades","pestañas de vista","barra de estado","acceso rápido","contraer la cinta","minimizar la cinta","ventana de la cinta","diseño clásico","cambiar de diseño"],"headings":[{"id":"elegirlo","text":"Elegirlo","level":2},{"id":"qu-hay-en-cada-lugar","text":"Qué hay en cada lugar","level":2},{"id":"usar-la-cinta-con-el-teclado","text":"Usar la cinta con el teclado","level":2},{"id":"contraer-la-cinta-o-darle-su-propia-ventana","text":"Contraer la cinta o darle su propia ventana","level":2},{"id":"sus-paneles-siguen-siendo-suyos","text":"Sus paneles siguen siendo suyos","level":2},{"id":"volver-al-dise-o-cl-sico","text":"Volver al diseño clásico","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECinta y navegador de proyecto\u003C/strong\u003E es uno de los tres diseños de espacio de trabajo de la aplicación. Una cinta de pestañas recorre la parte de arriba, Propiedades y un navegador de proyecto comparten el panel izquierdo, cada vista que abre tiene su pestaña sobre el dibujo y abajo hay una barra de estado. Las herramientas son las de la aplicación: todo lo que puede hacer en el diseño clásico funciona aquí también, y su dibujo, su estilo de menú y sus teclas siguen igual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"elegirlo\"\u003EElegirlo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfiguración\u003C/span\u003E (el engrane, o \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElija la pestaña \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E y vaya a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiseño\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEspacio de trabajo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElija \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECinta y navegador de proyecto\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEl diseño cambia de inmediato. La elección vale para todas las aplicaciones en este navegador: cada una abre con él desde entonces, y está listo antes de que se dibuje la página, así que nunca verá primero el diseño clásico.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"qu-hay-en-cada-lugar\"\u003EQué hay en cada lugar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra de título:\u003C/strong\u003E el nombre de la aplicación, botones de acceso rápido (abrir, guardar, deshacer, rehacer), el nombre del archivo abierto, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQué hace aquí cada herramienta\u003C/span\u003E (una lista de todas las herramientas con un cuadro de búsqueda, que dice cuáles están listas y por qué las demás no), pantalla completa, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAyuda\u003C/span\u003E y \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfiguración\u003C/span\u003E. En este diseño la barra de título ocupa el lugar de la barra de la aplicación.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECinta:\u003C/strong\u003E las herramientas de la aplicación en pestañas, cada pestaña en grupos. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArchivo\u003C/span\u003E, a la izquierda, abre, guarda y lo demás. Algunas aplicaciones agregan una pestaña propia mientras trabaja en un modo o espacio de trabajo; se ilumina mientras aplica y desaparece después.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra de opciones:\u003C/strong\u003E debajo de la cinta, lo que la herramienta en curso le pide, o lo que está seleccionado.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPanel izquierdo:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPropiedades\u003C/span\u003E arriba y, debajo, las paletas (el navegador de proyecto y el contenido propio del panel) como pestañas. Arrastre la línea entre ellas para repartir el espacio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPestañas de vista:\u003C/strong\u003E una pestaña por cada vista que abrió (una hoja, una planta, una escena…). Cierre una con su botón de cerrar; el archivo sigue abierto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra de vista y barra de estado:\u003C/strong\u003E debajo del dibujo, los controles de la vista (escala, zoom); abajo del todo, los mensajes de la aplicación.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUna herramienta que no puede usarse ahora aparece en gris, y su información emergente dice por qué (por ejemplo, &quot;Abra primero un archivo&quot;, o que la aplicación aún no tiene esa herramienta). Haga clic en ella, o pulse \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sobre ella, para leer el motivo en un cuadro.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"usar-la-cinta-con-el-teclado\"\u003EUsar la cinta con el teclado\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECon una pestaña de la cinta enfocada, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E y \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pasan de una pestaña a otra, y \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E y \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E van a la primera y a la última. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E termina primero la herramienta en curso, como en el diseño clásico, y todos los atajos y \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (modos de pantalla) funcionan como antes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"contraer-la-cinta-o-darle-su-propia-ventana\"\u003EContraer la cinta o darle su propia ventana\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHaga doble clic en una pestaña de la cinta para contraerla a sus pestañas; entonces un clic en una pestaña muestra sus herramientas hasta que siga con otra cosa. Haga doble clic otra vez para mantenerla abierta. El botón \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover la cinta\u003C/span\u003E, en el extremo derecho de la cinta, también tiene \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContraer la cinta\u003C/span\u003E y \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMantener la cinta abierta\u003C/span\u003E. Cada aplicación lo recuerda.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover la cinta\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover a una ventana nueva\u003C/span\u003E pone la cinta en una ventana propia, por ejemplo en un segundo monitor. Las teclas que pulse ahí llegan a la aplicación. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERegresar la cinta\u003C/span\u003E, o cerrar esa ventana, la devuelve a su lugar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELa configuración de la barra de herramientas se aplica a la cinta: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfiguración\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBarra de herramientas\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIconos y etiquetas\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolo iconos\u003C/span\u003E (la cinta muestra entonces dos filas de iconos pequeños por grupo) o \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolo etiquetas\u003C/span\u003E, y \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMostrar nombres de grupo (Archivo, Dibujo, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sus-paneles-siguen-siendo-suyos\"\u003ESus paneles siguen siendo suyos\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELos paneles izquierdo y derecho conservan todo lo que se describe en \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EOrganizar los paneles\u003C/a\u003E: acoplar, flotar, fijar, ocultar, pestañas, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover a una ventana nueva\u003C/span\u003E y \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERestablecer diseño\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"volver-al-dise-o-cl-sico\"\u003EVolver al diseño clásico\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAbra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfiguración\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiseño\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEspacio de trabajo\u003C/span\u003E y elija \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft clásico\u003C/span\u003E. Cada barra de herramientas, pestaña y panel vuelve a su lugar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEn un teléfono, o en una ventana de 760 píxeles de ancho o menos, la aplicación usa su diseño para teléfono sin importar lo que haya elegido. Una ventana más ancha devuelve su diseño.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPara probar otra disposición, vea \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Eel diseño de caja de herramientas y cuadro de información\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Cinta y navegador de proyecto es uno de los tres diseños de espacio de trabajo de la aplicación. Una cinta de pestañas recorre la parte de arriba, Propiedades y un navegador de proyecto comparten el panel izquierdo, cada vista que abre tiene su pestaña sobre el dibujo y abajo hay una barra de estado. Las herramientas son las de la aplicación: todo lo que puede hacer en el diseño clásico funciona aquí también, y su dibujo, su estilo de menú y sus teclas siguen igual. Elegirlo Abra Configuración (el engrane, o Ctrl+,). Elija la pestaña Global y vaya a Diseño › Espacio de trabajo. Elija Cinta y navegador de proyecto. El diseño cambia de inmediato. La elección vale para todas las aplicaciones en este navegador: cada una abre con él desde entonces, y está listo antes de que se dibuje la página, así que nunca verá primero el diseño clásico. Qué hay en cada lugar Barra de título: el nombre de la aplicación, botones de acceso rápido (abrir, guardar, deshacer, rehacer), el nombre del archivo abierto, Qué hace aquí cada herramienta (una lista de todas las herramientas con un cuadro de búsqueda, que dice cuáles están listas y por qué las demás no), pantalla completa, Ayuda y Configuración. En este diseño la barra de título ocupa el lugar de la barra de la aplicación. Cinta: las herramientas de la aplicación en pestañas, cada pestaña en grupos. Archivo, a la izquierda, abre, guarda y lo demás. Algunas aplicaciones agregan una pestaña propia mientras trabaja en un modo o espacio de trabajo; se ilumina mientras aplica y desaparece después. Barra de opciones: debajo de la cinta, lo que la herramienta en curso le pide, o lo que está seleccionado. Panel izquierdo: Propiedades arriba y, debajo, las paletas (el navegador de proyecto y el contenido propio del panel) como pestañas. Arrastre la línea entre ellas para repartir el espacio. Pestañas de vista: una pestaña por cada vista que abrió (una hoja, una planta, una escena…). Cierre una con su botón de cerrar; el archivo sigue abierto. Barra de vista y barra de estado: debajo del dibujo, los controles de la vista (escala, zoom); abajo del todo, los mensajes de la aplicación. Una herramienta que no puede usarse ahora aparece en gris, y su información emergente dice por qué (por ejemplo, \"Abra primero un archivo\", o que la aplicación aún no tiene esa herramienta). Haga clic en ella, o pulse Enter sobre ella, para leer el motivo en un cuadro. Usar la cinta con el teclado Con una pestaña de la cinta enfocada, ← y → pasan de una pestaña a otra, y Home y End van a la primera y a la última. Esc termina primero la herramienta en curso, como en el diseño clásico, y todos los atajos y Ctrl+Shift+F (modos de pantalla) funcionan como antes. Contraer la cinta o darle su propia ventana Haga doble clic en una pestaña de la cinta para contraerla a sus pestañas; entonces un clic en una pestaña muestra sus herramientas hasta que siga con otra cosa. Haga doble clic otra vez para mantenerla abierta. El botón Mover la cinta, en el extremo derecho de la cinta, también tiene Contraer la cinta y Mantener la cinta abierta. Cada aplicación lo recuerda. Mover la cinta › Mover a una ventana nueva pone la cinta en una ventana propia, por ejemplo en un segundo monitor. Las teclas que pulse ahí llegan a la aplicación. Regresar la cinta, o cerrar esa ventana, la devuelve a su lugar. La configuración de la barra de herramientas se aplica a la cinta: Configuración › Global › Barra de herramientas › Iconos y etiquetas, Solo iconos (la cinta muestra entonces dos filas de iconos pequeños por grupo) o Solo etiquetas, y Mostrar nombres de grupo (Archivo, Dibujo, …). Sus paneles siguen siendo suyos Los paneles izquierdo y derecho conservan todo lo que se describe en Organizar los paneles: acoplar, flotar, fijar, ocultar, pestañas, Mover a una ventana nueva y Restablecer diseño. Volver al diseño clásico Abra Configuración › Global › Diseño › Espacio de trabajo y elija CadCraft clásico. Cada barra de herramientas, pestaña y panel vuelve a su lugar. En un teléfono, o en una ventana de 760 píxeles de ancho o menos, la aplicación usa su diseño para teléfono sin importar lo que haya elegido. Una ventana más ancha devuelve su diseño. Para probar otra disposición, vea el diseño de caja de herramientas y cuadro de información.","lang":"es-MX"},{"slug":"layout-toolbox","scope":"basics","title":"Trabajar con el diseño de caja de herramientas y cuadro de información","summary":"Cambie la aplicación a una columna de herramientas a la izquierda con espacios para las suyas, un cuadro de información arriba que sigue a la herramienta en curso o a lo que eligió, un navegador a la derecha, una pestaña por cada vista, opciones rápidas y una paleta de edición, y vuelva al diseño clásico cuando quiera.","section":"start","order":37,"keywords":["diseño","espacio de trabajo","caja de herramientas","columna de herramientas","cuadro de información","barra de información","navegador","mapa del proyecto","barra rápida","opciones rápidas","paleta de edición","mis herramientas","herramientas favoritas","pestañas de vista","ventana de la caja de herramientas","diseño clásico","cambiar de diseño"],"headings":[{"id":"elegirlo","text":"Elegirlo","level":2},{"id":"qu-hay-en-cada-lugar","text":"Qué hay en cada lugar","level":2},{"id":"darle-a-la-caja-de-herramientas-su-propia-ventana","text":"Darle a la caja de herramientas su propia ventana","level":2},{"id":"sus-paneles-siguen-siendo-suyos","text":"Sus paneles siguen siendo suyos","level":2},{"id":"volver-al-dise-o-cl-sico","text":"Volver al diseño clásico","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECaja de herramientas y cuadro de información\u003C/strong\u003E es uno de los tres diseños de espacio de trabajo de la aplicación. Las herramientas de dibujo están en una columna a la izquierda, un cuadro de información arriba muestra lo que pide la herramienta en curso o lo que tiene seleccionado, cada vista tiene su pestaña y una barra rápida debajo del dibujo reúne algunas opciones de uso diario. Las herramientas son las de la aplicación: todo lo que puede hacer en el diseño clásico funciona aquí también, y su dibujo, su estilo de menú y sus teclas siguen igual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"elegirlo\"\u003EElegirlo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfiguración\u003C/span\u003E (el engrane, o \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElija la pestaña \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E y vaya a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiseño\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEspacio de trabajo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElija \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECaja de herramientas y cuadro de información\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEl diseño cambia de inmediato. La elección vale para todas las aplicaciones en este navegador: cada una abre con él desde entonces, y está listo antes de que se dibuje la página, así que nunca verá primero el diseño clásico.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"qu-hay-en-cada-lugar\"\u003EQué hay en cada lugar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra de herramientas:\u003C/strong\u003E la barra de herramientas de la aplicación sigue arriba del área de dibujo, con sus menús.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECuadro de información:\u003C/strong\u003E debajo de la barra de herramientas. Mientras una herramienta está en curso muestra la herramienta y lo que pide. Cuando elige algo muestra sus ajustes principales (para un muro: su tipo, espesor, longitud, línea de ubicación y altura), y puede cambiarlos ahí mismo: un cambio ahí es igual que en Propiedades, y \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E lo deshace. Si no hay nada elegido dice &quot;Nada seleccionado&quot;. Cada aplicación agrega aquí sus propios cuadros, como la capa o el nivel actual, o el documento abierto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECaja de herramientas:\u003C/strong\u003E la columna de la izquierda, las herramientas de la aplicación en grupos. Haga clic en el nombre de un grupo para contraerlo o abrirlo de nuevo. La herramienta en curso aparece iluminada.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMis herramientas:\u003C/strong\u003E el primer grupo de la caja de herramientas, ocho espacios vacíos para las herramientas que más usa. Haga clic en un espacio vacío para elegir cualquier herramienta o comando de la aplicación; o haga clic derecho en una herramienta de la caja y elija \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAgregar a Mis herramientas\u003C/span\u003E; o arrastre una herramienta a un espacio. Haga clic derecho en un espacio para cambiarlo o vaciarlo. Sus espacios se guardan para cada aplicación en este navegador.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENavegador:\u003C/strong\u003E el panel derecho. Arriba está el explorador del proyecto de la aplicación (plantas, cortes, alzados, vistas 3D, tablas, hojas, según las tenga la aplicación): haga clic en una para abrirla. Debajo, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPropiedades\u003C/span\u003E y una pestaña con el resto del panel derecho.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPestañas de vista:\u003C/strong\u003E una pestaña por cada vista del archivo abierto (una hoja, una planta, una escena…), sobre el dibujo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra rápida:\u003C/strong\u003E debajo del dibujo, los ajustes de vista y las opciones de uso diario que tenga la aplicación, como la escala, el nivel de detalle, el rango de vista, ajustar, planta, dividida o 3D, referencias y unidades.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaleta de edición:\u003C/strong\u003E haga clic en algo del dibujo y aparece junto al puntero una pequeña paleta con los comandos de edición de la aplicación para eso, como mover, copiar, girar, reflejar, duplicar o eliminar, y \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPropiedades\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, la rueda del ratón, otro clic o su botón de cerrar la quitan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUna herramienta que no puede usarse ahora aparece en gris, y su información emergente dice por qué. Haga clic en ella, o pulse \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sobre ella, para leer el motivo en un cuadro.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E termina primero la herramienta en curso, como en el diseño clásico, y todos los atajos y \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (modos de pantalla) funcionan como antes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"darle-a-la-caja-de-herramientas-su-propia-ventana\"\u003EDarle a la caja de herramientas su propia ventana\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEl botón \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover la caja de herramientas\u003C/span\u003E, arriba de la caja de herramientas, tiene \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover a una ventana nueva\u003C/span\u003E: la caja de herramientas se abre en una ventana angosta propia que puede poner junto a la aplicación o en un segundo monitor. Las teclas que pulse ahí llegan a la aplicación. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERegresar la caja de herramientas\u003C/span\u003E, o cerrar esa ventana, la devuelve a su lugar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELa configuración de la barra de herramientas también se aplica a la caja de herramientas: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfiguración\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBarra de herramientas\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIconos y etiquetas\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolo iconos\u003C/span\u003E o \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolo etiquetas\u003C/span\u003E, y \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMostrar nombres de grupo (Archivo, Dibujo, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sus-paneles-siguen-siendo-suyos\"\u003ESus paneles siguen siendo suyos\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELos paneles izquierdo y derecho conservan todo lo que se describe en \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EOrganizar los paneles\u003C/a\u003E: acoplar, flotar, fijar, ocultar, pestañas, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMover a una ventana nueva\u003C/span\u003E y \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERestablecer diseño\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"volver-al-dise-o-cl-sico\"\u003EVolver al diseño clásico\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAbra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfiguración\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiseño\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEspacio de trabajo\u003C/span\u003E y elija \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft clásico\u003C/span\u003E. Cada barra de herramientas, pestaña y panel vuelve a su lugar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEn un teléfono, o en una ventana de 760 píxeles de ancho o menos, la aplicación usa su diseño para teléfono sin importar lo que haya elegido. Una ventana más ancha devuelve su diseño.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPara probar otra disposición, vea \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Eel diseño de cinta y navegador de proyecto\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Caja de herramientas y cuadro de información es uno de los tres diseños de espacio de trabajo de la aplicación. Las herramientas de dibujo están en una columna a la izquierda, un cuadro de información arriba muestra lo que pide la herramienta en curso o lo que tiene seleccionado, cada vista tiene su pestaña y una barra rápida debajo del dibujo reúne algunas opciones de uso diario. Las herramientas son las de la aplicación: todo lo que puede hacer en el diseño clásico funciona aquí también, y su dibujo, su estilo de menú y sus teclas siguen igual. Elegirlo Abra Configuración (el engrane, o Ctrl+,). Elija la pestaña Global y vaya a Diseño › Espacio de trabajo. Elija Caja de herramientas y cuadro de información. El diseño cambia de inmediato. La elección vale para todas las aplicaciones en este navegador: cada una abre con él desde entonces, y está listo antes de que se dibuje la página, así que nunca verá primero el diseño clásico. Qué hay en cada lugar Barra de herramientas: la barra de herramientas de la aplicación sigue arriba del área de dibujo, con sus menús. Cuadro de información: debajo de la barra de herramientas. Mientras una herramienta está en curso muestra la herramienta y lo que pide. Cuando elige algo muestra sus ajustes principales (para un muro: su tipo, espesor, longitud, línea de ubicación y altura), y puede cambiarlos ahí mismo: un cambio ahí es igual que en Propiedades, y Ctrl+Z lo deshace. Si no hay nada elegido dice \"Nada seleccionado\". Cada aplicación agrega aquí sus propios cuadros, como la capa o el nivel actual, o el documento abierto. Caja de herramientas: la columna de la izquierda, las herramientas de la aplicación en grupos. Haga clic en el nombre de un grupo para contraerlo o abrirlo de nuevo. La herramienta en curso aparece iluminada. Mis herramientas: el primer grupo de la caja de herramientas, ocho espacios vacíos para las herramientas que más usa. Haga clic en un espacio vacío para elegir cualquier herramienta o comando de la aplicación; o haga clic derecho en una herramienta de la caja y elija Agregar a Mis herramientas; o arrastre una herramienta a un espacio. Haga clic derecho en un espacio para cambiarlo o vaciarlo. Sus espacios se guardan para cada aplicación en este navegador. Navegador: el panel derecho. Arriba está el explorador del proyecto de la aplicación (plantas, cortes, alzados, vistas 3D, tablas, hojas, según las tenga la aplicación): haga clic en una para abrirla. Debajo, Propiedades y una pestaña con el resto del panel derecho. Pestañas de vista: una pestaña por cada vista del archivo abierto (una hoja, una planta, una escena…), sobre el dibujo. Barra rápida: debajo del dibujo, los ajustes de vista y las opciones de uso diario que tenga la aplicación, como la escala, el nivel de detalle, el rango de vista, ajustar, planta, dividida o 3D, referencias y unidades. Paleta de edición: haga clic en algo del dibujo y aparece junto al puntero una pequeña paleta con los comandos de edición de la aplicación para eso, como mover, copiar, girar, reflejar, duplicar o eliminar, y Propiedades. Esc, la rueda del ratón, otro clic o su botón de cerrar la quitan. Una herramienta que no puede usarse ahora aparece en gris, y su información emergente dice por qué. Haga clic en ella, o pulse Enter sobre ella, para leer el motivo en un cuadro. Esc termina primero la herramienta en curso, como en el diseño clásico, y todos los atajos y Ctrl+Shift+F (modos de pantalla) funcionan como antes. Darle a la caja de herramientas su propia ventana El botón Mover la caja de herramientas, arriba de la caja de herramientas, tiene Mover a una ventana nueva: la caja de herramientas se abre en una ventana angosta propia que puede poner junto a la aplicación o en un segundo monitor. Las teclas que pulse ahí llegan a la aplicación. Regresar la caja de herramientas, o cerrar esa ventana, la devuelve a su lugar. La configuración de la barra de herramientas también se aplica a la caja de herramientas: Configuración › Global › Barra de herramientas › Iconos y etiquetas, Solo iconos o Solo etiquetas, y Mostrar nombres de grupo (Archivo, Dibujo, …). Sus paneles siguen siendo suyos Los paneles izquierdo y derecho conservan todo lo que se describe en Organizar los paneles: acoplar, flotar, fijar, ocultar, pestañas, Mover a una ventana nueva y Restablecer diseño. Volver al diseño clásico Abra Configuración › Global › Diseño › Espacio de trabajo y elija CadCraft clásico. Cada barra de herramientas, pestaña y panel vuelve a su lugar. En un teléfono, o en una ventana de 760 píxeles de ancho o menos, la aplicación usa su diseño para teléfono sin importar lo que haya elegido. Una ventana más ancha devuelve su diseño. Para probar otra disposición, vea el diseño de cinta y navegador de proyecto.","lang":"es-MX"},{"slug":"layout-own","scope":"basics","title":"Organizar su propio diseño y guardar sus configuraciones","summary":"Elija qué panel muestra el explorador del proyecto, Propiedades o el contenido propio del panel, dónde va la caja de herramientas y qué llevan las barras, guárdelo como su propio diseño, y guarde configuraciones completas con un nombre para cambiar entre ellas.","section":"start","order":39,"keywords":["diseño","espacio de trabajo","organizar","personalizar","mi diseño","diseño propio","paneles","editores","explorador del proyecto","propiedades","caja de herramientas","barra rápida","cuadro de información","entorno de trabajo","configuración","perfil","guardar configuración","cambiar de configuración","exportar configuración","importar configuración","ccenv"],"headings":[{"id":"organizar-su-propio-dise-o","text":"Organizar su propio diseño","level":2},{"id":"guardar-su-configuraci-n-como-entorno-de-trabajo","text":"Guardar su configuración como entorno de trabajo","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAdemás de los tres diseños incluidos, puede organizar uno propio y guardar toda su configuración con un nombre para volver a ella en un solo paso.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"organizar-su-propio-dise-o\"\u003EOrganizar su propio diseño\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAbra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfiguración\u003C/span\u003E (el engrane, o \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), elija \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E y luego \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiseño\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElija \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrganizar su propio diseño…\u003C/span\u003E. El cuadro parte del diseño que usa ahora.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDele un nombre y elija:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESobre el dibujo:\u003C/strong\u003E la barra de herramientas de la aplicación, una cinta o ninguna; una barra de título; un cuadro de información y qué controles lleva.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EJunto al dibujo:\u003C/strong\u003E la caja de herramientas a la izquierda, a la derecha o sin ella, con o sin \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMis herramientas\u003C/span\u003E; qué panel lleva las paletas, cuál va arriba (el explorador del proyecto o Propiedades, por ejemplo) y cuáles son pestañas debajo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEl área de dibujo:\u003C/strong\u003E pestañas de vista, una barra rápida y una barra de vista con los controles que marque, la paleta de edición, una barra de estado.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAspecto:\u003C/strong\u003E tamaño, contorno de los botones y estilo de las pestañas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElija \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUsar este diseño\u003C/span\u003E. La aplicación cambia a él de inmediato, y todas las aplicaciones que tienen diseños también lo usan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESolo se ofrece lo que tiene esta aplicación. Su diseño aparece en \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDe complementos y archivos\u003C/span\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExportar este diseño\u003C/span\u003E lo guarda como un archivo que puede pasar a un colega, y el botón de quitar lo elimina. Los paneles que llena se siguen acoplando, flotando y pasando a su propia ventana, como en \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EOrganizar los paneles\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"guardar-su-configuraci-n-como-entorno-de-trabajo\"\u003EGuardar su configuración como entorno de trabajo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUn entorno de trabajo es toda su configuración para todas las aplicaciones en este navegador: el diseño, el estilo de menú y sus teclas, el estilo de la barra de herramientas, los paneles y sus tamaños, y \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMis herramientas\u003C/span\u003E. El tema y el idioma no forman parte de él.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGuardar:\u003C/strong\u003E escriba un nombre en \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntornos de trabajo\u003C/span\u003E y elija \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGuardar mi configuración\u003C/span\u003E. Guardar con un nombre que ya existe reemplaza esa configuración.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECambiar:\u003C/strong\u003E elija una configuración en la lista y luego \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECambiar a esta\u003C/span\u003E. La aplicación se vuelve a cargar con esa configuración; si tiene trabajo sin guardar, la aplicación pregunta antes, como en cualquier recarga.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECompartir:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExportar\u003C/span\u003E guarda la configuración elegida como un archivo \u003Ccode\u003E.ccenv.json\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImportar una configuración…\u003C/span\u003E agrega una desde un archivo. Un archivo solo fija estas opciones, nada más.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EQuitar:\u003C/strong\u003E elija una configuración y luego \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuitar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPuede guardar hasta doce configuraciones.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPara probar primero los diseños incluidos, vea \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Eel diseño de caja de herramientas y cuadro de información\u003C/a\u003E y \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Eel diseño de cinta y explorador del proyecto\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Además de los tres diseños incluidos, puede organizar uno propio y guardar toda su configuración con un nombre para volver a ella en un solo paso. Organizar su propio diseño Abra Configuración (el engrane, o Ctrl+,), elija Global y luego Diseño. Elija Organizar su propio diseño…. El cuadro parte del diseño que usa ahora. Dele un nombre y elija: Sobre el dibujo: la barra de herramientas de la aplicación, una cinta o ninguna; una barra de título; un cuadro de información y qué controles lleva. Junto al dibujo: la caja de herramientas a la izquierda, a la derecha o sin ella, con o sin Mis herramientas; qué panel lleva las paletas, cuál va arriba (el explorador del proyecto o Propiedades, por ejemplo) y cuáles son pestañas debajo. El área de dibujo: pestañas de vista, una barra rápida y una barra de vista con los controles que marque, la paleta de edición, una barra de estado. Aspecto: tamaño, contorno de los botones y estilo de las pestañas. Elija Usar este diseño. La aplicación cambia a él de inmediato, y todas las aplicaciones que tienen diseños también lo usan. Solo se ofrece lo que tiene esta aplicación. Su diseño aparece en De complementos y archivos; Exportar este diseño lo guarda como un archivo que puede pasar a un colega, y el botón de quitar lo elimina. Los paneles que llena se siguen acoplando, flotando y pasando a su propia ventana, como en Organizar los paneles. Guardar su configuración como entorno de trabajo Un entorno de trabajo es toda su configuración para todas las aplicaciones en este navegador: el diseño, el estilo de menú y sus teclas, el estilo de la barra de herramientas, los paneles y sus tamaños, y Mis herramientas. El tema y el idioma no forman parte de él. Guardar: escriba un nombre en Entornos de trabajo y elija Guardar mi configuración. Guardar con un nombre que ya existe reemplaza esa configuración. Cambiar: elija una configuración en la lista y luego Cambiar a esta. La aplicación se vuelve a cargar con esa configuración; si tiene trabajo sin guardar, la aplicación pregunta antes, como en cualquier recarga. Compartir: Exportar guarda la configuración elegida como un archivo .ccenv.json; Importar una configuración… agrega una desde un archivo. Un archivo solo fija estas opciones, nada más. Quitar: elija una configuración y luego Quitar. Puede guardar hasta doce configuraciones. Para probar primero los diseños incluidos, vea el diseño de caja de herramientas y cuadro de información y el diseño de cinta y explorador del proyecto.","lang":"es-MX"},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Working offline","summary":"Save an app for offline use, what works with no connection, and what needs one.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["offline","no internet","airplane","site visit","cache","service worker","install","download","connection"],"headings":[{"id":"save-an-app-for-offline-use","text":"Save an app for offline use","level":2},{"id":"what-works-offline","text":"What works offline","level":2},{"id":"what-needs-a-connection","text":"What needs a connection","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app&#039;s files saved first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-an-app-for-offline-use\"\u003ESave an app for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the app while you are online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for &quot;Offline ready&quot;. The app&#039;s files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDo this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don&#039;t reload it until you are online again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-works-offline\"\u003EWhat works offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis help, including search.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-needs-a-connection\"\u003EWhat needs a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGetting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E refreshes the saved copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks to the website, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E and links to other pages.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser&#039;s site data removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install, is on getcadcraft.com/download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app's files saved first. Save an app for offline use Open the app while you are online. Choose Save for offline in the bar at the top. Wait for \"Offline ready\". The app's files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time. Do this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it. You are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don't reload it until you are online again. What works offline Opening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking. Saving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer. This help, including search. What needs a connection Opening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use. Getting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and Save for offline refreshes the saved copy. Links to the website, such as Open on the website and links to other pages. Cloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet. Things to know A saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser's site data removes it. Private windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save. Safari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip. CadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install, is on getcadcraft.com/download.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Where your files go","summary":"Files stay on your computer, how saving works, and what the browser remembers.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["privacy","upload","cloud","save","download","storage","local","security","confidential"],"headings":[{"id":"your-files-stay-on-your-computer","text":"Your files stay on your computer","level":2},{"id":"how-saving-works","text":"How saving works","level":2},{"id":"what-the-browser-remembers","text":"What the browser remembers","level":2},{"id":"cloud-saving","text":"Cloud saving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-stay-on-your-computer\"\u003EYour files stay on your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. \u003Cstrong\u003EThe file is not uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E We never see it, and it works the same with no connection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-saving-works\"\u003EHow saving works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by \u003Cstrong\u003Edownloading\u003C/strong\u003E a new file:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as &quot;(edited)&quot; or &quot;(marked up)&quot;, so your original stays untouched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-browser-remembers\"\u003EWhat the browser remembers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app&#039;s help says what it keeps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cloud-saving\"\u003ECloud saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Your files stay on your computer When you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. The file is not uploaded. We never see it, and it works the same with no connection. How saving works A browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by downloading a new file: The download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see Browsers and computers). Apps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as \"(edited)\" or \"(marked up)\", so your original stays untouched. If you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"). What the browser remembers Some apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app's help says what it keeps. Cloud saving Cloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"assistants-recipes","scope":"basics","title":"Recipes for AI assistants","summary":"Worked examples for using your own AI assistant with the CadCraft apps: what to ask, the tools it calls, and the permissions each needs. Every recipe here is run by our tests with the same calls.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["ai","assistant","mcp","recipes","examples","prompts","automation","batch","convert","plot","check","takeoff","cut","fill","parameters","script"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"recipes","text":"Recipes","level":2},{"id":"tidy-a-drawing","text":"Tidy a drawing","level":3},{"id":"plot-a-set-of-sheets","text":"Plot a set of sheets","level":3},{"id":"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant","text":"Convert drawings for a consultant","level":3},{"id":"check-drawings-before-sending-them","text":"Check drawings before sending them","level":3},{"id":"ask-a-building-model-questions","text":"Ask a building model questions","level":3},{"id":"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft","text":"Draw a room in BimCraft","level":3},{"id":"total-a-takeoff","text":"Total a takeoff","level":3},{"id":"site-numbers","text":"Site numbers","level":3},{"id":"try-a-part-variant","text":"Try a part variant","level":3},{"id":"fill-in-a-title-block","text":"Fill in a title block","level":3},{"id":"your-files-and-your-assistant","text":"Your files and your assistant","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReady-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps&#039; tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are two ways in. In \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). \u003Cstrong\u003EWithout a window\u003C/strong\u003E, the CadCraft file server for assistants (\u003Ccode\u003Enpx @getcadcraft/mcp --root &lt;folder&gt;\u003C/code\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"recipes\"\u003ERecipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tidy-a-drawing\"\u003ETidy a drawing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_open {path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg&quot;}            → d1\ndrawing_describe {doc: &quot;d1&quot;}                                    layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses\ncommand_run {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, input: &quot;PURGE&quot;}                         one undo step: &quot;Assistant: PURGE&quot;\ndrawing_save {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plot-a-set-of-sheets\"\u003EPlot a set of sheets\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_open {path: …}  ·  drawing_describe {doc}                the sheets of each drawing\ndrawing_plot {doc, layout: &quot;&lt;sheet&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Plots/&lt;drawing&gt; &lt;sheet&gt;.pdf&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant\"\u003EConvert drawings for a consultant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_convert {path: &quot;Kitchen/&lt;file&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Out/&lt;file&gt;.dwg&quot;, version: &quot;2013&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"check-drawings-before-sending-them\"\u003ECheck drawings before sending them\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [&quot;0&quot;]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. Keep your office&#039;s rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json\u003C/code\u003E (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ask-a-building-model-questions\"\u003EAsk a building model questions\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eifc_open {path: &quot;house.ifc&quot;}                                    → d1\nifc_query {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, class: &quot;IfcDoor&quot;}                         each door with its level\nifc_properties {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, id: &lt;door&gt;}                          Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. In Studio, BimCraft&#039;s own tools answer the same about the model you have open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft\"\u003EDraw a room in BimCraft\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ewalls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true}     one undo step\nopening_add {wall: &quot;&lt;the first wall&#039;s GlobalId&gt;&quot;, kind: &quot;door&quot;}                    one undo step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_new\u003C/code\u003E starts a building first and \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_save\u003C/code\u003E writes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"total-a-takeoff\"\u003ETotal a takeoff\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_open {path: &quot;job.takeoff.json&quot;}  ·  takeoff_estimate {doc}  ·  takeoff_export {doc, out: &quot;job.xlsx&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"site-numbers\"\u003ESite numbers\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How much cut and fill does the pad need?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esite_open {path: &quot;site.civilcraft.json&quot;}  ·  surface_volume {doc}       cut, fill and net, per pad\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. With a level instead of a design: \u003Ccode\u003Esurface_volume {doc, datum: 100}\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"try-a-part-variant\"\u003ETry a part variant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epart_open {path: &quot;screw.partcraft.json&quot;}  ·  params_list {doc}\nparam_set {doc, name: &quot;l&quot;, value: 40}                           one undo step, the part rebuilt\nmass_properties {doc}                                            volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit; Save (\u003Ccode\u003Epart_save\u003C/code\u003E) to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fill-in-a-title-block\"\u003EFill in a title block\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eblock_insert {doc, block: &quot;TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD&quot;, at: [0, 0], attributes: {&quot;SHEET-NO&quot;: &quot;A-101&quot;, &quot;SHEET-TITLE&quot;: &quot;Kitchen plan&quot;}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-and-your-assistant\"\u003EYour files and your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps never send your files anywhere. \u003Cstrong\u003EBut what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant:\u003C/strong\u003E a cloud assistant&#039;s maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job&#039;s folders out of the ones you allow.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn Ready-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps' tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working. There are two ways in. In CadCraft Studio, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). Without a window, the CadCraft file server for assistants (npx @getcadcraft/mcp --root \u003Cfolder\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the cadcraft command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways. Start read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with. Recipes Tidy a drawing Ask: \"List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.\" The assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE: drawing_open {path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg\"} → d1 drawing_describe {doc: \"d1\"} layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses command_run {doc: \"d1\", input: \"PURGE\"} one undo step: \"Assistant: PURGE\" drawing_save {doc: \"d1\", path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg\"} Needs Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay. Plot a set of sheets Ask: \"Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_open {path: …} · drawing_describe {doc} the sheets of each drawing drawing_plot {doc, layout: \"\u003Csheet\u003E\", out: \"Plots/\u003Cdrawing\u003E \u003Csheet\u003E.pdf\"} Needs Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: cadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all. Convert drawings for a consultant Ask: \"Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_convert {path: \"Kitchen/\u003Cfile\u003E\", out: \"Out/\u003Cfile\u003E.dwg\", version: \"2013\"} Needs Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: cadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013. Check drawings before sending them Ask: \"Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.\" drawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [\"0\"]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}} Needs Read. Keep your office's rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: cadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script). Ask a building model questions Ask: \"How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?\" ifc_open {path: \"house.ifc\"} → d1 ifc_query {doc: \"d1\", class: \"IfcDoor\"} each door with its level ifc_properties {doc: \"d1\", id: \u003Cdoor\u003E} Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal Needs Read. In Studio, BimCraft's own tools answer the same about the model you have open. Draw a room in BimCraft Ask: \"Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.\" walls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true} one undo step opening_add {wall: \"\u003Cthe first wall's GlobalId\u003E\", kind: \"door\"} one undo step Needs Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, ifc_new starts a building first and ifc_save writes it. Total a takeoff Ask: \"Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.\" takeoff_open {path: \"job.takeoff.json\"} · takeoff_estimate {doc} · takeoff_export {doc, out: \"job.xlsx\"} Needs Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so. Site numbers Ask: \"How much cut and fill does the pad need?\" site_open {path: \"site.civilcraft.json\"} · surface_volume {doc} cut, fill and net, per pad Needs Read. With a level instead of a design: surface_volume {doc, datum: 100}. Try a part variant Ask: \"Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.\" part_open {path: \"screw.partcraft.json\"} · params_list {doc} param_set {doc, name: \"l\", value: 40} one undo step, the part rebuilt mass_properties {doc} volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia Needs Read and Edit; Save (part_save) to keep it. Fill in a title block Ask: \"Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.\" block_insert {doc, block: \"TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD\", at: [0, 0], attributes: {\"SHEET-NO\": \"A-101\", \"SHEET-TITLE\": \"Kitchen plan\"}} Needs Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults. Your files and your assistant The apps never send your files anywhere. But what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant: a cloud assistant's maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job's folders out of the ones you allow.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-together-live","scope":"basics","title":"Work together live","summary":"Share the file open in an app so teammates edit it with you on your own network with a short code, and join someone else's session. Invite links over the internet are not open yet.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["live","studio live","work together","collaborate","share live","join","code","qr","invite link","link","host","approve","guest","network","relay","license","many people","limit","allow anyway","slow","recent edits","edit log","who changed","history"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"share-the-file-you-have-open","text":"Share the file you have open","level":2},{"id":"let-people-in","text":"Let people in","level":2},{"id":"when-many-people-join","text":"When many people join","level":2},{"id":"codes-on-your-own-network","text":"Codes on your own network","level":2},{"id":"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet","text":"Invite links over the internet: not open yet","level":2},{"id":"join-someone-else-s-session","text":"Join someone else's session","level":2},{"id":"who-changed-what-recent-edits","text":"Who changed what: Recent edits","level":2},{"id":"assistants-in-a-session","text":"Assistants in a session","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app&#039;s own page about live sessions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-the-file-you-have-open\"\u003EShare the file you have open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E in the app&#039;s bar, type the name others should see, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare live\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStudio shows a code like \u003Ccode\u003EK7-Q4M2X\u003C/code\u003E with a picture to scan and the computer&#039;s address. Give the code to the people you want to invite.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-people-in\"\u003ELet people in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E lets them change the file with you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E turns them away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E shows everyone. Change a person&#039;s role there, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can&#039;t come back without a new invitation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWhen many people join\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA session&#039;s usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo allow more before anyone knocks, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow more people…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to 20\u003C/span\u003E puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"codes-on-your-own-network\"\u003ECodes on your own network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA code works for 10 minutes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew code\u003C/span\u003E makes another; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop inviting\u003C/span\u003E closes it. People already in stay.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne-time code\u003C/span\u003E lets one person in per code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESessions on your own network are free, for everyone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet\"\u003EInvite links over the internet: not open yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInvite links aren&#039;t open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E part of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen invite links open, this is how they will work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvite by link\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy the link and send it like a meeting link.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe link&#039;s secret part (after \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can&#039;t read, and you still approve each person. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew link\u003C/span\u003E makes the old one stop working at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop the link\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaking an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-someone-else-s-session\"\u003EJoin someone else&#039;s session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft Studio&#039;s home screen, find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin a live session\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host&#039;s link here too.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host&#039;s file once the host lets you in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout Studio, scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E, then under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin with the drawing open here\u003C/span\u003E type the code or the address and code, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin here\u003C/span\u003E. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host&#039;s drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can&#039;t find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-changed-what-recent-edits\"\u003EWho changed what: Recent edits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Live panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ERecent edits\u003C/strong\u003E: who made each change and what it was (the step&#039;s name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow more\u003C/span\u003E lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly what is selected\u003C/span\u003E keeps just the edits that touched it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is made in your own window from the session&#039;s edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants-in-a-session\"\u003EAssistants in a session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf someone turned on assistants in their app&#039;s settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host&#039;s own assistant joins at once; anyone else&#039;s waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host chooses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd the session\u003C/span\u003E, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave\u003C/span\u003E does the same for you alone.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app's own page about live sessions. Share the file you have open Open the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer. Choose Live in the app's bar, type the name others should see, then choose Share live. Studio shows a code like K7-Q4M2X with a picture to scan and the computer's address. Give the code to the people you want to invite. Your file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves. Let people in Nobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet: Edit lets them change the file with you. View only lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back. Deny turns them away. The list under In the session shows everyone. Change a person's role there, or Remove them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can't come back without a new invitation. When many people join A session's usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you. When someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose: Allow anyway lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your Edit, View only or Deny. Keep it at 20 turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small. To allow more before anyone knocks, choose Allow more people… under In the session. Back to 20 puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again. If things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps. Codes on your own network A code works for 10 minutes. New code makes another; Stop inviting closes it. People already in stay. One-time code lets one person in per code. After 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you. Sessions on your own network are free, for everyone. Invite links over the internet: not open yet Invite links aren't open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the Over the internet part of the Live panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link. When invite links open, this is how they will work: Under Over the internet, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then Invite by link. Copy the link and send it like a meeting link. The link's secret part (after #) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can't read, and you still approve each person. New link makes the old one stop working at once; Stop the link ends it. Making an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license. Join someone else's session In CadCraft Studio's home screen, find Join a live session. Type the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host's link here too.) Type your name and choose Join. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host's file once the host lets you in. Without Studio, scan the picture under the host's code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser. In CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose Live, then under Join with the drawing open here type the code or the address and code, and choose Join here. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host's drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is. Guest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can't find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet. Who changed what: Recent edits The Live panel lists Recent edits: who made each change and what it was (the step's name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. Show more lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and Only what is selected keeps just the edits that touched it. The list is made in your own window from the session's edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so. Assistants in a session If someone turned on assistants in their app's settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host's own assistant joins at once; anyone else's waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: Allow or Deny. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves. When the session ends The host chooses End the session, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. Leave does the same for you alone.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"pen-and-touch","scope":"basics","title":"Draw with a pen on a tablet","summary":"What the app does when you draw with a pen on a touch screen: it ignores your palm, can leave the drawing to the pen while your fingers move the view, and lets the pen's buttons and a two-finger tap undo.","section":"tasks","order":94,"keywords":["pen","stylus","tablet","touch","palm","palm rejection","finger","fingers","two fingers","tap","undo","redo","side button","barrel button","eraser","pressure","hover","phone"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time","text":"A pen is noticed the first time","level":2},{"id":"your-palm-is-ignored","text":"Your palm is ignored","level":2},{"id":"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view","text":"Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view","level":2},{"id":"undo-with-a-tap","text":"Undo with a tap","level":2},{"id":"the-pen-s-buttons","text":"The pen's buttons","level":2},{"id":"these-are-the-device-s-settings","text":"These are the device's settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time\"\u003EA pen is noticed the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-palm-is-ignored\"\u003EYour palm is ignored\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIgnore my palm while I use a pen\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view\"\u003ELet the pen draw and your fingers move the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw and select\u003C/span\u003E (the start): a finger does what the pen does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly move the view\u003C/span\u003E: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-with-a-tap\"\u003EUndo with a tap\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETap with two fingers to undo, three to redo\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-pen-s-buttons\"\u003EThe pen&#039;s buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen side button\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does (a right click)\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen eraser end\u003C/span\u003E (on pens that have one): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"these-are-the-device-s-settings\"\u003EThese are the device&#039;s settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E and resetting an app&#039;s settings leave them as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPen pressure and tilt don&#039;t change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen&#039;s pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in Settings › General › Pen and touch, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen. A pen is noticed the first time Nothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual. Your palm is ignored With Ignore my palm while I use a pen on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw. If a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work. Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view Under Fingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here: Draw and select (the start): a finger does what the pen does. Only move the view: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual. The toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way. Undo with a tap With Tap with two fingers to undo, three to redo on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap. The pen's buttons Pen side button: What the app does (a right click) at the start, or Undo or Redo. Pen eraser end (on pens that have one): What the app does at the start, or Undo or Redo. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches. These are the device's settings The pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. Reset the layout and resetting an app's settings leave them as they are. Not built yet Pen pressure and tilt don't change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen's pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"unit-lists","scope":"basics","title":"Units, typed values and the unit lists","summary":"Type a length in any unit with its mark, choose which units the unit lists show, and star the unit new work starts in. A file always keeps its own unit.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["units","unit list","metric","imperial","feet","inches","feet and inches","meters","millimeters","US survey foot","survey feet","international foot","yards","miles","nautical mile","chain","rod","link","furlong","fathom","point","pica","acre","hectare","square","roofing square","acre-foot","board foot","gallon","gon","grad","radian","degrees minutes seconds","slope","percent","H:V","rise run","default unit","star","INSUNITS"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"type-a-length-in-any-unit","text":"Type a length in any unit","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-units-the-lists-show","text":"Choose the units the lists show","level":2},{"id":"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used","text":"Where the lists and the star are used","level":2},{"id":"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit","text":"A file keeps its own unit","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-a-length-in-any-unit\"\u003EType a length in any unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit&#039;s mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ft 6 in\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet and inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E450 mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3.2 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.5 km\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2 dm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E400 nm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emetric lengths (\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is the nanometer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2.5 mi\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E250 mil\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyards, miles, thousandths of an inch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 US mi\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe US survey foot and mile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2 rd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100 li\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ch\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 fur\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erods, links, chains, furlongs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1 nmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1 NM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6 ftm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enautical miles, fathoms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12 pt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 pc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoints and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type \u003Ccode\u003Enmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ENM\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ENm\u003C/code\u003E is refused, because it could be either.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eft\u003C/code\u003E is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type \u003Ccode\u003EUS ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eusft\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Esurvey ft\u003C/code\u003E. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: \u003Ccode\u003E2*3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot; + 3 ch\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so \u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 and \u003Ccode\u003E10 ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 project feet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-units-the-lists-show\"\u003EChoose the units the lists show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear on the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E, and find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E. There is one list for each kind: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVolume\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file&#039;s unit, or the one for your region).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the unit lists\u003C/span\u003E puts the starting lists back, with no stars.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used\"\u003EWhere the lists and the star are used\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EApp\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat follows the switches\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the star sets\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s units list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModelCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list beside the value box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit of a new model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list on the plan toolbar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring and position units in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, and the unit in the calibrate dialog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring unit, until MarkupCraft&#039;s own settings have been saved\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit lists of an item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength and area: the unit of a new item\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe angle and slope lists are saved, but no app&#039;s box follows them yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit\"\u003EA file keeps its own unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give. Type a length in any unit In a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit's mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in. Type Means 12'6\", 12' 6 1/2\", 12'-6\", 3 ft 6 in feet and inches 450 mm, 3.2 m, 1.5 km, 2 dm, 400 nm metric lengths (nm is the nanometer) 3 yd, 2.5 mi, 250 mil yards, miles, thousandths of an inch 10 US ft, 1 US mi the US survey foot and mile 2 rd, 100 li, 3 ch, 1 fur rods, links, chains, furlongs 1 nmi or 1 NM, 6 ftm nautical miles, fathoms 12 pt, 3 pc points and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch) nm is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type nmi or NM. Nm is refused, because it could be either. ft is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type US ft, usft or survey ft. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones. Sums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: 2*3 yd, 12'6\" + 3 ch. In CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch. In CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so 10 US ft is 10 and 10 ft is 10 project feet. Choose the units the lists show Open Settings (the gear on the app bar, or Ctrl+,), choose General, and find Units. There is one list for each kind: Length, Area, Volume, Angle and Slope. Each unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps. Click the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file's unit, or the one for your region). Reset the unit lists puts the starting lists back, with no stars. The choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them. Where the lists and the star are used App What follows the switches What the star sets CadCraft The units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry CivilCraft The project's units list Length: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in ModelCraft The units list beside the value box The unit of a new model BimCraft The units list on the plan toolbar The display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept) MarkupCraft The measuring and position units in Settings, and the unit in the calibrate dialog The measuring unit, until MarkupCraft's own settings have been saved TakeoffCraft The unit lists of an item Length and area: the unit of a new item The angle and slope lists are saved, but no app's box follows them yet. A file keeps its own unit The settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in Settings.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"lost-toolbar","scope":"basics","title":"Where did my toolbar go?","summary":"The bars, menus or panels went missing. Here is what hides them on purpose, the keys that bring them back, and one button that puts the whole layout back.","section":"trouble","order":5,"keywords":["toolbar gone","toolbar missing","lost toolbar","lost menu","menu disappeared","ribbon disappeared","bars hidden","show bars","full screen","panels gone","reset layout","layout","ribbon","collapsed","locked toolbar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout","text":"The quickest fix: reset the layout","level":2},{"id":"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen","text":"The bars are hidden (full screen)","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-looks-different","text":"The toolbar looks different","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-s-own-menu","text":"The toolbar's own menu","level":2},{"id":"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small","text":"Everything is too big or too small","level":2},{"id":"the-panels-are-gone","text":"The panels are gone","level":2},{"id":"on-a-phone","text":"On a phone","level":2},{"id":"still-missing","text":"Still missing?","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ENothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout\"\u003EThe quickest fix: reset the layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear button in the app bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app&#039;s own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E in Studio&#039;s own window also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. Each app&#039;s windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen\"\u003EThe bars are hidden (full screen)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe screen-mode button in the app bar, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the bars are hidden, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave this mode, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScreen\u003C/span\u003E: the app does what is chosen there when it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab, or tap the top edge of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-looks-different\"\u003EThe toolbar looks different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools in another order, or tools you can&#039;t find:\u003C/strong\u003E someone arranged the toolbar. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOriginal order\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly icons, or only words:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar buttons\u003C/span\u003E in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools won&#039;t move:\u003C/strong\u003E the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E, or clear \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA ribbon that shows only its tab names:\u003C/strong\u003E it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar:\u003C/strong\u003E a menu style is on. Pick the app&#039;s own style under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › the app&#039;s tab › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E. Every command is in every style; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finds any of them by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-s-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone arranging\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, when it looks right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: a locked toolbar can&#039;t be arranged or reset by accident.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: tools back in their first order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon menu style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollapse the ribbon\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin the ribbon open\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a keyboard, the menu key (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F10\"\u003E⇧ F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens it for the tool that has the focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanel text size\u003C/span\u003E beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio&#039;s own window has one in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-panels-are-gone\"\u003EThe panels are gone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for a second window called after the app with &quot;panels&quot;, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanels\u003C/span\u003E, which brings every panel home.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-phone\"\u003EOn a phone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-missing\"\u003EStill missing?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind any tool by name with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app&#039;s name and what you were doing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back. The quickest fix: reset the layout Open Settings with the gear button in the app bar, or press Ctrl+,. Choose the General tab. Under Layout, choose Reset the layout. The toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app's own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under Menus, keys and mouse). In CadCraft Studio, Settings in Studio's own window also has Reset the layout in every app. Each app's windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout. The bars are hidden (full screen) The screen-mode button in the app bar, and Ctrl+Shift+F, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back. While the bars are hidden, a small Show bars tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press Ctrl+Shift+F to leave this mode, or Esc to leave full screen. If the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at Settings › General › Screen: the app does what is chosen there when it opens. Tap the Show bars tab, or tap the top edge of the screen. The toolbar looks different Tools in another order, or tools you can't find: someone arranged the toolbar. Choose Original order under Settings › General › Toolbar. Only icons, or only words: Toolbar buttons in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name. Tools won't move: the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose Unlock the toolbar, or clear Lock the toolbar under Settings › General › Toolbar. A ribbon that shows only its tab names: it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open. Menus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar: a menu style is on. Pick the app's own style under Settings › the app's tab › Menus, keys and mouse. Every command is in every style; Ctrl+K finds any of them by name. The toolbar's own menu Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu: Arrange the toolbar: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose Done arranging in the same menu, or press Esc, when it looks right. Lock the toolbar / Unlock the toolbar: a locked toolbar can't be arranged or reset by accident. Reset the toolbar: tools back in their first order. With a ribbon menu style: Collapse the ribbon or Pin the ribbon open. On a keyboard, the menu key (or Shift+F10) opens it for the tool that has the focus. Everything is too big or too small Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. Panel text size beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio's own window has one in its Settings too. The panels are gone Panels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window). Look for a second window called after the app with \"panels\", and choose Put the panels back in it. Or choose Reset layout under Settings › General › Panels, which brings every panel home. On a phone On a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The Tools button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and Move puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen. Still missing? Find any tool by name with Ctrl+K, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app's name and what you were doing.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does.","lang":"en"}],"locale":"es-MX","chain":["es-MX","es","en"]};
